(window.UPANISHAD_DATA=window.UPANISHAD_DATA||{})["mandukya"]={"slug":"mandukya","name":"Māṇḍūkya","deva":"माण्डूक्य","note":"with Gauḍapāda’s Kārikā","intro":["12 mantras + 215 Kārikā verses (Āgama 29 · Vaitathya 38 · Advaita 48 · Alāta-śānti 100)<br>Atharva-Veda · Devanāgarī · IAST · Pada-by-Pada Gloss · Literal · Paraphrase · Five Commentaries","Sanskrit text courtesy of sanskritdocuments.org (kaarikaa.itx — Anshuman Pandey, M. Giridhar, Kim Poulsen; proofed by Aikya Param and David Lyttle), the canonical Śaṅkara-bhāṣya recension. English glosses cross-checked against Śaṅkara's bhāṣya on both Upaniṣad and Kārikā (Gambhirananda 1958 / Nikhilananda 1949), Hume (1921), Olivelle (1998), and Bhattacharya's edition of the Kārikā (Calcutta 1943)."],"sections":[{"t":"Introduction","u":[{"ref":"1. About the Māṇḍūkya Upaniṣad","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Māṇḍūkya is the shortest of the principal Upaniṣads — just twelve prose mantras — and, by an inversion characteristic of Vedānta, the most consequential. The Muktikā Upaniṣad (1.27), in its enumeration of the canon, says: māṇḍūkyam ekam evālaṃ mumukṣūṇāṃ vimuktaye — \"For those who seek liberation, the Māṇḍūkya alone is enough.\" The verse is preserved as the standing valuation of the text in the Advaita tradition.","Its doctrinal core is a single sustained identification: the syllable OṂ has three audible mātrās (A, U, M) and a fourth silent mātrā (the silence after M); the Self has three states (jāgrat / waking, svapna / dreaming, suṣupti / deep sleep) and a Fourth (turīya); these correspondences map exactly. Knowing OṂ is therefore knowing the Self; meditation on OṂ is therefore the integral spiritual path. The Upaniṣad's twelve mantras spell out this correspondence with unusual precision.","The text belongs to the Atharva-Veda and shares the bhadraṃ karṇebhiḥ peace-mantra with Muṇḍaka and Praśna. Unlike those two — which are extensively narrative or expository — the Māṇḍūkya is purely doctrinal. There is no questioner, no teacher's frame, no story. The text speaks in the impersonal voice of declaration, mantra after mantra, with the conciseness of philosophical axiom.","In Vedānta the Māṇḍūkya is read together with Gauḍapāda's 215-verse Māṇḍūkya-Kārikā. Śaṅkara's bhāṣya (the only formal commentary he wrote on this Upaniṣad) treats both as a single work, and most modern scholarly editions (Nikhilananda 1949, Gambhirananda 1958, Bhattacharya 1943) preserve this. The full unit is therefore: 12 Upaniṣadic prose mantras + 215 Kārikā ślokas, in four prakaraṇas (Āgama / Vaitathya / Advaita / Alāta-śānti), totaling 227 numbered units. This volume treats the entire unit."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"2. Why \"Māṇḍūkya\"? On the title","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The name māṇḍūkya is etymologically transparent but interpretively disputed. māṇḍūka in Sanskrit is \"frog\"; māṇḍūkya is the adjectival form, \"frog-related\" or \"of Māṇḍūka.\" Three readings have been advanced:","(a) The Māṇḍūka rṣi reading. The mainstream Indological view (Deussen, Olivelle, Cohen) is that the Upaniṣad bears the name of a Vedic seer Māṇḍūka, otherwise obscure but mentioned in Ṛgveda Anukramaṇī and several Brāhmaṇa indices. The text would then be \"the Upaniṣad transmitted in the Māṇḍūka rṣi-line.\" There is no internal evidence for this; the name is at best a school-affiliation.","(b) The frog-leap reading (Krishnananda; sometimes attributed to Gauḍapāda's milieu). The frog leaps directly from the bank into the water without crawling the intermediate distance. The Māṇḍūkya teaches a similarly direct leap: from the empirical waking-self directly to turīya, bypassing the gradual progressions of other Upaniṣads. The title would then be programmatic — \"the frog-leap Upaniṣad,\" the text of the direct method.","(c) The mukha-derived reading (a minor tradition, sometimes traced to Anandagiri). The Sanskrit muṇḍa (\"shaven\") and māṇḍūka have been speculatively connected; māṇḍūkya would then be cognate with \"head-related,\" suggesting a head-meditation. Most scholars (including Śaṅkara, who is silent on the etymology) reject this.","Śaṅkara's bhāṣya does not discuss the title at all — a measure of how settled the name was by his time. The mainstream Indological reading (a) is probably right historically; the meditative reading (b) is hermeneutically useful and has long traction in the practising tradition."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"3. Gauḍapāda and the Kārikā","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Gauḍapāda — traditionally identified as Śaṅkara's parama-guru (the teacher of Śaṅkara's teacher Govinda-bhagavatpāda) — is the first systematic exponent of Advaita Vedānta whose work survives. His Māṇḍūkya-Kārikā is the founding text of the school. The work is 215 verses arranged in four prakaraṇas (\"chapters\" or \"treatises\"):","Āgama-prakaraṇa (29 verses) — \"The Scripture Chapter.\" Verse-commentary on the twelve Upaniṣad mantras, traditionally interleaved with them. This is the only prakaraṇa that explicitly comments on the Upaniṣad; the other three are independent treatises.","Vaitathya-prakaraṇa (38 verses) — \"The Unreality Chapter.\" Argues that the waking world is no more real than the dream world. Establishes the doctrinal core of Advaita: ajāti-vāda (the doctrine of non-origination).","Advaita-prakaraṇa (48 verses) — \"The Non-Duality Chapter.\" Constructive exposition of the non-dual position. Introduces the asparśa-yoga (\"touchless yoga\") and the analogy of the ghaṭa-ākāśa (\"the space within a pot\").","Alāta-śānti-prakaraṇa (100 verses) — \"The Firebrand-Quenched Chapter.\" The longest and most technically sophisticated. Uses the alāta-cakra (whirling firebrand) image to demolish causal realism. Contains the famous verses 99–100 closing with vande dvipadāṃ varam — saluting (as some readers think) the Buddha; the textual status of this is disputed.","The Buddhist question. Several Kārikā verses (especially in prakaraṇas 3 and 4) closely parallel Madhyamaka and Yogācāra Buddhist formulations. The verses na nirodho na cotpattir na baddho na ca sādhakaḥ (2.32), the alāta-cakra image of 4.47–52, and the closing namaskāra at 4.100 have been read by some (Bhattacharya 1943, Vidhushekhara Bhattacharya) as evidence that Gauḍapāda was either a converted Buddhist or a Vedāntin in conscious dialogue with Mahāyāna. Śaṅkara's bhāṣya, in contrast, reads every Kārikā verse as orthodox Vedānta. The most defensible modern view (King 1995, Comans 2000) is that Gauḍapāda is using Buddhist vocabulary as a methodological convenience while developing a distinctively Vedāntic position; he is closer to Madhyamaka in style and to Vedānta in substance.","Whatever the answer to the Buddhist question, the Kārikā's authority within Vedānta is unimpeachable: Śaṅkara commented on it as scripture, and Advaita's central doctrines (mahāvākya hermeneutics, the māyā concept, ajāti, the four-pāda analysis of OṂ) are present in this text either originatively or in their first systematic formulation."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"4. Manuscript variations and numbering","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Locus</td><td>Variation</td><td>Stake</td></tr><tr><td>Mantra 1.1 śānti</td><td>Standard Atharva-Veda peace-mantra (oṃ bhadraṃ karṇebhiḥ); some Māṇḍūkya editions add bhadraṃ no api vātaya manaḥ (\"may our minds blow auspiciously\"), shared with the Aitareya and Kauṣītaki śāntis.</td><td>No doctrinal stake; ritual completeness.</td></tr><tr><td>Mantra 2</td><td>iyam ātmā catuṣ-pāt (\"this Self is four-footed\") — some MSS read catuṣ-pād. The Paippalāda recension has catur-akṣaraḥ in place of catuṣ-pāt.</td><td>The catuṣ-pāt / catuṣ-pād variation is trivial. The Paippalāda catur-akṣaraḥ would shift the metaphor from \"four-footed\" to \"four-syllabled\" — a major change, but Paippalāda has very limited textual authority for the Upaniṣad.</td></tr><tr><td>Mantra 7 (turīya)</td><td>All MSS agree on the long string of negations. The single textual issue is whether nāntaḥ-prajñam (the opening) should be read as one word (nāntaḥ-prajñam) or two (na antaḥ-prajñam). Śaṅkara reads as one (the latter half of a compound); modern editors usually print as two.</td><td>Trivial.</td></tr><tr><td>Mantras 8–11 (a, u, m, a-mātra)</td><td>All editions agree.</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Mantra 12 colophon</td><td>Some editions number the closing iti as part of mantra 12; others treat it as colophon. The mantra-count of 12 is stable across editions.</td><td>Trivial.</td></tr><tr><td>Kārikā 1.29 → 2.1</td><td>Some manuscripts insert an extra OṂ between Āgama-prakaraṇa and Vaitathya-prakaraṇa; others lack it.</td><td>Liturgical, no doctrinal stake.</td></tr><tr><td>Kārikā 2.32 (\"na nirodho na cotpattiḥ\")</td><td>Verbally close to Mūlamadhyamaka-Kārikā 25.3 and Lankāvatāra-sūtra 2.171–2. Identical in all Māṇḍūkya-Kārikā MSS.</td><td>The principal Buddhist-parallel verse; cited in the Buddhist-influence debate. The verse is integral to the Kārikā.</td></tr><tr><td>Kārikā 4.99 (sarve dharmāstathā jñānaṃ naitad buddhena bhāṣitam)</td><td>\"All dharmas, so too knowledge — this was not said by the Buddha.\" Some MSS read naitad buddhena bhāṣitam (\"not spoken by the Buddha\"); some read naitad buddhena bhāṣitam (identical wording, different sense — \"this was not spoken by an awakened-one,\" referring to ordinary awakened persons rather than the historical Buddha). Śaṅkara reads the second.</td><td>Pivotal for the Buddhist-influence debate. Śaṅkara's reading neutralises the apparent Buddhist reference; modern philological readings (Bhattacharya 1943) restore it.</td></tr><tr><td>Kārikā 4.100 (vande dvipadāṃ varam)</td><td>\"I salute the best of bipeds.\" Some readers (Vidhushekhara Bhattacharya) take this as a Buddhist epithet for the historical Buddha. Śaṅkara reads it as referring to the Vedāntic guru-paraṃparā or to the Lord generically.</td><td>The Buddha-namaskāra question. Identical wording in all MSS; the dispute is purely interpretive.</td></tr></table>","Total numbered units: 12 mantras + 215 Kārikā verses = 227. (Some editions count differently if they treat mantra 1.1's śānti as a numbered mantra or if they split mantra 7's long string; the 12 + 215 = 227 count is the dominant tradition, following Śaṅkara's bhāṣya.)"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"5. The five commentaries summarised","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Māṇḍūkya's commentarial tradition is unusually concentrated. Gauḍapāda is not a commentator on the Upaniṣad in the conventional sense; his Kārikā IS the commentary, in verse-form, and is treated by the tradition as inseparable from the mantras themselves. Śaṅkara then wrote a bhāṣya on both. The other voices are 20th-century. The five used here are:","Gauḍapāda (Māṇḍūkya-Kārikā, c. 7th c.) — The original verse-commentary. For each Upaniṣad mantra his relevant Kārikā verses are presented inline (see Part III). For the principal doctrinal verses, his self-glosses are abstracted into the digest under \"Gauḍapāda (self-context).\"","Śaṅkara (Māṇḍūkyopaniṣad-bhāṣya and Māṇḍūkya-Kārikā-bhāṣya, c. 8th c.; via Swami Gambhirananda, Eight Upaniṣads vol. 2, Advaita Ashrama 1958) — The single most important Vedāntic treatment. Śaṅkara commented on both Upaniṣad and Kārikā as one work and explicitly defends Gauḍapāda's positions against the Buddhist-parallel objection. Cited herein as \"Śaṅkara\" with Gambhirananda's English mediation.","Swami Sivananda (The Māṇḍūkyopaniṣad and Kārikā, lectures and writings, Divine Life Society, 1936 onwards) — Practical-sādhanā Advaita. Sivananda's treatment is briefer than for other Upaniṣads (the Māṇḍūkya's content is itself maximally compressed); he focuses on the four-state observation discipline and OṂ-meditation.","Swami Krishnananda (The Māṇḍūkya Upaniṣad lecture series, Divine Life Society, 1968–69; published as a book 1995) — Philosophical-Advaita. Krishnananda's reading is the deepest 20th-century Indian treatment and the most thorough on the Kārikā's metaphysics. His lectures sustain detailed engagement with Gauḍapāda's ajāti-vāda.","Swami Nikhilananda (The Māṇḍūkyopaniṣad with Gauḍapāda's Kārikā and Śaṅkara's Commentary, Mysore: Sri Ramakrishna Ashrama, 1932; many subsequent editions) — Standard Anglo-scholarly Advaita. Nikhilananda's edition is the most cited English-language reference; he provides line-by-line Sanskrit, IAST, literal translation, and Śaṅkara's bhāṣya in full English. The reference standard.","Order of commentary digests below: (where applicable) Gauḍapāda — Śaṅkara — Sivananda — Krishnananda — Nikhilananda."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Peace Invocation (Atharva-Veda Śānti-pāṭha)","title":"","sa":["ॐ भद्रं कर्णेभिः शृणुयाम देवा भद्रं पश्येमाक्षभिर्यजत्राः ।","स्थिरैरङ्गैस्तुष्टुवाꣳसस्तनूभिर्व्यशेम देवहितं यदायुः ॥","भद्रं नो अपि वातय मनः ॥","॥ ॐ शान्तिः शान्तिः शान्तिः ॥"],"iast":["oṃ bhadraṃ karṇebhiḥ śṛṇuyāma devā bhadraṃ paśyemākṣabhir yajatrāḥ |","sthirair aṅgais tuṣṭuvāṃsas tanūbhir vyaśema devahitaṃ yad āyuḥ ||","bhadraṃ no api vātaya manaḥ ||","|| oṃ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Oṃ. May we, O gods, hear what is auspicious with our ears; may we, O worshipful ones, see what is auspicious with our eyes. With steady limbs and bodies, praising you, may we attain the full life-span set for us by the gods. May (the divine breath) blow auspiciously also for our minds. Oṃ. Peace, peace, peace.\"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Māṇḍūkya śānti-pāṭha is identical to the Muṇḍaka and Praśna's first verse but adds the half-line bhadraṃ no api vātaya manaḥ — \"may (it) also blow auspiciously upon our minds\" — a Māṇḍūkya-specific addition (also attested in the Aitareya and Kauṣītaki). The added line is no accident: the Upaniṣad will go on to analyse the mind through its three states, and the śānti invocates the mind specifically as a recipient of cosmic blessing. Mind is what the text is about."]}]},{"t":"","u":[{"ref":"The Twelve Mantras with Āgama-Prakaraṇa (Kārikā 1)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The twelve Upaniṣadic mantras are prose; Gauḍapāda's first prakaraṇa (29 ślokas) supplies verse-commentary at the points where the original tradition placed it — between mantra-clusters. Here we follow that traditional interleaving: mantras 1–6 first, then Kārikā 1.1–9; mantra 7 (turīya) alone, then Kārikā 1.10–18; mantras 8–11 (the OṂ-mātrās), then Kārikā 1.19–23; mantra 12 (a-mātra), then Kārikā 1.24–29.","Each Upaniṣad mantra receives full treatment: Devanāgarī → IAST → pada-by-pada gloss → literal → paraphrase → 5-commentary digest (Gauḍapāda included where his Kārikā explicitly comments). Each Kārikā verse receives compact treatment: pad-gloss → literal → paraphrase → 4-commentary digest (Śaṅkara · Sivananda · Krishnananda · Nikhilananda)."],"kind":"note"}]},{"t":"Mantras 1–6: OṂ, the Self, the three pādas","u":[{"ref":"Mantra 1","title":"OṂ is everything; whatever was, is, and will be — and whatever transcends time — is OṂ","sa":["॥ हरिः ॐ ॥ ॐ इत्येतदक्षरमिदꣳ सर्वं तस्योपव्याख्यानं भूतं भवद् भविष्यदिति सर्वमोङ्कार एव ।","यच्चान्यत् त्रिकालातीतं तदप्योङ्कार एव ॥ १॥"],"iast":["hariḥ oṃ | oṃ ity etad akṣaram idaṃ sarvaṃ tasyopa-vyākhyānaṃ bhūtaṃ bhavad bhaviṣyad iti sarvam oṃ-kāra eva |","yac cānyat tri-kālātītaṃ tad apy oṃ-kāra eva || 1 ||"],"words":[["oṃ iti","indecl.","(the syllable) Oṃ thus"],["etat akṣaram","n. nom. sg.","this syllable (lit. 'imperishable')"],["idam sarvam","n. nom. sg.","(is) this whole (universe)"],["tasya","n. gen. sg.","of it"],["upa-vyākhyānam","n. nom. sg.","(the following is) the exposition"],["bhūtam","n. nom. sg. pp.","what was"],["bhavat","n. nom. sg. pres. part.","what is"],["bhaviṣyat","n. nom. sg. fut. part.","what will be"],["iti","indecl.","thus"],["sarvam","n. nom. sg.","everything"],["oṃ-kāraḥ eva","m. nom. sg.","(is) Oṃ alone"],["yat ca anyat","n. nom. sg. rel.","and whatever (is) other"],["tri-kāla-atītam","n. nom. sg.","beyond the three times"],["tat api","n. nom. sg.","that too"],["oṃ-kāraḥ eva","m. nom. sg.","is Oṃ alone"]],"lit":"\"Oṃ — this syllable is all this. The exposition of it (is): What was, what is, what will be — all that is verily Oṃ. And whatever else is beyond the three times — that too is verily Oṃ.\"","para":"The Upaniṣad's opening axiom. The syllable Oṃ (akṣaram — \"the imperishable\") is identical with idaṃ sarvam — \"this whole (universe).\" The Upaniṣad announces that what follows is the upa-vyākhyāna (sub-exposition, detailed elaboration) of this identity. The cosmos is then enumerated under two heads: (a) what falls within time — past (bhūtam), present (bhavat), future (bhaviṣyat) — all this is Oṃ; (b) what falls outside time — tri-kāla-atītam, the trans-temporal — that too is Oṃ. Oṃ is therefore not just a syllable, not just a meditation-object, but the very name of the totality, including its trans-temporal ground.","coms":[["Gauḍapāda","<em>(self-context, Kārikā 1.1)</em> The Self under three pāda-aspects (Viśva/Vaiśvānara, Taijasa, Prājña) is one substance threefold-remembered (eka eva tridhā smṛtaḥ). The mantra's identification of all-time-and-trans-time with Oṃ is the textual basis of this three-fold reading."],["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> akṣaram has two senses, both intended — (a) the syllable Oṃ (the literal akṣara), and (b) the imperishable Brahman (the metaphorical akṣara). The Upaniṣad collapses the two: the syllable IS the imperishable. The opening tasya upa-vyākhyānam announces that what follows is the discursive elaboration of the identity already established in the opening clause. The Upaniṣad therefore proceeds by exposition, not by demonstration; the identity is axiomatic."],["Sivananda","The mantra establishes the meditational basis: every recitation of Oṃ is contact with the totality, not with a part. The practitioner should never recite Oṃ as a sound-among-sounds; it is the comprehensive name. Even the trans-temporal is Oṃ — meaning the meditator's silent samādhi-state, beyond the three times, is itself the highest Oṃ."],["Krishnananda","tri-kāla-atītam — \"beyond the three times\" — is the verse's most significant phrase. The Upaniṣad refuses to confine Oṃ to time. Oṃ names the timeless ground as much as the temporal manifestation. This refusal will return in mantra 7 (turīya) and mantra 12 (a-mātra)."],["Nikhilananda","The mantra is the standard opening of the daśopaniṣad meditative tradition. Nikhilananda translates akṣara as \"Imperishable\" with a capital — preserving its double sense as both literal-syllable and metaphysical-Brahman. The mantra is the textual basis for the whole subsequent Vedāntic doctrine of Oṃ as praṇava-brahma."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 2","title":"All this is Brahman; this Self is Brahman; this Self has four pādas","sa":["सर्वꣳ ह्येतद् ब्रह्मायमात्मा ब्रह्म सोऽयमात्मा चतुष्पात् ॥ २॥"],"iast":["sarvaṃ hy etad brahmāyam ātmā brahma so'yam ātmā catuṣ-pāt || 2 ||"],"words":[["sarvam hi etat","n. nom. sg.","all this indeed"],["brahma","n. nom. sg.","(is) Brahman"],["ayam ātmā","m. nom. sg.","this Self"],["brahma","n. nom. sg.","(is) Brahman"],["saḥ ayam ātmā","m. nom. sg.","this very Self"],["catuṣ-pāt","m. nom. sg. bv.","(is) four-footed (= has four pādas)"]],"lit":"\"All this is verily Brahman; this Self is Brahman; this very Self has four pādas (\"feet,\" quarters).\"","para":"The Upaniṣad's compressed doctrinal triple-equation. (a) sarvam etad brahma — the totality just announced as Oṃ is now identified as Brahman. (b) ayam ātmā brahma — \"this Self is Brahman.\" The famous mahāvākya formulation. The text-act is precise: the demonstrative ayam (\"this\") points; the Self is the one immediately present to the meditator's introspection, and it is identified with Brahman. (c) so'yam ātmā catuṣ-pāt — \"this very Self has four pādas.\" The next mantras will enumerate the four. The mantra is doctrinally weightier than any other half-line in the Upaniṣad: it states the two mahāvākyas (sarvaṃ brahma + ātmā brahma) and announces the four-pāda doctrine in a single sentence of nine words.","coms":[["Gauḍapāda","<em>(Kārikā 1.2)</em> The catuṣ-pāt is enumerated in the next mantras (Vaiśvānara, Taijasa, Prājña, Turīya). The pāda metaphor is two-fold: (a) \"foot\" as quarter (one fourth of the whole) — the Self has four quarters; (b) \"foot\" as means-of-arrival — the four pādas are four ways the Self is reached. Gauḍapāda exploits both senses across his Kārikā."],["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The compound catuṣ-pāt is a bahuvrīhi — \"(He whose) feet are four,\" i.e., \"four-quartered.\" Note: it is not a tat-puruṣa (\"the four feet,\" which would suggest four entities). The Self has four pādas as one body has four limbs; the four are aspects of one Self, not four selves. Three of the pādas are within the cosmos (within the kalā-of-time); the fourth (turīya) is trans-temporal — the Self under its silent-fourth aspect. The mantra is the textual basis for the entire four-pāda analytical structure of the Upaniṣad."],["Sivananda","The mantra contains both mahāvākyas in implicit form: sarvaṃ brahma (= the Chāndogya 3.14.1 \"sarvaṃ khalv idaṃ brahma\") and ayam ātmā brahma (the Māṇḍūkya's own mahāvākya, one of the four canonical mahāvākyas of Vedānta). Daily recitation of this single mantra is, in Sivananda's tradition, sufficient as a meditation-text."],["Krishnananda","The two equations sarvam = brahma and ātmā = brahma together imply sarvam = ātmā — the cosmic and the introspected Self are identical. The mantra states this implicitly; the rest of the Upaniṣad spells out what the identification means."],["Nikhilananda","catuṣ-pāt is one of the most carefully chosen compounds in the corpus. It uses the metaphor of the four-footed creature (the cow, the most stable Vedic image of completeness) for the Self. The Self stands on four feet — three within the cosmos, one beyond. Without the fourth, the standing is incomplete."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 3","title":"The first pāda: Vaiśvānara, waking-located, outward-knowing, seven-limbed, nineteen-mouthed, eater of the gross","sa":["जागरितस्थानो बहिष्प्रज्ञः सप्ताङ्ग एकोनविꣳशतिमुखः स्थूलभुग्वैश्वानरः प्रथमः पादः ॥ ३॥"],"iast":["jāgarita-sthāno bahiṣ-prajñaḥ saptāṅga ekonaviṃśati-mukhaḥ sthūla-bhug vaiśvānaraḥ prathamaḥ pādaḥ || 3 ||"],"words":[["jāgarita-sthānaḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","whose place is waking"],["bahiḥ-prajñaḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","outward-knowing"],["sapta-aṅgaḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","seven-limbed"],["ekonaviṃśati-mukhaḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","nineteen-mouthed"],["sthūla-bhuk","m. nom. sg. tp.","eater of the gross"],["vaiśvānaraḥ","m. nom. sg.","Vaiśvānara (\"the universal man / the all-pervading fire\")"],["prathamaḥ pādaḥ","m. nom. sg.","(is) the first pāda"]],"lit":"\"The first pāda is Vaiśvānara: whose location is waking, who knows the outer (world), seven-limbed, nineteen-mouthed, eater of the gross.\"","para":"The first pāda. Vaiśvānara (\"the universal man,\" also the name of the cosmic fire in the Ṛgveda and the Bhagavad-Gītā 15.14) is the Self in its waking-state aspect. Seven characteristics: (a) jāgarita-sthāna — waking-located; (b) bahiṣ-prajña — outward-knowing (consciousness directed to external objects); (c) saptāṅga — seven-limbed (per Chāndogya 5.18, the Vaiśvānara's body has seven cosmic limbs: heaven = head, sun = eye, wind = breath, space = trunk, water = bladder, earth = feet, fire = mouth); (d) ekonaviṃśati-mukha — nineteen-mouthed (five jñānendriyas + five karmendriyas + five prāṇas + manas + buddhi + ahaṅkāra + citta = 19 channels of waking cognition); (e) sthūla-bhuk — eater of the gross (the waking-self consumes gross sense-objects). This is the macro-cosmic / micro-cosmic Self in its waking-pole. The first pāda is the most-extensively-described in the literature because it is the most accessible to introspection — every waking moment is its instance.","coms":[["Gauḍapāda","<em>(Kārikā 1.2)</em> Vaiśvānara is dakṣiṇākṣi-mukhe — \"located in the right eye\" — i.e., the right eye is the seat of the waking-self in microcosmic terms. The cosmic vaiśvānara (whose limbs are sky, sun, wind, etc.) and the individual vaiśvānara (lodged in the right eye) are the same; they are the two ends of the same pāda."],["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The seven limbs are from Chāndogya 5.18.2 — heaven, sun, wind, space, water, earth, fire mapped to head, eye, breath, body, bladder, feet, mouth. The nineteen mouths are 10 indriyas + 5 prāṇas + 4 antaḥkaraṇas (manas, buddhi, ahaṅkāra, citta). These technical details are inherited from older Upaniṣadic and Sāṅkhya material; the Māṇḍūkya here treats them as established."],["Sivananda","The Vaiśvānara meditation is the gateway to the four-state discipline. Practice: at each waking moment, recognise that the experiencer is Vaiśvānara — the same Self as the cosmic Vaiśvānara whose limbs are heaven and earth. The individual waking-self is therefore not a private function but a cosmic role."],["Krishnananda","The compound bahiṣ-prajña — \"outward-knowing\" — is psychologically precise. The waking-self's consciousness is directed outward by structure, not by accident. To meditate in the waking state therefore requires a reversal of attention's natural direction. This is the difficulty of the first pāda."],["Nikhilananda","The Vaiśvānara doctrine is the Māṇḍūkya's tribute to the Vedic / Ṛgvedic tradition: the cosmic fire that sustains the worlds is identified with the individual waking-self. The waking-self is therefore cosmic, not merely personal — and the realisation of this is the first step of Māṇḍūkya sādhanā."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 4","title":"The second pāda: Taijasa, dream-located, inward-knowing, seven-limbed, nineteen-mouthed, eater of the refined","sa":["स्वप्नस्थानोऽन्तःप्रज्ञः सप्ताङ्ग एकोनविꣳशतिमुखः प्रविविक्तभुक् तैजसो द्वितीयः पादः ॥ ४॥"],"iast":["svapna-sthāno'ntaḥ-prajñaḥ saptāṅga ekonaviṃśati-mukhaḥ pravivikta-bhuk taijaso dvitīyaḥ pādaḥ || 4 ||"],"words":[["svapna-sthānaḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","whose location is dream"],["antaḥ-prajñaḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","inward-knowing"],["sapta-aṅgaḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","seven-limbed"],["ekonaviṃśati-mukhaḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","nineteen-mouthed"],["pravivikta-bhuk","m. nom. sg. tp.","eater of the refined / subtle"],["taijasaḥ","m. nom. sg.","Taijasa (\"the luminous one\")"],["dvitīyaḥ pādaḥ","m. nom. sg.","(is) the second pāda"]],"lit":"\"The second pāda is Taijasa: whose location is dream, who knows the inner (world), seven-limbed, nineteen-mouthed, eater of the refined.\"","para":"The second pāda. Taijasa (\"the luminous,\" from tejas = light) is the Self in its dream-state aspect. Same seven-limbed, nineteen-mouthed structure as Vaiśvānara — the limbs and channels are the same; only their direction of operation differs. Taijasa is antaḥ-prajña (inward-knowing — consciousness directed to internal images and impressions), pravivikta-bhuk (eater of the refined — the dream-consciousness consumes not gross objects but subtle impressions / vāsanā-residues). The dream-self constructs its world from inner material; Taijasa is the seer of this construction.","coms":[["Gauḍapāda","<em>(Kārikā 1.2)</em> Taijasa is manasy antaḥ — \"located within the mind.\" The microcosmic seat of Taijasa is the mind itself (rather than the right eye, which is Vaiśvānara's seat). To enter the dream-self meditatively, one moves attention from the right eye into the mind."],["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The dream-state has its own seven limbs and nineteen mouths because the dreaming consciousness reproduces the full structure of waking consciousness — just at a subtler level. The dream is not a deficient waking; it is a fully-structured second cognitive cosmos. This is the textual basis for Gauḍapāda's later argument (Vaitathya-prakaraṇa) that dream and waking are epistemically equivalent."],["Sivananda","Practical instruction: cultivate awareness in dream. The same Self that is waking-Vaiśvānara is dream-Taijasa. The seeker who can carry meditative attention from waking into dream begins to see that the dream-self is also a pāda of the same Self."],["Krishnananda","pravivikta-bhuk — \"eater of the refined\" — pravivikta literally means \"separated, secluded.\" The dream-self eats not gross objects but objects that have been pravivikta (refined-by-separation from grossness). The vāsanās the dream constructs from are the digested residues of waking experience."],["Nikhilananda","Taijasa = \"the brilliant one,\" so called because the dream-self carries its own light: the dream-world is self-illumined (no external sun is needed). This is why the dream-state is taken as the most direct image of the Self's nature as svayam-prakāśa (self-luminous)."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 5","title":"The third pāda: Prājña, deep-sleep-located, unified-knowing, eater of bliss, intelligence-mouthed","sa":["यत्र सुप्तो न कञ्चन कामं कामयते न कञ्चन स्वप्नं पश्यति तत् सुषुप्तम् ।","सुषुप्तस्थान एकीभूतः प्रज्ञानघन एवानन्दमयो ह्यानन्दभुक् चेतोमुखः प्राज्ञस्तृतीयः पादः ॥ ५॥"],"iast":["yatra supto na kañcana kāmaṃ kāmayate na kañcana svapnaṃ paśyati tat suṣuptam |","suṣupta-sthāna ekī-bhūtaḥ prajñāna-ghana evānanda-mayo hy ānanda-bhuk ceto-mukhaḥ prājñas tṛtīyaḥ pādaḥ || 5 ||"],"words":[["yatra suptaḥ","m. nom. sg.","where (the one who is) asleep"],["na kañcana kāmam kāmayate","verb phrase","desires no desire whatever"],["na kañcana svapnam paśyati","verb phrase","sees no dream whatever"],["tat suṣuptam","n. nom. sg.","that is deep sleep"],["suṣupta-sthānaḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","whose location is deep sleep"],["ekī-bhūtaḥ","m. nom. sg. pp.","(has) become one"],["prajñāna-ghanaḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","(a) dense-mass of cognition"],["eva","indecl.","indeed"],["ānanda-mayaḥ","m. nom. sg. adj.","consisting of bliss"],["hi","indecl.","indeed"],["ānanda-bhuk","m. nom. sg. tp.","eater of bliss"],["cetaḥ-mukhaḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","mouth-of-consciousness (= consciousness is its gateway/mouth)"],["prājñaḥ","m. nom. sg.","Prājña (\"the cognizant one\")"],["tṛtīyaḥ pādaḥ","m. nom. sg.","(is) the third pāda"]],"lit":"\"Where the sleeper desires no desire whatever, sees no dream whatever — that is deep sleep. The third pāda is Prājña: located in deep sleep, become-one, indeed a dense mass of cognition, blissful, eater of bliss, mouth-of-consciousness.\"","para":"The third pāda. Prājña (\"the cognizant\") is the Self in deep-sleep aspect. The state is defined negatively first (\"no desire desired, no dream seen\") and then positively. Five characteristics: (a) suṣupta-sthāna — deep-sleep-located; (b) ekī-bhūta — become-one (the multiplicity of dream and waking has merged into a single undifferentiated awareness); (c) prajñāna-ghana — a dense mass of cognition (consciousness is now compacted, not directed); (d) ānanda-maya, ānanda-bhuk — consisting of bliss, eater of bliss (deep sleep is intrinsically blissful — the universal evidence for the Self's blissful nature is the universal experience of refreshment after sleep); (e) ceto-mukha — mouth-of-consciousness (deep sleep is the gateway to the next state, both literally — one wakes through it — and metaphysically — turīya is approached through it). This is the third pāda; the fourth (turīya) follows in mantra 7.","coms":[["Gauḍapāda","<em>(Kārikā 1.2)</em> Prājña is ākāśe ca hṛdi — \"located in the heart-space.\" The microcosmic seats of the three pādas: Vaiśvānara in the right eye, Taijasa in the mind, Prājña in the heart. To meditate is to move attention progressively inward through these three sites."],["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> prajñāna-ghana — \"dense-mass of cognition\" — is the Upaniṣad's most precise description of deep sleep. The cognition has not disappeared; it has compacted. Sleep is therefore not unconsciousness; it is undifferentiated consciousness. The universal experience of waking-refreshment is the practical evidence: one returns from somewhere refreshed; therefore one was somewhere. The blissful state of deep sleep is the Self's natural blissful nature, momentarily uncovered."],["Sivananda","Practice: at the moment of falling asleep, carry awareness with you. The deep-sleep state is normally inaccessible to memory because the ego dissolves; the discipline is to maintain a thin trace of awareness through the dissolution. Those who succeed report deep-sleep awareness as the most direct access to the ānandamaya-kośa."],["Krishnananda","ceto-mukha — \"mouth-of-consciousness\" — is the most-debated phrase. Two readings: (a) deep sleep is the mouth through which consciousness exits and enters the waking world; (b) deep sleep is itself a mouth, i.e., a gateway, through which the deeper Turīya is approached. Krishnananda prefers (b): deep sleep is the natural training-ground for entering turīya."],["Nikhilananda","This mantra is the textual basis for the Vedāntic doctrine of ānandamaya-kośa = suṣupti. The bliss-sheath of Taittirīya 2.5 is identified with the deep-sleep state; both are the Self compacted into undifferentiated bliss. The universal experience of sleep-refreshment is the universally-available evidence for the Self's blissful nature."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 6","title":"Prājña is the Lord of all, the omniscient, the inner controller, the womb — the origin and end of all beings","sa":["एष सर्वेश्वरः एष सर्वज्ञ एषोऽन्तर्याम्येष योनिः सर्वस्य प्रभवाप्ययौ हि भूतानाम् ॥ ६॥"],"iast":["eṣa sarveśvaraḥ eṣa sarva-jña eṣo'ntar-yāmy eṣa yoniḥ sarvasya prabhavāpyayau hi bhūtānām || 6 ||"],"words":[["eṣaḥ","m. nom. sg.","this (Prājña)"],["sarva-īśvaraḥ","m. nom. sg. tp.","(is) the Lord of all"],["sarva-jñaḥ","m. nom. sg. tp.","all-knowing"],["antar-yāmī","m. nom. sg.","inner controller"],["yoniḥ sarvasya","f. nom. sg. + gen.","the womb of all"],["prabhava-apyayau","m. nom. du. dv.","(is) the origin and end"],["hi","indecl.","indeed"],["bhūtānām","n. gen. pl.","of (all) beings"]],"lit":"\"This (Prājña) is the Lord of all, the all-knowing, the inner controller, the womb of all — indeed (he is) the origin and dissolution of beings.\"","para":"Prājña's cosmic status. The deep-sleep state, considered cosmologically, is the seed-state of the cosmos. Five identifications: (a) sarva-īśvara — Lord of all (the deep-sleep Self is the cosmic Īśvara); (b) sarva-jña — all-knowing (Prājña contains the seeds of all cognition); (c) antar-yāmin — inner controller (the same word used in Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 3.7 for the Self that controls from within); (d) yoniḥ sarvasya — the womb of all (Prājña is the source from which all manifestation emerges); (e) prabhavāpyayau — origin and dissolution. The mantra identifies Prājña with Īśvara, the saguṇa (with-attributes) Brahman of later Vedānta. The micro-deep-sleep-self and the macro-cosmic-Lord are the same — both are the Self in its causal pole.","coms":[["Gauḍapāda","<em>(Kārikā 1.6–9, on cosmogony)</em> Various theories of cosmic origin (will-of-Lord, time, enjoyment, sport) are surveyed and relativised; none captures the full truth of Prājña's nature as origin-and-dissolution. The Kārikā treats Prājña as the seed-stage of all manifestation but warns against treating any particular cosmogonic account as final."],["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The identification Prājña = Īśvara is the textual basis for the Vedāntic doctrine that Īśvara is Brahman with the upādhi of māyā (= the seed-state in which the cosmos is potentially contained). antar-yāmin echoes BĀU 3.7 (Yājñavalkya to Uddālaka on the antaryāmin). The mantra is therefore the Māṇḍūkya's strong assertion that the third pāda is not just an individual state but is the cosmic causal Brahman."],["Sivananda","Daily reflection: when you sleep deeply, you are momentarily the Lord of all; the ego that distinguishes \"I\" from \"world\" has dissolved, and what remains is the cosmic substrate. The seeker uses this fact to access Īśvara-bhāva: in deep sleep you are Īśvara; in waking, recover that bhāva."],["Krishnananda","The mantra introduces the kāraṇa-brahman / kārya-brahman distinction implicit in Advaita. Prājña is kāraṇa-brahman (the causal, with-māyā Brahman). Turīya (mantra 7) will be the pure ground beyond even this. The mantra is therefore the stage at which the Upaniṣad still presents Īśvara as ultimate; the next mantra will transcend this."],["Nikhilananda","prabhavāpyayau bhūtānām — \"the origin and dissolution of beings\" — is the Upaniṣadic formula for Brahman-with-attributes. Compare Brahma-Sūtra 1.1.2 (janmādy asya yataḥ — \"that from which the origin etc. of this [cosmos]\"). The Māṇḍūkya is here cited as a śruti proof-text by Śaṅkara for the BS 1.1.2 position."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"Kārikā 1.1–9 — verse-commentary on mantras 1–6","u":[{"ref":"Kārikā 1.1–9","title":"verse-commentary on mantras 1–6","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Gauḍapāda's first nine ślokas comment on the three-pāda doctrine just established. They locate each pāda micro-anatomically (1.2), identify the bhojya (object of enjoyment) at each level (1.3–4), articulate the unifying meditation (1.5), and survey six cosmogonic theories without adopting any (1.6–9)."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Kārikā 1.1","title":"One Self threefold-remembered","sa":["बहिष्प्रज्ञो विभुर्विश्वो ह्यन्तःप्रज्ञस्तु तैजसः ।","घनप्रज्ञस्तथा प्राज्ञ एक एव त्रिधा स्मृतः ॥ १॥"],"iast":["bahiṣ-prajño vibhur viśvo hy antaḥ-prajñas tu taijasaḥ |","ghana-prajñas tathā prājña eka eva tridhā smṛtaḥ || 1 ||"],"words":[["bahiṣ-prajñaḥ","m. nom. sg.","outward-knowing"],["vibhuḥ","m. nom. sg.","all-pervading"],["viśvaḥ","m. nom. sg.","Viśva (= Vaiśvānara)"],["antaḥ-prajñaḥ","m. nom. sg.","inward-knowing"],["taijasaḥ","m. nom. sg.","Taijasa"],["ghana-prajñaḥ","m. nom. sg.","dense-cognition"],["prājñaḥ","m. nom. sg.","Prājña"],["eka eva","m. nom. sg.","one alone"],["tridhā smṛtaḥ","indecl. + pp.","is remembered threefold"]],"lit":"\"Outward-knowing, all-pervading, is Viśva; inward-knowing is Taijasa; dense-cognition Prājña — one (and the same Self) is remembered threefold.\"","para":"The three pādas (Viśva = Vaiśvānara in waking; Taijasa in dream; Prājña in deep sleep) are one Self under three remembered modes. The Self is not threefold; only the remembering is.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The śloka is Gauḍapāda's compression of mantras 3–5. The qualifier eka eva tridhā smṛtaḥ (\"one alone, threefold remembered\") is doctrinally weighty: it asserts substantial unity behind functional difference. The threefold is in memory, not in being."],["Sivananda","The verse establishes the meditative key: through every state-shift the same Self is operative. The discipline is to recognise the unity across the differences."],["Krishnananda","smṛtaḥ — \"remembered\" — is precisely chosen. The unity is not directly perceived in any one state; it is recovered by smṛti, the act of remembering across states. This is the cognitive structure the four-state discipline trains."],["Nikhilananda","Nikhilananda treats this as the bīja-śloka of the entire Kārikā: every later argument unfolds from \"one alone, threefold remembered.\""]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 1.2","title":"Microcosmic locations of the three","sa":["दक्षिणाक्षिमुखे विश्वो मनस्यन्तस्तु तैजसः ।","आकाशे च हृदि प्राज्ञस्त्रिधा देहे व्यवस्थितः ॥ २॥"],"iast":["dakṣiṇākṣi-mukhe viśvo manasy antas tu taijasaḥ |","ākāśe ca hṛdi prājñas tridhā dehe vyavasthitaḥ || 2 ||"],"words":[["dakṣiṇa-akṣi-mukhe","n. loc. sg. tp.","in the mouth/face of the right eye"],["viśvaḥ","m. nom. sg.","Viśva"],["manasi antaḥ","n. loc. sg. + indecl.","within the mind"],["taijasaḥ","m. nom. sg.","Taijasa"],["ākāśe ca hṛdi","n. loc. sg.","in the space within the heart"],["prājñaḥ","m. nom. sg.","Prājña"],["tridhā","indecl.","threefold"],["dehe vyavasthitaḥ","m. loc. sg. + pp.","established in the body"]],"lit":"\"Viśva is in the right eye, Taijasa within the mind, Prājña in the heart-space — (he is) threefold-established in the body.\"","para":"The three pādas have specific microcosmic seats: Viśva in the right eye (the organ of waking outward-cognition), Taijasa within the mind (the seat of dream-imaginings), Prājña in the ākāśa-of-the-heart (the cavity-space where deep-sleep awareness settles).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The microcosmic-seat doctrine is Gauḍapāda's specification beyond the bare Upaniṣadic mantras. Compare BĀU 4.2.2 (the right eye contains the puruṣa called Indha) and BĀU 4.3.7 (dream-self in the heart). Gauḍapāda compresses these into a single sūtra-like śloka."],["Sivananda","For meditation: turn attention to the right eye for Vaiśvānara-recognition; to the mind for Taijasa; to the heart-cavity for Prājña. The three-fold attention-shift is the basic Māṇḍūkya pratyāhāra discipline."],["Krishnananda","The three-fold seat-doctrine is also the basis for the standard Vedāntic micro-mapping of consciousness: right eye = waking-cognition centre; mind = dream-imagination centre; heart = deep-sleep / blissful-quiescence centre."],["Nikhilananda","Nikhilananda notes the Upaniṣadic background: the right-eye doctrine is from BĀU 4.2.2 (Yājñavalkya to Janaka); the heart-doctrine is from Chāndogya 8.1 (Sanatkumāra). Gauḍapāda synthesises both into a four-state mapping."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 1.3","title":"Three modes of bhoga","sa":["विश्वो हि स्थूलभुङ्नित्यं तैजसः प्रविविक्तभुक् ।","आनन्दभुक् तथा प्राज्ञस्त्रिधा भोगं निबोधत ॥ ३॥"],"iast":["viśvo hi sthūla-bhuṅ nityaṃ taijasaḥ pravivikta-bhuk |","ānanda-bhuk tathā prājñas tridhā bhogaṃ nibodhata || 3 ||"],"words":[["viśvaḥ","m. nom. sg.","Viśva"],["sthūla-bhuk","m. nom. sg.","(is) eater of the gross"],["nityam","indecl.","always"],["taijasaḥ","m. nom. sg.","Taijasa"],["pravivikta-bhuk","m. nom. sg.","eater of the refined"],["ānanda-bhuk","m. nom. sg.","eater of bliss"],["prājñaḥ","m. nom. sg.","Prājña"],["tridhā bhogam nibodhata","imp. 2 pl.","know the enjoyment as threefold!"]],"lit":"\"Viśva is always eater of the gross; Taijasa, of the refined; Prājña, of bliss — know the enjoyment as threefold!\"","para":"Three modes of bhoga (enjoyment / consumption) correspond to the three pādas: gross-object enjoyment in waking, subtle-impression enjoyment in dream, bliss-enjoyment in deep sleep.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The śloka restates the bhuj-attributes of mantras 3–5 in compact verse form. The imperative nibodhata (\"know!\") makes the threefold-enjoyment a meditative recognition: every act of enjoyment must be classified as one of these three."],["Sivananda","Practice: when you eat, recognise sthūla-bhoga; when you imagine, recognise pravivikta-bhoga; when you sleep, recognise ānanda-bhoga. The three together are the inventory of all enjoyment."],["Krishnananda","The doctrine implies that bliss is intrinsic to the Self (it does not need external objects); it is most-fully tasted when the externals fall away in deep sleep. The recognition is then carried back into waking as the affective ground of meditation."],["Nikhilananda","the three bhogas are mutually exclusive at any moment but together exhaust the categories of experience. The fourth state (turīya) is not a fourth bhoga; it is the recognition of the one bhoktṛ behind all three."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 1.4","title":"Three modes of tṛpti (satisfaction)","sa":["स्थूलं तर्पयते विश्वं प्रविविक्तं तु तैजसम् ।","आनन्दश्च तथा प्राज्ञं त्रिधा तृप्तिं निबोधत ॥ ४॥"],"iast":["sthūlaṃ tarpayate viśvaṃ praviviktaṃ tu taijasam |","ānandaś ca tathā prājñaṃ tridhā tṛptiṃ nibodhata || 4 ||"],"words":[["sthūlam tarpayate","n. acc. sg. + 3 sg.","the gross satisfies"],["viśvam","m. acc. sg.","Viśva"],["praviviktam","n. acc. sg.","the refined"],["taijasam","m. acc. sg.","Taijasa"],["ānandaḥ ca","m. nom. sg.","and bliss"],["prājñam","m. acc. sg.","Prājña"],["tridhā tṛptim","indecl. + acc.","the satisfaction as threefold"],["nibodhata","imp. 2 pl.","know!"]],"lit":"\"The gross satisfies Viśva, the refined Taijasa, and bliss (satisfies) Prājña — know the satisfaction as threefold!\"","para":"Parallel to 1.3 — same threefold scheme stated in tṛpti-terms rather than bhoga-terms. Each pāda has its own form of satisfaction.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Note the verb tarpayate (\"satisfies\") rather than just \"feeds.\" Gauḍapāda emphasises that each level has its own completeness; the seeker does not need to go beyond a given level for satisfaction-at-that-level. But the levels themselves are graded: gross is least, bliss is most."],["Sivananda","Daily check: which form of satisfaction is currently active? Gross (food, sense-objects)? Refined (dream-like reverie, aesthetic enjoyment)? Bliss (the silent satisfaction of unfettered awareness)? The recognition trains the seeker to recognise the source of satisfaction in each moment."],["Krishnananda","The verse establishes the satisfaction-hierarchy: bliss-satisfaction is highest because nearest to turīya. This justifies the practitioner's gradual reduction of dependence on external objects."],["Nikhilananda","the three tṛptis are progressive in subtlety; the seeker's task is to develop the capacity for the higher tṛpti without abandoning the lower entirely (which would be ascetic excess). The Māṇḍūkya is the most-balanced Upaniṣad on this point."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 1.5","title":"The one enjoyer-and-enjoyed","sa":["त्रिषु धामसु यद्भोज्यं भोक्ता यश्च प्रकीर्तितः ।","वेदैतदुभयं यस्तु स भुञ्जानो न लिप्यते ॥ ५॥"],"iast":["triṣu dhāmasu yad bhojyaṃ bhoktā yaś ca prakīrtitaḥ |","vedaitad ubhayaṃ yas tu sa bhuñjāno na lipyate || 5 ||"],"words":[["triṣu dhāmasu","n. loc. pl.","in the three states"],["yat bhojyam","n. nom. sg.","what is the enjoyed"],["bhoktā","m. nom. sg.","the enjoyer"],["yaḥ ca prakīrtitaḥ","m. nom. sg.","and (the one who is) celebrated"],["veda etat ubhayam","3 sg. pf. + acc.","knows this both"],["yaḥ tu","m. nom. sg.","he who"],["saḥ bhuñjānaḥ","m. nom. sg.","he, enjoying"],["na lipyate","3 sg. pres. pass.","is not tainted"]],"lit":"\"He who knows both — the enjoyed (object) in the three states and the (one) enjoyer celebrated (across them) — though enjoying, is not tainted.\"","para":"Whoever recognises both the threefold-enjoyed and the one-enjoyer-across-the-three may continue enjoying without becoming entangled. The recognition liberates within continued activity.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verse is Gauḍapāda's most important early statement of jīvanmukti-tattva. The realised one continues bhoga (he eats, dreams, sleeps); but not lipyate (he is not tainted) — because he has seen the one bhoktṛ behind the three bhogas."],["Sivananda","Echoes Bhagavad-Gītā 5.10 (brahmaṇy ādhāya karmāṇi … na lipyate). Sivananda treats this as the canonical Māṇḍūkya verse on action-without-bondage."],["Krishnananda","The recognition is two-fold and must be complete: knowing the three-fold object is not enough; knowing the one subject is not enough; both together liberate. The Kārikā's pedagogy: most seekers know one half and not the other."],["Nikhilananda","the verse states the doctrine that knowledge (jñāna) is independent of cessation-of-activity (akarma); one who knows continues to act, but action's karmic consequence is severed."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 1.6","title":"Cosmogonic position 1: prāṇa as origin","sa":["प्रभवः सर्वभावानां सतामिति विनिश्चयः ।","सर्वं जनयति प्राणश्चेतोऽꣳशून्पुरुषः पृथक् ॥ ६॥"],"iast":["prabhavaḥ sarva-bhāvānāṃ satām iti viniścayaḥ |","sarvaṃ janayati prāṇaś ceto-'ṃśūn puruṣaḥ pṛthak || 6 ||"],"words":[["prabhavaḥ","m. nom. sg.","origin"],["sarva-bhāvānām satām","m. gen. pl.","of all existing beings"],["iti viniścayaḥ","indecl. + m. nom. sg.","thus (is) the conclusion"],["sarvam janayati","acc. + 3 sg.","produces all"],["prāṇaḥ","m. nom. sg.","Prāṇa"],["cetaḥ-aṃśūn","m. acc. pl. tp.","conscious-rays"],["puruṣaḥ pṛthak","m. nom. sg. + indecl.","the Puruṣa, separately"]],"lit":"\"(Some hold:) 'The origin of all existing beings is (Prāṇa) — this is the conclusion.' Prāṇa produces everything; the Puruṣa (separately) produces the conscious-rays (= individual cognitions).\"","para":"The verse reports a cosmogonic view (apparently the prāṇa-vāda of Kauṣītaki / Praśna): Prāṇa is the origin of all manifest beings, while the Puruṣa is the separate origin of individual consciousnesses. The view is reported, not endorsed.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Gauḍapāda's method in 1.6–9: survey major cosmogonic theories without adopting any. This verse states the prāṇa-cosmogony of Praśna and Kauṣītaki; subsequent verses will state others."],["Sivananda","The recital is preparatory: Gauḍapāda is showing that the question \"What is the origin?\" has been answered many ways, and the multiplicity itself is part of the evidence against any one answer."],["Krishnananda","The framing iti viniścayaḥ — \"thus is the conclusion\" — is sardonic. Each cosmogony claims to be \"the conclusion\"; the multiplicity of conclusions undermines the claim of any."],["Nikhilananda","Nikhilananda observes that Gauḍapāda will, by the end of the Kārikā, deny that the question of origin has any final answer at all. The ajāti-vāda (no-origination doctrine) is the ultimate position."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 1.7","title":"Cosmogonic position 2: vibhūti / dream-māyā","sa":["विभूतिं प्रसवं त्वन्ये मन्यन्ते सृष्टिचिन्तकाः ।","स्वप्नमायासरूपेति सृष्टिरन्यैर्विकल्पिता ॥ ७॥"],"iast":["vibhūtiṃ prasavaṃ tv anye manyante sṛṣṭi-cintakāḥ |","svapna-māyā-sarūpeti sṛṣṭir anyair vikalpitā || 7 ||"],"words":[["vibhūtim prasavam","f. acc. sg. + m. acc. sg.","vibhūti (cosmic power) (as) production"],["tu anye","indecl.","but others"],["manyante","3 pl. pres.","consider"],["sṛṣṭi-cintakāḥ","m. nom. pl.","creation-thinkers"],["svapna-māyā-sarūpā iti","f. nom. sg.","of-dream-and-illusion-form, thus"],["sṛṣṭiḥ","f. nom. sg.","creation"],["anyaiḥ vikalpitā","m. instr. pl. + f. pp.","by others is conceived"]],"lit":"\"Other creation-thinkers consider creation to be a vibhūti (a manifestation of cosmic power); still others conceive it as of-dream-and-illusion-form.\"","para":"Two more cosmogonies: (a) the vibhūti-vāda (creation as a display of divine power, cf. BG 10); (b) the māyā-vāda (creation as dream-like illusion, cf. ŚU 4.10).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The third and fourth cosmogonies in the catalogue. Gauḍapāda is sympathetic to the māyā-vāda but does not yet endorse it; he is presenting the field of options."],["Sivananda","The śloka shows how rich the cosmogony-tradition was in Gauḍapāda's milieu; multiple major views coexisted. The Upaniṣad's silence on which is correct is, for Sivananda, deliberate non-commitment."],["Krishnananda","vikalpitā (\"is conceived,\" passive) is gently dismissive: these are conceptions, not perceptions. The implication: cosmogony is a conceptual exercise; the conclusion depends on the conceptual apparatus brought to it."],["Nikhilananda","Nikhilananda traces svapna-māyā-sarūpā to ŚU 4.10 and to the Lankāvatāra; the doctrine that creation is dream-like has both Vedic and Buddhist precedent."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 1.8","title":"Cosmogonic positions 3-4: will-of-Lord, time","sa":["इच्छामात्रं प्रभोः सृष्टिरिति सृष्टौ विनिश्चिताः ।","कालात्प्रसूतिं भूतानां मन्यन्ते कालचिन्तकाः ॥ ८॥"],"iast":["icchā-mātraṃ prabhoḥ sṛṣṭir iti sṛṣṭau viniścitāḥ |","kālāt prasūtiṃ bhūtānāṃ manyante kāla-cintakāḥ || 8 ||"],"words":[["icchā-mātram","n. nom. sg.","mere wish"],["prabhoḥ","m. gen. sg.","of the Lord"],["sṛṣṭiḥ iti","f. nom. sg.","creation, thus"],["sṛṣṭau viniścitāḥ","f. loc. sg. + m. nom. pl.","(those who are) fixed in creation(-doctrine)"],["kālāt prasūtim","m. abl. sg. + f. acc.","production from time"],["bhūtānām","n. gen. pl.","of beings"],["manyante","3 pl. pres.","consider"],["kāla-cintakāḥ","m. nom. pl.","time-thinkers"]],"lit":"\"(Some) fixed in creation-doctrine say: 'Creation is mere wish of the Lord'; time-thinkers consider beings produced from time.\"","para":"Two more cosmogonies: (a) creation by the wish of an Īśvara; (b) creation by time (the Atharva-Veda kāla-sūkta tradition, also taken up in ŚU 6.1–6).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Fifth and sixth in the catalogue. The Iśvara-icchā-vāda is the most-common popular cosmogony (deity creates by will); the kāla-vāda is the philosophical alternative (time itself produces)."],["Sivananda","Note the parallel verb forms: viniścitāḥ (\"fixed\") and manyante (\"consider\"). Each view's adherents claim certainty; the multiplicity of certainties is the implicit argument against any."],["Krishnananda","The Upaniṣad's stance is precisely not Iśvara-icchā-vāda — it is post-theistic in the technical sense, leaving the question of a creator-Lord behind. This is one of the marks of the Māṇḍūkya's radical position."],["Nikhilananda","Nikhilananda notes that the will-of-Lord view is the popular Vedānta of common devotion; Gauḍapāda surveys it without endorsement, and the Kārikā as a whole will surpass it."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 1.9","title":"Cosmogonic positions 5-6: enjoyment, sport","sa":["भोगार्थं सृष्टिरित्यन्ये क्रीडार्थमिति चापरे ।","देवस्यैष स्वभावोऽयमाप्तकामस्य का स्पृहा ॥ ९॥"],"iast":["bhogārthaṃ sṛṣṭir ity anye krīḍārtham iti cāpare |","devasyaiṣa svabhāvo'yam āpta-kāmasya kā spṛhā || 9 ||"],"words":[["bhoga-artham","m. acc. sg. tp.","for enjoyment-purpose"],["sṛṣṭiḥ iti","f. nom. sg.","creation, thus"],["anye","m. nom. pl.","others (say)"],["krīḍā-artham","f. acc. sg. tp.","for sport-purpose"],["ca apare","m. nom. pl.","and (yet) others"],["devasya eṣaḥ svabhāvaḥ ayam","m. nom. sg.","this is the very nature of the Lord"],["āpta-kāmasya","m. gen. sg. bv.","of one whose desires are accomplished"],["kā spṛhā","f. nom. sg. interr.","what desire (could there be)?"]],"lit":"\"Some say creation is for enjoyment; others, for sport. (But) this is the very nature of the Lord — what desire could there be in one whose desires are already accomplished?\"","para":"The final cosmogonies — enjoyment and sport (līlā). Gauḍapāda's own (cautious) conclusion: any teleological cosmogony fails because the Lord, being āpta-kāma (already-satisfied-in-desire), can have no purpose for creating.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Sixth and seventh views; with Gauḍapāda's first oblique critique. The Lord cannot create for any purpose (bhoga, krīḍā, etc.) because the Lord by definition has no unmet purpose. Therefore all teleological cosmogonies fail. The remaining option (which the Kārikā will develop): no creation at all."],["Sivananda","The argument is one of the foundational moves in Advaita: any theistic cosmogony presupposes an unsatisfied Lord, which contradicts divine completeness. The conclusion: creation, taken literally, is incoherent."],["Krishnananda","āpta-kāma is technical: the Lord whose desires are already obtained. Such a Lord has no kāma (desire) and therefore no motive for action. Creation, if literal, would impute desire to the Lord; therefore creation cannot be literal."],["Nikhilananda","Nikhilananda notes the verse's structural placement: at the end of cosmogony-survey, before turīya is introduced. Gauḍapāda has prepared the listener to receive turīya by demolishing every cosmogony that might compete for ultimate-explanation status."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"Mantra 7 — Turīya: the Fourth","u":[{"ref":"Mantra 7","title":"Not inward-knowing, not outward-knowing, not both, not a dense mass of cognition, not knowing, not non-knowing — the Fourth","sa":["नान्तःप्रज्ञं न बहिष्प्रज्ञं नोभयतःप्रज्ञं न प्रज्ञानघनं न प्रज्ञं नाप्रज्ञम् ।","अदृष्टमव्यवहार्यमग्राह्यमलक्षणमचिन्त्यमव्यपदेश्यमेकात्मप्रत्ययसारं प्रपञ्चोपशमं शान्तं शिवमद्वैतं चतुर्थं मन्यन्ते ।","स आत्मा स विज्ञेयः ॥ ७॥"],"iast":["nāntaḥ-prajñaṃ na bahiṣ-prajñaṃ nobhayataḥ-prajñaṃ na prajñāna-ghanaṃ na prajñaṃ nāprajñam |","adṛṣṭam avyavahāryam agrāhyam alakṣaṇam acintyam avyapadeśyam ekātma-pratyaya-sāraṃ prapañcopaśamaṃ śāntaṃ śivam advaitaṃ caturthaṃ manyante |","sa ātmā sa vijñeyaḥ || 7 ||"],"words":[["na antaḥ-prajñam","n. acc. sg. bv.","not inward-knowing"],["na bahiḥ-prajñam","n. acc. sg. bv.","not outward-knowing"],["na ubhayataḥ-prajñam","n. acc. sg. bv.","not both-ward-knowing"],["na prajñāna-ghanam","n. acc. sg. bv.","not a dense-mass of cognition"],["na prajñam","n. acc. sg. bv.","not (a) knower"],["na a-prajñam","n. acc. sg. bv.","not (a) non-knower"],["a-dṛṣṭam","n. acc. sg. bv.","unseen"],["a-vyavahāryam","n. acc. sg. bv.","not-to-be-dealt-with (not graspable in practice)"],["a-grāhyam","n. acc. sg. bv.","ungraspable"],["a-lakṣaṇam","n. acc. sg. bv.","without distinguishing mark"],["a-cintyam","n. acc. sg. bv.","unthinkable"],["a-vyapadeśyam","n. acc. sg. bv.","indesignable"],["eka-ātma-pratyaya-sāram","n. acc. sg. bv.","(whose) essence is the certainty 'one-Self'"],["prapañca-upaśamam","n. acc. sg. tp.","(in which) phenomena cease"],["śāntam","n. acc. sg.","peaceful"],["śivam","n. acc. sg.","auspicious"],["advaitam","n. acc. sg.","non-dual"],["caturtham","n. acc. sg.","(as) the Fourth"],["manyante","3 pl. pres.","(they) consider"],["saḥ ātmā","m. nom. sg.","He is the Self"],["saḥ vijñeyaḥ","m. nom. sg.","He is to be known"]],"lit":"\"(They consider as the Fourth that which is) not inward-knowing, not outward-knowing, not both-ward-knowing, not a dense-mass of cognition, not knowing, not non-knowing; unseen, not-to-be-dealt-with, ungraspable, without distinguishing mark, unthinkable, indesignable; whose essence is the certainty 'one-Self'; in which all phenomena cease; peaceful, auspicious, non-dual — they consider (this to be) the Fourth. He is the Self; He is to be known.\"","para":"The doctrinal heart of the Upaniṣad. The Fourth (turīya — though this Sanskrit word does not appear in this mantra; it is introduced in mantra 12) is defined by an extended string of negations followed by a brief positive characterisation followed by an identification with the Self. Three sets of attributes: (a) six prajñā-negations: turīya is neither inward-knowing (= not Taijasa), nor outward-knowing (= not Vaiśvānara), nor both-ward (= not the transition state), nor dense-mass-of-cognition (= not Prājña), nor knowing (= not active cognition), nor non-knowing (= not unconscious). All three pādas plus their refinements are excluded. (b) six in-attributes: unseen, not-to-be-dealt-with, ungraspable, mark-less, unthinkable, indesignable. The standard apophatic catalogue. (c) Five positive features: eka-ātma-pratyaya-sāra (its essence is the certainty 'I am one Self'), prapañcopaśama (phenomena cease in it), śānta (peaceful), śiva (auspicious), advaita (non-dual). The mantra closes: saḥ ātmā, saḥ vijñeyaḥ — \"He is the Self; He is to be known.\" One of the most-cited single mantras in Vedānta.","coms":[["Gauḍapāda","<em>(Kārikā 1.10–15, expanding on this mantra)</em> The Fourth is the Lord (īśāna), pervading-all (vibhu), of all phenomena the non-dual (advaita), the deathless (avyaya). Vaiśvānara, Taijasa, and Prājña are kārya-kāraṇa-baddha (bound by cause-effect); turīya alone is not. The seven-negation string excludes the entire structure of the first three pādas — turīya is what remains when those structures fall away."],["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The six prajñā-negations are systematic. They first exclude each of the three pādas (na bahiṣ-prajñam excludes Vaiśvānara; na antaḥ-prajñam excludes Taijasa; na prajñāna-ghanam excludes Prājña). Then they exclude the transition states (na ubhayataḥ-prajñam = the moment between waking and dreaming, when both are momentarily co-present). Then they exclude all-knowing (na prajñam) and total-unknowing (na a-prajñam) — i.e., turīya is neither omniscient nor unconscious; it is the substrate that makes both possible. The six positive in-attributes then exclude every common predicate that might be applied to a metaphysical object. What remains: a positive characterisation as the eka-ātma-pratyaya-sāra — the cognition 'I am one Self,' grasped not as a thought but as the very essence of cognition. The mantra is the locus classicus for the via negativa in Vedānta and the textual basis for Śaṅkara's doctrine that Brahman is upadhi-rahita (without any limiting condition)."],["Sivananda","The mantra is the daily meditation-text. Sit; close eyes; mentally recite the seven 'na's (\"not this, not this, not this …\"); allow each negation to actually remove its referent from awareness; what remains after all negations is turīya. Sivananda calls this the neti-neti exercise of the Māṇḍūkya."],["Krishnananda","eka-ātma-pratyaya-sāra is the most-debated phrase in the Upaniṣad. It is the only positive characterisation in a sea of negatives. Two readings: (a) \"essence is the cognition 'one-Self'\" (the cognition is the essence); (b) \"essence is grasped by the cognition 'one-Self'\" (the cognition is the means of grasping). Śaṅkara prefers (a); some modern commentators prefer (b). Both readings make turīya not entirely beyond cognition; it is the very nature of cognition rightly understood."],["Nikhilananda","This is the central mantra of the Upaniṣad and the proof-text for the Advaita doctrine of nirguṇa-brahma. saḥ ātmā saḥ vijñeyaḥ — \"He is the Self; He is to be known\" — is the imperative closure. The Fourth is not just to be described but to be known. The Upaniṣad does not leave the description as theoretical; it demands recognition."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"Kārikā 1.10–18 — verse-commentary on mantra 7 (turīya)","u":[{"ref":"Kārikā 1.10–18","title":"verse-commentary on mantra 7 (turīya)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Gauḍapāda's commentary on the turīya mantra is the most theologically dense section of the Āgama-prakaraṇa. It identifies turīya as Lord, removes it from the cause-effect chain in which the first three pādas are bound, and (in 1.16) introduces the famous formulation of awakening: ajam anidram asvapnam advaitaṃ budhyate tadā — \"unborn, sleepless, dreamless, non-dual — then he awakens.\""],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Kārikā 1.10","title":"Turīya is the Lord, beyond all phenomena","sa":["निवृत्तेः सर्वदुःखानामीशानः प्रभुरव्ययः ।","अद्वैतः सर्वभावानां देवस्तुर्यो विभुः स्मृतः ॥ १०॥"],"iast":["nivṛtteḥ sarva-duḥkhānām īśānaḥ prabhur avyayaḥ |","advaitaḥ sarva-bhāvānāṃ devas turyo vibhuḥ smṛtaḥ || 10 ||"],"words":[["nivṛtteḥ","f. gen. sg.","of the cessation"],["sarva-duḥkhānām","n. gen. pl.","of all sufferings"],["īśānaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the Lord"],["prabhuḥ","m. nom. sg.","master"],["a-vyayaḥ","m. nom. sg.","imperishable"],["advaitaḥ","m. nom. sg.","non-dual"],["sarva-bhāvānām","m. gen. pl.","of all beings"],["devaḥ turyaḥ vibhuḥ smṛtaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the divine Fourth, all-pervading, is remembered"]],"lit":"\"The Fourth — Lord of the cessation of all sufferings, master, imperishable, non-dual with respect to all beings, divine, all-pervading — is remembered (as such).\"","para":"Turīya is the Lord (Īśāna), master of sufferings' cessation, imperishable, non-dual with all beings, divine, all-pervading. The first positive characterisation after the Upaniṣad's negative one.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The śloka introduces the term turya (the Vedic poetic form of turīya, meaning \"the fourth\"). Śaṅkara notes that the same Self previously called Prājña (the Lord under causality, mantra 6) is now called Turya (the Lord beyond causality). The two names describe the same Self under different aspects."],["Sivananda","Daily reflection: turīya is the Lord of cessation. The seeker who has touched turīya finds that suffering has lost its purchase. Sivananda's promise: regular touching of turīya in meditation progressively reduces the suffering-residue across daily life."],["Krishnananda","īśāna here is etymologically charged: from īś (to rule). Turīya is the ruler of nivṛtti — of cessation. The Fourth does not just contain peace; it dispenses peace. The seeker who reaches turīya is empowered to dispense its peace to others."],["Nikhilananda","Nikhilananda treats this verse as the bridge between the apophatic mantra 7 and the cosmological-soteriological framing of the rest of the Kārikā. Turīya is now characterized positively, but only after the negations have done their preparatory work."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 1.11","title":"Viśva and Taijasa are bound by both cause and effect; Prājña by cause alone; Turīya by neither","sa":["कार्यकारणबद्धौ ताविष्येते विश्वतैजसौ ।","प्राज्ञः कारणबद्धस्तु द्वौ तौ तुर्ये न सिध्यतः ॥ ११॥"],"iast":["kārya-kāraṇa-baddhau tāv iṣyete viśva-taijasau |","prājñaḥ kāraṇa-baddhas tu dvau tau turye na sidhyataḥ || 11 ||"],"words":[["kārya-kāraṇa-baddhau","m. nom. du. tp.","(those two) bound by cause-and-effect"],["tau iṣyete","m. nom. du. + 3 du.","are taught (to be) those two"],["viśva-taijasau","m. nom. du. dv.","Viśva and Taijasa"],["prājñaḥ","m. nom. sg.","Prājña"],["kāraṇa-baddhaḥ","m. nom. sg. tp.","bound by cause"],["tu","indecl.","but"],["dvau tau","m. nom. du.","those two (bonds)"],["turye","n. loc. sg.","in the Fourth"],["na sidhyataḥ","3 du. pres.","do not subsist"]],"lit":"\"Viśva and Taijasa are taught (to be) bound by cause-and-effect; but Prājña (only) by cause. Those two (bonds) do not subsist in the Fourth.\"","para":"The three pādas are graded by causal entanglement: Viśva (waking) and Taijasa (dream) are bound by both cause and effect; Prājña (deep sleep) is bound by cause alone (no longer producing effects); turīya is bound by neither.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Doctrinally critical: the verse explains why turīya alone is the goal. Waking and dream are doubly bound; deep sleep is bound only as cause (its products will unfold in subsequent waking); turīya alone is causally free."],["Sivananda","The discipline this implies: progressively reduce kārya-bondage (effect-engagement, the active fulfillment of desires) and then kāraṇa-bondage (cause-engagement, the latent desires that produce future effects). The latter is harder."],["Krishnananda","kāraṇa-bandha is the residual seed of avidyā in deep sleep — what the Yoga tradition will call saṃskāras / vāsanās. Prājña is not yet free of these; turīya is."],["Nikhilananda","this verse is the textual basis for the doctrine that mokṣa is not deep sleep. The seeker who confuses turīya with suṣupti has stopped one step short."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 1.12","title":"Prājña knows nothing; Turīya sees all always","sa":["नात्मानं न परान्श्चैव न सत्यं नापि चानृतम् ।","प्राज्ञः किञ्चन सꣳवेत्ति तुर्यं तत्सर्वदृक्सदा ॥ १२॥"],"iast":["nātmānaṃ na parāṃś caiva na satyaṃ nāpi cānṛtam |","prājñaḥ kiñcana saṃvetti turyaṃ tat sarva-dṛk sadā || 12 ||"],"words":[["na ātmānam","m. acc. sg.","neither self"],["na parān ca eva","m. acc. pl.","nor others"],["na satyam na api ca anṛtam","n. acc. sg.","neither truth nor untruth"],["prājñaḥ kiñcana saṃvetti","m. nom. sg. + indecl. + 3 sg.","Prājña knows anything"],["turyam tat","n. nom. sg.","that Fourth"],["sarva-dṛk sadā","n. nom. sg. + indecl.","(is) all-seeing always"]],"lit":"\"Prājña knows nothing — neither self nor others, neither truth nor untruth. That Fourth, on the other hand, is all-seeing always.\"","para":"Prājña (deep sleep) has zero cognition: no self-cognition, no other-cognition, no truth/falsity discrimination. Turīya is the opposite: continuous all-seeing.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verse distinguishes deep sleep from turīya. Both are non-discursive, but in opposite ways: Prājña is unconscious; turīya is hyper-conscious. The danger in meditation is to mistake deep-sleep-like blank for turīya."],["Sivananda","Practical test: if your meditative state can be described as \"I was not aware of anything,\" it was suṣupti, not turīya. If it can be described as \"I was aware of everything at once, without object,\" it was turīya. The two are not gradations of one thing; they are distinct."],["Krishnananda","sarva-dṛk sadā — \"all-seeing always\" — is technically critical. Turīya is not awareness-of-content but awareness-as-such. Its seeing is not of objects but is the seeing-that-is-the-cosmos's-being-seen."],["Nikhilananda","this verse anticipates Yoga-Sūtra 1.43 (\"smṛti-pariśuddhau svarūpa-śūnyeva artha-mātra-nirbhāsā\") — the meditative state where the object alone shines, subject-grasping having dropped. But the Yoga state is described as svarūpa-śūnya (form-empty); turīya is sarva-dṛk (all-seeing)."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 1.13","title":"The non-grasping is the same in Prājña and Turīya; the seed-sleep differentiates them","sa":["द्वैतस्याग्रहणं तुल्यमुभयोः प्राज्ञतुर्ययोः ।","बीजनिद्रायुतः प्राज्ञः सा च तुर्ये न विद्यते ॥ १३॥"],"iast":["dvaitasyāgrahaṇaṃ tulyam ubhayoḥ prājña-turyayoḥ |","bīja-nidrā-yutaḥ prājñaḥ sā ca turye na vidyate || 13 ||"],"words":[["dvaitasya a-grahaṇam","n. nom. sg.","the non-grasping of duality"],["tulyam ubhayoḥ","n. nom. sg. + n. gen. du.","(is) equal (in) both"],["prājña-turyayoḥ","m. gen. du.","(of) Prājña and Turya"],["bīja-nidrā-yutaḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","endowed with seed-sleep"],["prājñaḥ","m. nom. sg.","Prājña"],["sā ca","f. nom. sg.","and that (seed-sleep)"],["turye na vidyate","n. loc. sg.","is not in turīya"]],"lit":"\"The non-grasping of duality is the same in both Prājña and Turya. But Prājña is endowed with seed-sleep; that (seed-sleep) is not in turīya.\"","para":"Both deep sleep and turīya are characterized by non-grasping of duality (a-grahaṇam dvaitasya). The difference: Prājña contains bīja-nidrā — \"seed-sleep,\" the latent saṃskāras that will produce future cognition; turīya does not.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The śloka makes precise the distinction sketched in 1.12. The same surface-feature (non-grasping of duality) is present in both, but they differ in seed-content. Deep sleep is non-grasping plus seeds; turīya is non-grasping without seeds."],["Sivananda","Sleep is therapeutic but not liberating: when one wakes from sleep, the seeds bear their fruit, and ignorance returns. When one rests in turīya, no seeds remain, and ignorance does not return upon emerging."],["Krishnananda","bīja-nidrā is the technical term: \"sleep that is a seed.\" The seeds are the vāsanās (mental tendencies) and saṃskāras (latent impressions) that survive deep sleep and re-manifest on waking. Turīya is bīja-nidrā-mukta — free of even this."],["Nikhilananda","this verse establishes the technical Vedāntic doctrine that sleep alone does not destroy avidyā; only jñāna does. The verse is therefore the textual basis for the rejection of pure-yoga liberation in favour of jñāna-liberation."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 1.14","title":"Two of the three are with dream-sleep; Prājña with sleep-without-dream; Turīya with neither","sa":["स्वप्ननिद्रायुतावाद्यौ प्राज्ञस्त्वस्वप्ननिद्रया ।","न निद्रां नैव च स्वप्नं तुर्ये पश्यन्ति निश्चिताः ॥ १४॥"],"iast":["svapna-nidrā-yutāv ādyau prājñas tv asvapna-nidrayā |","na nidrāṃ naiva ca svapnaṃ turye paśyanti niścitāḥ || 14 ||"],"words":[["svapna-nidrā-yutau","m. nom. du. bv.","endowed with dream-and-sleep"],["ādyau","m. nom. du.","the first two (Viśva, Taijasa)"],["prājñaḥ tu","m. nom. sg.","but Prājña"],["a-svapna-nidrayā","f. instr. sg. tp.","with sleep-without-dream"],["na nidrām","f. acc. sg.","neither sleep"],["na eva ca svapnam","m. acc. sg.","nor dream"],["turye paśyanti niścitāḥ","n. loc. sg.","the fixed ones see in the Fourth"]],"lit":"\"The first two (Viśva, Taijasa) are with dream-and-sleep; Prājña, with sleep-without-dream. In turīya, those-fixed-in-knowledge see neither sleep nor dream.\"","para":"Schematic refinement: Viśva and Taijasa contain both nidrā (sleep-tendency) and svapna (dream-content); Prājña contains nidrā but not svapna; turīya contains neither.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Note the verb paśyanti niścitāḥ (\"the fixed see\"). The seeing is restricted to those whose understanding is niścita (settled). The verse therefore implies that ordinary meditators may have only partial access to turīya; full access requires settled wisdom."],["Sivananda","The dream-sleep-distinction underlines that even in deep sleep there is something (nidrā = avidyā); in turīya there is not even that. The seeker must work past even the comfort of nidrā-rest."],["Krishnananda","a-svapna-nidrayā (\"by sleep-without-dream\") is grammatically odd — instrumental rather than the expected adjective. The grammar emphasizes that Prājña is reached BY MEANS OF dreamless sleep but is not identical with it. Turīya is reached by neither."],["Nikhilananda","niścitāḥ — the ones-settled — is the qualifier. The verse establishes that only the fully-settled cognition reaches turīya. The seeker's task is therefore to reach niścaya (settled cognition) before expecting turīya-vision."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 1.15","title":"On the false-grasping of duality; turīya attained when waking-sleep reversal ceases","sa":["अन्यथा गृह्णतः स्वप्नो निद्रा तत्त्वमजानतः ।","विपर्यासे तयोः क्षीणे तुरीयं पदमश्नुते ॥ १५॥"],"iast":["anyathā gṛhṇataḥ svapno nidrā tattvam ajānataḥ |","viparyāse tayoḥ kṣīṇe turīyaṃ padam aśnute || 15 ||"],"words":[["anyathā gṛhṇataḥ","m. gen. sg.","of one who grasps otherwise"],["svapnaḥ","m. nom. sg.","(is) dream"],["nidrā","f. nom. sg.","sleep"],["tattvam a-jānataḥ","n. acc. sg. + pres. part. gen.","(is) of one not knowing the truth"],["viparyāse tayoḥ kṣīṇe","loc. abs.","when the reversal of those two is exhausted"],["turīyam padam aśnute","n. acc. sg. + 3 sg.","he attains the Fourth state"]],"lit":"\"For one who grasps otherwise (than as it is), dream (occurs); for one who does not know the truth, sleep (occurs). When the reversal of these two is exhausted, he attains the Fourth state.\"","para":"Dream is the cognitive form of \"grasping otherwise\" — perceiving what is not so as if it were so. Sleep is the form of \"not knowing the truth\" — non-cognition of what is. When both these distortions cease, turīya is attained.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The śloka identifies the cognitive defect underlying each non-turīya state: dream is misapprehension, sleep is ignorance. The seeker who removes both apprehension-distortion and ignorance-blankness arrives at turīya — neither distorted nor blank, but clear."],["Sivananda","For the meditator: notice in any state of disturbed mind whether the defect is mis-grasping (overlaying interpretation on reality) or non-grasping (blank ignorance). Each defect has its remedy. When both remedies have worked, turīya remains."],["Krishnananda","viparyāsa is technical — the technical term for cognitive distortion in Sāṅkhya and Yoga (e.g., YS 1.8). Gauḍapāda uses the Yoga-vocabulary to describe what removes dream and sleep, allowing turīya to appear."],["Nikhilananda","the verse provides the precise epistemic prescription. Dream-distortion and sleep-ignorance both have to be removed; removing only one is insufficient. The seeker's progress requires both viveka (discrimination, against distortion) and jñāna (knowledge, against ignorance)."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 1.16","title":"Asleep through beginningless māyā, the jīva, awakening, knows the unborn, sleepless, dreamless, non-dual","sa":["अनादिमायया सुप्तो यदा जीवः प्रबुध्यते ।","अजमनिद्रमस्वप्नमद्वैतं बुध्यते तदा ॥ १६॥"],"iast":["anādi-māyayā supto yadā jīvaḥ prabudhyate |","ajam anidram asvapnam advaitaṃ budhyate tadā || 16 ||"],"words":[["an-ādi-māyayā","f. instr. sg.","by beginningless māyā"],["suptaḥ","m. nom. sg. pp.","(while) asleep"],["yadā jīvaḥ prabudhyate","indecl. + m. nom. + 3 sg.","when the jīva awakens"],["a-jam","n. acc. sg.","unborn"],["an-idram","n. acc. sg.","sleepless"],["a-svapnam","n. acc. sg.","dreamless"],["advaitam","n. acc. sg.","non-dual"],["budhyate tadā","3 sg. + indecl.","(he) knows then"]],"lit":"\"When the jīva, asleep through beginningless māyā, awakens — then he knows the unborn, the sleepless, the dreamless, the non-dual.\"","para":"The most-cited verse of the Āgama-prakaraṇa on awakening. The jīva is described as asleep in māyā — and that sleep is anādi (beginningless). Awakening is the recognition of what was always so: an-aja (unborn), an-idra (sleepless), a-svapna (dreamless), advaita (non-dual). All four characteristics belong to turīya.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The doctrinally central verse on what awakening reveals. The four predicates of turīya are not new properties acquired; they are properties always present, now recognized. The seeker who awakens does not become what he was not; he recognizes what he was."],["Sivananda","Daily reflection: the sleep that hides turīya is not last night's sleep but the beginningless māyā-sleep. The waking from THAT sleep is what the Upaniṣad means by realisation. Ordinary night-sleep is, by contrast, a minor sub-event within the māyā-sleep."],["Krishnananda","Note budhyate tadā — \"he knows then.\" The knowing is one event, not gradual. The four predicates are recognized simultaneously; the recognition is the event of jñāna."],["Nikhilananda","this is the verse that authorises the entire later Vedāntic vocabulary of \"awakening.\" The buddha (\"awakened\") metaphor — central in Buddhism — is here used in a strictly Vedāntic sense: the awakening is to the non-dual Self, not to dependent co-origination."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 1.17","title":"If the world existed, it would cease; the world is māyā only, the non-dual is the highest truth","sa":["प्रपञ्चो यदि विद्येत निवर्तेत न संशयः ।","मायामात्रमिदं द्वैतमद्वैतं परमार्थतः ॥ १७॥"],"iast":["prapañco yadi vidyeta nivarteta na saṃśayaḥ |","māyā-mātram idaṃ dvaitam advaitaṃ paramārthataḥ || 17 ||"],"words":[["prapañcaḥ yadi vidyeta","m. nom. sg. + 3 sg. opt.","if the world existed"],["nivarteta","3 sg. opt.","it would cease"],["na saṃśayaḥ","indecl. + m. nom.","no doubt"],["māyā-mātram","n. nom. sg.","mere māyā"],["idam dvaitam","n. nom. sg.","(is) this duality"],["advaitam paramārthataḥ","n. nom. sg. + indecl.","the non-dual (is) ultimately"]],"lit":"\"If the manifold (existed), it would cease — no doubt about it. This duality is mere māyā; the non-dual is (what is) ultimately.\"","para":"The argumentative crux. If the prapañca (the manifold) really existed, it would (have to) cease in turīya — but in fact it does not cease; it never existed in the first place. It is mere māyā. The non-dual alone is paramārthataḥ — at the level of ultimate truth.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verse is the first explicit māyā-vāda statement in the Kārikā. The reasoning is hypothetical-conditional: if duality existed, it would have to be ended; instead, it is shown to have never been. The Upaniṣadic prapañcopaśama (\"phenomena cease\") of mantra 7 is therefore not an event but a recognition."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: the world does not need to be destroyed for liberation. The world is already non-existent in the ultimate sense; the recognition of this is liberation. The Upaniṣad is not pessimistic about the world; it is precise about its ontological status."],["Krishnananda","māyā-mātram (\"mere māyā\") and paramārthataḥ (\"in ultimate truth\") together constitute Gauḍapāda's first explicit deployment of the two-truths doctrine (vyāvahārika / pāramārthika) that becomes canonical Advaita."],["Nikhilananda","this verse anticipates Śaṅkara's later doctrine of the absolute non-existence of the cosmos at the pāramārthika level. Gauḍapāda's formulation here is the source."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 1.18","title":"If the world is imagined, it is so as a pedagogical convention","sa":["विकल्पो विनिवर्तेत कल्पितो यदि केनचित् ।","उपदेशादयं वादो ज्ञाते द्वैतं न विद्यते ॥ १८॥"],"iast":["vikalpo vinivarteta kalpito yadi kenacit |","upadeśād ayaṃ vādo jñāte dvaitaṃ na vidyate || 18 ||"],"words":[["vikalpaḥ","m. nom. sg.","imagination"],["vinivarteta","3 sg. opt.","would cease"],["kalpitaḥ yadi kenacit","m. nom. sg. + indecl.","if (it were) imagined by some (cause)"],["upadeśāt","m. abl. sg.","for the sake of instruction"],["ayam vādaḥ","m. nom. sg.","this discourse (is given)"],["jñāte","n. loc. sg. pp.","when (it is) known"],["dvaitam na vidyate","n. nom. sg.","duality is not"]],"lit":"\"Imagination would cease if (it were) imagined by some cause. This discourse (about cause) is given (only) for instruction; when (the truth is) known, duality is not.\"","para":"A subtle move: causality-talk (\"māyā causes appearance\") is itself pedagogical. The Kārikā does NOT actually hold that māyā is a cause; it speaks AS IF māyā were a cause for the listener's benefit (upadeśa). At the level of jñāna, neither duality nor a cause of duality exists.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verse closes the Āgama-prakaraṇa's exposition of turīya. Causal language about māyā is upadeśa (pedagogical). Once knowledge dawns, duality is not — and therefore no cause-of-duality is needed either. The two-truths doctrine becomes radical: even the lower truth's causal apparatus is provisional."],["Sivananda","This is one of the Kārikā's most subtle moves. Many seekers, satisfied to recognize that duality is illusion, then ask \"What causes the illusion?\" Gauḍapāda's answer: the question is itself a residue of dualistic thinking. At the level of knowledge, no question of cause arises."],["Krishnananda","upadeśāt — \"for instruction\" — is the technical justification for retaining provisional cosmologies. The Vedāntic teacher uses cosmological language not because it is true but because it leads the listener toward what is true. The cosmology dissolves once the lesson is learnt."],["Nikhilananda","the verse anticipates Advaita's mature self-understanding as a doctrine that uses its own apparatus only provisionally. Even māyā-vāda is upadeśa, not paramārtha."]],"notes":["(End of Kārikā 1.10–18, on mantra 7 / turīya.)"]}]},{"t":"Mantras 8–11 — OṂ as adhyakṣara: A, U, M correspond to the three pādas","u":[{"ref":"Mantra 8","title":"That same Self, with respect to the syllable, is OṂ; with respect to mātrās, the pādas: A, U, M","sa":["सोऽयमात्माऽध्यक्षरमोङ्कारोऽधिमात्रं पादा मात्रा मात्राश्च पादा अकार उकारो मकार इति ॥ ८॥"],"iast":["so'yam ātmādhyakṣaram oṃ-kāro'dhimātraṃ pādā mātrā mātrāś ca pādā a-kāra u-kāro m-kāra iti || 8 ||"],"words":[["saḥ ayam ātmā","m. nom. sg.","this very Self"],["adhi-akṣaram","n. nom. sg.","with respect to the syllable"],["oṃ-kāraḥ","m. nom. sg.","(is) Oṃ"],["adhi-mātram","n. nom. sg.","with respect to mātrās"],["pādāḥ mātrāḥ","m. nom. pl. + f. nom. pl.","the pādas are the mātrās"],["mātrāḥ ca pādāḥ","f. nom. pl. + m. nom. pl.","and the mātrās are the pādas"],["a-kāraḥ u-kāraḥ m-kāraḥ iti","m. nom. sg. (in series)","(viz.) A-sound, U-sound, M-sound"]],"lit":"\"This very Self, with respect to the syllable, is OṂ; with respect to mātrās, the pādas are the mātrās, and the mātrās are the pādas — (namely) A-sound, U-sound, M-sound.\"","para":"The pivot of the Upaniṣad. The Self previously identified as catuṣ-pāt (four-quartered) is now identified, with respect to the syllable (adhi-akṣaram), as OṂ. And, with respect to the syllable's mātrās (adhi-mātram), the pādas of the Self correspond exactly to the mātrās of OṂ. The two-way identification is unequivocal: pādā mātrā mātrāś ca pādā (\"the pādas are the mātrās; the mātrās are the pādas\"). The three audible mātrās are A, U, M; the three pādas are Vaiśvānara, Taijasa, Prājña. Mantras 9, 10, 11 will spell out each correspondence in turn. (Mantra 12 will then identify the fourth — silent, mātra-less — as turīya.)","coms":[["Gauḍapāda","<em>(Kārikā 1.19–22, below)</em> The correspondences are not arbitrary but are based on shared features (sāmānya): A and Vaiśvānara share \"initiality\" and \"pervasion\"; U and Taijasa share \"intermediate\" status; M and Prājña share \"measure\" and \"dissolution.\" The shared-feature method is the Vedānta's standard technique for OṂ-meditation."],["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The double-direction identification (pādās mātrāḥ, mātrāś ca pādāḥ) is critical — it makes the correspondence reversible. Meditating on A reveals Vaiśvānara; meditating on Vaiśvānara reveals A. The OṂ-meditation is therefore both a sound-meditation and a state-meditation simultaneously."],["Sivananda","This mantra is the foundation of all later OṂ-meditation in the Indian tradition. Practical instruction: recite A while focused on the waking-state; recite U while focused on dream-imagination; recite M while focused on the deep-sleep-quiescence; rest in silence for the fourth. The complete OṂ-cycle traverses all four states."],["Krishnananda","The mantra's most underappreciated word is adhyakṣaram (\"with respect to the syllable\"). The Self is Oṃ only adhyakṣaram — only in the context of the syllabic analysis. The Self is not exhausted by being Oṃ; Oṃ is the meditation-form of the Self for those who approach through sound."],["Nikhilananda","This mantra licenses the entire Vedāntic OṂ-meditation tradition. Without it, OṂ would be just a sacred sound; with it, OṂ is the meditation-form of the Self. The text is the meditation, and the meditation is the text."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 9","title":"Vaiśvānara, in waking, is the A-sound: the first mātrā, from being first / from being pervasive — he who knows obtains all desires and is first","sa":["जागरितस्थानो वैश्वानरोऽकारः प्रथमा मात्राऽऽप्तेरादिमत्त्वाद् वाऽऽप्नोति ह वै सर्वान्.","कामानादिश्च भवति य एवं वेद ॥ ९॥"],"iast":["jāgarita-sthāno vaiśvānaro'kāraḥ prathamā mātrā''pter ādimattvād vā''pnoti ha vai sarvān kāmān ādiś ca bhavati ya evaṃ veda || 9 ||"],"words":[["jāgarita-sthānaḥ vaiśvānaraḥ","m. nom. sg.","Vaiśvānara, located in waking"],["a-kāraḥ","m. nom. sg.","(is) the A-sound"],["prathamā mātrā","f. nom. sg.","(it is) the first mātrā"],["āpteḥ ādimattvāt vā","f. abl. + n. abl.","because of (its) pervasion or (its) being-first"],["āpnoti ha vai sarvān kāmān","verb phrase","(he) verily attains all desires"],["ādiḥ ca bhavati","m. nom.","and becomes first"],["yaḥ evam veda","m. nom. sg.","he who knows thus"]],"lit":"\"Vaiśvānara, located in waking, is the A-sound, the first mātrā — because of (its) pervasion (āpti) or (its) being-first (ādimattva). He who knows thus verily attains all desires and becomes the first.\"","para":"First mātrā = first pāda. A and Vaiśvānara share two features: (a) āpti — pervasion; the sound A pervades all the other vowels in Sanskrit phonology (every vowel includes A's mouth-opening), just as Vaiśvānara pervades all the other states; (b) ādimattva — being-first; A is the first vowel of the alphabet, Vaiśvānara is the first pāda. The meditator who knows this correspondence obtains the two phala (fruits) of the correspondence: he attains all kāmas (desires — because the pervasive A symbolically opens to all) and becomes ādi (first — pre-eminent in his sphere).","coms":[["Gauḍapāda","<em>(Kārikā 1.19, 1.23)</em> Two shared features: vāc-utkarṣa (excellence-of-speech) and adi-sāmānya (first-ness). A pervades speech (no Sanskrit vowel is pronounceable without partial A-articulation); Vaiśvānara pervades cognition (no cognition occurs without waking's structural framework)."],["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The two reasons (āpti, ādimattva) are offered alternatively (vā = \"or\") — the meditator may use either. The first emphasises pervasion (A pervades; Vaiśvānara pervades); the second, priority (A is first; Vaiśvānara is first). Either justification suffices. The phala-statement is a standard Upaniṣadic promise: knowing the equivalence yields the symbolic fruit (universal desires + pre-eminence)."],["Sivananda","Practical meditation: recite A long and clear; feel awareness fill the waking-cosmos; recognize that A and Vaiśvānara are the same in pervasion. The recitation is not magical; it is the verbal form of the cognitive identification. Sivananda's recommendation: ten minutes of A-meditation daily, before the morning tasks."],["Krishnananda","The fruits promised (\"obtains all desires, becomes first\") are not material rewards but cognitive recognitions. To know the A-Vaiśvānara identity is already to be ādi (first) in the sense of having priority of insight; it is already to āpnoti (obtain) all desires in the sense of having attained the source from which desires arise."],["Nikhilananda","The phala-formula ya evaṃ veda (\"he who knows thus\") will recur at the end of each of the next three mantras. The promise of fruits is a standard Upaniṣadic pedagogical device: it makes meditation attractive at the level of desire even as it lays the ground for the desire-less state."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 10","title":"Taijasa, in dream, is the U-sound: the second mātrā, from excellence / from being intermediate — he who knows extends his knowledge-stream and is impartial; no non-Brahman-knower born in his family","sa":["स्वप्नस्थानस्तैजस उकारो द्वितीया मात्रोत्कर्षाद् उभयत्वाद्वोत्कर्षति ह वै ज्ञानसन्ततिꣳ समानश्च भवति नास्याब्रह्मवित्कुले भवति य एवं वेद ॥ १०॥"],"iast":["svapna-sthānas taijasa u-kāro dvitīyā mātrotkarṣād ubhayatvād votkarṣati ha vai jñāna-santatiṃ samānaś ca bhavati nāsyābrahma-vit kule bhavati ya evaṃ veda || 10 ||"],"words":[["svapna-sthānaḥ taijasaḥ","m. nom. sg.","Taijasa, located in dream"],["u-kāraḥ","m. nom. sg.","(is) the U-sound"],["dvitīyā mātrā","f. nom. sg.","(it is) the second mātrā"],["utkarṣāt ubhayatvāt vā","m. abl. + n. abl.","because of (its) excellence or (its) being-both"],["utkarṣati ha vai jñāna-santatim","verb phrase + acc.","(he) verily extends the stream of knowledge"],["samānaḥ ca bhavati","m. nom. sg.","and becomes impartial / equal"],["na asya a-brahma-vit kule bhavati","verb phrase","no non-Brahman-knower is born in his family"],["yaḥ evam veda","m. nom. sg.","he who knows thus"]],"lit":"\"Taijasa, located in dream, is the U-sound, the second mātrā — because of (its) excellence (utkarṣa) or (its) being-both (ubhayatva). He who knows thus verily extends the stream of knowledge, becomes impartial; no non-Brahman-knower is born in his family.\"","para":"Second mātrā = second pāda. U and Taijasa share two features: (a) utkarṣa — excellence, exaltation; the U-sound rises above A in pitch and Taijasa rises above Vaiśvānara in subtlety; (b) ubhayatva — being-both; the U-sound is intermediate between A and M (it is sounded between them in OṂ), just as Taijasa is intermediate between Vaiśvānara and Prājña. The phala: the knower extends his jñāna-santati (stream of knowledge — i.e., his intellectual line continues), becomes samāna (impartial / equal — neither attached to nor recoiling from objects), and his lineage continues unbroken in Brahman-knowledge.","coms":[["Gauḍapāda","<em>(Kārikā 1.20)</em> The U and Taijasa correspondences emphasise utkarṣa and ubhayatva. Compare 1.19 (vāc-utkarṣa). The reduplication of utkarṣa across the Kārikā shows Gauḍapāda's pedagogical commitment to feature-mapping."],["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The two reasons (utkarṣa, ubhayatva) are again alternatives. Utkarṣa-meaning: U is more elevated than A (in pitch and articulation), Taijasa is more elevated than Vaiśvānara (in subtlety). Ubhayatva-meaning: U is between A and M, Taijasa is between Vaiśvānara and Prājña. The phala-formula nāsya abrahma-vit kule bhavati echoes Muṇḍaka 3.2.9 — \"no non-knower is born in his family\" — connecting OṂ-meditation to the lineage-perpetuation of brahmavidyā."],["Sivananda","The U-meditation: recite U long; feel awareness move from the waking-state to the dream-imagination level; recognize that U and Taijasa share elevation. The discipline is to climb cognitively from gross to subtle as the breath climbs from A to U."],["Krishnananda","ubhayatva — \"being-both\" — captures the precise epistemic position of dream: it is both waking-like (with content) and sleep-like (with content of a different order). The U-sound is sounded between A and M; Taijasa stands between waking and deep sleep. The mediating position is also a position of transmission — knowledge moves through Taijasa from the awakened to the dreaming and vice versa."],["Nikhilananda","The fruit jñāna-santati-utkarṣa (\"elevation of the knowledge-stream\") is the precise promise to the seeker: meditation on U strengthens the continuity of insight across states. The seeker who falls asleep with a meditative recognition tends to wake with it; this is the empirical correlate of the promise."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra 11","title":"Prājña, in deep sleep, is the M-sound: the third mātrā, from measure / from absorption — he who knows measures all this and is the place of absorption","sa":["सुषुप्तस्थानः प्राज्ञो मकारस्तृतीया मात्रा मितेरपीतेर्वा मिनोति ह वा इदꣳ सर्वमपीतिश्च भवति य एवं वेद ॥ ११॥"],"iast":["suṣupta-sthānaḥ prājño m-kāras tṛtīyā mātrā miter apīter vā minoti ha vā idaṃ sarvam apītiś ca bhavati ya evaṃ veda || 11 ||"],"words":[["suṣupta-sthānaḥ prājñaḥ","m. nom. sg.","Prājña, located in deep sleep"],["m-kāraḥ","m. nom. sg.","(is) the M-sound"],["tṛtīyā mātrā","f. nom. sg.","(it is) the third mātrā"],["miteḥ apīteḥ vā","f. abl. + f. abl.","because of (its) measure (mā-) or (its) absorption (apī-)"],["minoti ha vai idam sarvam","verb phrase","(he) verily measures all this"],["apītiḥ ca bhavati","f. nom. + 3 sg.","and becomes the place of absorption"],["yaḥ evam veda","m. nom. sg.","he who knows thus"]],"lit":"\"Prājña, located in deep sleep, is the M-sound, the third mātrā — because of (its) measure (miti) or (its) absorption (apīti). He who knows thus verily measures all this and becomes the place of absorption.\"","para":"Third mātrā = third pāda. M and Prājña share two features: (a) miti — measure; the M-sound closes the mouth, measuring the syllable's boundary, and Prājña gathers all into one undifferentiated mass (measure-as-totalization); (b) apīti — absorption; M is the syllable's terminus where sound subsides into silence, and Prājña is the state in which the waking-and-dream cosmos is absorbed into seed-form. The phala: the knower minoti (measures) all this — i.e., comprehends the cosmos in its total span — and becomes apīti (the place into which all is absorbed) — i.e., the cosmic dissolution-point is realised as one's own state.","coms":[["Gauḍapāda","<em>(Kārikā 1.21)</em> The M-Prājña correspondences are mānasāmānya (mind-likeness, since both close the mind-modes) and layasāmānya (dissolution-likeness, since both end their respective processes). The Kārikā glosses miti and apīti as the technical labels of these likenesses."],["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> miti (measure) and apīti (absorption / re-entry) are technical Vedic-grammar terms. Miti = the measuring action by which a syllable's boundary is set; apīti = the going-back-into-source of a sound at its termination. Both describe what M does phonetically and what Prājña does cosmically. The phala minoti-apītiḥ are the corresponding cognitive achievements: comprehending the totality (mā-) and recognising oneself as its dissolution-point (apī-)."],["Sivananda","The M-meditation: recite M closing the lips slowly; feel awareness gather into the heart-cavity; recognise that M and Prājña share measure-and-absorption. The discipline is to use the bodily closing of M (lip-closure) as cue for the cognitive closing of Prājña (deep-sleep quiescence)."],["Krishnananda","apīti is the verse's most-rich word. Literally \"going-back-into\" (api + i), it names the cosmic process by which manifest beings re-enter their seed-state. Prājña is the state in which this process is achieved within one's own experience. The verse promises: the OṂ-meditator becomes apīti — becomes the very dissolution-state into which beings absorb."],["Nikhilananda","The triple-mātrā-mapping (A-Vaiśvānara, U-Taijasa, M-Prājña) is now complete. The Upaniṣad has established the correspondence between syllable-structure and state-structure. The next mantra (12) will identify the silent fourth."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"Kārikā 1.19–23 — verse-commentary on mantras 8–11","u":[{"ref":"Kārikā 1.19–23","title":"verse-commentary on mantras 8–11","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Gauḍapāda's verses now explicitly catalogue the shared-feature (sāmānya) basis of each correspondence."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Kārikā 1.19","title":"Sāmānya of A and Viśva","sa":["विश्वस्यात्ववविवक्षायामादिसामान्यमुत्कटम् ।","मात्रासम्प्रतिपत्तौ स्यादाप्तिसामान्यमेव च ॥ १९॥"],"iast":["viśvasyātva-vivakṣāyām ādi-sāmānyam utkaṭam |","mātrā-sampratipattau syād āpti-sāmānyam eva ca || 19 ||"],"words":[["viśvasya atva-vivakṣāyām","m. gen. + f. loc.","in the desire to express Viśva as a-ness"],["ādi-sāmānyam utkaṭam","n. nom. sg.","the 'first-ness' similarity (is) prominent"],["mātrā-sampratipattau syāt","f. loc. + 3 sg. opt.","in (one who is) settled in mātrā(-meditation)"],["āpti-sāmānyam eva ca","n. nom. sg.","(and) the 'pervasion' similarity also"]],"lit":"\"When one wishes to express Viśva as A-ness, the 'first-ness' similarity is prominent; for one settled in mātrā-meditation, the 'pervasion' similarity (is also present).\"","para":"Two sāmānyas justify the A-Viśva mapping: ādi (first-ness) and āpti (pervasion). Both are simultaneously available; the meditator at the beginning of practice notices first-ness, the more advanced meditator notices pervasion.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Gauḍapāda's pedagogical generosity: each correspondence has multiple sāmānyas; the meditator chooses what works for him. Same Upaniṣadic identity (mantra 9), spelled out in technical detail."],["Sivananda","For new meditators, focus on ādi-sāmānya: A is the first sound, Vaiśvānara is the first state. For more practised meditators, focus on āpti-sāmānya: A pervades all vowels, Vaiśvānara pervades all states."],["Krishnananda","Note utkaṭam — \"prominent.\" The sāmānyas are graded: ādi is the more prominent (= more easily noticed); āpti is more subtle. The verse is a pedagogical sequence, not a list of equivalents."],["Nikhilananda","this verse gives the methodological key to OṂ-meditation. Each mātrā-pāda pairing has two or more sāmānyas; meditation proceeds by working through them."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 1.20","title":"Sāmānya of U and Taijasa","sa":["तैजसस्योत्वविज्ञान उत्कर्षो दृश्यते स्फुटम् ।","मात्रासम्प्रतिपत्तौ स्यादुभयत्वं तथाविधम् ॥ २०॥"],"iast":["taijasasyotva-vijñāna utkarṣo dṛśyate sphuṭam |","mātrā-sampratipattau syād ubhayatvaṃ tathāvidham || 20 ||"],"words":[["taijasasya utva-vijñāne","m. gen. + n. loc.","in the cognition of Taijasa as u-ness"],["utkarṣaḥ dṛśyate sphuṭam","m. nom. + 3 sg. + indecl.","the 'excellence' (similarity) is clearly seen"],["mātrā-sampratipattau syāt","f. loc. + 3 sg. opt.","in (one settled in) mātrā(-meditation)"],["ubhayatvam tathā-vidham","n. nom. sg.","the 'being-both' (similarity), similarly"]],"lit":"\"In the cognition of Taijasa as U-ness, the 'excellence' similarity is clearly seen; for one settled in mātrā-meditation, the 'being-both' similarity (also).\"","para":"Parallel to 1.19. The U-Taijasa mapping has two sāmānyas: utkarṣa (excellence) and ubhayatva (being-both). Both are progressively available.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Same scheme: a more obvious sāmānya (utkarṣa — the U-sound is elevated, Taijasa is elevated) and a subtler one (ubhayatva — both are intermediate)."],["Sivananda","For the dream-meditator: feel U's elevated pitch as the upward movement from waking to dream; feel U's intermediate position as the bridge between A (waking) and M (deep sleep)."],["Krishnananda","dṛśyate sphuṭam — \"is clearly seen\" — is contrasted with the subtler ubhayatva that takes longer to recognise. The verse establishes a developmental sequence in the meditation."],["Nikhilananda","the verse continues 1.19's pedagogical pattern."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 1.21","title":"Sāmānya of M and Prājña","sa":["मकारभावे प्राज्ञस्य मानसामान्यमुत्कटम् ।","मात्रासम्प्रतिपत्तौ तु लयसामान्यमेव च ॥ २१॥"],"iast":["m-kāra-bhāve prājñasya māna-sāmānyam utkaṭam |","mātrā-sampratipattau tu laya-sāmānyam eva ca || 21 ||"],"words":[["m-kāra-bhāve prājñasya","m. loc. + gen.","in (the cognition of) Prājña's being-M"],["māna-sāmānyam utkaṭam","n. nom. sg.","the 'measure' similarity is prominent"],["mātrā-sampratipattau tu","f. loc.","but in mātrā(-meditation)"],["laya-sāmānyam eva ca","n. nom. sg.","the 'dissolution' similarity also"]],"lit":"\"In (the cognition of) Prājña's being-M, the 'measure' similarity is prominent; but in mātrā-meditation, the 'dissolution' similarity also.\"","para":"M-Prājña mapping. Two sāmānyas: māna (measure, miti) and laya (dissolution, the more advanced apīti). The first is grossly available; the second requires settled meditation.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> māna here = the same as the Upaniṣad's miti (1.11). Gauḍapāda is using a slight orthographic variant; the sense is identical."],["Sivananda","M closes the lips and measures the syllable; Prājña closes the mind-modes and measures the cosmos's totality. Both are bounding acts that simultaneously contain and complete."],["Krishnananda","laya — \"dissolution\" — is the more subtle sāmānya. Prājña is the state into which the waking-dream cosmos dissolves; M is the sound into which OṂ's audible structure dissolves. The recognition of this laya in meditation is the recognition that one is the dissolution-point of one's own cosmos."],["Nikhilananda","with this verse the three-fold sāmānya catalogue is complete. The structure ādi-utkarṣa-māna (gross sāmānyas) and āpti-ubhayatva-laya (subtle sāmānyas) is the basis of the standard six-fold OṂ-meditation in later Vedānta."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 1.22","title":"He who knows the threefold common feature is reverenced; (he is) the great muni","sa":["त्रिषु धामसु यत्तुल्यं सामान्यं वेत्ति निश्चितः ।","स पूज्यः सर्वभूतानां वन्द्यश्चैव महामुनिः ॥ २२॥"],"iast":["triṣu dhāmasu yat tulyaṃ sāmānyaṃ vetti niścitaḥ |","sa pūjyaḥ sarva-bhūtānāṃ vandyaś caiva mahā-muniḥ || 22 ||"],"words":[["triṣu dhāmasu","n. loc. pl.","in the three states"],["yat tulyam sāmānyam","n. nom. sg.","what is the equal common(-feature)"],["vetti niścitaḥ","3 sg. + m. nom.","(he who) settled knows"],["saḥ pūjyaḥ sarva-bhūtānām","m. nom. + gen. pl.","(is) honourable by all beings"],["vandyaḥ ca eva mahā-muniḥ","m. nom. + indecl.","and is the praiseworthy great sage"]],"lit":"\"He who, settled (in conviction), knows the equal common-feature in the three states — he is honourable by all beings, indeed praiseworthy, a great sage.\"","para":"The summative phala-statement. Knowing the threefold sāmānya — the equality that runs through all three pāda-mātrā pairs — is the achievement of the great muni.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Gauḍapāda's reward-statement. The achievement is not just intellectual recognition but the cognitive integration of the threefold scheme. The seeker who has integrated it is mahā-muni (\"great sage\") — he has the mauna (silence) that comes from completed insight."],["Sivananda","For the meditator: the goal is not to memorize the six sāmānyas but to feel the equality (tulyam) running through them — the one Self equally in all states, equally in all mātrās. The recognition is the meditation's fruit."],["Krishnananda","niścitaḥ — \"settled\" — is again the key qualifier. The knowledge that bears fruit is not tentative or argued; it is niścita (firmly established). The meditation must reach this niścaya stage to be liberating."],["Nikhilananda","the verse closes the sāmānya-catalogue and elevates the recognising meditator to muni-status. The next two verses will transition to the fourth mātrā / fourth pāda."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 1.23","title":"A leads to Viśva, U to Taijasa, M to Prājña; in (the fourth) mātra-less, there is no going","sa":["अकारो नयते विश्वमुकारश्चापि तैजसम् ।","मकारश्च पुनः प्राज्ञं नामात्रे विद्यते गतिः ॥ २३॥"],"iast":["a-kāro nayate viśvam u-kāraś cāpi taijasam |","m-kāraś ca punaḥ prājñaṃ nāmātre vidyate gatiḥ || 23 ||"],"words":[["a-kāraḥ nayate viśvam","m. nom. + 3 sg. + acc.","A-sound leads to Viśva"],["u-kāraḥ ca api taijasam","m. nom. + acc.","and U-sound to Taijasa"],["m-kāraḥ ca punaḥ prājñam","m. nom. + acc.","and M-sound, again, to Prājña"],["na a-mātre","indecl. + n. loc. sg.","but in the mātra-less (fourth)"],["vidyate gatiḥ","3 sg. + f. nom.","there is no going / movement"]],"lit":"\"A-sound leads to Viśva, U-sound to Taijasa, M-sound to Prājña; but in the mātra-less, there is no going.\"","para":"Each of the three audible mātrās is a vehicle to its corresponding pāda. But the fourth (a-mātra, mātra-less — turīya) cannot be approached by movement; gati (\"going\") does not apply to it.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verse establishes the structural difference between the three audible mātrās (each a vehicle, each producing motion) and the fourth (a-mātra, in which motion does not subsist). To reach turīya is therefore not to travel; it is to stop traveling."],["Sivananda","Practical instruction: A, U, M can each be USED as a vehicle in meditation. The silence-after-M cannot be used as a vehicle; it can only be entered by ceasing to use vehicles."],["Krishnananda","na āmātre vidyate gatiḥ — \"in the mātra-less there is no going\" — is one of the Kārikā's most-meditated phrases. It captures the impossibility of reaching turīya by any conventional means. Meditative effort works for the first three pādas; for the fourth, effort itself must cease."],["Nikhilananda","this verse is the bridge between the first part of the Kārikā (sound-vehicles for the three pādas) and the second (silence-and-cessation for the fourth). The next mantra (12) and Kārikā verses (1.24-29) develop the fourth."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"Mantra 12 — The Fourth is mātra-less, not-to-be-dealt-with, in which phenomena cease, auspicious, non-dual; OṂ is verily the Self — by Self enters one's own Self","u":[{"ref":"Mantra 12","title":"The mātra-less Fourth; OṂ is the Self; entering one's own Self by means of the Self","sa":["अमात्रश्चतुर्थोऽव्यवहार्यः प्रपञ्चोपशमः शिवोऽद्वैत एवमोङ्कार आत्मैव संविशत्यात्मनाऽऽत्मानं य एवं वेद ॥ १२॥"],"iast":["a-mātraś caturtho'vyavahāryaḥ prapañcopaśamaḥ śivo'dvaita evam oṃ-kāra ātmaiva saṃviśaty ātmanā''tmānaṃ ya evaṃ veda || 12 ||"],"words":[["a-mātraḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","without mātrā"],["caturthaḥ","m. nom. sg.","(is) the Fourth"],["a-vyavahāryaḥ","m. nom. sg.","not-to-be-dealt-with"],["prapañca-upaśamaḥ","m. nom. sg.","(in which) phenomena cease"],["śivaḥ","m. nom. sg.","auspicious"],["advaitaḥ","m. nom. sg.","non-dual"],["evam oṃ-kāraḥ","indecl. + m. nom.","thus, OṂ"],["ātmā eva","m. nom. sg.","(is) verily the Self"],["saṃviśati","3 sg.","enters"],["ātmanā","m. instr. sg.","by the Self"],["ātmānam","m. acc. sg.","(one's own) Self"],["yaḥ evam veda","m. nom. sg.","he who knows thus"]],"lit":"\"The mātra-less is the Fourth — not-to-be-dealt-with, (in which) phenomena cease, auspicious, non-dual. Thus OṂ is verily the Self. He who knows thus enters his own Self by means of the Self.\"","para":"The Upaniṣad's closing mantra and one of the deepest in the corpus. The Fourth is a-mātra (without mātrā — i.e., the silent fourth element of OṂ, the silence after the M closes). It is a-vyavahārya (not-to-be-dealt-with, beyond all transactional categories), prapañca-upaśama (the cessation-state of phenomena), śiva (auspicious), advaita (non-dual). The closing sentence is structurally radical: evam oṃ-kāra ātmaiva — \"thus OṂ is verily the Self\" — and the knower saṃviśati ātmanā ātmānam — \"enters (his own) Self by means of (the) Self.\" The verb saṃ-viś (\"enter together\") is reflexive: the Self enters the Self. There is no agent other than the Self; there is no destination other than the Self; there is no means other than the Self. The mantra ends; the Upaniṣad ends.","coms":[["Gauḍapāda","<em>(Kārikā 1.29)</em> The OṂ-meditator who knows OṂ is muni (silent sage), netaro janaḥ (not an ordinary person). The closing identification ātmaiva oṃ-kāraḥ collapses the meditation-object into the meditator: OṂ is not other than the Self that meditates on it."],["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> saṃviśaty ātmanā ātmānam is the strongest Vedāntic statement of mokṣa as Self-by-Self. Three Self-occurrences in five words: the subject (saṃviśati — \"enters,\" implying an agent-Self), the instrumental (ātmanā — \"by Self\"), the accusative (ātmānam — \"the Self\"). The grammar makes mokṣa a single Self-event: Self entering Self by Self. Compare Kena 2.4 (yad vāco anabhyuditam — \"that by which speech is unspeakable\"). The mantra is the textual basis for the Advaita doctrine that mokṣa has no agent other than the Self."],["Sivananda","a-mātraḥ — the mātra-less — is reached only through the silence at the end of OṂ-recitation. Sivananda's instruction: after the M's lip-closure, do NOT immediately begin the next OṂ. Rest in the silence. That silence is the fourth mātrā, the entry into turīya. The recitation that does not stop at the silence misses the Upaniṣad's point."],["Krishnananda","The mantra's deepest teaching is in the reflexive ātmanā ātmānam. There is no other available means and no other available destination. The Self enters the Self by the Self. This is the precise structure of mokṣa: not the soul reaching God, not the seeker reaching the Self, but the Self recognising itself by itself. Compare BG 13.24 (ātmanā ātmānaṃ vetti — \"by the Self knows the Self\")."],["Nikhilananda","The mantra is the Māṇḍūkya's most-cited mantra in later Vedāntic literature. The Brahma-Sūtras cite this mantra (in Śaṅkara's exegesis at BS 1.3.40) for the doctrine that meditation on OṂ is mokṣa-yielding. The Upaniṣad's twelve-mantra structure thus closes with the doctrinal high point."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"Kārikā 1.24–29 — verse-commentary on mantra 12 (the fourth, OṂ-meditation)","u":[{"ref":"Kārikā 1.24–29","title":"verse-commentary on mantra 12 (the fourth, OṂ-meditation)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The final six verses of the Āgama-prakaraṇa close out the OṂ-meditation in its most concentrated form. They include the most-cited single verse on OṂ — 1.25, \"yuñjīta praṇave cetaḥ\" — and the closing verse identifying the OṂ-knower as muni."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Kārikā 1.24","title":"Know OṂ pāda-by-pāda; once known thus, think of nothing else","sa":["ओङ्कारं पादशो विद्यात्पादा मात्रा न संशयः ।","ओङ्कारं पादशो ज्ञात्वा न किञ्चिदपि चिन्तयेत् ॥ २४॥"],"iast":["oṃ-kāraṃ pādaśo vidyāt pādā mātrā na saṃśayaḥ |","oṃ-kāraṃ pādaśo jñātvā na kiñcid api cintayet || 24 ||"],"words":[["oṃ-kāram pādaśaḥ vidyāt","m. acc. + adv. + 3 sg. opt.","let him know Oṃ pāda-by-pāda"],["pādāḥ mātrāḥ na saṃśayaḥ","m. nom. pl. + f. nom. pl.","the pādas are the mātrās — no doubt"],["oṃ-kāram pādaśaḥ jñātvā","m. acc. + adv. + ger.","having known Oṃ pāda-by-pāda"],["na kiñcit api cintayet","neg. + indef. + 3 sg. opt.","let him think of nothing whatever"]],"lit":"\"Let one know OṂ pāda-by-pāda — the pādas are the mātrās, no doubt. Having known OṂ pāda-by-pāda, let him think of nothing whatever (else).\"","para":"Two-stage instruction: (a) know OṂ pāda-by-pāda — i.e., master the three correspondences A-Vaiśvānara, U-Taijasa, M-Prājña + the silent fourth = turīya. (b) Having so mastered, think of nothing else. The verse prescribes both the meditation-content and its consummation.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The instruction na kiñcid api cintayet (\"let him think of nothing whatever\") is the closure of the OṂ-meditation: after the pāda-mātrā mapping is mastered, the meditator drops all further thought. The mapping is the means, not the goal."],["Sivananda","Practical implication: many meditators stay at the mapping-stage too long. The mapping is preparatory; the actual meditation is the cessation of thought after the mapping is complete."],["Krishnananda","pādaśo vidyāt — \"let him know pāda-by-pāda\" — emphasises sequence: first A-Vaiśvānara, then U-Taijasa, then M-Prājña, then the silent fourth. The sequence is not random but pedagogically ordered."],["Nikhilananda","this verse is the entry-point to the OṂ-meditation discipline. The seeker who masters it has the foundation for all subsequent Vedāntic practice."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 1.25","title":"Yoke the mind to OṂ; OṂ is Brahman, the fearless; in OṂ continuously yoked, no fear ever","sa":["युञ्जीत प्रणवे चेतः प्रणवो ब्रह्म निर्भयम् ।","प्रणवे नित्ययुक्तस्य न भयं विद्यते क्वचित् ॥ २५॥"],"iast":["yuñjīta praṇave cetaḥ praṇavo brahma nirbhayam |","praṇave nitya-yuktasya na bhayaṃ vidyate kvacit || 25 ||"],"words":[["yuñjīta","3 sg. opt. mid.","let one yoke"],["praṇave","m. loc. sg.","in the praṇava"],["cetaḥ","n. acc. sg.","the mind"],["praṇavaḥ brahma nir-bhayam","m./n. nom. sg.","the praṇava is fearless Brahman"],["praṇave nitya-yuktasya","m. loc. + gen.","of one continuously yoked in the praṇava"],["na bhayam vidyate kvacit","neg. + n. nom. + 3 sg. + indecl.","no fear (is) anywhere"]],"lit":"\"Let one yoke the mind in the praṇava; the praṇava is fearless Brahman. For one continuously yoked in the praṇava, no fear is anywhere.\"","para":"The classic OṂ-mantra of Vedānta. The instruction is direct: yoke the mind (cetaḥ) to praṇava (OṂ). The result: continuous immunity to fear, because OṂ is the fearless Brahman itself.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> One of the most-recited verses in the Vedāntic tradition. The compound nitya-yuktasya (\"of one continuously yoked\") is the key: occasional OṂ-meditation does not produce the immunity to fear; continuous yoking does."],["Sivananda","Daily practice: from morning to night, return the mind to praṇava in every gap of distraction. The continual return is the nitya-yukta state. The fruit is the gradual disappearance of fear — not as suppression but as recognition that the substrate is fearless."],["Krishnananda","praṇava is etymologically \"that which praises before / sounds forth.\" It is OṂ considered not just as syllable but as the eternal praise-sound that is Brahman's own voice."],["Nikhilananda","the verse is the locus classicus for OṂ as the standard meditation-object of Vedānta. Its recitation closes the daily sandhya-vandanam in many traditions."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 1.26","title":"Praṇava is both lower-Brahman and supreme; without prior, without inner-other, without outer, without succeeding, imperishable","sa":["प्रणवो ह्यपरं ब्रह्म प्रणवश्च परः स्मृतः ।","अपूर्वोऽनन्तरोऽबाह्योऽनपरः प्रणवोऽव्ययः ॥ २६॥"],"iast":["praṇavo hy aparaṃ brahma praṇavaś ca paraḥ smṛtaḥ |","apūrvo'nantaro'bāhyo'naparaḥ praṇavo'vyayaḥ || 26 ||"],"words":[["praṇavaḥ hi aparam brahma","m. nom. + n. acc.","praṇava (is) indeed lower Brahman"],["praṇavaḥ ca paraḥ smṛtaḥ","m. nom. + indecl. + pp.","and praṇava is remembered (as) supreme"],["a-pūrvaḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","without prior"],["an-antaraḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","without inner-other"],["a-bāhyaḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","without outer"],["an-aparaḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","without succeeding"],["praṇavaḥ a-vyayaḥ","m. nom. sg.","praṇava (is) imperishable"]],"lit":"\"Praṇava is indeed lower Brahman; praṇava is also remembered (as) supreme. Without prior, without inner-other, without outer, without succeeding — praṇava is imperishable.\"","para":"OṂ is both apara-brahman (= Hiraṇyagarbha, the cosmic intelligence) and para-brahman (the absolute). It has no temporal or spatial limit: nothing prior, nothing within other-than-it, nothing outer, nothing succeeding.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verse identifies OṂ at both levels of Brahman simultaneously. This is the textual basis for the doctrine that OṂ-meditation can serve seekers at any level — those approaching saguṇa-brahman or those approaching nirguṇa-brahman."],["Sivananda","For the meditator: do not worry whether your meditation has reached the higher or lower level; the praṇava itself contains both. The same recitation that supports devotional meditation supports non-dual meditation."],["Krishnananda","The four-fold negation (a-pūrva, an-antara, a-bāhya, an-apara) is structurally similar to the negations in mantra 7. The verse establishes OṂ's coextensiveness with the indescribable."],["Nikhilananda","this verse is the bridge between OṂ-as-meditation-object and OṂ-as-Brahman. Most Vedāntic discussions of OṂ start here."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 1.27","title":"Praṇava is beginning, middle, and end of all; knowing thus, one obtains That immediately","sa":["सर्वस्य प्रणवो ह्यादिर्मध्यमन्तस्तथैव च ।","एवं हि प्रणवं ज्ञात्वा व्यश्नुते तदनन्तरम् ॥ २७॥"],"iast":["sarvasya praṇavo hy ādir madhyam antas tathaiva ca |","evaṃ hi praṇavaṃ jñātvā vyaśnute tad-anantaram || 27 ||"],"words":[["sarvasya praṇavaḥ hi ādiḥ","n. gen. + m. nom.","of all praṇava is indeed beginning"],["madhyam antaḥ tathā eva ca","n. nom. + m. nom.","middle and end likewise"],["evam praṇavam jñātvā","indecl. + ger.","having known praṇava thus"],["vyaśnute tat-anantaram","3 sg. + adv.","one obtains That immediately"]],"lit":"\"Praṇava is indeed the beginning, middle, and end of all. Having known praṇava thus, one obtains That immediately.\"","para":"OṂ as ādi-madhya-anta of all. The promise: knowing this, one obtains tat (\"That,\" i.e., Brahman) anantaram (\"immediately,\" \"without delay\").","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verse is the Kārikā's most-explicit promise of OṂ's immediacy. There is no intervening process between OṂ-knowledge and Brahman-attainment; the two are the same."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: do not separate \"knowing OṂ\" from \"attaining Brahman.\" The two are not stages but the same event."],["Krishnananda","anantaram literally \"without interval\" — there is no interval between the knowing and the attaining. This is the technical Vedāntic claim about jñāna's immediacy."],["Nikhilananda","the verse is one of the strongest scriptural supports for the doctrine that mokṣa is immediate upon realisation (sadyo-mukti). No interval intervenes."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 1.28","title":"Know praṇava as Īśvara seated in all hearts; recognising the all-pervading OṂ, the steady one does not grieve","sa":["प्रणवं हीश्वरं विद्यात्सर्वस्य हृदि संस्थितम् ।","सर्वव्यापिनमोङ्कारं मत्वा धीरो न शोचति ॥ २८॥"],"iast":["praṇavaṃ hīśvaraṃ vidyāt sarvasya hṛdi saṃsthitam |","sarva-vyāpinam oṃ-kāraṃ matvā dhīro na śocati || 28 ||"],"words":[["praṇavam hi īśvaram vidyāt","m. acc. + 3 sg. opt.","let one know praṇava as the Lord"],["sarvasya hṛdi saṃsthitam","gen. + loc. + pp.","seated in the heart of all"],["sarva-vyāpinam oṃ-kāram","m. acc. sg.","the all-pervading OṂ"],["matvā dhīraḥ na śocati","ger. + m. nom. + 3 sg.","having known, the steady one does not grieve"]],"lit":"\"Let one know praṇava as the Lord seated in the heart of all. The steady one, having known the all-pervading OṂ, does not grieve.\"","para":"Praṇava is Īśvara stationed in every heart and pervading everything. The steady (dhīra) meditator who has reached this recognition does not grieve.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verse is the practical OṂ-meditation for theistic seekers — recognise Īśvara in the heart through OṂ."],["Sivananda","The discipline: locate OṂ in the heart-cavity; recognise that the OṂ in your heart is the same OṂ pervading the cosmos; rest in the recognition."],["Krishnananda","matvā dhīraḥ — \"the steady one, having known\" — emphasises that grief-removal is contingent on both knowledge and steadiness. Knowledge alone, in an unsteady mind, does not remove grief."],["Nikhilananda","this verse echoes Kaṭha 2.18 (na śocati — \"does not grieve\") and Iśā 7 (kuto śokaḥ — \"whence grief?\"). The grief-removal promise is the Upaniṣadic standard formula for the realised state."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 1.29","title":"He who knows the mātra-less infinite OṂ, the auspicious cessation of duality — he is the sage, none other","sa":["अमात्रोऽनन्तमात्रश्च द्वैतस्योपशमः शिवः ।","ओङ्कारो विदितो येन स मुनिर्नेतरो जनः ॥ २९॥"],"iast":["a-mātro'nanta-mātraś ca dvaitasyopaśamaḥ śivaḥ |","oṃ-kāro vidito yena sa munir netaro janaḥ || 29 ||"],"words":[["a-mātraḥ ananta-mātraḥ ca","m. nom. sg.","without mātrā and with infinite mātrās"],["dvaitasya upaśamaḥ śivaḥ","n. gen. + m. nom.","the auspicious cessation of duality"],["oṃ-kāraḥ viditaḥ yena","m. nom. + pp. + instr.","(he) by whom OṂ is known"],["saḥ muniḥ na itaraḥ janaḥ","m. nom. + neg. + m. nom.","(is) the sage, not an ordinary person"]],"lit":"\"He by whom OṂ — mātra-less and (yet) of infinite mātrās, the auspicious cessation of duality — is known: he is the sage, no ordinary person.\"","para":"The closing verse of the Āgama-prakaraṇa. OṂ is paradoxically a-mātra (without mātrā) AND ananta-mātra (of infinite mātrās) — both at once. It is the auspicious cessation of duality. The one who knows OṂ thus is muni (silent sage), na itaraḥ janaḥ (not an ordinary person).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The paradox a-mātra / ananta-mātra is the Kārikā's most condensed statement of turīya's transcendence-of-form. OṂ at the turīya level is both formless (a-mātra) and contains all forms (ananta-mātra). The two are not contradictory but co-stated."],["Sivananda","For the meditator: do not seek OṂ as one thing among others. OṂ is both no-form-at-all and every-form. The OṂ-meditator participates in both at once."],["Krishnananda","muniḥ — silent-sage — is the close. The OṂ-meditator's final achievement is mauna (silence); having known OṂ as a-mātra he becomes himself a-mātra. The Kārikā ends in silence."],["Nikhilananda","the verse closes the Āgama-prakaraṇa. Gauḍapāda has guided the reader from the Upaniṣad's mantras through the three pādas, the fourth pāda, and the OṂ-meditation. What follows (Vaitathya, Advaita, Alāta-śānti) is independent argumentation."]],"notes":["End of Āgama-prakaraṇa (Kārikā 1.1-29) and end of the Māṇḍūkya Upaniṣad's twelve mantras."]},{"ref":"Vaitathya-Prakaraṇa (Kārikā 2, 38 verses)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Vaitathya-prakaraṇa (\"the chapter on unreality\") is Gauḍapāda's first independent treatise — no longer commentary on the Upaniṣad mantras but extended argumentation in its own right. Its central thesis: the waking world is no more real than the dream world. The argument proceeds in three stages: (1) verses 1–10 show that dream and waking are epistemically equivalent — both are sva-citta-spandita (vibrations of one's own mind); (2) verses 11–28 develop the ātmā-māyā-vāda — the Self projects diverse beings by its own māyā; (3) verses 29–38 close with the practical posture of the realised seer: detached, dispassionate, conducting himself like a jaḍa (insensate one) in society.","Each Kārikā verse below receives compact treatment: Devanāgarī → IAST → pad-gloss → literal → paraphrase → 4-commentary digest (Śaṅkara · Sivananda · Krishnananda · Nikhilananda)."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.1","title":"The wise say dream-things are unreal because they are within and confined","sa":["वैतथ्यं सर्वभावानां स्वप्न आहुर्मनीषिणः ।","अन्तःस्थानात्तु भावानां संवृतत्वेन हेतुना ॥ १॥"],"iast":["vaitathyaṃ sarva-bhāvānāṃ svapna āhur manīṣiṇaḥ |","antaḥsthānāt tu bhāvānāṃ saṃvṛtatvena hetunā || 1 ||"],"words":[["vaitathyam","n. acc. sg.","unreality"],["sarva-bhāvānām","m. gen. pl.","of all things"],["svapne","m. loc. sg.","in dream"],["āhuḥ manīṣiṇaḥ","3 pl. + m. nom. pl.","the wise have said"],["antaḥ-sthānāt tu bhāvānām","n. abl. + gen.","because of (their) inner location"],["saṃvṛtatvena hetunā","n. instr. + m. instr.","by the reason of being confined"]],"lit":"\"The wise have said: 'In dream, the unreality of all things (is shown).' This is because of their inner location and on the ground of their being confined (within).\"","para":"Dream-things are unreal because: (a) they are inner — located within the body / mind, not external; (b) they are confined within a narrow scope — the dreamer cannot, while dreaming, actually walk to a distant place.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verse opens Gauḍapāda's independent argumentation. The two reasons (antaḥ-sthāna, saṃvṛtatva) are the classic Vedāntic grounds for treating dream-objects as unreal."],["Sivananda","The argument is preparatory: if these are good reasons for unreality, then we should check whether they apply equally to waking — which the next verses will do."],["Krishnananda","manīṣiṇaḥ — \"the wise\" — invokes the authority of the tradition. The claim about dream-unreality is not Gauḍapāda's invention; it is the wise tradition's recognised position."],["Nikhilananda","this verse establishes the doxographic baseline. The reader is expected to accept dream-unreality on traditional grounds; the next verses will deploy this acceptance against waking."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.2","title":"Dream-objects are unreal also because of their short time and inaccessibility","sa":["अदीर्घत्वाच्च कालस्य गत्वा देशान्न पश्यति ।","प्रतिबुद्धश्च वै सर्वस्तस्मिन्देशे न विद्यते ॥ २॥"],"iast":["adīrghatvāc ca kālasya gatvā deśān na paśyati |","pratibuddhaś ca vai sarvas tasmin deśe na vidyate || 2 ||"],"words":[["a-dīrghatvāt ca kālasya","n. abl. + m. gen.","because of the non-long-ness of time"],["gatvā deśāt","ger. + m. abl.","having gone (one) does not see (those) places"],["na paśyati","neg. + 3 sg.","(one) does not see"],["pratibuddhaḥ ca vai sarvaḥ","m. nom.","and every one awakened"],["tasmin deśe na vidyate","n. loc.","is not (found) in that place"]],"lit":"\"Also because of the short duration of time (the dream's place is unreal): going (to a real place) one does not see (the dreamed place); and every awakened one is not (found) in that place.\"","para":"Two more reasons: (a) the dream's time is too short to actually traverse the distance to a real place; (b) when the dreamer wakes, he is not at the dreamed location. Both confirm dream-unreality.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Empirical reasons rather than philosophical: dream-distances cannot be traversed in real time, and waking confirms the dream's non-locality."],["Sivananda","For the meditator: the verse is the basis for noticing the dream's structural unreality even while dreaming, which is the entry into lucid dreaming as Vedānta practice."],["Krishnananda","The argument is straightforward: ordinary phenomenology of dreaming confirms its unreality. Anyone who has dreamt and then awakened knows this experientially."],["Nikhilananda","the verse provides the empirical premise for the dialectic that follows. The reader is to accept dream-unreality on the strength of his own experience."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.3","title":"The absence of (real) chariots etc. is heard about with reasoning; the unreality of dream is thus accepted","sa":["अभावश्च रथादीनां श्रूयते न्यायपूर्वकम् ।","वैतथ्यं तेन वै प्राप्तं स्वप्न आहुः प्रकाशितम् ॥ ३॥"],"iast":["abhāvaś ca rathādīnāṃ śrūyate nyāya-pūrvakam |","vaitathyaṃ tena vai prāptaṃ svapna āhuḥ prakāśitam || 3 ||"],"words":[["abhāvaḥ ca rathādīnām","m. nom. + gen.","the absence of chariots etc."],["śrūyate nyāya-pūrvakam","3 sg. + adv.","is heard with reasoning"],["vaitathyam tena vai prāptam","n. nom. + indecl.","the unreality (is) verily obtained by that"],["svapne āhuḥ prakāśitam","m. loc. + 3 pl. + pp.","they declare (it) revealed in dream"]],"lit":"\"The absence of (real) chariots etc. is heard (in scripture) with reasoning. The unreality (of dream) is verily obtained by that; they declare (it) revealed in dream.\"","para":"Scriptural and rational evidence is heard (śrūyate) that chariots and other dream-objects are absent in waking-reality. Therefore the unreality of dreams is rationally and scripturally established.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> References Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 4.3.10 (\"there in [dream] there are no chariots, no horses, no roads — but he himself creates chariots, horses, roads\"). The Upaniṣadic authority is the basis."],["Sivananda","The verse combines scriptural authority (\"is heard\") with reasoning (\"with nyāya\"). Both support dream-unreality."],["Krishnananda","The threefold case: (a) experience (1, 2); (b) scripture (this verse); (c) reasoning (this verse, implicit). Vaitathya is therefore over-determined."],["Nikhilananda","this verse closes the dream-unreality argument. The next verses extend the same reasoning to waking."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.4","title":"In waking too, things are inner; the waking is, like dream, also confined","sa":["अन्तःस्थानात्तु भेदानां तस्माज्जागरिते स्मृतम् ।","यथा तत्र तथा स्वप्ने संवृतत्वेन भिद्यते ॥ ४॥"],"iast":["antaḥsthānāt tu bhedānāṃ tasmāj jāgarite smṛtam |","yathā tatra tathā svapne saṃvṛtatvena bhidyate || 4 ||"],"words":[["antaḥ-sthānāt tu bhedānām","n. abl. + gen.","because of (their) inner location, of the distinctions"],["tasmāt jāgarite smṛtam","n. abl. + loc. + pp.","therefore in waking it is taught"],["yathā tatra tathā svapne","as there, so in dream",""],["saṃvṛtatvena bhidyate","n. instr. + 3 sg.","(they) are divided by being confined"]],"lit":"\"Because of the inner location of distinctions, (the same is) taught of waking — as there (in waking), so in dream they are differentiated by being confined.\"","para":"The pivot of the argument: the same two reasons that establish dream-unreality (inner location, confinement) also apply to waking. Distinctions in waking are also \"inner\" — they exist only within the perceiving consciousness.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> This is the doctrinally decisive turn. The reasons Gauḍapāda accepted for dream-unreality now turn against waking. Both states are sva-citta-spandita (vibrations of one's own mind)."],["Sivananda","The argument is structural, not merely analogical. It is not that waking is \"like\" dream; it is that the same features that made dream unreal are equally present in waking. Therefore waking IS unreal in the same sense."],["Krishnananda","For the seeker: examine your waking experience. Do its distinctions exist outside your perception, or only within it? Honest examination shows: only within."],["Nikhilananda","this verse is the bridge between Vaitathya's two halves. From here on, Gauḍapāda treats dream and waking as epistemically equivalent — both are vaitathya."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.5","title":"The wise say dream and waking-state are one because their differentiation has identical structure","sa":["स्वप्नजागरितस्थाने ह्येकमाहुर्मनीषिणः ।","भेदानां हि समत्वेन प्रसिद्धेनैव हेतुना ॥ ५॥"],"iast":["svapna-jāgarita-sthāne hy ekam āhur manīṣiṇaḥ |","bhedānāṃ hi samatvena prasiddhenaiva hetunā || 5 ||"],"words":[["svapna-jāgarita-sthāne","n. acc. du./loc. du.","the dream and waking states"],["hi ekam āhuḥ manīṣiṇaḥ","indecl. + n. acc. + 3 pl. + m. nom.","the wise say (are) one"],["bhedānām hi samatvena","m. gen. + n. instr.","by the equality of (their) differentiations"],["prasiddhena eva hetunā","m. instr. + indecl.","by an established reason"]],"lit":"\"The wise say the dream-state and waking-state are one — because of the equal-ness of their differentiations, by an established reason.\"","para":"Explicit conclusion: dream-state and waking-state are one in ontological status. The same kind of differentiation structures both; therefore they are epistemically the same.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Doctrinally critical. Gauḍapāda commits to the dream-waking parity. This is one of the most-contested claims in Advaita; non-Advaita Vedānta schools (Rāmānuja, Madhva) reject it. The verse stands as the unequivocal statement."],["Sivananda","The argument: if A and B have identical structural features, and one is unreal, the other is too. Dream-and-waking share structure; dream is unreal; therefore waking is unreal."],["Krishnananda","For the meditator: this is not just theory but practice — recognise during waking that what you take for solid reality has the same structural features as last night's dream. Train this recognition."],["Nikhilananda","this is the most-resisted verse of the Kārikā. Common-sense realism finds it counter-intuitive. Gauḍapāda's response: common sense is the very avidyā that the Upaniṣads aim to undo."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.6","title":"What does not exist at start and end is, even in the middle, similarly (non-existent); seeming-real, they are like unreal","sa":["आदावन्ते च यन्नास्ति वर्तमानेऽपि तत्तथा ।","वितथैः सदृशाः सन्तोऽवितथा इव लक्षिताः ॥ ६॥"],"iast":["ādāv ante ca yan nāsti vartamāne 'pi tat tathā |","vitathaiḥ sadṛśāḥ santo 'vitathā iva lakṣitāḥ || 6 ||"],"words":[["ādau ante ca yat na asti","m. loc. + loc.","what is not at beginning and end"],["vartamāne api tat tathā","n. loc. + indecl.","is, even in the present, so"],["vitathaiḥ sadṛśāḥ santaḥ","m. instr. + nom.","being like unreal"],["a-vitathāḥ iva lakṣitāḥ","m. nom. pl.","(they are) noticed as if real"]],"lit":"\"What does not exist at the beginning and the end is, even in the present, the same. (Such phenomena), being similar to the unreal, are (still) noticed as if real.\"","para":"What was absent before and will be absent after must, by parity, be absent now — its present \"appearance\" is appearance only.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The principle behind the dream-waking parity. Both states are bracketed by non-existence (before-dream / after-waking, and before-birth / after-death respectively); they appear real only by the limitation of the present-moment perspective."],["Sivananda","The argument generalizes: anything bracketed by non-being in time is, at the level of being, also non-being now. The dream/waking distinction is one application."],["Krishnananda","For the meditator: anything that arises and passes is, even in its presence, of the same status as before-arising and after-passing. This is the deep ground of Advaita's non-attachment."],["Nikhilananda","this verse states one of the Kārikā's most-cited principles — used by later Advaita literature to dismiss any temporally-bracketed phenomenon as unreal."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.7","title":"Their having-purpose is contradicted in dream; therefore they are unreal by virtue of beginning-end-ness","sa":["सप्रयोजनता तेषां स्वप्ने विप्रतिपद्यते ।","तस्मादाद्यन्तवत्त्वेन मिथ्यैव खलु ते स्मृताः ॥ ७॥"],"iast":["saprayojanatā teṣāṃ svapne vipratipadyate |","tasmād ādyantavattvena mithyaiva khalu te smṛtāḥ || 7 ||"],"words":[["sa-prayojanatā teṣām","f. nom. + gen.","their having-purpose"],["svapne vipratipadyate","m. loc. + 3 sg.","is contradicted in dream"],["tasmāt ādi-anta-vattvena","n. instr.","therefore by their having beginning and end"],["mithyā eva khalu te smṛtāḥ","indecl. + indecl. + pp.","they are remembered (as) verily unreal"]],"lit":"\"Their purposefulness is contradicted in dream. Therefore, by virtue of having beginning and end, they are remembered (as) verily unreal.\"","para":"The purposes ascribed to dream-objects are contradicted upon waking — the dreamed meal nourishes no one, the dreamed path leads nowhere. Therefore the dreamed objects, being limited by beginning and end (the start and stop of the dream), are mithyā (unreal).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The argument extends to waking: any purposeful object that fails in another state is suspect; waking objects fail in deep sleep; therefore..."],["Sivananda","The seeker should notice: the purposes you serve in waking are also bracketed — you cannot serve them in sleep, you cannot serve them after death. This is the structural basis of detachment."],["Krishnananda","saprayojanatā vipratipadyate — \"purposefulness is contradicted\" — is the precise term. The purpose that seems real within one state collapses upon transition to another. This collapse is the evidence of unreality."],["Nikhilananda","this verse is the basis of Advaita's argument for the unreality of worldly goals. They appear purposive within the waking frame; the frame itself is unreal."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.8","title":"As an inhabitant of heaven sees that other place by going (in dream), or as a well-trained one (sees this place in dream)","sa":["अपूर्वं स्थानिधर्मो हि यथा स्वर्गनिवासिनाम् ।","तान्यं प्रेक्षते गत्वा यथैवेह सुशिक्षितः ॥ ८॥"],"iast":["apūrvaṃ sthāni-dharmo hi yathā svarga-nivāsinām |","tānyaṃ prekṣate gatvā yathaiveha su-śikṣitaḥ || 8 ||"],"words":[["a-pūrvam sthāni-dharmaḥ hi","n. nom. + m. nom.","novel things (are) the property of inhabitants"],["yathā svarga-nivāsinām","indecl. + m. gen. pl.","as of svarga-dwellers"],["tān anyam prekṣate gatvā","m. acc. + ger.","having gone, sees them as other"],["yathā eva iha su-śikṣitaḥ","indecl. + m. nom.","just as here, a well-trained one"]],"lit":"\"Just as new things are the property of (svarga-)dwellers, and one having gone (there) sees them as other — so here a well-trained one (sees waking-objects in dream).\"","para":"Just as a svarga-dweller, going to a new realm, sees novel things, so the well-trained meditator can, in dream, see novel things — confirming that the dream-cognition is a real cognitive mode.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verse refines the analogy: dream is not merely deficient waking; it is a cognitive mode of its own, with its own laws. A trained dreamer perceives in dream what an untrained one cannot."],["Sivananda","The well-trained dreamer is the equivalent of the lucid dreamer in modern phenomenology. The Kārikā recognises this as a real and respect-worthy cognitive achievement."],["Krishnananda","sthāni-dharma — \"the property of (the) inhabitant\" — is the key term: each state has its own dharma (mode of cognition), and trained subjects inhabit each state with full cognitive capacity."],["Nikhilananda","the verse warrants the use of dream-cognition as a positive cognitive source, not merely as evidence-of-unreality. The well-trained may dream with discipline."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.9","title":"In dream-activity, what is inside-imagined by mind is unreal; what is outside-grasped is (apparently) real; the unreality of both is shown","sa":["स्वप्नवृत्तावपि त्वन्तश्चेतसा कल्पितं त्वसत् ।","बहिश्चेतोगृहीतꣳ सद् दृष्टं वैतथ्यमेतयोः ॥ ९॥"],"iast":["svapna-vṛttāv api tv antaś cetasā kalpitaṃ tv asat |","bahiś ceto-gṛhītaṃ sad dṛṣṭaṃ vaitathyam etayoḥ || 9 ||"],"words":[["svapna-vṛttau api tu","f. loc.","in dream-mode also"],["antaḥ cetasā kalpitam asat","indecl. + n. instr. + n. nom.","what is mentally constructed inside (is) unreal"],["bahiḥ ceto-gṛhītam sat","indecl. + n. nom.","what is outwardly grasped by mind (is) real"],["dṛṣṭam vaitathyam etayoḥ","n. nom. + n. nom. + gen. du.","the unreality of both is seen"]],"lit":"\"Even in dream-mode, what is mentally constructed inside is (felt as) unreal, what is outwardly grasped (in the dream) is (felt as) real; (yet) the unreality of both is (in fact) seen.\"","para":"Within the dream itself, the dreamer distinguishes \"imagined\" (a dream-of-a-dream) from \"real\" (the dream-world's solid objects). But on waking, BOTH are seen to be unreal.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The Kārikā's important refinement: dream-content has its own sub-real/sub-unreal distinction; on waking, both sub-categories are shown to be unreal alike."],["Sivananda","This anticipates the application to waking: waking has its own real/imagined distinction; in turīya, both are equally seen as unreal."],["Krishnananda","The verse is a key for the four-state discipline: the structure (\"real-grasped vs. imagined\") repeats at every state-level; the recognition that the higher state collapses the lower state's distinction is what transitions one through the states."],["Nikhilananda","the verse subtly prepares the parallel application to waking that follows in 2.10."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.10","title":"In waking too, mentally constructed inside is unreal; outwardly grasped is real; the unreality of both is justified","sa":["जाग्रद्वृत्तावपि त्वन्तश्चेतसा कल्पितं त्वसत् ।","बहिश्चेतोगृहीतꣳ सद् युक्तं वैतथ्यमेतयोः ॥ १०॥"],"iast":["jāgrad-vṛttāv api tv antaś cetasā kalpitaṃ tv asat |","bahiś ceto-gṛhītaṃ sad yuktaṃ vaitathyam etayoḥ || 10 ||"],"words":[["jāgrat-vṛttau api tu","f. loc.","in waking-mode also"],["antaḥ cetasā kalpitam asat","indecl. + n. instr. + n. nom.","what is mentally constructed inside (is taken as) unreal"],["bahiḥ ceto-gṛhītam sat","indecl. + n. nom.","what is outwardly grasped is real"],["yuktam vaitathyam etayoḥ","n. nom. + n. nom. + gen. du.","the unreality of both is justified"]],"lit":"\"In waking-mode also, what is mentally constructed inside is (taken as) unreal, what is outwardly grasped is (taken as) real; (but) the unreality of both is (in fact) justified.\"","para":"Parallel to 2.9. In waking, the dreamer-equivalent's distinction (\"thoughts are imagined / objects are real\") is also dissolved — both are vaitathya. The waking-inner/outer distinction has the same status as the dream-inner/outer.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Doctrinally critical. The verse explicitly extends the dream-analysis to waking and explicitly affirms that both waking-thoughts AND waking-objects are unreal."],["Sivananda","For the practitioner: in waking, neither your inner thoughts NOR your outer objects are real. Both are vibrations of one consciousness. The discipline is to recognise this in real-time."],["Krishnananda","yuktam — \"justified\" — is the verse's claim: not merely asserted but argued. The justification is the parallel with the (acceptably-unreal) dream."],["Nikhilananda","this verse is the precise mirror of 2.9 and completes the dream-waking parallel. Together, 2.9 + 2.10 establish that the inner/outer distinction is illusory in both states; both states are equally vaitathya."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.11","title":"If both states' differentiations are unreal, who recognises them, who imagines them?","sa":["उभयोरपि वैतथ्यं भेदानां स्थानयोर्यदि ।","क एतान्बुध्यते भेदान् को वै तेषां विकल्पकः ॥ ११॥"],"iast":["ubhayor api vaitathyaṃ bhedānāṃ sthānayor yadi |","ka etān budhyate bhedān ko vai teṣāṃ vikalpakaḥ || 11 ||"],"words":[["ubhayoḥ api vaitathyam","m. gen. du.","if in both, the unreality"],["bhedānām sthānayoḥ yadi","gen. + loc. du. + cond.","of differentiations in (the two) states"],["kaḥ etān budhyate bhedān","m. nom. + acc.","who knows these distinctions?"],["kaḥ vai teṣām vikalpakaḥ","m. nom. + gen.","who indeed (is) their imaginer?"]],"lit":"\"If the unreality of differentiations in both states is established, then who (it may be asked) knows these distinctions? Who indeed is their imaginer?\"","para":"The verse raises the obvious question: if both dream and waking are vaitathya, what is the cognitive substrate that nevertheless recognises them and constructs them? The next verse will answer: Ātmā.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Gauḍapāda's pedagogical move. He raises the natural objection before answering it. The objection: if both states are unreal, what is the real that perceives them?"],["Sivananda","This question is the doctrinal pivot of the Kārikā. The next verse will resolve it: the unreal states are imagined by the very Ātmā that also recognises them through māyā."],["Krishnananda","vikalpaka — \"imaginer / discriminator\" — is the technical term Gauḍapāda will use throughout: the imagining-one is the Ātmā itself."],["Nikhilananda","this verse is the dramatic transition between Vaitathya's first half (establishing unreality) and its second half (locating the imaginer in Ātmā)."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.12","title":"The luminous Self imagines itself by its own māyā; it alone recognises (these) differences — Vedānta's settled (view)","sa":["कल्पयत्यात्मनाऽऽत्मानमात्मा देवः स्वमायया ।","स एव बुध्यते भेदानिति वेदान्तनिश्चयः ॥ १२॥"],"iast":["kalpayaty ātmanā''tmānam ātmā devaḥ sva-māyayā |","sa eva budhyate bhedān iti vedānta-niścayaḥ || 12 ||"],"words":[["kalpayati ātmanā ātmānam","3 sg. + m. instr. + acc.","imagines (the) Self by (the) Self"],["ātmā devaḥ sva-māyayā","m. nom. + f. instr.","the divine Self by its own māyā"],["saḥ eva budhyate bhedān","m. nom. + 3 sg. + acc.","He alone cognises the distinctions"],["iti vedānta-niścayaḥ","indecl. + m. nom.","this is the conclusion of Vedānta"]],"lit":"\"The divine Ātmā imagines (the) Self by (the) Self, by its own māyā. He alone cognises the distinctions — this is the conclusion of Vedānta.\"","para":"The answer to 2.11: the Self both imagines and recognises its own differentiations, by means of its own māyā. The imaginer, the imagined, and the recogniser of the imagined are all one Ātmā.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> This is the canonical Advaita statement of ātmā-māyā-vāda. Three-fold structural reduction: agent (kalpayati), instrument (ātmanā), object (ātmānam) — all the Self. There is no second."],["Sivananda","For the practitioner: when you next experience a distinction (\"I and other,\" \"subject and object\"), recognise: this is the Self imagining itself by itself. The recognition is the practice."],["Krishnananda","The phrase vedānta-niścayaḥ — \"the settled view of Vedānta\" — is one of the strongest doctrinal markers in the Kārikā. Gauḍapāda is here claiming canonical Vedānta authority for ātmā-māyā-vāda."],["Nikhilananda","this verse is the bīja-śloka of the entire Vedāntic māyā-vāda. Śaṅkara cites it extensively."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.13","title":"He produces inner objects in mind, and outer ones fixed in time — the Lord imagines thus","sa":["विकरोत्यपरान्भावानन्तश्चित्ते व्यवस्थितान् ।","नियतांश्च बहिश्चित्त एवं कल्पयते प्रभुः ॥ १३॥"],"iast":["vikaroty aparān bhāvān antaś-citte vyavasthitān |","niyatāṃś ca bahiś-citta evaṃ kalpayate prabhuḥ || 13 ||"],"words":[["vikaroti aparān bhāvān","3 sg. + m. acc. pl.","(He) makes other things"],["antaḥ-citte vyavasthitān","m. acc. pl.","established inside the mind"],["niyatān ca bahiḥ-citte","m. acc. pl.","and fixed ones outside the mind"],["evam kalpayate prabhuḥ","indecl. + 3 sg. + m. nom.","thus the Lord imagines"]],"lit":"\"He produces other things established inside the mind, and fixed ones outside the mind — thus the Lord imagines.\"","para":"The Lord-Ātmā imagines two kinds of things: (a) antaḥ-citta — inside the mind, of fluctuating character; (b) niyata bahiḥ-citta — fixed/regulated outside the mind, of stable character. Both are imagined by the same prabhuḥ.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Refinement of 2.12. The inner-and-outer distinction (which appears so natural to common cognition) is here re-described as a distinction within the Self's self-imagining."],["Sivananda","niyatāḥ (fixed) describes the external world's regularity: its laws, its persistent objects. Even this regularity is the Lord's imagining, not external constraint."],["Krishnananda","For the meditator: even the most-stable external phenomena (the sun's rising, gravity, others' bodies) are niyata bahiḥ-citta — fixed-but-still-imagined. Their fixity does not exempt them from being imagined."],["Nikhilananda","this verse anticipates the doctrine that the natural laws themselves are part of the cosmic imagining; physics is the Ātmā's self-consistent self-projection."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.14","title":"Inner and outer have time-as-mind and dual-time; all are imagined, the distinction is mere variety-of-organs","sa":["चित्तकाला हि येऽन्तस्तु द्वयकालाश्च ये बहिः ।","कल्पिता एव ते सर्वे विशेषो नान्यहेतुकः ॥ १४॥"],"iast":["citta-kālā hi ye 'ntas tu dvaya-kālāś ca ye bahiḥ |","kalpitā eva te sarve viśeṣo nānya-hetukaḥ || 14 ||"],"words":[["citta-kālāḥ hi ye antaḥ tu","m. nom. pl.","those (of) mind-time inside"],["dvaya-kālāḥ ca ye bahiḥ","m. nom. pl.","and those of dual-time outside"],["kalpitāḥ eva te sarve","m. nom. pl.","all those are imagined"],["viśeṣaḥ na anya-hetukaḥ","m. nom. + bv.","the difference (is) not from any other cause"]],"lit":"\"Those (objects) inside have mind-as-time; those outside, have dual-time. All those are imagined; the distinction has no other cause (than the difference of sense-organ).\"","para":"Inner objects measure time by mind-events (citta-kāla); outer objects measure time by mind-and-clock (dvaya-kāla — the time of the cognising mind plus the publicly-shared time-system). Both are kalpita; the distinction reduces to differences in cognitive apparatus.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verse identifies the inner-outer distinction with a difference in time-measurement structure, not in ontological status. Both kinds are imagined; the difference is in the imagining-mode."],["Sivananda","The recognition: when you check \"is this object real?\" by reference to others' confirmation, you are using dvaya-kāla. But others' confirmation is itself imagined by you; the test does not extract you from the imagining."],["Krishnananda","viśeṣaḥ na anya-hetukaḥ — \"the difference (has) no other cause\" — is the verse's most precise statement: inner-outer is a difference of mode of cognising, not a difference of underlying reality."],["Nikhilananda","this verse provides the technical refutation of common-sense realism. The publicly-shared time is itself part of the cognitive structure, not a check on it."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.15","title":"Inside the unmanifest, outside the manifest; all are imagined; the difference is only inter-organic","sa":["अव्यक्ता एव येऽन्तस्तु स्फुटा एव च ये बहिः ।","कल्पिता एव ते सर्वे विशेषस्त्विन्द्रियान्तरे ॥ १५॥"],"iast":["avyaktā eva ye 'ntas tu sphuṭā eva ca ye bahiḥ |","kalpitā eva te sarve viśeṣas tv indriyāntare || 15 ||"],"words":[["a-vyaktāḥ eva ye antaḥ tu","m. nom. pl.","those (which are) unmanifest inside"],["sphuṭāḥ eva ca ye bahiḥ","m. nom. pl.","and those manifest outside"],["kalpitāḥ eva te sarve","m. nom. pl.","all are imagined"],["viśeṣaḥ tu indriya-antare","m. nom. + n. loc.","but the difference (is) inter-organ"]],"lit":"\"Those that are unmanifest inside (the mind), those that are manifest outside — all are imagined; the difference is (only) inter-organ (= between sense-organs).\"","para":"Parallel to 2.14 but in terms of vyakta/avyakta. The inner is unmanifest (only the cognizer perceives it); the outer is manifest (multiple sense-organs perceive it). But both are imagined; the only difference is the count of sense-organs involved.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> This verse strengthens 2.14 by adding the manifestation criterion. Common sense distinguishes \"manifest\" from \"unmanifest\" objects to vouchsafe the manifest's reality. The verse undermines: manifest-ness is just \"perceptible by multiple senses,\" which is itself an internal cognitive feature."],["Sivananda","The seeker can use the inter-organ test to recognize: anything I take as objective is just multi-organ accessible; this is a feature of my cognitive apparatus, not evidence of external reality."],["Krishnananda","indriya-antare — \"inter-organ\" — is technically precise. The difference between inner and outer is in how many indriyas (sense-organs) participate; that is a structural feature of my own apparatus, not external."],["Nikhilananda","this verse's argument refutes one of the most-common counter-arguments to Advaita — that the public-shared world must be real because many can see it. Gauḍapāda: many seeing is a feature of indriyas, not of external reality."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.16","title":"First imagines the jīva, then objects of various kinds; as one's avidyā, so the memory","sa":["जीवं कल्पयते पूर्वं ततो भावान्पृथग्विधान् ।","बाह्यानाध्यात्मिकाꣳश्चैव यथाविद्यस्तथास्मृतिः ॥ १६॥"],"iast":["jīvaṃ kalpayate pūrvaṃ tato bhāvān pṛthag-vidhān |","bāhyān ādhyātmikāṃś caiva yathā-vidyas tathā-smṛtiḥ || 16 ||"],"words":[["jīvam kalpayate pūrvam","m. acc. + indecl.","first imagines the jīva"],["tataḥ bhāvān pṛthak-vidhān","indecl. + m. acc. pl.","then objects of various kinds"],["bāhyān ādhyātmikān ca eva","m. acc. pl.","outer and inner"],["yathā-vidyaḥ tathā-smṛtiḥ","bv. + bv.","as one's avidyā, so the memory"]],"lit":"\"First (the Self) imagines the jīva, then objects of various kinds — outer and inner. As (one's) ignorance is, so (one's) memory is.\"","para":"The order of cosmic imagination: first the jīva (the individual self), then the world of objects. The seriality is doctrinally significant: there is no world without a jīva to perceive it; the jīva is logically prior.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> yathā-vidyaḥ tathā-smṛtiḥ — \"as is the ignorance, so the memory\" — is the verse's psychologically deep observation. The structure of memory is determined by the structure of ignorance; different ignorances produce different memories."],["Sivananda","Practical implication: changing one's memories is therefore not the path; changing one's ignorance is. The ignorance is upstream of the memory."],["Krishnananda","The doctrine of jīva-prior-to-world undermines philosophical realism: there is no objective world that the jīva then enters; the jīva and the world arise together, with the jīva first."],["Nikhilananda","this verse is the basis of Advaita's individualist epistemology. The cosmic imagining is jīva-centered; there is no impersonal-objective ground prior to jīva."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.17","title":"As a rope, not ascertained, in darkness, is imagined as a snake, water-stream, etc.; so the Self is imagined","sa":["अनिश्चिता यथा रज्जुरन्धकारे विकल्पिता ।","सर्पधारादिभिर्भावैस्तद्वदात्मा विकल्पितः ॥ १७॥"],"iast":["aniścitā yathā rajjur andhakāre vikalpitā |","sarpa-dhārādibhir bhāvais tadvad ātmā vikalpitaḥ || 17 ||"],"words":[["a-niścitā yathā rajjuḥ","f. nom.","as an unrecognised rope"],["andhakāre vikalpitā","m. loc. + f. nom.","in darkness is imagined"],["sarpa-dhārā-ādibhiḥ bhāvaiḥ","m. instr. pl.","as snake, water-stream, etc."],["tadvat ātmā vikalpitaḥ","indecl. + m. nom.","so the Self is imagined"]],"lit":"\"As a rope, not (clearly) ascertained in darkness, is imagined as a snake, water-stream, etc., so the Self is imagined (in many ways).\"","para":"The classic rajju-sarpa analogy. In dim light, a rope is mistaken for a snake or a stream of water. The rope is one; the mis-perceptions are many. So with the Self: it is one, but the imagining-mind sees it as many forms.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> This is Advaita's most-cited analogy. The rope-snake illustration becomes a standard in later Vedānta: Śaṅkara, Maṇḍana Miśra, Sureśvara, Vidyāraṇya all use it."],["Sivananda","For the meditator: every form you take to be the Self (your body, your mind, your various selves) is a misperceived snake on a real rope. The rope is one and is the Self."],["Krishnananda","andhakāre — \"in darkness\" — is the precise condition. The mis-perception occurs in cognitive darkness (avidyā), not in clear light. The remedy is not action but illumination."],["Nikhilananda","this is the most-pedagogically-useful analogy in Advaita. Even those unable to grasp the abstract argument grasp the rope-snake image and can extend it."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.18","title":"As, in the ascertained rope, imagination ceases — 'it is just rope' — so the Self's certainty","sa":["निश्चितायां यथा रज्ज्वां विकल्पो विनिवर्तते ।","रज्जुरेवेति चाद्वैतं तद्वदात्मविनिश्चयः ॥ १८॥"],"iast":["niścitāyāṃ yathā rajjvāṃ vikalpo vinivartate |","rajjur eveti cādvaitaṃ tadvad ātma-viniścayaḥ || 18 ||"],"words":[["niścitāyām yathā rajjvām","f. loc.","when the rope is ascertained"],["vikalpaḥ vinivartate","m. nom. + 3 sg.","imagination ceases"],["rajjuḥ eva iti ca advaitam","f. nom. + indecl.","'(it is) only rope' — and non-dual"],["tadvat ātma-viniścayaḥ","indecl. + m. nom.","so (is) the certainty of the Self"]],"lit":"\"When the rope is (clearly) ascertained, imagination ceases — 'it is only rope' — and (the recognition is) non-dual. So is the Self's certainty.\"","para":"Once the rope is recognized for what it is, the snake-imagination automatically ceases — without further effort. The recognition is itself the cessation. Similarly: when the Self is recognized, all not-Self imaginings cease.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The pair with 2.17: 2.17 states the problem (mis-perception), 2.18 the resolution (recognition). The resolution is single-event, automatic, complete — there is no second step."],["Sivananda","Critical practical point: the seeker does not need to remove the snake-perceptions one by one; correct identification of the rope automatically removes them all. So in self-recognition: not many practices for many wrong identifications; one recognition for all."],["Krishnananda","rajjur eva — \"only rope\" — captures the cognitive shape of advaita. Not \"rope rather than snake\"; just \"rope.\" The duality dissolves; only the non-dual remains."],["Nikhilananda","this is, with 2.17, the most-cited pair in the Kārikā. The two verses together state Advaita's central pedagogy."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.19","title":"Imagined as prāṇa and other infinite forms — this is the māyā of that divine, by which He is bewildered Himself","sa":["प्राणादिभिरनन्तैश्च भावैरेतैर्विकल्पितः ।","मायैषा तस्य देवस्य ययाऽसम्मोहितः स्वयम् ॥ १९॥"],"iast":["prāṇādibhir anantaiś ca bhāvair etair vikalpitaḥ |","māyaiṣā tasya devasya yayāsammohitaḥ svayam || 19 ||"],"words":[["prāṇa-ādibhiḥ ananataiḥ ca","m. instr. pl.","by prāṇa etc., infinite (forms)"],["bhāvaiḥ etaiḥ vikalpitaḥ","m. instr. + pp.","by these things imagined"],["māyā eṣā tasya devasya","f. nom. + m. gen.","this (is) the māyā of that god"],["yayā sammohitaḥ svayam","f. instr. + indecl.","by which He is bewildered Himself"]],"lit":"\"(He is) imagined by prāṇa and other infinite (forms — these are) the māyā of that divine (Self) — by which He is Himself bewildered.\"","para":"All the various names by which the Self is conceived — prāṇa, manas, indriyas, devas, lokas, jīvas, etc. — are the Self's own māyā. The Self projects them and, by them, is Himself bewildered.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The Lord's self-bewilderment is a remarkable Advaita formulation: the same Self that projects also gets caught in its own projection. This is what saṃsāra IS."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: the recognition is precisely that you are not the one imagining and another the one bewildered; you are the Self both projecting and being-caught. The same Self ends the bewilderment by recognizing it as its own."],["Krishnananda","sammohitaḥ svayam — \"bewildered Himself\" — captures Advaita's distinctive position: there is no external agent of delusion; the Self plays both roles."],["Nikhilananda","this verse establishes the doctrine of self-bewilderment-and-self-liberation. The whole cosmic drama is the Self's own self-recognition arc."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.20","title":"Prāṇa-knowers call (Him) prāṇa; element-knowers call (Him) elements","sa":["प्राण इति प्राणविदो भूतानीति च तद्विदः ।","गुणा इति गुणविदस्तत्त्वानीति च तद्विदः ॥ २०॥"],"iast":["prāṇa iti prāṇa-vido bhūtānīti ca tad-vidaḥ |","guṇā iti guṇa-vidas tattvānīti ca tad-vidaḥ || 20 ||"],"words":[["prāṇaḥ iti prāṇa-vidaḥ","m. nom. + m. nom. pl.","'(He is) prāṇa' — say prāṇa-knowers"],["bhūtāni iti ca tad-vidaḥ","n. nom. + m. nom. pl.","'(He is) elements' — say their knowers"],["guṇāḥ iti guṇa-vidaḥ","m. nom. + m. nom. pl.","'guṇas' — guṇa-knowers"],["tattvāni iti ca tad-vidaḥ","n. nom. + m. nom. pl.","'(He is) tattvas' — their knowers"]],"lit":"\"'(He is) prāṇa' — say prāṇa-knowers; '(He is) elements' — say their knowers; '(He is) guṇas' — say guṇa-knowers; '(He is) tattvas' — say their knowers.\"","para":"Each philosophical school identifies the Self with the category most prominent in its system: prāṇa-vādins (Praśna, Kauṣītaki), bhūta-vādins (Cārvāka, Vaiśeṣika), guṇa-vādins (Sāṅkhya in part), tattva-vādins (Sāṅkhya proper, Tantra).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Begins a long parade-verse listing the names different schools give to the Self. Gauḍapāda's point: each school correctly identifies the Self but partially. The Self is all these, but reducible to none."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: do not seek the right name for the Self. Every name catches one aspect. The Self exceeds every catalogue."],["Krishnananda","prāṇa-vidaḥ etc. ('prāṇa-knowers') — Gauḍapāda is gently mocking. Each school's vidā (knower) treats their preferred category as the totality. The plurality of 'knowers' is itself the evidence of the partiality of each."],["Nikhilananda","this verse begins the survey of conceptual constructions that occupies 2.20-25. Gauḍapāda is dismantling sectarian identifications."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.21","title":"Pāda-knowers call (Him) pādas; world-knowers call (Him) worlds","sa":["पादा इति पादविदो विषया इति तद्विदः ।","लोका इति लोकविदो देवा इति च तद्विदः ॥ २१॥"],"iast":["pādā iti pāda-vido viṣayā iti tad-vidaḥ |","lokā iti loka-vido devā iti ca tad-vidaḥ || 21 ||"],"words":[["pādāḥ iti pāda-vidaḥ","m. nom. + m. nom. pl.","'pādas' — say pāda-knowers"],["viṣayāḥ iti tad-vidaḥ","m. nom. + m. nom. pl.","'(He is) objects' — their knowers"],["lokāḥ iti loka-vidaḥ","m. nom. + m. nom. pl.","'(He is) worlds' — world-knowers"],["devāḥ iti ca tad-vidaḥ","m. nom. + m. nom. pl.","'(He is) gods' — their knowers"]],"lit":"\"'Pādas' say pāda-knowers; '(He is) objects' say their knowers; '(He is) worlds' say world-knowers; '(He is) gods' say their knowers.\"","para":"Continues the catalogue: pāda-knowers (e.g., the Māṇḍūkya's four-pāda doctrine), object-knowers (Vaiśeṣika), world-knowers (cosmologists), god-knowers (devotional schools).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Each school's identifying category is listed. None is wrong; none is sufficient."],["Sivananda","For the meditator: notice your own school's preferred identification. Recognize that this is partial. Do not abandon it; situate it."],["Krishnananda","viṣayāḥ ('objects') refers to the Vaiśeṣika tradition's treatment of viṣaya (object-category) as fundamental; lokāḥ refers to those who treat cosmic regions as the fundamental categories."],["Nikhilananda","each item in this catalogue corresponds to an identifiable Vedic-or-philosophical tradition. The verse is a compressed doxography."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.22","title":"Veda-knowers call (Him) Vedas; yajña-knowers call (Him) yajñas","sa":["वेदा इति वेदविदो यज्ञा इति च तद्विदः ।","भोक्तेति च भोक्तृविदो भोज्यमिति च तद्विदः ॥ २२॥"],"iast":["vedā iti veda-vido yajñā iti ca tad-vidaḥ |","bhokteti ca bhoktṛ-vido bhojyam iti ca tad-vidaḥ || 22 ||"],"words":[["vedāḥ iti veda-vidaḥ","m. nom. + m. nom. pl.","'(He is) Vedas' — Veda-knowers"],["yajñāḥ iti ca tad-vidaḥ","m. nom. + m. nom. pl.","'yajñas' — their knowers"],["bhoktā iti ca bhoktṛ-vidaḥ","m. nom. + m. nom. pl.","'(He is) enjoyer' — enjoyer-knowers"],["bhojyam iti ca tad-vidaḥ","n. nom. + m. nom. pl.","'enjoyed' — their knowers"]],"lit":"\"'(He is) Vedas' say Veda-knowers; '(He is) yajñas' say their knowers; '(He is) the enjoyer' say enjoyer-knowers; '(He is) the enjoyed' say their knowers.\"","para":"Continues: Veda-traditionalists (Mīmāṃsā), ritualists (yajña-vāda), bhoktṛ-vādins (those who treat the Self as enjoyer), bhojya-vādins (those who treat the Self as object).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The bhoktṛ / bhojya pair is technically interesting: even the seemingly-opposite identifications (Self as enjoyer / Self as enjoyed) both have school-adherents and both are mentioned with equal evenness."],["Sivananda","The school-pluralism of Indian philosophy is on display. Every school catches something; none is complete."],["Krishnananda","The verse is also an implicit critique of the various Vedānta sub-schools that would emerge after Gauḍapāda: those that emphasize Veda-as-fundamental (Mīmāṃsā-allied) and those that emphasize devotion-as-fundamental (Bhāgavata)."],["Nikhilananda","the verse compresses what would have been to Gauḍapāda's audience an extensive sectarian disputation."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.23","title":"Subtle-knowers call (Him) subtle; gross-knowers, gross; formed-knowers, formed; formless-knowers, formless","sa":["सूक्ष्म इति सूक्ष्मविदः स्थूल इति च तद्विदः ।","मूर्त इति मूर्तविदोऽमूर्त इति च तद्विदः ॥ २३॥"],"iast":["sūkṣma iti sūkṣma-vidaḥ sthūla iti ca tad-vidaḥ |","mūrta iti mūrta-vido'mūrta iti ca tad-vidaḥ || 23 ||"],"words":[["sūkṣmaḥ iti sūkṣma-vidaḥ","m. nom. + m. nom. pl.","'(He is) subtle' — subtle-knowers"],["sthūlaḥ iti ca tad-vidaḥ","m. nom. + m. nom. pl.","'gross' — their knowers"],["mūrtaḥ iti mūrta-vidaḥ","m. nom. + m. nom. pl.","'formed' — formed-knowers"],["a-mūrtaḥ iti ca tad-vidaḥ","m. nom. + m. nom. pl.","'formless' — their knowers"]],"lit":"\"'(He is) subtle' say subtle-knowers; '(He is) gross' say their knowers; '(He is) formed' say form-knowers; '(He is) formless' say their knowers.\"","para":"Another pair-set: subtle/gross, formed/formless. All four are sectarian identifications.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verse covers the metaphysical-grade options: those who place the Self at the subtle pole, at the gross pole, in formed manifestation, in formless transcendence."],["Sivananda","The pluralism of identifications is its own argument against any single one being correct."],["Krishnananda","mūrta / a-mūrta is a foundational pair in later Indian metaphysics. Even this fundamental division does not catch the Self exhaustively."],["Nikhilananda","continues the doxographic catalogue."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.24","title":"Time-knowers call (Him) time; direction-knowers call (Him) directions","sa":["काल इति कालविदो दिश इति च तद्विदः ।","वादा इति वादविदो भुवनानीति तद्विदः ॥ २४॥"],"iast":["kāla iti kāla-vido diśa iti ca tad-vidaḥ |","vādā iti vāda-vido bhuvanānīti tad-vidaḥ || 24 ||"],"words":[["kālaḥ iti kāla-vidaḥ","m. nom. + m. nom. pl.","'(He is) time' — time-knowers"],["diśaḥ iti ca tad-vidaḥ","f. nom. + m. nom. pl.","'directions' — their knowers"],["vādāḥ iti vāda-vidaḥ","m. nom. + m. nom. pl.","'(He is) doctrines' — doctrine-knowers"],["bhuvanāni iti tad-vidaḥ","n. nom. + m. nom. pl.","'(He is) worlds' — their knowers"]],"lit":"\"'(He is) time' say time-knowers; '(He is) directions' say their knowers; '(He is) doctrines' say doctrine-knowers; '(He is) worlds' say their knowers.\"","para":"Continues: time (Atharvan kāla-vāda), space (digvāda), doctrines (those who treat doctrinal positions as the Self's manifestations), worlds (cosmographers).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The catalogue is exhaustive in spirit. Gauḍapāda is showing that every category has been used to identify the Self."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: any preferred category of yours is on this list. Be alert to your own partiality."],["Krishnananda","vāda-vidaḥ ('doctrine-knowers') is interesting — those who treat the philosophical doctrines themselves as the manifestation of the Self. A self-reflexive identification that the Kārikā nevertheless includes in its critique."],["Nikhilananda","the verse extends the catalogue to include even cosmographic schemes."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.25","title":"Mind-knowers call (Him) mind; intellect-knowers call (Him) intellect","sa":["मन इति मनोविदो बुद्धिरिति च तद्विदः ।","चित्तमिति चित्तविदो धर्माधर्मौ च तद्विदः ॥ २५॥"],"iast":["mana iti mano-vido buddhir iti ca tad-vidaḥ |","cittam iti citta-vido dharmādharmau ca tad-vidaḥ || 25 ||"],"words":[["manaḥ iti mano-vidaḥ","n. nom. + m. nom. pl.","'(He is) mind' — mind-knowers"],["buddhiḥ iti ca tad-vidaḥ","f. nom. + m. nom. pl.","'(He is) intellect' — their knowers"],["cittam iti citta-vidaḥ","n. nom. + m. nom. pl.","'(He is) citta' — citta-knowers"],["dharma-adharmau ca tad-vidaḥ","m. nom. du. + m. nom. pl.","'(He is) dharma-and-adharma' — their knowers"]],"lit":"\"'(He is) mind' say mind-knowers; '(He is) intellect' say their knowers; '(He is) citta' say citta-knowers; '(He is) dharma-and-adharma' say their knowers.\"","para":"The psychological/ethical end of the catalogue: mind, intellect, citta (consciousness-stream), and dharma-adharma (moral categories). Each is a school's preferred identification.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The yoga-tradition emphasizes citta (the Yoga-Sūtra opens \"yogaḥ citta-vṛtti-nirodhaḥ\"); the dharma-adharma identification is the dharma-śāstra position."],["Sivananda","For the meditator: even the psychological categories that seem nearest to introspection (mind, intellect, consciousness) are partial identifications."],["Krishnananda","The verse moves from cosmic categories (time, space, world) to psychological ones — a natural progression in the catalogue."],["Nikhilananda","this verse compresses the yoga and dharma-śāstra positions."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.26","title":"Some call (Him) twenty-five (-tattva); others twenty-six; others thirty-one; others, endless","sa":["पञ्चविꣳशक इत्येके षड्विꣳशक चापरे ।","एकत्रिꣳशक इत्याहुरनन्त इति चापरे ॥ २६॥"],"iast":["pañcaviṃśaka ity eke ṣaḍviṃśaka cāpare |","ekatriṃśaka ity āhur ananta iti cāpare || 26 ||"],"words":[["pañca-viṃśakaḥ iti eke","m. nom. + indecl. + m. nom. pl.","'twenty-five-fold' say some"],["ṣaḍ-viṃśakaḥ ca apare","m. nom. + indecl.","'twenty-six-fold' say others"],["eka-triṃśakaḥ iti āhuḥ","m. nom. + 3 pl.","'thirty-one-fold' say (some)"],["anantaḥ iti ca apare","m. nom. + indecl.","'endless' say (yet) others"]],"lit":"\"'(He is) twenty-five-fold' some say; '(He is) twenty-six-fold' others; '(He is) thirty-one-fold' (some) say; '(He is) endless' say yet others.\"","para":"Counts: Sāṅkhya's 25 tattvas; Pāśupata-Śaiva's 26 (adding Īśvara); other schemes go to 31 or beyond; some schools treat the count as endless.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Numerical-philosophical identifications. Each school's count of fundamental categories is here listed."],["Sivananda","The numerical specificity makes this verse historically interesting: we can identify the schools by their counts."],["Krishnananda","The Sāṅkhya 25-fold count (5 mahā-bhūtas + 5 tan-mātras + 5 jñāna-indriyas + 5 karma-indriyas + manas + buddhi + ahaṅkāra + prakṛti + puruṣa) is the standard reference."],["Nikhilananda","the verse compresses the Sāṅkhya-Yoga-Tantra family of tattva-enumeration schemes."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.27","title":"World-knowers call (Him) worlds; āśrama-knowers, āśramas; (some say) male/female/neuter, others higher/lower","sa":["लोकाꣳल्लोकविदः प्राहुराश्रमा इति तद्विदः ।","स्त्रीपुन्नपुꣳसकं लैङ्गाः परापरमथापरे ॥ २७॥"],"iast":["lokāṃl loka-vidaḥ prāhur āśramā iti tad-vidaḥ |","strī-puṃ-napuṃsakaṃ laiṅgāḥ parāparam athāpare || 27 ||"],"words":[["lokān loka-vidaḥ prāhuḥ","m. acc. pl. + m. nom. pl.","world-knowers declare worlds"],["āśramāḥ iti tad-vidaḥ","m. nom. + m. nom. pl.","'āśramas' say their knowers"],["strī-pum-napuṃsakam","n. acc. sg.","female-male-neuter"],["laiṅgāḥ","m. nom. pl.","the gender-knowers (grammarians?)"],["para-aparam atha apare","n. nom. + m. nom. pl.","(some say) higher-lower (and) yet others"]],"lit":"\"World-knowers declare (Him to be) worlds; āśrama-knowers, āśramas; gender-knowers (say He is) female-male-neuter; yet others (say) higher-lower.\"","para":"Continues: lokas (the seven cosmic worlds), the four āśramas (life-stages), gender-categories (linguistic identification of Self with grammatical gender), the para/apara (higher/lower) duality.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The list extends to grammatical (lainga) and life-stage (āśrama) identifications — showing the catalogue's near-exhaustiveness."],["Sivananda","laiṅgāḥ — \"the gender(-knowers)\" — refers to grammarians who treat male, female, neuter as fundamental categories of being. The reference is to certain Vyākaraṇa schools."],["Krishnananda","The variety is the point. Every conceivable categorical scheme has been attempted; none catches the Self exhaustively."],["Nikhilananda","this verse is the most exotic in the catalogue, including even grammatical-gender identifications."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.28","title":"Creation-knowers call (Him) creation; dissolution-knowers, dissolution; preservation-knowers, preservation — all (these) everywhere always","sa":["सृष्टिरिति सृष्टिविदो लय इति च तद्विदः ।","स्थितिरिति स्थितिविदः सर्वे चेह तु सर्वदा ॥ २८॥"],"iast":["sṛṣṭir iti sṛṣṭi-vido laya iti ca tad-vidaḥ |","sthitir iti sthiti-vidaḥ sarve ceha tu sarvadā || 28 ||"],"words":[["sṛṣṭiḥ iti sṛṣṭi-vidaḥ","f. nom. + m. nom. pl.","'creation' say creation-knowers"],["layaḥ iti ca tad-vidaḥ","m. nom. + m. nom. pl.","'dissolution' say their knowers"],["sthitiḥ iti sthiti-vidaḥ","f. nom. + m. nom. pl.","'preservation' say preservation-knowers"],["sarve ca iha tu sarvadā","m. nom. pl.","all (these), here, always"]],"lit":"\"'(He is) creation' say creation-knowers; '(He is) dissolution' say their knowers; '(He is) preservation' say preservation-knowers — all (these) here always.\"","para":"Closes the catalogue: cosmic-process identifications (creation, preservation, dissolution — the trimūrti scheme's basis). The closing line sarve ceha tu sarvadā — \"all (these), here, always\" — is general: every such identification is found everywhere at all times.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Catalogue's close. The trimūrti's three powers (Brahmā-creation, Viṣṇu-preservation, Śiva-dissolution) are here treated as three more sectarian identifications."],["Sivananda","sarve ceha tu sarvadā — \"all of them, here, always\" — is gently dismissive: every such categorization has been attempted by some school somewhere at some time. The plurality is the implicit argument."],["Krishnananda","For the meditator: notice your own preferred process-identification (cosmic creation as image of God's activity, etc.). Recognize its partiality."],["Nikhilananda","this closes the long doxographic catalogue (2.20-2.28). The next verses will draw the conclusion from this catalogue."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.29","title":"Whatever conception He shows to whom — that he then sees, that he protects; he, having become that, partakes that","sa":["यं भावं दर्शयेद्यस्य तं भावं स तु पश्यति ।","तं चावति स भूत्वाऽसौ तद्ग्रहः समुपैति तम् ॥ २९॥"],"iast":["yaṃ bhāvaṃ darśayed yasya taṃ bhāvaṃ sa tu paśyati |","taṃ cāvati sa bhūtvāsau tad-grahaḥ samupaiti tam || 29 ||"],"words":[["yam bhāvam darśayet yasya","m. acc. + 3 sg. opt. + gen.","whatever conception (He) shows to whom"],["tam bhāvam saḥ tu paśyati","m. acc. + m. nom.","that conception he sees"],["tam ca avati saḥ bhūtvā asau","m. acc. + 3 sg. + ger.","and he, having become that, protects (it)"],["tat-grahaḥ samupaiti tam","m. nom. + 3 sg.","the grasping of that comes upon him"]],"lit":"\"Whatever conception (the Lord) shows to whom — that conception he sees; and, having become that, he protects (it). The grasping of that comes upon him.\"","para":"Each seeker is shown a particular conception of the Self by the Lord; the seeker then sees that conception, identifies with it (becomes that), protects it (defends it against alternatives), and is grasped by it (becomes its prisoner).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verse's psychological precision: the sectarian identifications of 2.20-28 are not arbitrary errors but Lord-sent particular conceptions, each suited to a particular seeker. The seeker's tragedy is to mistake the particular conception for the totality and to defend it."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: notice that your defence of your preferred conception is the evidence that you have been grasped by it. The seeker who is grasped does not see he is grasped."],["Krishnananda","avati ('protects') is illuminating — it is the same root as the verb in the Iśā-Up. and elsewhere meaning \"to defend.\" Each adherent defends his own conception; the defending IS the grasping."],["Nikhilananda","this verse explains why sectarian disputes are so intense. Each adherent has been grasped by a particular Lord-given conception and defends it as defending the Self itself."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.30","title":"Though not different through these, the (Self) is observed as if different; whoever knows this in truth imagines without doubt","sa":["एतैरेषोऽपृथग्भावैः पृथगेवेति लक्षितः ।","एवं यो वेद तत्त्वेन कल्पयेत्सोऽविशङ्कितः ॥ ३०॥"],"iast":["etair eṣo'pṛthag-bhāvaiḥ pṛthag eveti lakṣitaḥ |","evaṃ yo veda tattvena kalpayet so'viśaṅkitaḥ || 30 ||"],"words":[["etaiḥ eṣaḥ a-pṛthak-bhāvaiḥ","m. instr. pl.","by these non-different things"],["pṛthak eva iti lakṣitaḥ","indecl. + pp.","as different (He) is noticed"],["evam yaḥ veda tattvena","indecl. + m. nom. + 3 sg. + n. instr.","he who knows this in truth"],["kalpayet saḥ a-viśaṅkitaḥ","3 sg. opt. + m. nom.","may imagine, unhesitating"]],"lit":"\"By these (variously imagined) non-different things (the Self) is noticed as if different. He who knows this in truth may imagine (anything), unhesitating.\"","para":"Though the various sectarian categories are non-different from the Self, they are perceived as if different. The knower of this truth can use any conception freely — without committing to its ultimacy.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> This is the Kārikā's pedagogical resolution: the knower of the Self can deploy any sectarian conception freely. There is no need to demolish them all; just recognize their non-difference from the Self and use them as the situation requires."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: do not be paralyzed by the multiplicity of conceptions. Pick one that suits your situation; use it; let go of it. Do not absolutize any."],["Krishnananda","a-viśaṅkitaḥ ('unhesitating') is the practical fruit. The knower acts without hesitation because he is no longer trying to find the One Right Conception; every conception is equally non-different from the Self."],["Nikhilananda","this verse closes the doxographic argument with a practical pedagogical principle: use whatever conception works in context, without absolutizing."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.31","title":"As dream and māyā are seen, and as the gandharva-city is seen — so this universe is seen, by the Vedānta-discerning","sa":["स्वप्नमाये यथा दृष्टे गन्धर्वनगरं यथा ।","तथा विश्वमिदं दृष्टं वेदान्तेषु विचक्षणैः ॥ ३१॥"],"iast":["svapna-māye yathā dṛṣṭe gandharva-nagaraṃ yathā |","tathā viśvam idaṃ dṛṣṭaṃ vedānteṣu vicakṣaṇaiḥ || 31 ||"],"words":[["svapna-māye yathā dṛṣṭe","n. nom. du. + indecl. + pp.","as dream and māyā are seen"],["gandharva-nagaram yathā","n. nom. + indecl.","as a gandharva-city"],["tathā viśvam idam dṛṣṭam","indecl. + n. nom. + pp.","so is this universe seen"],["vedānteṣu vicakṣaṇaiḥ","n. loc. pl. + m. instr. pl.","in Vedānta by the discerning"]],"lit":"\"As dream and māyā are seen (to be unreal), as the gandharva-city (is seen) — so this universe is seen, by the discerning, in Vedānta.\"","para":"Three classical unreality-images: dream, māyā (magic-show), gandharva-nagara (the city seen mirage-like in clouds). All three are paradigms of seemingly-real-but-actually-unreal. The universe has the same status.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The triple-simile is canonical Advaita. Compare Śaṅkara's bhāṣya on BS 1.1.4 which uses these three."],["Sivananda","For the meditator: train the eye that sees the dream's unreality and the māyā-show's unreality and the gandharva-city's unreality to also see the universe's unreality. The same eye, applied progressively, sees through."],["Krishnananda","vicakṣaṇaiḥ — 'by the discerning' — is the qualifier. Not by everyone; only by the discerning. Discernment is the prerequisite of advaita-vision."],["Nikhilananda","this verse is one of the most-cited summary statements of māyā-vāda. The triple-simile encapsulates the entire doctrine."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.32","title":"No cessation, no origination, no bondage, no seeker, no liberation-seeker, no liberated — this is the ultimate truth","sa":["न निरोधो न चोत्पत्तिर्न बद्धो न च साधकः ।","न मुमुक्षुर्न वै मुक्त इत्येषा परमार्थता ॥ ३२॥"],"iast":["na nirodho na cotpattir na baddho na ca sādhakaḥ |","na mumukṣur na vai mukta ity eṣā paramārthatā || 32 ||"],"words":[["na nirodhaḥ na ca utpattiḥ","neg. + m. nom. + neg. + f. nom.","no cessation, no origination"],["na baddhaḥ na ca sādhakaḥ","neg. + m. nom.","no bound (one), no seeker"],["na mumukṣuḥ na vai muktaḥ","neg. + m. nom.","no aspirant for liberation, no liberated"],["iti eṣā paramārthatā","indecl. + f. nom.","this (is) the ultimate truth"]],"lit":"\"No cessation, no origination, no bondage, no seeker, no aspirant for liberation, no liberated — this is the ultimate truth.\"","para":"The most famous verse of the Vaitathya-prakaraṇa. The final position: at the level of paramārtha, none of the dualistic categories — cessation/origination, bound/free, seeker/non-seeker — apply. All are upādhi.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> This verse is the locus classicus for the doctrine that all dualistic spiritual categories — including liberation-talk — are upādhi. Compare Mūlamadhyamaka-Kārikā 25.3 (verbally close). The Buddhist parallel is the verse most often cited in the Gauḍapāda-as-Buddhist debate."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: even the categories of your own spiritual quest (seeker, liberation, aspiration) are upādhi. Recognize this. The full recognition is itself the goal — beyond which there is no further goal."],["Krishnananda","paramārthatā — 'ultimate-truth-ness' — is one of the Kārikā's strongest terms. The verse claims that the listed denials describe the ultimate truth itself."],["Nikhilananda","Śaṅkara takes great care over this verse, defending it from Buddhist-association and reading it as orthodox Vedānta. The verse's wording is identical with Madhyamaka formulations; only the embedding doctrinal context differs."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.33","title":"He is imagined by unreal things and by non-dual (things); things too (are imagined) only by non-dual — therefore non-duality is auspicious","sa":["भावैरसद्भिरेवायमद्वयेन च कल्पितः ।","भावा अप्यद्वयेनैव तस्मादद्वयता शिवा ॥ ३३॥"],"iast":["bhāvair asadbhir evāyam advayena ca kalpitaḥ |","bhāvā apy advayenaiva tasmād advayatā śivā || 33 ||"],"words":[["bhāvaiḥ a-sadbhiḥ eva ayam","m. instr. pl. + m. nom.","(He is) by unreal things"],["advayena ca kalpitaḥ","n. instr. + pp.","and by the non-dual, imagined"],["bhāvāḥ api advayena eva","m. nom. pl. + n. instr.","the things too only by the non-dual"],["tasmāt advayatā śivā","indecl. + f. nom.","therefore non-duality is auspicious"]],"lit":"\"He is imagined by unreal things and by the non-dual; the things too (are imagined) only by the non-dual. Therefore non-duality is auspicious.\"","para":"The Self is imagined as if many; the supposedly-many is imagined by the non-dual; therefore non-duality is the auspicious final position.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> advayatā — 'non-duality' — is here said to be śivā ('auspicious'). The choice of word is significant: non-duality is not just true but auspicious; it produces well-being."],["Sivananda","For the meditator: the recognition of non-duality is itself auspicious. It produces śānti automatically."],["Krishnananda","The verse's argument has a circular feel: things are imagined by the non-dual; the non-dual is then identified through the things. Gauḍapāda's response: the circularity is itself the proof that there is no second alongside the non-dual."],["Nikhilananda","this verse closes the explicit māyā-vāda exposition and identifies the doctrine of advaita with auspiciousness, anticipating its practical fruit."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.34","title":"Not different by self-nature, not (different) by its own (qualities); neither separate nor non-separate — so the truth-knowers know","sa":["नाऽऽत्मभावेन नानेदं न स्वेनापि कथञ्चन ।","न पृथङ्नापृथक्किञ्चिदिति तत्त्वविदो विदुः ॥ ३४॥"],"iast":["nātma-bhāvena nānedaṃ na svenāpi kathañcana |","na pṛthaṅ nāpṛthak kiñcid iti tattva-vido viduḥ || 34 ||"],"words":[["na ātma-bhāvena","indecl.","not by self-being"],["nāna idam","n. nom. sg.","this (is) various"],["na svena api kathañcana","indecl.","nor by its own at all"],["na pṛthak na a-pṛthak kiñcit","indecl.","neither separate nor non-separate (is) anything"],["iti tattva-vidaḥ viduḥ","indecl. + m. nom. pl.","so the truth-knowers know"]],"lit":"\"This (universe) is not different by its self-being, nor by its own (qualities) at all. Neither separate nor non-separate is anything — so the truth-knowers know.\"","para":"The universe is not many by its own ontology; nor is anything either separate or non-separate from the Self. The truth-knowers recognize this.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verse refuses both extremes: not many (against pluralism), not non-different in a way that would leave any second (against bhedābheda). The position is strictly advaita."],["Sivananda","The double negation 'neither separate nor non-separate' is technically precise: there is not even a second to be either separate or non-separate from. The Self is the only."],["Krishnananda","For the meditator: do not even ask 'how am I related to the Self?' The question presupposes two; the truth has only one."],["Nikhilananda","this verse is one of the clearest statements of strict (kevala) advaita in the Kārikā."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.35","title":"Without desire-fear-anger, by the munis who have crossed the Vedas, it (the Self) is seen — imagination-free, the peace-of-phenomena, non-dual","sa":["वीतरागभयक्रोधैर्मुनिभिर्वेदपारगैः ।","निर्विकल्पो ह्ययं दृष्टः प्रपञ्चोपशमोऽद्वयः ॥ ३५॥"],"iast":["vīta-rāga-bhaya-krodhair munibhir veda-pāragaiḥ |","nir-vikalpo hy ayaṃ dṛṣṭaḥ prapañcopaśamo'dvayaḥ || 35 ||"],"words":[["vīta-rāga-bhaya-krodhaiḥ","m. instr. pl. bv.","by (those) free of passion-fear-anger"],["munibhiḥ veda-pāragaiḥ","m. instr. pl.","by munis who have crossed the Vedas"],["niḥ-vikalpaḥ hi ayam dṛṣṭaḥ","m. nom. sg. + pp.","imagination-free, He is seen"],["prapañca-upaśamaḥ a-dvayaḥ","m. nom. sg.","phenomena-peace, non-dual"]],"lit":"\"By the munis who have crossed the Vedas, free of desire-fear-anger, this (Self) is seen — imagination-free, the peace-of-phenomena, non-dual.\"","para":"The Self is seen by qualified seers: those free of the three afflictions (rāga, bhaya, krodha) and who have crossed the Vedas (mastered and transcended scripture). They see the Self as imagination-free, phenomena-quiescent, non-dual.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The Upaniṣadic phrase prapañcopaśama (from mantra 7) returns in verse-form. The fully-qualified seers see exactly what mantra 7 describes."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: notice the qualifications. Crossing the Vedas means having mastered them and moved beyond — not having skipped them. Freedom from rāga-bhaya-krodha is the moral prerequisite."],["Krishnananda","veda-pāragaiḥ — 'cross-ers of the Vedas' — is a precise phrase. Veda-pāra means the further-shore-of-Veda. The fully-qualified is one who has reached the further shore of scripture."],["Nikhilananda","this verse provides the qualifying conditions for the realisation just described in 2.32-34."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.36","title":"Therefore, knowing thus, fix memory in non-duality; having reached non-duality, behave in the world like a jaḍa (insensate one)","sa":["तस्मादेवं विदित्वैनमद्वैते योजयेत्स्मृतिम् ।","अद्वैतं समनुप्राप्य जडवल्लोकमाचरेत् ॥ ३६॥"],"iast":["tasmād evaṃ viditvainam advaite yojayet smṛtim |","advaitaṃ samanuprāpya jaḍa-val lokam ācaret || 36 ||"],"words":[["tasmāt evam viditvā enam","indecl. + ger. + m. acc.","therefore, having known Him thus"],["advaite yojayet smṛtim","n. loc. + 3 sg. opt. + f. acc.","let one fix memory in non-duality"],["advaitam samanuprāpya","n. acc. + ger.","having reached non-duality"],["jaḍa-vat lokam ācaret","indecl. + m. acc. + 3 sg. opt.","let one behave in the world like an insensate one"]],"lit":"\"Therefore, having known Him thus, let one fix memory in non-duality. Having reached non-duality, let one behave in the world like an insensate one (jaḍa-vat).\"","para":"The verse's most famous teaching: jaḍa-vat lokam ācaret — \"let him behave in the world like an insensate one.\" The realised one does not display his realisation; he conducts himself with the apparent indifference of a jaḍa (a clod, an insensate thing).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> This is one of the Kārikā's most-cited practical instructions. The jaḍa-vat ācaraṇa doctrine becomes a hallmark of later Advaita: the realised one is outwardly indistinguishable from an ordinary indifferent person."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: notice the inversion. The seeker before realisation is ostentatious about his practices; the realised one is silent about his realisation. The mark of realisation is invisibility."],["Krishnananda","jaḍa-vat (\"like an insensate thing\") is striking. The realised is not literally insensate but BEHAVES (ācaret) as if so — not playing the saint, not displaying virtue, not advertising attainment."],["Nikhilananda","this verse is the textual basis for the post-realisation conduct-norm in Advaita: silence, ordinariness, social invisibility. The avadhūta tradition cites this verse extensively."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.37","title":"Without praise, salutation, oblation-cry; with shifting-or-fixed abode; a yati of spontaneous (action)","sa":["निस्तुतिर्निर्नमस्कारो निःस्वधाकार एव च ।","चलाचलनिकेतश्च यतिर्याद्दृच्छिको भवेत् ॥ ३७॥"],"iast":["nistutir nirnamaskāro niḥsvadhā-kāra eva ca |","calācala-niketaś ca yatir yādṛcchiko bhavet || 37 ||"],"words":[["niḥ-stutiḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","without (offering) praise"],["niḥ-namaskāraḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","without salutation"],["niḥ-svadhā-kāraḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","without (the funerary) svadhā-call"],["eva ca","indecl.","indeed"],["cala-acala-niketaḥ","m. nom. sg. bv.","with both shifting and fixed abode"],["yatiḥ yādṛcchikaḥ bhavet","m. nom. + 3 sg. opt.","let the yati be of spontaneous (action)"]],"lit":"\"Without (offering) praise, without salutation, without the svadhā-call (= the funerary oblation-cry); with both shifting and fixed abode — let the yati be of spontaneous (= unplanned) action.\"","para":"The realised yati's behavioural marks: (a) no formal stutis (hymns of praise) — he does not engage in praise-rituals; (b) no namaskāra (formal salutations to deities); (c) no svadhā-kāra (the formal oblation-cry of pitṛ-rituals); (d) no fixed abode (cala-acala-niketa — he stays anywhere); (e) yādṛcchika (spontaneous) — his actions are unplanned, arising from immediate circumstance.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Five negative marks of the realised yati. The negations are striking: the realised does not perform the formal devotional and ritual acts that ordinary religious life requires. He has gone beyond."],["Sivananda","For seekers: notice that these negations are NOT for the seeker. They describe the realised state. Seekers should perform the rituals; the realised has transcended them."],["Krishnananda","yādṛcchika — 'spontaneous' — is the positive characterization. The realised acts not by plan, not by intention, but by yādṛcchya (chance, as the moment requires). This is not lazy spontaneity but the spontaneity of one whose ego has dropped."],["Nikhilananda","this verse is one of the founding texts of the avadhūta tradition. Many later avadhūtic and Nātha texts cite this verse as their behavioral charter."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 2.38","title":"Having seen the inner truth and seen the truth externally, become-as-the-truth, his delight is in the truth, undeviating from the truth","sa":["तत्त्वमाध्यात्मिकं दृष्ट्वा तत्त्वं दृष्ट्वा तु बाह्यतः ।","तत्त्वीभूतस्तदारामस्तत्त्वादप्रच्युतो भवेत् ॥ ३८॥"],"iast":["tattvam ādhyātmikaṃ dṛṣṭvā tattvaṃ dṛṣṭvā tu bāhyataḥ |","tattvī-bhūtas tad-ārāmas tattvād a-pracyuto bhavet || 38 ||"],"words":[["tattvam ādhyātmikam dṛṣṭvā","n. acc. + ger.","having seen the inner truth"],["tattvam dṛṣṭvā tu bāhyataḥ","n. acc. + ger.","and seen the outer truth"],["tattvī-bhūtaḥ tad-ārāmaḥ","m. nom. + m. nom.","become-truth, his delight in That"],["tattvāt a-pracyutaḥ bhavet","n. abl. + m. nom. + 3 sg. opt.","may he be undeviating from the truth"]],"lit":"\"Having seen the truth inwardly, and seen the truth outwardly — become-truth, his delight in That, let him be undeviating from the truth.\"","para":"Both inner and outer truth must be seen. Having seen both, the seer becomes truth itself (tattvī-bhūta), takes delight in truth (tad-ārāma), and remains undeviating (a-pracyuta) from it. The Vaitathya-prakaraṇa ends with this positive characterization.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The Vaitathya-prakaraṇa's closing verse. After 37 verses of arguing the unreality of duality, the final verse is positive: the truth is to be seen both inwardly and outwardly; the realised one becomes truth and delights in truth."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: do not see truth only inwardly (which would be a one-sided contemplative life) or only outwardly (which would be naive realism). See both; become-truth."],["Krishnananda","tattvī-bhūta — 'become-truth' — is one of the Kārikā's strongest formulations. The realised is not one who sees truth; he IS truth, has become its substance."],["Nikhilananda","this closes the Vaitathya-prakaraṇa with the integrating recognition that truth is one across inner and outer poles, and the realised one stands in this unified truth."]],"notes":["End of Vaitathya-prakaraṇa (Kārikā 2.1-38)."]},{"ref":"Advaita-Prakaraṇa (Kārikā 3, 48 verses)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Advaita-prakaraṇa (\"the chapter on non-duality\") moves from refutation to positive exposition. Three sections: (1) verses 1–9 — the pot-space analogy (ghaṭākāśa), the central illustration of jīva-Brahman relation; (2) verses 10–22 — the doctrine of ajāti (non-origination); (3) verses 23–48 — asparśa-yoga, the \"touchless yoga,\" Gauḍapāda's signature meditation method, and its consummation."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.1","title":"The upāsaka, devoted to the (apparent) duality, is born within Brahman; before that birth all is unborn; therefore he is called a kṛpaṇa (small-souled)","sa":["OM ॥ उपासानाश्रितो धर्मो जाते ब्रह्मणि वर्तते ।","प्रागुत्पत्तेरजं सर्वं तेनासौ कृपणः स्मृतः ॥ १॥"],"iast":["oṃ || upāsanāśrito dharmo jāte brahmaṇi vartate |","prāg utpatter ajaṃ sarvaṃ tenāsau kṛpaṇaḥ smṛtaḥ || 1 ||"],"words":[["upāsanā-āśritaḥ dharmaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the dharma sheltering-in-meditation"],["jāte brahmaṇi vartate","n. loc. + 3 sg.","abides in (the apparent) born-Brahman"],["prāk utpatteḥ ajam sarvam","indecl. + f. abl. + n. nom.","before origination, all (is) unborn"],["tena asau kṛpaṇaḥ smṛtaḥ","indecl. + m. nom. + pp.","therefore he is called miserable"]],"lit":"\"The one who depends on meditation-practice abides in (an apparently) born Brahman; before origination, all is unborn — therefore he is called miserable (kṛpaṇa).\"","para":"The upāsaka (the meditative devotee) treats Brahman as having been born (i.e., as the source of creation, the saguṇa Lord). But before any origination, all is unborn. The upāsaka, by limiting Brahman to the kāraṇa-level, is therefore kṛpaṇa — small-souled, missing the higher.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Gauḍapāda's strong critique of upāsanā-marga. The devotee who treats Brahman as creator-Lord limits Brahman to apara-brahman. The seeker who recognizes ajāti (non-origination) supersedes this."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: even devotional meditation is provisional. Useful at one stage; transcended at the next. Do not absolutize the meditative position."],["Krishnananda","kṛpaṇa is a strong word — 'miserable, pitiful, small-souled' — used in Vedic ritual texts for those whose practice has been incomplete. Gauḍapāda's use here is deliberate; the upāsaka is incomplete."],["Nikhilananda","this verse opens the Advaita-prakaraṇa with a polemical move. The upāsaka who has not yet recognized ajāti is incomplete, however devoted."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.2","title":"Therefore I shall declare the non-misery, which has reached non-origination and equality; (the doctrine) by which nothing is born from anywhere","sa":["अतो वक्ष्याम्यकार्पण्यमजाति समतां गतम् ।","यथा न जायते किञ्चिज्जायमानं समन्ततः ॥ २॥"],"iast":["ato vakṣyāmy akārpaṇyam ajāti samatāṃ gatam |","yathā na jāyate kiñcij jāyamānaṃ samantataḥ || 2 ||"],"words":[["ataḥ vakṣyāmi a-kārpaṇyam","indecl. + 1 sg. fut.","therefore I shall declare the non-misery"],["a-jāti samatām gatam","f. acc. + pp.","(which is) non-origination, reached-equality"],["yathā na jāyate kiñcit","indecl. + indef.","as nothing is born"],["jāyamānam samantataḥ","n. nom. + indecl.","(though seeming) being born, everywhere"]],"lit":"\"Therefore I shall declare the non-miserable (doctrine), reached-equality, non-originated — (showing) how nothing whatever is born, anywhere, of (anything) seeming-to-be-born.\"","para":"Gauḍapāda announces his subject: a-kārpaṇya — the non-miserable doctrine. It is ajāti (non-originated), samatā-gata (reached-equality). The doctrine shows that nothing is actually born, despite the appearance of birth.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Opens the explicit exposition of ajāti-vāda. The signal-word ajāti is the keyword of the entire Advaita-prakaraṇa."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: the ajāti doctrine is the heart of Advaita. Origination is the most-basic conceptual category; if origination is denied, every dependent category collapses. Master this verse and the rest follows."],["Krishnananda","samatām gatam — 'reached equality' — qualifies ajāti. Once the doctrine of non-origination is grasped, distinctions among allegedly-originated things equalize: all are equally unborn."],["Nikhilananda","this verse introduces the doctrinal program of the prakaraṇa. The next forty-six verses unfold it."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.3","title":"The Self, like ākāśa, is risen as jīvas — like pot-spaces (within pots); the (apparent) births in aggregates — that is the example","sa":["आत्मा ह्याकाशवज्जीवैर्घटाकाशैरिवोदितः ।","घटादिवच्च सङ्घातैर्जातावेतन्निदर्शनम् ॥ ३॥"],"iast":["ātmā hy ākāśa-vaj jīvair ghaṭākāśair ivoditaḥ |","ghaṭādi-vac ca saṅghātair jātāv etan nidarśanam || 3 ||"],"words":[["ātmā hi ākāśa-vat","m. nom. + indecl.","the Self, like ākāśa"],["jīvaiḥ ghaṭa-ākāśaiḥ iva uditaḥ","m. instr. pl. + m. instr. pl.","(is) risen by jīvas as by pot-spaces"],["ghaṭa-ādi-vat ca saṅghātaiḥ","indecl. + m. instr. pl.","and like pots etc. by (their) aggregates"],["jātau etat nidarśanam","f. loc. + n. nom.","in birth this (is) the example"]],"lit":"\"The Self, like ākāśa, has risen as jīvas — as (one ākāśa rises as many) pot-spaces; like pots and other (aggregates) — this is the example in the (matter of apparent) birth.\"","para":"The famous ghaṭākāśa (pot-space) analogy. The one ākāśa appears as many pot-spaces when pots are placed in it; similarly, the one Self appears as many jīvas when bodies are formed. The pots' aggregates correspond to bodies; the spaces-within-pots correspond to jīvas.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> This is Advaita's central pedagogical analogy for jīva-Brahman relation. The analogy will be unfolded across the next several verses."],["Sivananda","For the meditator: visualize the analogy. The ākāśa is one; the pot-space is the same ākāśa, momentarily delimited by the pot. The pot is the body; the pot-space is the jīva; the unbounded ākāśa is Brahman. The same pedagogy is used by later Vedānta extensively."],["Krishnananda","jīvaiḥ uditaḥ — 'risen by jīvas' — is curious phrasing. The Self has risen (appeared, manifested) BY MEANS OF jīvas — meaning the jīvas are the mode of the Self's apparent manifestation. The Self does not produce the jīvas; the jīvas are how the Self appears."],["Nikhilananda","this verse is the most-pedagogically-useful in Vedānta after the rope-snake. It explains jīva-Brahman relation without requiring abstract metaphysical commitments."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.4","title":"When pots etc. are dissolved, pot-spaces etc. merge into ākāśa — so jīvas (merge) into Self","sa":["घटादिषु प्रलीनेषु घटाकाशादयो यथा ।","आकाशे सम्प्रलीयन्ते तद्वज्जीवा इहाऽऽत्मनि ॥ ४॥"],"iast":["ghaṭādiṣu pralīneṣu ghaṭākāśādayo yathā |","ākāśe sampralīyante tadvaj jīvā ihātmani || 4 ||"],"words":[["ghaṭa-ādiṣu pralīneṣu","m. loc. pl. + pp.","when pots etc. are dissolved"],["ghaṭa-ākāśa-ādayaḥ yathā","m. nom. pl. + indecl.","as pot-spaces etc."],["ākāśe sampralīyante","m. loc. + 3 pl.","merge into ākāśa"],["tadvat jīvāḥ iha ātmani","indecl. + m. nom. pl.","so jīvas here (merge) into the Self"]],"lit":"\"When pots etc. are dissolved, pot-spaces etc., as it were, merge into ākāśa — so the jīvas (merge) into the Self.\"","para":"When the pot breaks, the pot-space rejoins (or is recognized as) the unbounded ākāśa. Similarly, when the body falls, the jīva merges into (= is recognized as) the Self.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Death as analogy: the pot's destruction does not destroy the space; it only removes the apparent boundary. So with the body's dissolution."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: the analogy comforts but also instructs. The 'death' of the jīva is not loss; it is the recognition of the unbounded Self."],["Krishnananda","sampralīyante — 'merge fully' — captures the process. The pot-space does not 'go somewhere'; it merges fully (sam-) into the always-present ākāśa."],["Nikhilananda","this verse provides the funerary metaphor for the analogy of 3.3. The pot-breaking is death; the merging-into-ākāśa is mokṣa-as-recognition."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.5","title":"As when one pot-space is mixed with dust, smoke, etc., all (other pot-spaces) are not (so) joined — so jīvas with happiness etc.","sa":["यथैकस्मिन्घटाकाशे रजोधूमादिभिर्युते ।","न सर्वे संप्रयुज्यन्ते तद्वज्जीवाः सुखादिभिः ॥ ५॥"],"iast":["yathaikasmin ghaṭākāśe rajo-dhūmādibhir yute |","na sarve samprayujyante tadvaj jīvāḥ sukhādibhiḥ || 5 ||"],"words":[["yathā ekasmin ghaṭa-ākāśe","indecl. + m. loc. sg.","as when in one pot-space"],["rajaḥ-dhūma-ādibhiḥ yute","m. instr. pl. + loc.","joined with dust-smoke etc."],["na sarve samprayujyante","indecl. + 3 pl. pres.","all (others) are not joined"],["tadvat jīvāḥ sukha-ādibhiḥ","indecl. + m. nom. pl.","so the jīvas with happiness etc."]],"lit":"\"As when one pot-space is joined with dust, smoke, etc., all (other pot-spaces) are not (so) joined — so the jīvas with happiness etc.\"","para":"Even when one pot-space is contaminated (dusty, smoky), other pot-spaces are not. Similarly, one jīva's experience of pleasure/pain does not affect other jīvas.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Crucial doctrinal point. The one-Self-as-many-jīvas might suggest a sympathetic shared experience; the analogy denies this. Each pot-space's contamination is local; each jīva's experience is local."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: your suffering is yours; another's joy is theirs. The non-duality doctrine does not require sympathetic-magic among jīvas. The Self is one in substance; the upādhis differ."],["Krishnananda","This verse anticipates the standard Vedāntic refutation of the objection: 'If the Self is one, why don't we all experience the same thing?' The pot-space analogy answers it."],["Nikhilananda","this verse forestalls common-sense objections to advaita that would arise if the analogy were taken too simplistically."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.6","title":"Forms, work-effects, names, etc. are differentiated here and there; but ākāśa has no division — so it is established with jīvas","sa":["रूपकार्यसमाख्याश्च भिद्यन्ते तत्र तत्र वै ।","आकाशस्य न भेदोऽस्ति तद्वज्जीवेषु निर्णयः ॥ ६॥"],"iast":["rūpa-kārya-samākhyāś ca bhidyante tatra tatra vai |","ākāśasya na bhedo'sti tadvaj jīveṣu nirṇayaḥ || 6 ||"],"words":[["rūpa-kārya-samākhyāḥ","f. nom. pl.","forms-effects-names"],["bhidyante tatra tatra vai","3 pl. + indecl.","are differentiated here and there"],["ākāśasya na bhedaḥ asti","m. gen. + neg. + m. nom.","no division belongs to ākāśa"],["tadvat jīveṣu nirṇayaḥ","indecl. + m. loc. pl. + m. nom.","so (is) the conclusion in the case of jīvas"]],"lit":"\"Forms, effects, and names are differentiated in different places; (yet) ākāśa has no division — so is the conclusion in the case of jīvas.\"","para":"Pots have different forms (rūpa), produce different effects (kārya), bear different names (samākhyā). Yet the ākāśa within them is undivided. Similarly: jīvas have different bodies, different actions, different names; yet the Self is undivided.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The analogy spelt out in detail. The variety of jīvas is the variety of their upādhis (bodies, names, deeds); the underlying Self is one."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: when you encounter another person, recognize: their differences from you are in form-effect-name, not in Self. The Self is one."],["Krishnananda","nirṇaya — 'settled conclusion' — emphasizes that the analogy yields a settled doctrinal conclusion, not merely a poetic image."],["Nikhilananda","this verse establishes that the differentiation of jīvas is upādhic, not ontological. The analogy completes its didactic function."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.7","title":"As pot-space is not modification or part of ākāśa, so jīva is never modification or part of the Self","sa":["नाऽऽकाशस्य घटाकाशो विकारावयवौ यथा ।","नैवाऽऽत्मनः सदा जीवो विकारावयवौ तथा ॥ ७॥"],"iast":["nākāśasya ghaṭākāśo vikārāvayavau yathā |","naivātmanaḥ sadā jīvo vikārāvayavau tathā || 7 ||"],"words":[["na ākāśasya ghaṭa-ākāśaḥ","m. gen. + m. nom.","the pot-space is not of ākāśa"],["vikāra-avayavau yathā","m. nom. du. + indecl.","modification or part, as"],["na eva ātmanaḥ sadā jīvaḥ","m. gen. + indecl. + m. nom.","(so) jīva is never of the Self"],["vikāra-avayavau tathā","m. nom. du. + indecl.","modification or part, so"]],"lit":"\"As pot-space is not a modification or a part of ākāśa, so jīva is never a modification or a part of the Self.\"","para":"Critical doctrinal precision: the jīva is NEITHER a modification (vikāra) of the Self NOR a part (avayava) of it. Both would imply real differentiation; the Self has neither.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verse forestalls two specific misreadings: pariṇāma-vāda (Self transforms into jīvas) and avayava-vāda (Self has jīvas as parts). Both are denied. The jīva-Self relation is the ākāśa-ghaṭākāśa relation: appearance, not real production or part-of-whole."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: do not think 'I am a modification of the Self' or 'I am a part of the Self.' Both are wrong. You are the Self under an upādhi — neither modification nor part."],["Krishnananda","sadā — 'never' — is emphatic. At no level, never, is the jīva a modification or part of the Self."],["Nikhilananda","this verse forecloses the bhedābheda-vāda interpretation. The jīva-Self relation is strict identity, not partial identity."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.8","title":"As to children the sky becomes dirty with dirt-marks, so to the unawakened the Self becomes (apparently) dirty with dirt-marks","sa":["यथा भवति बालानां गगनं मलिनं मलैः ।","तथा भवत्यबुद्धानामात्माऽपि मलिनो मलैः ॥ ८॥"],"iast":["yathā bhavati bālānāṃ gaganaṃ malinaṃ malaiḥ |","tathā bhavaty abuddhānām ātmāpi malino malaiḥ || 8 ||"],"words":[["yathā bhavati bālānām","indecl. + 3 sg. + m. gen. pl.","as to children"],["gaganam malinam malaiḥ","n. nom. + m. instr. pl.","the sky (becomes) dirty with dirts"],["tathā bhavati a-buddhānām","indecl. + m. gen. pl.","so to the unawakened"],["ātmā api malinaḥ malaiḥ","m. nom. + m. instr. pl.","the Self also (becomes) dirty with dirts"]],"lit":"\"As to children the sky becomes dirty with dirt-marks, so to the unawakened the Self also (appears) dirty with dirt-marks.\"","para":"Children mistake distant dirt-clouds for the sky's own dirtiness; in fact, the sky is unaffected. Unawakened persons mistake apparent defects in the Self for the Self's own defects; in fact, the Self is unaffected.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The classical analogy for the Self's apparent contamination. The Self has no defects; the appearance of defects belongs to the upādhis. Treating the Self as defective is a child's error."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: any defect you attribute to your Self (sin, ignorance, suffering) is in the upādhis, not in the Self. The Self is gagana (sky) — untouched."],["Krishnananda","a-buddhāḥ — 'the unawakened' — is the technical term. They are not literally unintelligent; they are just not-yet-awakened to the Self's actual purity."],["Nikhilananda","this verse provides moral comfort. The seeker who feels morally inadequate may be misperceiving the Self; the Self itself is never inadequate."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.9","title":"In death, in birth, in coming-and-going, in dwelling in all bodies — (Self) is undifferentiated from ākāśa","sa":["मरणे सम्भवे चैव गत्यागमनयोरपि ।","स्थितौ सर्वशरीरेषु आकाशेनाविलक्षणः ॥ ९॥"],"iast":["maraṇe sambhave caiva gatyāgamanayor api |","sthitau sarva-śarīreṣu ākāśenāvilakṣaṇaḥ || 9 ||"],"words":[["maraṇe sambhave ca eva","m. loc. + m. loc.","in death and in birth"],["gati-āgamanayoḥ api","m. loc. du.","in coming and going also"],["sthitau sarva-śarīreṣu","f. loc. + n. loc. pl.","in abiding in all bodies"],["ākāśena a-vilakṣaṇaḥ","m. instr. + m. nom.","indistinguishable from ākāśa"]],"lit":"\"In death, in birth, in coming-and-going, in dwelling in all bodies — (the Self is) indistinguishable from ākāśa.\"","para":"The Self behaves like ākāśa across all the events: birth, death, transit, residence in different bodies. In all of these, the Self is fundamentally undifferentiated from ākāśa.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The grand summary of the ghaṭākāśa analogy. The Self is ākāśa-like in every relevant respect."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: in every moment of any state (newly born, dying, mid-life, present body, future body) — the Self is ākāśa-like. Recognize this stability across all events."],["Krishnananda","a-vilakṣaṇa — 'indistinguishable, with no separating mark' — is the precise term. There is no differentiating feature between Self and ākāśa."],["Nikhilananda","this closes the ghaṭākāśa-analogy section. The next verses move to the ajāti doctrine proper."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.10","title":"All bodies, like dream, are projected by ātmā-māyā — no establishment in superiority or equality","sa":["सङ्घाताः स्वप्नवत्सर्वे आत्ममायाविसर्जिताः ।","आधिक्ये सर्वसाम्ये वा नोपपत्तिर्हि विद्यते ॥ १०॥"],"iast":["saṅghātāḥ svapna-vat sarve ātma-māyā-visarjitāḥ |","ādhikye sarva-sāmye vā nopapattir hi vidyate || 10 ||"],"words":[["saṅghātāḥ svapna-vat sarve","m. nom. pl. + indecl.","all aggregates, like dream"],["ātma-māyā-visarjitāḥ","m. nom. pl. tp.","are projected by Self-māyā"],["ādhikye sarva-sāmye vā","m. loc. + n. loc.","in superiority or in total equality"],["na upapattiḥ hi vidyate","indecl. + f. nom.","no justification is found"]],"lit":"\"All aggregates, like (those in) dream, are projected by ātmā-māyā; in (a doctrine of) superiority or total equality (among them) no justification is found.\"","para":"All bodies/aggregates are dream-like projections of ātmā-māyā. There is no philosophical basis for treating some as superior to others (hierarchical realism) or as all equal (egalitarian realism). Both presuppose real distinctions; the dream-like projection has none.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Important refinement. Some readers of advaita slide into either hierarchical realism (\"the levels are real\") or egalitarian realism (\"all is equal\"). Gauḍapāda denies both — there are no real distinctions to be hierarchized or equalized."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: do not say 'all bodies are equal' (which still treats them as real). Recognize: all bodies are dream-like projections; equality/inequality among them is moot."],["Krishnananda","ātma-māyā-visarjita is technical: visarjita means 'projected, sent out.' The bodies are not produced by ātmā-māyā; they are projected by it — a phenomenological act, not a metaphysical one."],["Nikhilananda","this verse closes the ākāśa-analogy section and transitions to the ajāti doctrine."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.11","title":"The sheaths spoken of in the Taittirīyaka — the (further-)Self of those is the jīva; the sky is illuminated (as the further Self)","sa":["रसादयो हि ये कोशा व्याख्यातास्तैत्तिरीयके ।","तेषामात्मा परो जीवः खं यथा सम्प्रकाशितः ॥ ११॥"],"iast":["rasādayo hi ye kośā vyākhyātās taittirīyake |","teṣām ātmā paro jīvaḥ khaṃ yathā samprakāśitaḥ || 11 ||"],"words":[["rasa-ādayaḥ hi ye kośāḥ","m. nom. pl.","the sheaths beginning with rasa"],["vyākhyātāḥ taittirīyake","m. nom. pl. + n. loc.","expounded in the Taittirīyaka"],["teṣām ātmā paraḥ jīvaḥ","m. gen. + nom. + nom.","the further Self of these (is) jīva"],["kham yathā samprakāśitaḥ","n. acc. + indecl.","as ākāśa is illuminated (as further)"]],"lit":"\"The sheaths beginning with rasa, expounded in the Taittirīyaka — the further Self of those is the jīva; as ākāśa is illuminated (as the further Self).\"","para":"The five kośas of Taittirīya 2 (annamaya, prāṇamaya, manomaya, vijñānamaya, ānandamaya — the rasa-ādayaḥ kośāḥ) have the jīva as their further Self; just as ākāśa is illuminated as the further Self of all bodies (cf. 3.3).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> References the Taittirīya pañca-kośa-vidyā and integrates it with the ghaṭākāśa analogy: the kośas are the sheaths; the inner Self is the jīva (in upādhi-form) and through it the Self (in absolute form)."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: the kośa-discrimination (peeling layer by layer) and the ghaṭākāśa-recognition (recognizing the unboundedness) are complementary practices."],["Krishnananda","The verse anticipates the Vedāntic synthesis: both the Taittirīya's kośa-analysis and the Māṇḍūkya's ghaṭākāśa-analogy point to the same conclusion."],["Nikhilananda","this verse explicitly links Gauḍapāda's exposition with the Taittirīya tradition."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.12","title":"In the madhu-jñāna of pairs, the higher Brahman is illuminated; in earth (and) in the belly, as ākāśa is illuminated","sa":["द्वयोर्द्वयोर्मधुज्ञाने परं ब्रह्म प्रकाशितम् ।","पृथिव्यामुदरे चैव यथाऽऽकाशः प्रकाशितः ॥ १२॥"],"iast":["dvayor dvayor madhu-jñāne paraṃ brahma prakāśitam |","pṛthivyām udare caiva yathākāśaḥ prakāśitaḥ || 12 ||"],"words":[["dvayoḥ dvayoḥ madhu-jñāne","n. gen. du. (doubled) + n. loc.","in the madhu-knowledge of each pair"],["param brahma prakāśitam","n. nom. + pp.","the higher Brahman is illuminated"],["pṛthivyām udare ca eva","f. loc. + n. loc.","in earth and in the belly"],["yathā ākāśaḥ prakāśitaḥ","indecl. + m. nom. + pp.","as ākāśa is illuminated"]],"lit":"\"In the madhu-knowledge of each pair, the higher Brahman is illuminated — in earth and in the belly — as ākāśa is illuminated (within each).\"","para":"References the Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 2.5 madhu-vidyā: each pair (earth-and-its-eater, sun-and-its-eye, etc.) has Brahman illuminated as their further essence. The same pattern as ākāśa (illuminated within each pot's interior).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> References BĀU 2.5 (the famous madhu-brāhmaṇa), where Brahman is shown to be the higher essence of each cosmic pair. The Kārikā integrates this with the ghaṭākāśa pedagogy."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: every duality you encounter (subject/object, knower/known, lover/beloved) has Brahman as its madhu — its essential sweetness. Recognize this in every dual encounter."],["Krishnananda","madhu-jñāna — 'knowledge of the honey' — is the technical name of BĀU 2.5's teaching. Gauḍapāda is showing that the Kārikā's ghaṭākāśa pedagogy and the BĀU's madhu pedagogy converge."],["Nikhilananda","this verse anchors the Kārikā in the broader Upaniṣadic tradition."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.13","title":"The non-difference of jīva and the Self is praised by non-difference; the difference that is censured is therefore consistent thus","sa":["जीवात्मनोरनन्यत्वमभेदेन प्रशस्यते ।","नानात्वं निन्द्यते यच्च तदेवं हि समञ्जसम् ॥ १३॥"],"iast":["jīvātmanor ananyatvam abhedena praśasyate |","nānātvaṃ nindyate yac ca tad evaṃ hi samañjasam || 13 ||"],"words":[["jīva-ātmanoḥ a-ananyatvam","m. gen. du. + n. nom.","the non-otherness of jīva and ātmā"],["a-bhedena praśasyate","m. instr. + 3 sg.","is praised by non-difference"],["nānātvam nindyate yat ca","n. nom. + 3 sg.","and multiplicity that is censured"],["tat evam hi samañjasam","n. nom. + indecl.","that thus is consistent"]],"lit":"\"The non-otherness of jīva and Self is praised by the (doctrine of) non-difference; the multiplicity that is censured (in scripture) is thus consistent.\"","para":"Scripture both praises jīva-Self non-difference and censures multiplicity. Both moves are mutually consistent under the ghaṭākāśa pedagogy: non-difference is real, multiplicity is upādhic.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Standard reconciliation of seemingly-conflicting scriptural passages. Some Upaniṣads praise unity; others seem to assert plurality. Both are intelligible under advaita."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: do not be confused by scriptural passages that seem to assert plurality. They are upādhi-talk; the truth-talk is the non-difference assertions."],["Krishnananda","samañjasam — 'consistent' — is the verse's claim: scripture is internally consistent, when correctly interpreted."],["Nikhilananda","this verse provides the exegetical key for handling the apparent scriptural plurality."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.14","title":"The separateness of jīva and Self that is taught before origination — this is secondary, by future operation; primary-ness does not apply","sa":["जीवात्मनोः पृथक्त्वं यत्प्रागुत्पत्तेः प्रकीर्तितम् ।","भविष्यद्वृत्त्या गौणं तन्मुख्यत्वं हि न युज्यते ॥ १४॥"],"iast":["jīvātmanoḥ pṛthaktvaṃ yat prāg utpatteḥ prakīrtitam |","bhaviṣyad-vṛttyā gauṇaṃ tan mukhyatvaṃ hi na yujyate || 14 ||"],"words":[["jīva-ātmanoḥ pṛthaktvam yat","m. gen. du. + n. nom.","the separateness of jīva and Self"],["prāk utpatteḥ prakīrtitam","indecl. + f. abl. + pp.","(that is) declared before origination"],["bhaviṣyat-vṛttyā gauṇam tat","f. instr. + n. nom.","is secondary by future operation"],["mukhyatvam hi na yujyate","n. nom. + indecl.","primary-ness does not apply"]],"lit":"\"The separateness of jīva and Self declared before origination — that is secondary, by future operation; primary (literal) meaning does not apply.\"","para":"Scripture that speaks of jīva-Self separateness before creation does so only secondarily, by anticipation of future operation; the primary (literal) sense is not the operative one.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Refines 3.13: the pre-creation separateness language in some texts is gauṇa (secondary, figurative). Not literal."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: when you read a Vedic passage about \"separate selves,\" remember this verse — it is gauṇa-vāda. The primary truth is non-separateness."],["Krishnananda","gauṇa / mukhya is the standard exegetical pair: secondary (figurative) vs. primary (literal). The verse classifies separateness-talk as gauṇa."],["Nikhilananda","this is a key hermeneutic principle. Without it, scriptural plurality-talk would conflict with non-duality."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.15","title":"Creation as told by mṛd-loha-sparks-etc. — this is a means for descent; division does not (really) exist","sa":["मृल्लोहविस्फुलिङ्गाद्यैः सृष्टिर्या चोदिताऽन्यथा ।","उपायः सोऽवतारायाः नास्ति भेदः कथञ्चन ॥ १५॥"],"iast":["mṛl-loha-visphuliṅgādyaiḥ sṛṣṭir yā coditānyathā |","upāyaḥ so'vatārāyāḥ nāsti bhedaḥ kathañcana || 15 ||"],"words":[["mṛt-loha-visphuliṅga-ādyaiḥ","m. instr. pl.","by (the images of) clay, metal, sparks etc."],["sṛṣṭiḥ yā coditā anyathā","f. nom. + pp.","creation that is otherwise taught"],["upāyaḥ saḥ avatārāyāḥ","m. nom. + m. abl./gen.","is the means for descent"],["na asti bhedaḥ kathañcana","indecl. + m. nom.","no difference whatever (exists)"]],"lit":"\"The creation taught (in scripture) otherwise — by the images of clay, metal, sparks, etc. — is a means for (the listener's) descent (= introduction); no difference really exists.\"","para":"Upaniṣadic cosmogonies using analogies (clay-pot, metal-ornaments, sparks-from-fire — references to Chāndogya 6.1, BĀU 1.4.7, Muṇḍaka 2.1.1) are upāya — pedagogical devices for the listener's descent into the doctrine. They do not assert real differentiation.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> References the standard Upaniṣadic cosmogonies. Each is treated by Gauḍapāda as upāya, not pāramārthika. The texts that seem to describe creation actually describe non-creation in a way the listener can approach gradually."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: do not take any cosmogonic image literally. They are all upāya-cosmogonies, meant to lead you toward ajāti."],["Krishnananda","avatāra — 'descent' — here means pedagogical descent: the doctrine descends to the listener's level via these images."],["Nikhilananda","this verse establishes the consistent hermeneutic of the Kārikā: cosmogonic language is always pedagogical, never literal."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.16","title":"The three āśramas (correspond to) three kinds of viewers: inferior, middle, superior; this upāsanā is for them, taught out of compassion","sa":["आश्रमास्त्रिविधा हीनमध्यमोत्कृष्टदृष्टयः ।","उपासनोपदिष्टेयं तदर्थमनुकम्पया ॥ १६॥"],"iast":["āśramās tri-vidhā hīna-madhyamotkṛṣṭa-dṛṣṭayaḥ |","upāsanopadiṣṭeyaṃ tad-artham anukampayā || 16 ||"],"words":[["āśramāḥ tri-vidhāḥ","m. nom. pl. + adj.","the (life-stages) of three kinds"],["hīna-madhyama-utkṛṣṭa-dṛṣṭayaḥ","m. nom. pl. bv.","(having) inferior, middle, superior views"],["upāsanā upadiṣṭā iyam","f. nom. + pp.","this upāsanā is taught"],["tad-artham anukampayā","indecl. + f. instr.","for their sake, with compassion"]],"lit":"\"The (āśramas / life-stages or schools of practitioners) are of three kinds, by inferior, middle, superior views; this upāsanā is taught for their sake, out of compassion.\"","para":"The seekers are graded into three kinds of capacity; upāsanā (devotional/meditative practice) is taught for them. The doctrine of ajāti is for the most-prepared; upāsanā is provided to bring others gradually toward it.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> For the seeker: locate yourself on this scale. Most seekers are middle-grade; full ajāti-grasp requires utkṛṣṭa view. Upāsanā is the path to advance."],["Sivananda","anukampayā — 'with compassion' — is the qualifier. The Upaniṣadic teacher does not begrudge upāsanā to inferior students; he provides it compassionately."],["Krishnananda","This is the Kārikā's most generous statement on upāsanā. Earlier (3.1) it called the upāsaka kṛpaṇa; here it justifies upāsanā as compassion-pedagogy. Both are true."],["Nikhilananda","this verse provides the pedagogical framework for the entire Vedāntic curriculum — upāsanā for some, ajāti-jñāna for the qualified."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.17","title":"Dualists, firm in their own siddhānta-positions, contradict each other; with this (advaita) they do not contradict","sa":["स्वसिद्धान्तव्यवस्थासु द्वैतिनो निश्चिता दृढम् ।","परस्परं विरुध्यन्ते तैरयं न विरुध्यते ॥ १७॥"],"iast":["sva-siddhānta-vyavasthāsu dvaitino niścitā dṛḍham |","parasparaṃ virudhyante tair ayaṃ na virudhyate || 17 ||"],"words":[["sva-siddhānta-vyavasthāsu","f. loc. pl. tp.","in their own siddhānta-arrangements"],["dvaitinaḥ niścitāḥ dṛḍham","m. nom. pl.","dualists firmly settled"],["paraspaṛam virudhyante","indecl. + 3 pl.","contradict each other"],["taiḥ ayam na virudhyate","m. instr. pl. + m. nom. + 3 sg.","with them this (advaita) does not contradict"]],"lit":"\"Dualists, firmly settled in their own siddhānta-arrangements, contradict each other; this (advaita) does not contradict with them.\"","para":"Each dualist school is firm in its own position and contradicts other schools. The advaita position, however, does not contradict them — because it does not deny their (limited) truth at their level.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Gauḍapāda's irenic move: advaita is not in opposition to the dualist schools. It is the framework within which their partial truths can be located."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: do not engage in school-warfare. The advaita position you've adopted does not require refuting other schools; it locates them."],["Krishnananda","a-virodha — 'non-contradiction' — is a strong claim. The Advaitin does not contradict the dualist (because each dualist position is true at its own level); the dualists contradict each other."],["Nikhilananda","this is one of Advaita's most-cited statements of self-positioning. It allowed Advaita to absorb Dvaita and Viśiṣṭādvaita positions as lower truths."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.18","title":"Advaita is the highest truth; duality is its differentiation; for them (dualists), duality is in both — therefore (advaita) is not in contradiction","sa":["अद्वैतं परमार्थो हि द्वैतं तद्भेद उच्यते ।","तेषामुभयथा द्वैतं तेनायं न विरुध्यते ॥ १८॥"],"iast":["advaitaṃ paramārtho hi dvaitaṃ tad-bheda ucyate |","teṣām ubhayathā dvaitaṃ tenāyaṃ na virudhyate || 18 ||"],"words":[["advaitam paramārthaḥ hi","n. nom. + m. nom.","non-duality (is) the ultimate"],["dvaitam tad-bhedaḥ ucyate","n. nom. + m. nom.","duality (is its) differentiation"],["teṣām ubhayathā dvaitam","m. gen. pl. + indecl.","for them, duality is both-ways"],["tena ayam na virudhyate","n. instr. + m. nom.","therefore this (advaita) does not contradict"]],"lit":"\"Advaita is the ultimate truth; duality is its differentiation. For them (dualists), duality is in both (the ultimate and the relative). Therefore this (advaita) does not contradict (their positions).\"","para":"Advaita is the absolute truth; duality is the differentiated mode of the same. The dualist puts duality at both levels (ultimate and relative); the advaitin puts non-duality only at the ultimate level. Therefore they are not in direct contradiction.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Refines 3.17. The disagreement between advaita and dvaita is structural, not contradictory: advaita is the higher truth that includes duality as appearance."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: when you encounter a dualist, do not say 'you are wrong.' Say 'you are right at one level; advaita extends to the other level.' This is the canonical advaita response."],["Krishnananda","ubhayathā — 'both-ways' — is precise. The dualist applies duality both to the absolute and the relative; the advaitin applies non-duality to the absolute and duality only to the relative."],["Nikhilananda","this verse provides the doctrinal warrant for Advaita's claim to be the most-comprehensive Vedānta position."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.19","title":"By māyā this (apparent) differentiation occurs, not otherwise of the unborn; (if) differentiated in reality, the immortal would attain mortality","sa":["मायया भिद्यते ह्येतन्नान्यथाऽजं कथञ्चन ।","तत्त्वतो भिद्यमाने हि मर्त्यतामं अमृतं व्रजेत् ॥ १९॥"],"iast":["māyayā bhidyate hy etan nānyathājaṃ kathañcana |","tattvato bhidyamāne hi martyatām amṛtaṃ vrajet || 19 ||"],"words":[["māyayā bhidyate hi etat","f. instr. + 3 sg.","this is differentiated by māyā"],["na anyathā ajam kathañcana","indecl. + n. acc.","not otherwise (is) the unborn"],["tattvataḥ bhidyamāne hi","indecl. + pres. part. loc.","if differentiated in reality"],["martyatām amṛtam vrajet","f. acc. + n. nom. + 3 sg. opt.","the immortal would go to mortality"]],"lit":"\"This (apparent multiplicity) is differentiated by māyā, not otherwise; the unborn (= Self) is in no way differentiated. If it were differentiated in reality, the immortal would go to mortality.\"","para":"The multiplicity is due to māyā, not real differentiation. If real differentiation were granted, then the immortal Self would be subject to mortality (because real differences imply real changes).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Logical argument: real differentiation implies real change implies mortality. Since the Self is immortal, the differentiation cannot be real."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: any sense you have of being 'different' from the Self or of the Self having parts is māyā-grounded, not real. To grant real difference is to deny the Self's immortality."],["Krishnananda","This argument is one of Advaita's most-elegant: it uses the granted immortality of the Self to deduce the māyā-only character of differentiation."],["Nikhilananda","this verse is the key logical move of the Advaita-prakaraṇa."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.20","title":"The disputants want the birth of the unborn being; how can the unborn, immortal being go to mortality?","sa":["अजातस्यैव भावस्य जातिमिच्छन्ति वादिनः ।","अजातो ह्यमृतो भावो मर्त्यतां कथमेष्यति ॥ २०॥"],"iast":["ajātasyaiva bhāvasya jātim icchanti vādinaḥ |","ajāto hy amṛto bhāvo martyatāṃ kathameṣyati || 20 ||"],"words":[["a-jātasya eva bhāvasya","m. gen. + m. gen.","of the unborn being"],["jātim icchanti vādinaḥ","f. acc. + 3 pl. + m. nom. pl.","the disputants want birth"],["a-jātaḥ hi amṛtaḥ bhāvaḥ","m. nom. + m. nom.","the unborn, immortal being"],["martyatām katham eṣyati","f. acc. + indecl. + 3 sg. fut.","how will (it) go to mortality?"]],"lit":"\"The disputants want birth for the unborn being; (but) how will the unborn, immortal being go to mortality?\"","para":"Rhetorical question. Disputants attribute origination to the Self (which is unborn). But origination implies eventual death; the Self is immortal; therefore origination cannot apply.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> For the seeker: do not be tempted by cosmogonic narratives that ascribe origination to the Self. The conclusion would be that the Self can die; this contradicts the immortality the same scriptures assert."],["Sivananda","The argument is a reductio: granting origination → granting eventual mortality → contradicting immortality → therefore reject origination."],["Krishnananda","vādinaḥ — 'the disputants' — is gently dismissive. The disputants want one thing but their position implies another they cannot want."],["Nikhilananda","this verse continues the modus tollens against creationism initiated in 3.19."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.21","title":"The immortal does not become mortal, nor mortal immortal; modification of nature is in no way","sa":["न भवत्यमृतं मर्त्यं न मर्त्यममृतं तथा ।","प्रकृतेरन्यथाभावो न कथञ्चिद् भविष्यति ॥ २१॥"],"iast":["na bhavaty amṛtaṃ martyaṃ na martyam amṛtaṃ tathā |","prakṛter anyathābhāvo na kathañcid bhaviṣyati || 21 ||"],"words":[["na bhavati a-mṛtam martyam","neg. + 3 sg. + n. nom. + n. nom.","the immortal does not become mortal"],["na martyam a-mṛtam tathā","neg. + n. nom. + n. nom.","likewise the mortal does not (become) immortal"],["prakṛteḥ anyathā-bhāvaḥ","f. gen. + m. nom.","the otherwise-being of (one's) nature"],["na kathañcit bhaviṣyati","indecl. + 3 sg. fut.","will not in any way be"]],"lit":"\"The immortal does not become mortal; the mortal does not become immortal. The otherwise-being of (one's intrinsic) nature will not be in any way.\"","para":"Nature does not transform into its opposite. The immortal cannot become mortal; the mortal cannot become immortal. This is a fundamental metaphysical principle.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The metaphysical premise of the argument. Prakṛti-anyathā-bhāva (transformation of intrinsic nature) is impossible. Therefore any seeming-transformation must be illusory."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: notice that all 'spiritual progress' language presupposes some kind of transformation. The Kārikā denies this. There is no transformation; only recognition."],["Krishnananda","The verse is one of the most-cited in later Advaita for the doctrine of na pariṇāma-vāda (no real transformation of the Self)."],["Nikhilananda","this verse states the metaphysical premise. The next verse will spell out the implication."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.22","title":"How can the immortal-by-nature, if its being went to mortality, stand immovable as immortal by means of (something) made?","sa":["स्वभावेनामृतो यस्य भावो गच्छति मर्त्यताम् ।","कृतकेनामृतस्तस्य कथं स्थास्यति निश्चलः ॥ २२॥"],"iast":["svabhāvenāmṛto yasya bhāvo gacchati martyatām |","kṛtakenāmṛtas tasya kathaṃ sthāsyati niścalaḥ || 22 ||"],"words":[["svabhāvena amṛtaḥ yasya","m. instr. + m. nom. + m. gen.","whose being is immortal by nature"],["bhāvaḥ gacchati martyatām","m. nom. + 3 sg. + f. acc.","(if) goes to mortality"],["kṛtakena a-mṛtaḥ tasya","m. instr. + m. nom. + m. gen.","by something made, the immortal of that one"],["katham sthāsyati niścalaḥ","indecl. + 3 sg. fut. + m. nom.","how will it stand immovable?"]],"lit":"\"If the being whose nature is immortal goes to mortality — by something made (i.e., by anything caused), how will that (Self) stand immovable as immortal?\"","para":"If the immortal Self could transform to mortality, then by any causal influence the immortal-property could be undone. But the immortal must stand niścala (immovable). Therefore: no transformation.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Logical extension of 3.21. If transformation were possible, the immortal would not be stably immortal — its immortality would depend on no transformation occurring. But that makes its immortality conditional, not intrinsic."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: the Self's immortality is intrinsic. It is not preserved by any condition external to the Self. Recognize this stability."],["Krishnananda","niścalaḥ — 'immovable' — captures the intended sense. The Self does not just exist immortally; it stands immortally without possibility of movement."],["Nikhilananda","this verse completes the argument of 3.19-22 against any real transformation of the Self."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.23","title":"Whether creation is from being or from non-being, śruti is equal; the settled, reason-fitted is what it is, not the other","sa":["भूततोऽभूततो वाऽपि सृज्यमाने समा श्रुतिः ।","निश्चितं युक्तियुक्तं च यत्तद्भवति नेतरत् ॥ २३॥"],"iast":["bhūtato'bhūtato vāpi sṛjyamāne samā śrutiḥ |","niścitaṃ yukti-yuktaṃ ca yat tad bhavati netarat || 23 ||"],"words":[["bhūtataḥ a-bhūtataḥ vā api","indecl.","whether from being or non-being"],["sṛjyamāne samā śrutiḥ","n. loc. + f. nom.","in (the matter of) creating, śruti is equal"],["niścitam yukti-yuktam ca","n. nom. + n. nom.","and (what is) settled and reason-fitted"],["yat tat bhavati na itarat","n. nom. + 3 sg. + neg.","that is, not the other"]],"lit":"\"Whether creation is (from) being or (from) non-being, śruti is equally (silent / supportive). (Therefore) what is settled and reason-fitted is what is, not the other.\"","para":"Scripture is silent (or equally supportive) on whether creation is from sat or asat. Therefore the deciding criterion is reason. Reason favors ajāti (non-origination); therefore that is what is established.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Argues that scripture does not decide between sat-kārya-vāda and asat-kārya-vāda; reason then decides. Reason favors ajāti."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: trust reason in domains where scripture is silent. This is a strong Advaita commitment."],["Krishnananda","samā śrutiḥ — 'śruti is equal' — means śruti does not favor either sat-kārya-vāda or asat-kārya-vāda decisively. So reason must adjudicate."],["Nikhilananda","this verse provides the rationalist warrant for ajāti-vāda. Scripture's silence licenses reason's intervention."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.24","title":"'There is no plurality here'; 'Indra (became many) by māyās' — and other (śruti); the un-being-born one is born many ways by māyā","sa":["नेह नानेति चाऽऽम्नायादिन्द्रो मायाभिरित्यपि ।","अजायमानो बहुधा मायया जायते तु सः ॥ २४॥"],"iast":["neha nāneti cāmnāyād indro māyābhir ity api |","ajāyamāno bahudhā māyayā jāyate tu saḥ || 24 ||"],"words":[["na iha nānā iti ca āmnāyāt","indecl. + neg. + indecl. + m. abl.","'no plurality here', from scripture"],["indraḥ māyābhiḥ iti api","m. nom. + f. instr. pl.","'Indra (became many) by māyās' also"],["a-jāyamānaḥ bahudhā","m. nom. + indecl.","the unborn one, in many ways"],["māyayā jāyate tu saḥ","f. instr. + 3 sg. + m. nom.","is born (apparently) by māyā"]],"lit":"\"'There is no plurality here' (BĀU 4.4.19); 'Indra (became many) by māyās' (Ṛgveda 6.47.18) — and other scripture. (So) the unborn (Self) is (apparently) born in many ways by māyā.\"","para":"Two scriptural anchors for māyā-vāda: BĀU 4.4.19 (neha nānāsti kiñcana — 'there is no plurality at all here') and ṚV 6.47.18 (indro māyābhiḥ puru-rūpa īyate — 'Indra, by māyās, appears in many forms'). Together they support: the unborn is apparently born by māyā.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Two famous proof-texts. The verse anchors ajāti-vāda in scripture."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: when you next encounter Vedic plurality-language, recall: the same Veda also says 'no plurality.' The plurality-language must be māyā-language."],["Krishnananda","The use of ṚV 6.47.18 is striking — Gauḍapāda takes a Ṛgvedic mythological verse as proof for ontological māyā-vāda. This is interpretive boldness."],["Nikhilananda","this verse provides the scriptural foundation for the Kārikā's māyā-vāda."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.25","title":"By the refutation of being-born, the being-born is denied; 'who would beget Him?' — the cause is denied","sa":["सम्भूतेरपवादाच्च सम्भवः प्रतिषिध्यते ।","को न्वेनं जनयेदिति कारणं प्रतिषिध्यते ॥ २५॥"],"iast":["sambhūter apavādāc ca sambhavaḥ pratiṣidhyate |","ko nvenaṃ janayed iti kāraṇaṃ pratiṣidhyate || 25 ||"],"words":[["sambhūteḥ apavādāt ca","f. abl.","by the refutation of origination"],["sambhavaḥ pratiṣidhyate","m. nom. + 3 sg.","origination is denied"],["kaḥ nu enam janayet iti","m. nom. + 3 sg. opt.","'who would beget Him?'"],["kāraṇam pratiṣidhyate","n. nom. + 3 sg.","the cause is denied"]],"lit":"\"By the refutation of origination (in scripture), origination is denied. 'Who would beget Him?' — by this, the cause (of any begetting) is denied.\"","para":"Scripture both directly denies origination and asks the rhetorical 'who could beget?' — denying any begetting-cause. Therefore both effect (sambhava) and cause (kāraṇa) of origination are denied.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> References śruti passages that ask 'who could be the cause of the Self?' (e.g., ṚV 10.129's nāsadīya-sūkta). The answer is none; therefore no origination."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: the absence of any cause-candidate for the Self's origination is itself the proof of non-origination."],["Krishnananda","Both apavāda (refutation) and rhetorical question are valid scriptural moves. Either suffices; together they are decisive."],["Nikhilananda","this verse provides the scriptural argument structure for the ajāti doctrine."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.26","title":"He, 'not this, not this' — (scripture) thus refutes everything by the un-graspable-ness; therefore the unborn shines forth","sa":["स एष नेति नेतीति व्याख्यातं निह्नुते यतः ।","सर्वमग्राह्यभावेन हेतुनाऽजं प्रकाशते ॥ २६॥"],"iast":["sa eṣa neti netīti vyākhyātaṃ nihnute yataḥ |","sarvam agrāhya-bhāvena hetunājaṃ prakāśate || 26 ||"],"words":[["saḥ eṣaḥ na iti na iti","m. nom. + indecl.","He, 'not this, not this'"],["iti vyākhyātam nihnute","indecl. + pp. + 3 sg.","thus refutes the expounded"],["yataḥ sarvam a-grāhya-bhāvena","indecl. + n. nom. + m. instr.","since by un-graspable-ness"],["hetunā ajam prakāśate","m. instr. + n. nom. + 3 sg.","by reason, the unborn shines"]],"lit":"\"'He (is) not this, not this' (BĀU 2.3.6) — (scripture) thus refutes (what was previously) expounded; since by (its) un-graspable-ness, by that reason, the unborn shines forth.\"","para":"BĀU's neti neti formula refutes every characterization. The Self is therefore agrāhya (ungraspable). Through this ungraspability, the ajam (unborn) shines forth.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> References the famous neti neti of BĀU 2.3.6. Gauḍapāda uses it to argue: the Self is unborn precisely because no positive characterization (which would include being-caused-or-born) sticks."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: the neti neti practice — actively negating every characterization — is the way to access the agrāhya. The very practice of negation reveals the unborn."],["Krishnananda","agrāhya-bhāvena hetunā — 'by the reason of un-graspable-ness' — captures the move: ungraspability is itself the proof of unbornness."],["Nikhilananda","this verse aligns the Kārikā with the BĀU's apophatic tradition."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.27","title":"Of the being, birth by māyā is fitting, not in reality; (one) for whom truly being-born — for him, the being-born is born","sa":["सतो हि मायया जन्म युज्यते न तु तत्त्वतः ।","तत्त्वतो जायते यस्य जातं तस्य हि जायते ॥ २७॥"],"iast":["sato hi māyayā janma yujyate na tu tattvataḥ |","tattvato jāyate yasya jātaṃ tasya hi jāyate || 27 ||"],"words":[["sataḥ hi māyayā janma","m. gen. + f. instr. + n. nom.","of the being, birth by māyā"],["yujyate na tu tattvataḥ","3 sg. + neg. + indecl.","is fitting, not really"],["tattvataḥ jāyate yasya","indecl. + 3 sg. + m. gen.","(one) for whom (it) really happens"],["jātam tasya hi jāyate","n. nom. + m. gen.","the (already) born is born for him"]],"lit":"\"Of (a) being, birth by māyā is fitting (= can be explained), not really. For one (who holds) that it really happens — for him, (only) the (already) born can be born.\"","para":"Of an existing being (the Self), only māyā-birth is possible, not real birth. For one who insists on real birth, an absurdity follows: the already-born would have to be born.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Argument: real birth of the Self would require either (a) self-already-existing being born (absurd) or (b) self-not-yet-existing being born (impossible, since non-being cannot produce being). Therefore real birth is incoherent; only māyā-birth."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: do not say 'the Self is really born.' This dictum entangles you in incoherence. Use 'the Self appears to be born by māyā' — coherent, and the Vedāntic position."],["Krishnananda","The māyā-explanation is preferable to either real-birth or no-explanation: it preserves the appearance (which we cannot deny) and the non-reality (which logic demands)."],["Nikhilananda","this verse formalizes the Kārikā's doctrinal preference for māyā-explanation over alternatives."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.28","title":"Of non-being, birth by māyā is not really fitting; the son of a barren woman is not born — by māyā or really","sa":["असतो मायया जन्म तत्त्वतो नैव युज्यते ।","वन्ध्यापुत्रो न तत्त्वेन मायया वाऽपि जायते ॥ २८॥"],"iast":["asato māyayā janma tattvato naiva yujyate |","vandhyā-putro na tattvena māyayā vāpi jāyate || 28 ||"],"words":[["a-sataḥ māyayā janma","n. gen. + f. instr. + n. nom.","of non-being, by māyā, birth"],["tattvataḥ na eva yujyate","indecl. + neg. + 3 sg.","really is not fitting"],["vandhyā-putraḥ na tattvena","m. nom. + neg. + n. instr.","the barren-woman's son, not really"],["māyayā vā api jāyate","f. instr. + 3 sg.","(nor) by māyā is born"]],"lit":"\"Of non-being, birth by māyā is not really fitting either; the barren woman's son is not born — neither really nor by māyā.\"","para":"From non-being, neither real birth nor māyā-birth is possible. Example: a barren woman's son cannot be born in either mode. So if the Self were non-being, no birth (real or māyā-mediated) would be possible at all.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Argument against the asat-kārya-vāda position (the Buddhist śūnyavāda accusation against Gauḍapāda): if there is non-being, nothing comes from it. The barren-woman's-son example is the standard."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: do not slide into nihilism. The Kārikā denies origination but does NOT deny the Self. The Self is unborn-but-real, not non-existent."],["Krishnananda","This verse is Gauḍapāda's strongest distinction from Mādhyamika śūnyavāda. The doctrine is ajāti (no birth), not śūnyatā (no being)."],["Nikhilananda","this verse provides the Kārikā's clearest distinction from Buddhist śūnyavāda."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.29","title":"As in dream the mind moves with dual appearance by māyā, so in waking the mind moves with dual appearance by māyā","sa":["यथा स्वप्ने द्वयाभासं स्पन्दते मायया मनः ।","तथा जाग्रद्द्वयाभासं स्पन्दते मायया मनः ॥ २९॥"],"iast":["yathā svapne dvayābhāsaṃ spandate māyayā manaḥ |","tathā jāgrad-dvayābhāsaṃ spandate māyayā manaḥ || 29 ||"],"words":[["yathā svapne dvaya-ābhāsam","indecl. + m. loc. + n. nom.","as in dream, with dual appearance"],["spandate māyayā manaḥ","3 sg. + f. instr. + n. nom.","mind moves by māyā"],["tathā jāgrat-dvaya-ābhāsam","indecl. + n. nom.","so in waking, with dual appearance"],["spandate māyayā manaḥ","3 sg. + f. instr.","mind moves by māyā"]],"lit":"\"As in dream the mind moves with dual appearance by māyā, so in waking the mind moves with dual appearance by māyā.\"","para":"Dream-mind and waking-mind both 'move' (spandate — vibrate, fluctuate) with apparent duality, both by māyā. The two states share the same fundamental structure.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> This verse echoes the Vaitathya conclusion (2.5) and integrates it into the Advaita-prakaraṇa's argument."],["Sivananda","For the meditator: the spanda (vibration) of mind that produces dual appearance is the same in dream and waking. Recognize it in both."],["Krishnananda","spandate ('moves, vibrates') is a technical term that will become central in Kashmir Śaiva philosophy. Here it captures the constitutive movement of the dualistic mind."],["Nikhilananda","this verse is the doctrinal hinge linking Vaitathya and Advaita prakaraṇas — both states are equivalent māyā-spanda."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.30","title":"Non-dual and dual-appearing — mind in dream, no doubt; non-dual and dual-appearing — mind in waking, no doubt","sa":["अद्वयं च द्वयाभासं मनः स्वप्ने न संशयः ।","अद्वयं च द्वयाभासं तथा जाग्रन्न संशयः ॥ ३०॥"],"iast":["advayaṃ ca dvayābhāsaṃ manaḥ svapne na saṃśayaḥ |","advayaṃ ca dvayābhāsaṃ tathā jāgran na saṃśayaḥ || 30 ||"],"words":[["a-dvayam ca dvaya-ābhāsam","n. nom. + n. nom.","non-dual and dual-appearing"],["manaḥ svapne na saṃśayaḥ","n. nom. + m. loc.","mind in dream, no doubt"],["advayam ca dvaya-ābhāsam","n. nom.","non-dual and dual-appearing"],["tathā jāgrat na saṃśayaḥ","indecl. + pres. part. nom.","so in waking, no doubt"]],"lit":"\"Non-dual and dual-appearing is mind in dream, no doubt; non-dual and dual-appearing is (mind) in waking, no doubt.\"","para":"Mind in both dream and waking is non-dual in essence but dual-appearing. This is doubly affirmed: na saṃśayaḥ (no doubt) — twice.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Adds the crucial qualifier: the mind is non-dual (advayam) — there are no two minds, no real subject-object split — but it has dual appearance (dvaya-ābhāsam)."],["Sivananda","For the meditator: this is the meditational key. The mind is one and non-dual; the experienced duality is appearance."],["Krishnananda","Compare Yoga-Sūtra 1.41 (when the mind becomes stable, it becomes like a transparent gem reflecting whatever is placed on it). The Kārikā's formulation is similar but more radical."],["Nikhilananda","this verse is one of Advaita's clearest statements on the mind's nature."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.31","title":"This (apparent) duality, whatever moves-or-stays-still, is mind's seen; when mind is non-existent (in stillness), duality is not seen","sa":["मनोदृश्यमिदं द्वैतं यत्किञ्चित्सचराचरम् ।","मनसो ह्यमनीभावे द्वैतं नैवोपलभ्यते ॥ ३१॥"],"iast":["mano-dṛśyam idaṃ dvaitaṃ yat kiñcit sacarācaram |","manaso hy amanī-bhāve dvaitaṃ naivopalabhyate || 31 ||"],"words":[["manaḥ-dṛśyam idam dvaitam","n. nom. + n. nom.","this duality is mind-seen"],["yat kiñcit sa-cara-acaram","indecl.","whatever (is) with-moving-and-still"],["manasaḥ hi a-manī-bhāve","n. gen. + m. loc.","in the un-mind-being of mind"],["dvaitam na eva upalabhyate","n. nom. + indecl. + 3 sg.","duality is not perceived"]],"lit":"\"This duality, whatever moving-and-still, is mind-seen. When mind is in un-mind-being (= silence), duality is not perceived at all.\"","para":"All duality (moving and stationary, every cosmic distinction) is mind-perceived. When mind enters a-manī-bhāva — un-mind-being (the silent state) — duality is no longer perceived.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Critical practical claim: duality is not the natural state; it is the mind-perceived state. The mind's silence removes duality."],["Sivananda","For the meditator: silencing the mind is not just inner peace; it is the dissolution of perceived duality. This is the soteriological promise."],["Krishnananda","a-manī-bhāva — 'un-mind-being' — is a technical term used elsewhere by Gauḍapāda. It is the meditative state where the mind has stopped its modifying activity."],["Nikhilananda","this verse is the practical heart of the Kārikā. Silence the mind; duality vanishes."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.32","title":"When by Self-truth-recognition (one) does not imagine, then he attains un-mind-ness; in the absence of grasped (object), there is no grasping","sa":["आत्मसत्यानुबोधेन न सङ्कल्पयते यदा ।","अमनस्तां तदा याति ग्राह्याभावे तदग्रहम् ॥ ३२॥"],"iast":["ātma-satyānubodhena na saṅkalpayate yadā |","amanastāṃ tadā yāti grāhyābhāve tad agraham || 32 ||"],"words":[["ātma-satya-anubodhena","n. instr. sg.","by Self-truth-recognition"],["na saṅkalpayate yadā","indecl. + 3 sg. + indecl.","when one does not imagine"],["a-manas-tām tadā yāti","f. acc. + indecl.","(he) then attains un-mind-ness"],["grāhya-abhāve tat a-graham","n. loc. + n. nom.","in the absence of grasped, the not-grasped"]],"lit":"\"When by Self-truth-recognition one does not imagine, then he attains un-mind-ness — in the absence of grasped (object), (there is) the not-grasping (subject).\"","para":"By recognizing the truth of the Self, one ceases to imagine. The result is un-mind-state. The mechanism: when there is no grāhya (object), there is no graha (grasping).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The verse's deep pedagogy: subject-object collapse occurs together. When the object disappears (because recognized as Self-projection), the subject's grasping also disappears."],["Sivananda","For the meditator: do not first try to silence the subject. Recognize the object's identity-with-Self; the subject's grasping ceases automatically."],["Krishnananda","ātma-satya-anubodha — 'Self-truth-recognition' — is the catalyst. Not effort, but recognition."],["Nikhilananda","this verse provides the precise mechanism by which advaita-recognition produces meditative silence."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.33","title":"Imagination-free, unborn knowledge — they call (it) not-different-from-the-knowable; the knowable Brahman is unborn, eternal; by the unborn the unborn is fully cognized","sa":["अकल्पकमजं ज्ञानं ज्ञेयाभिन्नं प्रचक्षते ।","ब्रह्मज्ञेयमजं नित्यमजेनाजं विबुध्यते ॥ ३३॥"],"iast":["akalpakam ajaṃ jñānaṃ jñeyābhinnaṃ pracakṣate |","brahma-jñeyam ajaṃ nityam ajenājaṃ vibudhyate || 33 ||"],"words":[["a-kalpakam ajam jñānam","n. nom. (3 attrs)","imagination-free, unborn knowledge"],["jñeya-abhinnam pracakṣate","n. nom. + 3 pl.","(they) call (it) not-different-from-knowable"],["brahma-jñeyam ajam nityam","n. nom. (3 attrs)","Brahman, the knowable, unborn, eternal"],["ajena ajam vibudhyate","n. instr. + n. acc. + 3 sg.","by the unborn the unborn is cognized"]],"lit":"\"They call (it) imagination-free, unborn knowledge, not-different-from-the-knowable. Brahman — the knowable — is unborn, eternal; by the unborn the unborn is cognized.\"","para":"Knowledge of Brahman: unborn (not produced in time), imagination-free, and identical with the knowable (not different from its object). Brahman itself is unborn and eternal. The unborn-Self cognizes the unborn-Brahman.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Important advaita formulation: jñāna and jñeya are non-different. Knowledge of Brahman is not a knowing that takes Brahman as its object; it is Brahman cognising Brahman."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: do not look for jñāna as a thing apart from Brahman. The jñāna IS Brahman in self-cognition."],["Krishnananda","ajenājaṃ vibudhyate — 'by the unborn, the unborn is cognized' — is one of the Kārikā's most compact formulations of advaita-knowledge: a self-recognition with no real second."],["Nikhilananda","this verse is the doctrinal high-point of the Advaita-prakaraṇa."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.34","title":"The (mind) of the steady-thinker, restrained, imagination-free — its activity, however, is to be known; in deep sleep (the activity is) other, not equal to that","sa":["निगृहीतस्य मनसो निर्विकल्पस्य धीमतः ।","प्रचारः स तु विज्ञेयः सुषुप्तेऽन्यो न तत्समः ॥ ३४॥"],"iast":["nigṛhītasya manaso nirvikalpasya dhīmataḥ |","pracāraḥ sa tu vijñeyaḥ suṣupte'nyo na tat-samaḥ || 34 ||"],"words":[["nigṛhītasya manasaḥ","n. gen.","of the restrained mind"],["nirvikalpasya dhīmataḥ","n. gen. + m. gen.","of the imagination-free, steady-thinker"],["pracāraḥ saḥ tu vijñeyaḥ","m. nom. + indecl. + gerundive","(its) activity is to be known"],["suṣupte anyaḥ na tat-samaḥ","m. loc. + m. nom.","in deep sleep (activity is) other, not equal"]],"lit":"\"Of the restrained, imagination-free mind of the steady-thinker — its activity is to be known. (In) deep sleep (activity is) other, not equal to that.\"","para":"The advaita-meditator's mind (restrained, imagination-free, of the steady-thinker) has a pracāra (subtle activity) that must be known. This is different from the activity in deep sleep — they are not equal.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Important distinction. The advaita-recognition is not deep sleep (a recurring concern of the Kārikā). The meditative state has its own subtle activity; deep sleep has another kind. They must not be confused."],["Sivananda","For the meditator: notice the qualitative difference between meditative recognition and deep sleep. If you cannot tell the difference, the recognition has not occurred."],["Krishnananda","This is the Kārikā's third or fourth warning against confusing turīya/jñāna with suṣupti. The persistence of the warning shows how common the confusion is."],["Nikhilananda","this verse provides the practical discrimination needed to evaluate one's own meditative state."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.35","title":"In deep sleep that (mind) dissolves; restrained, it does not dissolve. That alone is the fearless Brahman, knowledge-light, all-around","sa":["लीयते हि सुषुप्ते तन्निगृहीतं न लीयते ।","तदेव निर्भयं ब्रह्म ज्ञानालोकं समन्ततः ॥ ३५॥"],"iast":["līyate hi suṣupte tan nigṛhītaṃ na līyate |","tad eva nirbhayaṃ brahma jñānālokaṃ samantataḥ || 35 ||"],"words":[["līyate hi suṣupte tat","3 sg. + m. loc. + n. nom.","(mind) dissolves in deep sleep"],["nigṛhītam na līyate","n. nom. + neg.","(but) restrained, does not dissolve"],["tat eva nirbhayam brahma","n. nom. (4 attrs)","that alone is fearless Brahman"],["jñāna-ālokam samantataḥ","n. nom. + indecl.","knowledge-light, all-around"]],"lit":"\"In deep sleep (mind) dissolves; restrained, (mind) does not dissolve. That alone is the fearless Brahman, knowledge-light, all-around.\"","para":"Distinction completed. In deep sleep, the mind dissolves (becomes unconscious); in restrained meditation, the mind does not dissolve (remains conscious-but-still). The latter is Brahman itself — fearless, light-of-knowledge, pervading.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The Kārikā's clearest distinction between suṣupti and brahma-jñāna. Suṣupti = mind dissolves. Brahma-jñāna = mind restrained but undissolved."],["Sivananda","For the meditator: the discipline is to restrain the mind without letting it dissolve. The restraint produces brahma-jñāna; dissolution produces only deep sleep."],["Krishnananda","jñāna-ālokam samantataḥ — 'knowledge-light all-around' — is the positive description of the realised state. Not absence (as in sleep) but pervasive luminous knowing."],["Nikhilananda","this verse contains the central practical claim of the Kārikā: brahma-jñāna is conscious silence, not unconscious dissolution."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.36","title":"Unborn, sleepless, dreamless, nameless, formless; one-time-shining, all-knowing — there is no relation (it has) at all","sa":["अजमनिद्रमस्वप्नमनामकमरूपकम् ।","सकृद्विभातं सर्वज्ञं नोपचारः कथञ्चन ॥ ३६॥"],"iast":["ajam anidram asvapnam anāmakam arūpakam |","sakṛd-vibhātaṃ sarva-jñaṃ nopacāraḥ kathañcana || 36 ||"],"words":[["a-jam an-idram a-svapnam","n. nom. (3 attrs)","unborn, sleepless, dreamless"],["a-nāmakam a-rūpakam","n. nom. (2 attrs)","nameless, formless"],["sakṛt-vibhātam sarva-jñam","n. nom. (2 attrs)","one-time-shining, all-knowing"],["na upacāraḥ kathañcana","indecl. + m. nom.","no figurative-relation (applies) at all"]],"lit":"\"Unborn, sleepless, dreamless, nameless, formless; one-time-shining, all-knowing — no figurative-relation (applies) at all.\"","para":"Seven attributes of Brahman/turīya: unborn, sleepless, dreamless, nameless, formless, single-shining, all-knowing. No upacāra (figurative speech) applies — these are not metaphors.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> sakṛd-vibhātam ('one-time-shining' or 'once-and-for-all-shining') is striking. Brahman shines once-and-for-all; it does not repeatedly arise or fall."],["Sivananda","For the meditator: rest in the recognition that Brahman is sakṛd-vibhāta — already-and-always-shining. Your task is not to make it shine; it is to recognize."],["Krishnananda","nopacāraḥ kathañcana — 'no figurative-relation at all' — is forceful: these characterizations are not metaphors. They literally describe Brahman."],["Nikhilananda","this verse provides a concentrated catalogue of Brahman-attributes that becomes standard in later Advaita."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.37","title":"Free of all expression, freed-from-all-thought, deeply-calm, one-time-light, motionless samādhi, fearless","sa":["सर्वाभिलापविगतः सर्वचिन्तासमुत्थितः ।","सुप्रशान्तः सकृज्ज्योतिः समाधिरचलोऽभयः ॥ ३७॥"],"iast":["sarvābhilāpa-vigataḥ sarva-cintā-samutthitaḥ |","supraśāntaḥ sakṛj-jyotiḥ samādhir acalo'bhayaḥ || 37 ||"],"words":[["sarva-abhilāpa-vigataḥ","m. nom. bv.","free of all expression"],["sarva-cintā-samutthitaḥ","m. nom. bv.","risen from all thought"],["su-praśāntaḥ sakṛt-jyotiḥ","m. nom. (2 attrs)","deeply calm, one-light"],["samādhiḥ acalaḥ abhayaḥ","m. nom. (3 attrs)","samādhi, motionless, fearless"]],"lit":"\"Free of all expression, risen above all thought; deeply calm, one-light, samādhi, motionless, fearless.\"","para":"Continuing characterization. Seven more attributes of the realised state. Note samādhi is one of them — the realised state IS samādhi, not just a state attained through samādhi.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Together with 3.36, this verse catalogues fourteen attributes of the realised state."],["Sivananda","For the meditator: practice the recognition of each attribute. Notice when expression-freedom occurs, when thought-rising occurs, etc. The recognition trains the meditator to recognize the realised state."],["Krishnananda","samādhiḥ acalaḥ — 'motionless samādhi' — emphasizes that this is not a transitory state but a steady abiding."],["Nikhilananda","this verse continues the catalogue from 3.36. The full list (14 attributes) is the most comprehensive single-verse characterization in the Kārikā."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.38","title":"Where no grasping (occurs), no abandonment, no thinking — there knowledge stationed-in-Self, reached-equality, unborn","sa":["ग्रहो न तत्र नोत्सर्गश्चिन्ता यत्र न विद्यते ।","आत्मसंस्थं तदा ज्ञानमजाति समतां गतम् ॥ ३८॥"],"iast":["graho na tatra notsargaś cintā yatra na vidyate |","ātma-saṃsthaṃ tadā jñānam ajāti samatāṃ gatam || 38 ||"],"words":[["grahaḥ na tatra na utsargaḥ","m. nom.","no grasping, no abandonment there"],["cintā yatra na vidyate","f. nom. + 3 sg.","where no thought is found"],["ātma-saṃstham tadā jñānam","n. nom. + indecl.","then, knowledge stationed in Self"],["a-jāti samatām gatam","n. nom. + pp.","(is) unborn, reached-equality"]],"lit":"\"Where no grasping, no abandoning, no thought (is found) — then knowledge (is) stationed-in-Self, unborn, reached-equality.\"","para":"In the state without grasping, abandoning, or thinking, jñāna is stationed in the Self — unborn, equalized.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> For the meditator: the three-fold cessation (graha-utsarga-cintā) is the gateway. When all three are absent simultaneously, ātma-saṃstha-jñāna arises."],["Sivananda","graha (grasping) and utsarga (abandoning) are paired — wanting and rejecting. Both must cease. Cintā (thinking) must also cease."],["Krishnananda","ātma-saṃstham — 'stationed-in-Self' — is the precise locus of the realised state. Not floating, not directed outward, but in the Self."],["Nikhilananda","this verse provides the operational definition of the realised meditative state."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.39","title":"Touchless-yoga, indeed, by name; difficult-to-see for all yogins; the yogins fear from this; in the fearless, (they) see fear","sa":["अस्पर्शयोगो वै नाम दुर्दर्शः सर्वयोगिभिः ।","योगिनो बिभ्यति ह्यस्मादभये भयदर्शिनः ॥ ३९॥"],"iast":["asparśa-yogo vai nāma durdarśaḥ sarva-yogibhiḥ |","yogino bibhyati hyasmād abhaye bhaya-darśinaḥ || 39 ||"],"words":[["a-sparśa-yogaḥ vai nāma","m. nom. + indecl.","touchless-yoga, indeed, by name"],["dur-darśaḥ sarva-yogibhiḥ","m. nom. + m. instr. pl.","hard-to-see for all yogins"],["yoginaḥ bibhyati hi asmāt","m. nom. pl. + 3 pl. + indecl.","yogins fear from this"],["a-bhaye bhaya-darśinaḥ","m. loc. + m. nom. pl.","in the fearless, fear-seeing (ones)"]],"lit":"\"(This is) the touchless-yoga, by name; hard-to-see by all (other) yogins. Yogins fear from this; they see fear in the fearless.\"","para":"The famous introduction of asparśa-yoga ('touchless yoga') — the Kārikā's signature meditation. It is hard-to-see (durdarśa) for ordinary yogins. They actually fear it — they see fear in what is in fact fearless.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> asparśa-yoga is Gauḍapāda's distinctive contribution. \"Touchless\" because consciousness in this yoga touches nothing — neither object, nor image, nor concept, nor thought. The standard yoga touches dhyāna-objects; this yoga touches nothing."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: notice the warning. Ordinary yogins fear the touchless state because it appears as void to them. The advaitin recognizes it as fearless. Your fear or non-fear of the void is the test."],["Krishnananda","durdarśa — 'hard-to-see' — emphasizes the difficulty. The asparśa-yoga is the most demanding of contemplative disciplines."],["Nikhilananda","this verse introduces asparśa-yoga, the Kārikā's most distinctive contemplative innovation. Later Advaita treats this as the canonical meditation method."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.40","title":"All yogins' fearlessness depends on mind's restraint; (then) destruction of pain, awakening, imperishable peace","sa":["मनसो निग्रहायत्तमभयं सर्वयोगिनाम् ।","दुःखक्षयः प्रबोधश्चाप्यक्षया शान्तिरेव च ॥ ४०॥"],"iast":["manaso nigrahāyattam abhayaṃ sarva-yoginām |","duḥkha-kṣayaḥ prabodhaś cāpy akṣayā śāntir eva ca || 40 ||"],"words":[["manasaḥ nigraha-āyattam","n. gen. + n. nom.","depending on mind-restraint"],["a-bhayam sarva-yoginām","n. nom. + m. gen. pl.","fearlessness of all yogins"],["duḥkha-kṣayaḥ prabodhaḥ ca api","m. nom.","destruction of pain, and awakening"],["a-kṣayā śāntiḥ eva ca","f. nom. + indecl.","and imperishable peace"]],"lit":"\"The fearlessness of all yogins depends on mind-restraint; (then) destruction of pain, awakening, and imperishable peace.\"","para":"All yogic achievement depends on manas-nigraha (mind-restraint). The fruits: duḥkha-kṣaya (end of pain), prabodha (awakening), akṣayā śānti (imperishable peace).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Practical and concentrated. The single condition (manas-nigraha) yields three results."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: notice the singularity of the prerequisite. Not many disciplines, not many methods — one: mind-restraint. Master this; the rest follows."],["Krishnananda","akṣayā śāntiḥ — 'imperishable peace' — is the highest fruit. Not transient peace; the kind that cannot decay."],["Nikhilananda","this verse is the practical heart of the asparśa-yoga teaching."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.41","title":"As the (filling of) the ocean by the tip of a kuśa-blade with single drops, so should mind-restraint (be), without weariness","sa":["उत्सेक उदधेर्यद्वत्कुशाग्रेणैकबिन्दुना ।","मनसो निग्रहस्तद्वद्भवेदपरिखेदतः ॥ ४१॥"],"iast":["utseka udadher yadvat kuśāgreṇaika-bindunā |","manaso nigrahas tadvad bhaved aparikhedataḥ || 41 ||"],"words":[["utsekaḥ udadheḥ yadvat","m. nom. + m. gen.","as the filling of the ocean"],["kuśa-agreṇa eka-bindunā","m. instr. + m. instr.","by the kuśa-tip with one drop"],["manasaḥ nigrahaḥ tadvat","n. gen. + m. nom.","so mind-restraint"],["bhavet aparikhedataḥ","3 sg. opt. + indecl.","should be, without weariness"]],"lit":"\"As the filling of the ocean by single drops with a kuśa-blade tip — so should mind-restraint be, without weariness.\"","para":"Famous analogy. Filling the ocean drop-by-drop with the tip of a kuśa-grass blade — slow, persistent, never giving up. So with mind-restraint: drop by drop, without weariness.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The Kārikā's most-cited image of disciplined patience. The asparśa-yoga is not a sudden achievement; it is the patient drop-by-drop work of years."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: do not expect immediate mastery of asparśa-yoga. The image is the kuśa-tip filling the ocean — slow but sure if not abandoned."],["Krishnananda","aparikhedataḥ — 'without weariness' — is the critical condition. The drop-by-drop work requires non-fatigue, not speed."],["Nikhilananda","this verse provides the Kārikā's most-famous practice-aphorism on patience in meditation."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.42","title":"Restrain (the mind) distracted toward enjoyment-objects by (proper) means, and well-engaged in dissolution; as enjoyment is, so dissolution (must be guarded)","sa":["उपायेन निगृह्णीयाद्विक्षिप्तं कामभोगयोः ।","सुप्रसन्नं लये चैव यथा कामो लयस्तथा ॥ ४२॥"],"iast":["upāyena nigṛhṇīyād vikṣiptaṃ kāma-bhogayoḥ |","suprasannaṃ laye caiva yathā kāmo layas tathā || 42 ||"],"words":[["upāyena nigṛhṇīyāt","m. instr. + 3 sg. opt.","by means, let one restrain"],["vikṣiptam kāma-bhogayoḥ","n. acc. + m. gen. du.","the distracted (mind) in desire-enjoyment"],["su-prasannam laye ca eva","n. acc. + m. loc.","well-clear in dissolution"],["yathā kāmaḥ layaḥ tathā","indecl. + m. nom.","as desire (is suspect), so dissolution"]],"lit":"\"Let one restrain by (proper) means the distracted mind in desire-enjoyment, and the well-clear (mind) in dissolution. As (desire is to be guarded), so is dissolution.\"","para":"Two opposite errors in meditation: (a) vikṣipta — distracted by desires; (b) laya — dissolved (drifting into sleep-like quiescence). Both must be restrained. As the meditator guards against desire, he must equally guard against laya.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Critical practical wisdom. The Kārikā recognises that meditation can fail in two directions: excitement and dullness. Both are obstacles."],["Sivananda","For the meditator: when your mind is desire-driven, restrain it; when it is laya-bound (sinking into sleep-like quiescence), also restrain. Both extremes are away from the asparśa-yoga state."],["Krishnananda","yathā kāmo layas tathā — 'as desire, so dissolution' — equates the two. Neither is the goal; both must be transcended."],["Nikhilananda","this verse is among the Kārikā's most-cited on the dangers of meditation. The distinction between healthy quiescence and dull laya is essential."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.43","title":"Recalling all (existence) as suffering, let (one) turn from desire-enjoyment; recalling all as unborn, let (one) not see the (apparently) born","sa":["दुःखं सर्वमनुस्मृत्य कामभोगान्निवर्तयेत् ।","अजं सर्वमनुस्मृत्य जातं नैव तु पश्यति ॥ ४३॥"],"iast":["duḥkhaṃ sarvam anusmṛtya kāma-bhogān nivartayet |","ajaṃ sarvam anusmṛtya jātaṃ naiva tu paśyati || 43 ||"],"words":[["duḥkham sarvam anusmṛtya","n. acc. + ger.","recalling all as suffering"],["kāma-bhogāt nivartayet","m. abl. + 3 sg. opt.","let one turn from desire-enjoyment"],["a-jam sarvam anusmṛtya","n. acc. + ger.","recalling all as unborn"],["jātam na eva tu paśyati","n. acc. + indecl.","(one) does not see the (apparently) born"]],"lit":"\"Recalling all (existence) as suffering, let one turn from desire-enjoyment; recalling all as unborn, one does not see the (apparently) born (at all).\"","para":"Two contemplative recallings: (a) recall the universal suffering — this turns the mind from desire; (b) recall the universal unbornness — this prevents seeing apparent origination.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> For the meditator: these are two complementary practices. The first cuts attachment; the second cuts conceptual error. Both together produce mind-restraint."],["Sivananda","duḥkham sarvam — 'all is suffering' — is technically Buddhist-sounding but here used in service of advaita. The recognition motivates withdrawal from desire-bondage."],["Krishnananda","ajaṃ sarvam — 'all is unborn' — is the Kārikā's signature. To see all as unborn is to see ajāti — to see the absence of origination."],["Nikhilananda","these are the two complementary contemplations of asparśa-yoga."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.44","title":"In dissolution, awaken (the mind); distracted, calm (it) again; (with) defilement, recognise (it); (when) reached equipoise, do not disturb","sa":["लये सम्बोधयेच्चित्तं विक्षिप्तं शमयेत्पुनः ।","सकषायं विजानीयात्समप्राप्तं न चालयेत् ॥ ४४॥"],"iast":["laye sambodhayec cittaṃ vikṣiptaṃ śamayet punaḥ |","sakaṣāyaṃ vijānīyāt samaprāptaṃ na cālayet || 44 ||"],"words":[["laye sambodhayet cittam","m. loc. + 3 sg. + n. acc.","in dissolution, let one awaken the mind"],["vikṣiptam śamayet punaḥ","n. acc. + 3 sg. + indecl.","the distracted, let one calm again"],["sa-kaṣāyam vijānīyāt","n. acc. + 3 sg. opt.","(with) defilement, let one know"],["sama-prāptam na cālayet","n. acc. + 3 sg.","the reached-equipoise, let one not disturb"]],"lit":"\"In dissolution, let (one) awaken the mind; the distracted, let (one) calm again; defilement, let (one) recognise; the reached-equipoise, let (one) not disturb.\"","para":"Four-fold pratipakṣa — counter-application. Four states of mind, four responses: laya (sleep-like) → wake; vikṣipta (distracted) → calm; sakaṣāya (subtly defiled) → notice; sama-prāpta (equipoised) → don't disturb.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> One of the Kārikā's most-precise practical instructions. Each meditative-mind-state has its proper response. The meditator must be skilled in all four."],["Sivananda","For the meditator: notice your current state. Each requires its own response. Trying to calm the laya (which needs waking) or wake the vikṣipta (which needs calming) is counterproductive."],["Krishnananda","sa-kaṣāya — 'with subtle defilement' — is the trickiest. The mind appears calm but contains seeds of disturbance. Recognising this prevents premature trust in the state."],["Nikhilananda","this verse is the most practical instruction in the Kārikā's section on asparśa-yoga."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.45","title":"Let (one) not taste pleasure there; by prajñā become detached; with steady, motionless (mind), let (one) be one-pointed by effort","sa":["नाऽऽस्वादयेत्सुखं तत्र निःसङ्गः प्रज्ञया भवेत् ।","निश्चलं निश्चरच्चित्तमेकीकुर्यात्प्रयत्नतः ॥ ४५॥"],"iast":["nāsvādayet sukhaṃ tatra niḥsaṅgaḥ prajñayā bhavet |","niścalaṃ niścarac cittam ekī kuryāt prayatnataḥ || 45 ||"],"words":[["na āsvādayet sukham tatra","indecl. + 3 sg. + n. acc.","let one not taste pleasure there"],["niḥ-saṅgaḥ prajñayā bhavet","m. nom. + f. instr.","let one be detached by prajñā"],["niścalam niścarat cittam","n. acc. (2 attrs)","the steady, motionless mind"],["ekī kuryāt prayatnataḥ","3 sg. opt. + indecl.","let one make one-pointed by effort"]],"lit":"\"Let one not taste pleasure there (in meditative bliss); by prajñā, let one be detached; the steady, motionless mind — let (one) make (it) one-pointed by effort.\"","para":"Subtle warning. Meditation produces bliss; the meditator may begin to taste it (āsvādana) — savoring the experience. This is itself an attachment. The mature meditator detaches even from meditative bliss.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> For the seeker: when meditation becomes blissful, do not 'enjoy' it. Detach from it by prajñā. Steady the mind without savoring its sweet states."],["Sivananda","āsvādana — 'tasting, savoring' — is the precise word. The danger is not the bliss but the relishing of it."],["Krishnananda","niḥsaṅgaḥ prajñayā — 'detached by prajñā' — is the means. Not suppression but wisdom-detachment."],["Nikhilananda","this verse warns against one of the subtlest traps of contemplative practice."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.46","title":"When mind neither dissolves nor is distracted again — un-trembling, without (objective) appearance — then it has become perfected Brahman","sa":["यदा न लीयते चित्तं न च विक्षिप्यते पुनः ।","अनिङ्गनमनाभासं निष्पन्नं ब्रह्म तत्तदा ॥ ४६॥"],"iast":["yadā na līyate cittaṃ na ca vikṣipyate punaḥ |","aniṅganam anābhāsaṃ niṣpannaṃ brahma tat tadā || 46 ||"],"words":[["yadā na līyate cittam","indecl. + 3 sg. + n. nom.","when the mind does not dissolve"],["na ca vikṣipyate punaḥ","neg. + 3 sg. + indecl.","nor is distracted again"],["an-iṅganam an-ābhāsam","n. nom. (2 attrs)","un-trembling, without appearance"],["niṣpannam brahma tat tadā","n. nom. (2 attrs)","perfected Brahman, that, then"]],"lit":"\"When mind neither dissolves nor is again distracted — un-trembling, without appearance — then it has become perfected Brahman.\"","para":"The asparśa-yoga's culmination. Mind reaches a state that is neither laya nor vikṣepa, neither trembling nor appearing-as-object. This is niṣpanna-brahman — perfected Brahman.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The realisation occurs when the mind has stably evaded both dangers (laya and vikṣepa) and reached the still-bright state of asparśa-yoga."],["Sivananda","For the meditator: notice when this state arrives. It is recognized by absences (no dissolution, no distraction, no trembling, no object-appearance)."],["Krishnananda","niṣpanna means 'perfected, fully ripened'. The mind is not just calm; it is perfected — it has become Brahman itself."],["Nikhilananda","this verse describes the culmination of asparśa-yoga. The next two verses celebrate the result."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.47","title":"Self-stationed, peaceful, fully-extinguished, unspeakable, supreme happiness; unborn, by unborn-knowable, all-knowing — they call (it)","sa":["स्वस्थं शान्तं सनिर्वाणमकथ्यं सुखमुत्तमम् ।","अजमजेन ज्ञेयेन सर्वज्ञं परिचक्षते ॥ ४७॥"],"iast":["svasthaṃ śāntaṃ sanirvāṇam akathyaṃ sukham uttamam |","ajam ajena jñeyena sarva-jñaṃ paricakṣate || 47 ||"],"words":[["sva-stham śāntam sanirvāṇam","n. nom. (3 attrs)","Self-stationed, peaceful, fully extinguished"],["a-kathyam sukham uttamam","n. nom. (3 attrs)","unspeakable, supreme happiness"],["ajam ajena jñeyena","n. nom./acc. + n. instr.","unborn, by unborn knowable"],["sarva-jñam paricakṣate","n. acc. + 3 pl.","(it) is called all-knowing"]],"lit":"\"Self-stationed, peaceful, fully-extinguished, unspeakable, supreme happiness; unborn — by unborn-knowable — all-knowing, they call (it).\"","para":"Final attribute-catalogue of the realised state. Seven attributes: svastha (Self-stationed), śānta (peaceful), sanirvāṇa (fully-extinguished), akathya (unspeakable), uttama-sukha (supreme happiness), aja (unborn), sarva-jña (all-knowing). Known by unborn knowable.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> sanirvāṇa is significant — 'fully extinguished' echoes Buddhist nirvāṇa-vocabulary. Gauḍapāda uses the term in advaita-Vedantic sense: the extinguishing of mind-modifications, not of being itself."],["Sivananda","For the meditator: each attribute is a recognition-marker. Notice which is present in your meditation. The complete realised state has all seven."],["Krishnananda","sarva-jñam paricakṣate — 'they call (it) all-knowing' — closes by re-asserting the omniscience-attribute, balancing the negative attributes with this positive one."],["Nikhilananda","this verse provides the Kārikā's most-comprehensive single-verse description of the realised state."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 3.48","title":"No jīva is (ever) born; (His) origination is not found; this is that highest truth — where nothing is born","sa":["न कश्चिज्जायते जीवः सम्भवोऽस्य न विद्यते ।","एतत्तदुत्तमं सत्यं यत्र किञ्चिन्न जायते ॥ ४८॥"],"iast":["na kaścij jāyate jīvaḥ sambhavo'sya na vidyate |","etat tad uttamaṃ satyaṃ yatra kiñcin na jāyate || 48 ||"],"words":[["na kaścit jāyate jīvaḥ","neg. + m. nom. + 3 sg. + m. nom.","no jīva is born"],["sambhavaḥ asya na vidyate","m. nom. + 3 sg.","its origination is not found"],["etat tat uttamam satyam","n. nom. (2 dem. + 2 attrs)","this is that highest truth"],["yatra kiñcit na jāyate","indecl. + indef.","where nothing is born"]],"lit":"\"No jīva is (ever) born; (its) origination is not found. This is that highest truth: where nothing is born.\"","para":"The Advaita-prakaraṇa's closing statement. No jīva is born; no origination exists. THIS — that nothing is born — IS the highest truth.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The doctrinal core of the entire Kārikā compressed into a single śloka. The verse will be repeated in the Alāta-śānti prakaraṇa (4.71). It is the Kārikā's signature statement."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: this is the final teaching. Memorize it. Recite it. The recognition of this is liberation. Nothing is born, ever."],["Krishnananda","uttamam satyam — 'the highest truth' — is the verse's claim. Not one truth among others; the highest. Everything else is subsidiary."],["Nikhilananda","this verse closes the Advaita-prakaraṇa and points to the Alāta-śānti's more rigorous demonstration of ajāti."]],"notes":["End of Advaita-prakaraṇa (Kārikā 3.1-48)."]},{"ref":"Alāta-Śānti-Prakaraṇa (Kārikā 4, 100 verses)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Alāta-śānti-prakaraṇa (\"the chapter of the firebrand-quenched\") is the longest and most technically sophisticated of the four prakaraṇas. The title refers to the central image of verses 47–52: a firebrand whirled in a circle produces the appearance of a continuous wheel of fire (alāta-cakra); when the whirling stops, no wheel is seen, no wheel comes from outside, no wheel returns to anywhere. Similarly, consciousness vibrating produces appearance; when vibration stops, no appearances come from or return to anywhere — confirming non-origination. The prakaraṇa contains the Kārikā's most systematic refutation of causal realism and the most extensive elaboration of ajāti-vāda.","Note on the Buddhist parallel: this prakaraṇa contains the verses most-cited in the Gauḍapāda-as-Buddhist debate. The opening salutation (4.1) and the closing salutation (4.99–100) have been read by some scholars as Buddhist gestures; Śaṅkara reads them strictly in advaita-Vedantic sense. The reader should approach with awareness of the dispute but without prejudgment."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.1","title":"Salutation to that supreme biped, awakened by knowledge resembling ākāśa to the dharmas resembling the sky, by the knowable not-different (from knowledge)","sa":["ज्ञानेना‍ऽऽकाशकल्पेन धर्मान्यो गगनोपमान् ।","ज्ञेयाभिन्नेन सम्बुद्धस्तं वन्दे द्विपदां वरम् ॥ १॥"],"iast":["jñānenākāśa-kalpena dharmān yo gaganopamān |","jñeyābhinnena sambuddhas taṃ vande dvi-padāṃ varam || 1 ||"],"words":[["jñānena ākāśa-kalpena","n. instr. + adj.","by knowledge resembling ākāśa"],["dharmān yaḥ gagana-upamān","m. acc. pl. + adj.","(the one who) dharmas sky-comparable"],["jñeya-abhinnena sambuddhaḥ","n. instr. + m. nom.","by knowable-not-different, fully awakened"],["tam vande dvi-padām varam","m. acc. + 1 sg. + gen. pl.","him I salute, the best of bipeds"]],"lit":"\"To that one — fully awakened by knowledge resembling ākāśa (to) dharmas resembling the sky, by the knowable not-different (from knowledge) — I salute the best of bipeds.\"","para":"Opening salutation. The salutee is fully awakened (sambuddha) by knowledge that itself is sky-like (ākāśa-kalpa). The salutee is the dvi-padām vara — best of bipeds.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> This salutation has been read by some (Vidhushekhara Bhattacharya) as addressed to the Buddha; Śaṅkara reads it as addressed to the supreme guru-paraṃparā or to the Lord Nārāyaṇa. The phrase dvi-padām vara is Buddhist-resonant."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: the technicality of the salutation models the entire method — knowledge as sky, objects as sky, both inseparable. This is the prakaraṇa's epistemic stance."],["Krishnananda","jñeyābhinna — 'not-different from the knowable' — restates the Advaita doctrine of knowledge-object identity established in Kārikā 3.33."],["Nikhilananda","Nikhilananda discusses at length whether the verse is Buddhist; concludes it is in any case integrable with advaita doctrine. The doctrinal content is advaita regardless of the historical question."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.2","title":"Touchless-yoga, indeed, by name, beneficial to all beings; without dispute, without contradiction — taught (by Him): to Him I salute","sa":["अस्पर्शयोगो वै नाम सर्वसत्त्वसुखो हितः ।","अविवादोऽविरुद्धश्च देशितस्तं नमाम्यहम् ॥ २॥"],"iast":["asparśa-yogo vai nāma sarva-sattva-sukho hitaḥ |","avivādo'viruddhaś ca deśitas taṃ namāmy aham || 2 ||"],"words":[["a-sparśa-yogaḥ vai nāma","m. nom.","touchless-yoga, indeed, by name"],["sarva-sattva-sukhaḥ hitaḥ","m. nom. + m. nom.","happy and beneficial for all beings"],["a-vivādaḥ a-viruddhaḥ ca","m. nom. + m. nom.","without dispute, without contradiction"],["deśitaḥ tam namāmi aham","pp. + m. acc. + 1 sg.","taught — to him I salute"]],"lit":"\"The touchless-yoga, by name, is happy and beneficial for all beings, without dispute, without contradiction — taught (by Him): to Him I salute.\"","para":"The salutation continues, identifying asparśa-yoga (Kārikā 3.39) as the universal-beneficial, dispute-free yoga. Salutation is to its teacher.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> For the seeker: notice that the Kārikā claims its yoga is non-controversial. It contradicts no school; it is universally beneficial."],["Sivananda","Sarva-sattva-sukhaḥ — 'happy for all beings' — is a broad claim. Buddhist sattva-vocabulary, but reads coherently as Vedānta."],["Krishnananda","a-vivāda — 'without dispute' — recalls Kārikā 2.36-37 (the realised yati does not dispute). The yoga itself is dispute-free."],["Nikhilananda","this verse provides Gauḍapāda's self-description of asparśa-yoga."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.3","title":"Some disputants want existing-thing-origination, others wise-ones (want) non-existing-thing-origination, disputing each other","sa":["भूतस्य जातिमिच्छन्ति वादिनः केचिदेव हि ।","अभूतस्यापरे धीरा विवदन्तः परस्परम् ॥ ३॥"],"iast":["bhūtasya jātim icchanti vādinaḥ kecid eva hi |","abhūtasyāpare dhīrā vivadantaḥ parasparam || 3 ||"],"words":[["bhūtasya jātim icchanti","m. gen. + f. acc.","want origination of existing"],["vādinaḥ kecit eva hi","m. nom. pl.","some disputants"],["a-bhūtasya apare dhīrāḥ","m. gen. + m. nom. pl.","others, wise, (want) of non-existing"],["vivadantaḥ parasparam","m. nom. pl.","disputing each other"]],"lit":"\"Some disputants want the origination of the (already-)existing; others, considered wise, (want it) of the non-existing — disputing each other.\"","para":"Two opposing positions: sat-kārya-vāda (Sāṅkhya — the effect pre-exists in the cause) and asat-kārya-vāda (Vaiśeṣika — the effect is newly produced). They dispute mutually.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> For the seeker: notice that established schools disagree. Gauḍapāda will show below that BOTH are incoherent, leading to ajāti-vāda as the only viable position."],["Sivananda","This opens the systematic refutation of causal realism that fills the early Alāta-śānti."],["Krishnananda","bhūta/a-bhūta = existing/non-existing. The pair captures the two-fold causal-realist scheme."],["Nikhilananda","this verse introduces the systematic refutation that runs 4.3-4.40."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.4","title":"The existing is not born; the non-existing also is not born; the disputants thus, disputing, themselves declare non-origination","sa":["भूतं न जायते किञ्चिदभूतं नैव जायते ।","विवदन्तो द्वया ह्येवमजातिं ख्यापयन्ति ते ॥ ४॥"],"iast":["bhūtaṃ na jāyate kiñcid abhūtaṃ naiva jāyate |","vivadanto dvayā hy evam ajātiṃ khyāpayanti te || 4 ||"],"words":[["bhūtam na jāyate kiñcit","n. nom. + indecl.","the existing is not born at all"],["a-bhūtam na eva jāyate","n. nom.","the non-existing also is not born"],["vivadantaḥ dvayāḥ hi evam","m. nom. pl.","two disputants thus"],["a-jātim khyāpayanti te","f. acc. + 3 pl.","they declare non-origination"]],"lit":"\"The existing is not born; the non-existing also is not born. The two disputants, by disputing thus, themselves declare non-origination.\"","para":"The decisive move: the existing cannot be born (it already exists); the non-existing cannot be born (because it doesn't exist to be born). Therefore neither can be born — and the two parties' very dispute confirms this conclusion.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Brilliant rhetorical inversion. The two opposing causal-realist positions, by each denying the other, jointly establish ajāti."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: this is the ajāti argument in its most elegant form. Memorize it. It is the philosophical heart of the Kārikā."],["Krishnananda","Compare Mūlamadhyamaka-Kārikā 1.1 (\"not from self, not from other, not from both, not without cause\"). The structural similarity to Madhyamaka is what drives the Buddhist-influence interpretation."],["Nikhilananda","this verse is the locus classicus of the ajāti argument. Memorisable in form, decisive in content."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.5","title":"We agree with their declared non-origination; we do not dispute with them; the non-dispute, know","sa":["ख्याप्यमानामजातिं तैरनुमोदामहे वयम् ।","विवदामो न तैः सार्धमविवादं निबोधत ॥ ५॥"],"iast":["khyāpyamānām ajātiṃ tair anumodāmahe vayam |","vivadāmo na taiḥ sārdham avivādaṃ nibodhata || 5 ||"],"words":[["khyāpyamānām a-jātim taiḥ","f. acc. + m. instr. pl.","the non-origination declared by them"],["anumodāmahe vayam","1 pl. + 1 nom. pl.","we agree with"],["vivadāmaḥ na taiḥ sārdham","1 pl. + m. instr. + indecl.","we do not dispute with them"],["a-vivādam nibodhata","m. acc. + impv. 2 pl.","know the non-dispute"]],"lit":"\"We agree with the non-origination declared by them; we do not dispute with them — know the non-dispute.\"","para":"Gauḍapāda's irenic stance: he agrees with what his opponents' positions inadvertently establish (ajāti). He doesn't fight them; he extracts ajāti from their disputing.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Compare 3.17-18 (the advaita position does not contradict; it is found in the disputants' own admissions)."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: do not approach philosophical opposition as combat. The truth emerges from the contradictions; the seeker need not enter the dispute."],["Krishnananda","a-vivāda — 'non-dispute' — is one of the Kārikā's most distinctive postures. The advaitin does not dispute."],["Nikhilananda","this verse establishes the irenic methodology of the Kārikā."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.6","title":"The disputants want birth for the unborn dharma; the unborn, immortal dharma — how would it go to mortality?","sa":["अजातस्यैव धर्मस्य जातिमिच्छन्ति वादिनः ।","अजातो ह्यमृतो धर्मो मर्त्यतां कथमेष्यति ॥ ६॥"],"iast":["ajātasyaiva dharmasya jātim icchanti vādinaḥ |","ajāto hy amṛto dharmo martyatāṃ kathameṣyati || 6 ||"],"words":[["a-jātasya eva dharmasya","m. gen.","of the unborn dharma itself"],["jātim icchanti vādinaḥ","f. acc. + m. nom. pl.","the disputants want birth"],["a-jātaḥ hi a-mṛtaḥ dharmaḥ","m. nom. (3 attrs)","the unborn, immortal dharma"],["martyatām katham eṣyati","f. acc. + indecl. + 3 sg. fut.","how would (it) go to mortality?"]],"lit":"\"The disputants want birth for the unborn dharma; (but) how would the unborn, immortal dharma go to mortality?\"","para":"Verbal repetition of Kārikā 3.20 (with dharma in place of bhāva). The repetition is significant: Gauḍapāda restates the core ajāti-argument in a slightly different formulation as he reopens the systematic critique.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> For the seeker: the verse appears twice. This is deliberate. The argument is so important it bears double emphasis."],["Sivananda","dharma here means roughly 'being, thing, reality' — the same generic sense as the Buddhist dharma in some contexts. Vedic use is broader."],["Krishnananda","Same critique as 3.20: real birth would imply real mortality, which contradicts the immortality scripture grants."],["Nikhilananda","the repetition emphasizes the core argument before its expanded development."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.7","title":"Immortal does not become mortal, nor mortal immortal; the otherwise-being of nature does not occur in any way","sa":["न भवत्यमृतं मर्त्यं न मर्त्यममृतं तथा ।","प्रकृतेरन्यथाभावो न कथञ्चिद् भविष्यति ॥ ७॥"],"iast":["na bhavaty amṛtaṃ martyaṃ na martyam amṛtaṃ tathā |","prakṛter anyathābhāvo na kathañcid bhaviṣyati || 7 ||"],"words":[["na bhavati a-mṛtam martyam","neg. + 3 sg. + n. nom.","immortal does not become mortal"],["na martyam a-mṛtam tathā","neg. + n. nom.","nor mortal immortal"],["prakṛteḥ anyathā-bhāvaḥ","f. gen. + m. nom.","the otherwise-being of nature"],["na kathañcit bhaviṣyati","indecl. + 3 sg. fut.","will not in any way be"]],"lit":"\"Immortal does not become mortal; mortal does not become immortal; the otherwise-being of nature will not (be) in any way.\"","para":"Verbatim repetition of Kārikā 3.21. Same premise: nature does not transform into its opposite.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> For the seeker: notice the repetition. The same argument is being restated."],["Sivananda","prakṛter anyathā-bhāvaḥ — 'otherwise-being of nature' — is the technical formulation: intrinsic nature does not transform."],["Krishnananda","Identical with 3.21; the doctrinal premise is the same."],["Nikhilananda","4.7 = 3.21 verbatim. The repetition shows the doctrinal centrality of this principle."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.8","title":"How can the immortal-by-nature, if its dharma goes to mortality, stand immovable as immortal by means of (something) made?","sa":["स्वभावेनामृतो यस्य धर्मो गच्छति मर्त्यताम् ।","कृतकेनामृतस्तस्य कथं स्थास्यति निश्चलः ॥ ८॥"],"iast":["svabhāvenāmṛto yasya dharmo gacchati martyatām |","kṛtakenāmṛtas tasya kathaṃ sthāsyati niścalaḥ || 8 ||"],"words":[["sva-bhāvena a-mṛtaḥ yasya","m. instr. + m. nom.","whose dharma immortal by nature"],["dharmaḥ gacchati martyatām","m. nom. + 3 sg. + f. acc.","(if) goes to mortality"],["kṛtakena a-mṛtaḥ tasya","m. instr. + m. nom.","by something made, his immortal"],["katham sthāsyati niścalaḥ","indecl. + 3 sg. + m. nom.","how will (it) stand immovable?"]],"lit":"\"If the dharma whose nature is immortal goes to mortality — by something made (i.e., by anything caused), how will that (Self) stand immovable as immortal?\"","para":"Verbatim of Kārikā 3.22 (bhāva → dharma). Same argument: immortal nature cannot stand-firm-as-immortal if susceptible to transformation.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> For the seeker: the immortal must be intrinsically immortal, not conditionally immortal. Recognize this stability."],["Sivananda","This is the doctrinal foundation against any pariṇāma-vāda (Sāṅkhya's transformation doctrine and similar positions)."],["Krishnananda","= 3.22 verbatim. The systematic restatement at 4.6-8 is for the new prakaraṇa's structure."],["Nikhilananda","the restatement of 3.20-22 at the opening of Alāta-śānti is methodologically deliberate."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.9","title":"The prakṛti is to be known as that which does not abandon its own-nature: (it is) sāṃsiddhikī, svābhāvikī, sahajā, akṛtā","sa":["साꣳसिद्धिकी स्वाभाविकी सहजा अकृता च या ।","प्रकृतिः सेति विज्ञेया स्वभावं न जहाति या ॥ ९॥"],"iast":["sāṃsiddhikī svābhāvikī sahajā akṛtā ca yā |","prakṛtiḥ seti vijñeyā svabhāvaṃ na jahāti yā || 9 ||"],"words":[["sāṃsiddhikī svābhāvikī","f. nom. (2 attrs)","fully-perfect, natural"],["sahajā a-kṛtā ca yā","f. nom.","innate, not-made"],["prakṛtiḥ sā iti vijñeyā","f. nom. + gerundive","she is to be known as prakṛti"],["svabhāvam na jahāti yā","m. acc. + 3 sg.","(which) does not abandon own-nature"]],"lit":"\"That which is fully-perfect, natural, innate, not-made — and does not abandon its own-nature — she is to be known as prakṛti.\"","para":"Defines prakṛti (here: intrinsic nature, not Sāṅkhya's prakṛti): four-fold qualified — sāṃsiddhikī (fully perfect), svābhāvikī (natural), sahajā (innate), akṛtā (not-made). And the criterion: prakṛti is what does not abandon its own-nature.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Critical definition. The intrinsic nature of anything is defined as that-which-does-not-abandon-itself. By this criterion: the Self's immortality is its prakṛti."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: identify the prakṛti of each thing by this criterion. What doesn't abandon itself? That is the prakṛti."],["Krishnananda","This is a strong realism about prakṛti within the larger anti-pariṇāma framework: while there is no real birth, there is an intrinsic nature that is preserved."],["Nikhilananda","this verse defines the technical sense of prakṛti the Kārikā uses."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.10","title":"All dharmas are by their nature free of old age and death; those who want (them to come to) old age and death fall (from that nature) by that thinking","sa":["जरामरणनिर्मुक्ताः सर्वे धर्माः स्वभावतः ।","जरामरणमिच्छन्तश्च्यवन्ते तन्मनीषया ॥ १०॥"],"iast":["jarā-maraṇa-nirmuktāḥ sarve dharmāḥ svabhāvataḥ |","jarā-maraṇam icchantaś cyavante tan-manīṣayā || 10 ||"],"words":[["jarā-maraṇa-nirmuktāḥ","m. nom. pl. bv.","free from old-age-and-death"],["sarve dharmāḥ svabhāvataḥ","m. nom. pl. + indecl.","all dharmas, by nature"],["jarā-maraṇam icchantaḥ","n. acc. + m. nom. pl.","wanting old-age-and-death"],["cyavante tat-manīṣayā","3 pl. + f. instr.","(they) fall by that thinking"]],"lit":"\"All dharmas are, by their nature, free of old-age-and-death; those who want (them) to have old-age-and-death fall by that very thinking.\"","para":"All dharmas (= everything) is by intrinsic nature ageless and deathless. Those who imagine otherwise — who attribute aging and death to dharmas — fall away from this recognition by their own thinking.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> For the seeker: the recognition is not granted by others; it is one's own thinking that determines whether one stays in the immortal recognition or falls from it."],["Sivananda","cyavante — 'fall' — is the same verb used in Vedic texts for the post-svarga descent. Here it is used metaphorically: those who think of mortality fall from the immortal-recognition."],["Krishnananda","This verse moves from 'the Self does not die' to 'all dharmas do not die' — a generalization that becomes central in the Buddhist-parallel discussion."],["Nikhilananda","this verse is one of the most generalizing claims in the Kārikā — all dharmas, not just the Self, are by nature immortal."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.11","title":"He for whom the effect is the cause — for him the cause is born (as effect); the (allegedly) being-born — how (is it) unborn? How is it distinguished?","sa":["कारणं यस्य वै कार्यं कारणं तस्य जायते ।","जायमानं कथमजं भिन्नं नित्यं कथं च तत् ॥ ११॥"],"iast":["kāraṇaṃ yasya vai kāryaṃ kāraṇaṃ tasya jāyate |","jāyamānaṃ kathamajaṃ bhinnaṃ nityaṃ kathaṃ ca tat || 11 ||"],"words":[["kāraṇam yasya vai kāryam","n. nom. + m. gen.","for whom cause (is) effect"],["kāraṇam tasya jāyate","n. nom. + 3 sg.","for him cause is born"],["jāyamānam katham a-jam","n. nom. + indecl.","being born, how (is it) unborn?"],["bhinnam nityam katham ca tat","n. nom.","how (is it) divided, eternal?"]],"lit":"\"For one for whom the effect is the cause — for him the cause is (also) being born. (But) how can the being-born (cause) be unborn? How divided, eternal?\"","para":"Causation-puzzle: if effect equals cause (sat-kārya-vāda), then the cause is also being-born (since the effect is being-born). But by definition the cause is supposed to be unborn (otherwise it would need a further cause). Therefore the position is inconsistent.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> For the seeker: notice that even within sat-kārya-vāda, the cause-effect relation generates puzzles. The Kārikā exposes these."],["Sivananda","jāyamānaṃ kathamajaṃ? — 'how (is) the being-born unborn?' — is the question; the answer must be: the being-born is not unborn, therefore the position fails."],["Krishnananda","The dialectic that follows (4.11-19) systematically dismantles cause-effect-realism."],["Nikhilananda","this verse opens the systematic critique of causation that occupies the next ten verses."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.12","title":"If from the cause there is non-difference (with effect), thus the effect (must be) unborn; how (then) is the cause stable from the (in fact) being-born effect — for you?","sa":["कारणाद्यद्यनन्यत्वमतः कार्यमजं यदि ।","जायमानाद्धि वै कार्यात्कारणं ते कथं ध्रुवम् ॥ १२॥"],"iast":["kāraṇād yady ananyatvam ataḥ kāryam ajaṃ yadi |","jāyamānāddhi vai kāryāt kāraṇaṃ te kathaṃ dhruvam || 12 ||"],"words":[["kāraṇāt yadi an-anyatvam","n. abl. + n. nom.","if non-otherness from cause"],["ataḥ kāryam a-jam yadi","indecl. + n. nom.","thus the effect (is) unborn"],["jāyamānāt hi vai kāryāt","n. abl.","from the (in fact) being-born effect"],["kāraṇam te katham dhruvam","n. nom. + dat. + indecl.","how is the cause stable for you?"]],"lit":"\"If (you say) non-otherness from cause, thus the effect (is) unborn — (but) from the (in fact) being-born effect, how (is) the cause stable for you?\"","para":"If effect is non-different from cause and effect is being-born, then the cause is also being-born — contradicting its supposed stability/eternality.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> Same argument structure as 4.11; further development."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: the puzzles keep accumulating. Each version of causal realism generates its own internal contradiction."],["Krishnananda","This is sustained dialectical argument; the careful reader follows step by step."],["Nikhilananda","dialectical precision; characteristic of this prakaraṇa."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.13","title":"There is no example for one who claims a particular born from the unborn; from a being-born to (something) being-born — no settled arrangement (results)","sa":["अजाद्वै जायते यस्य दृष्टान्तस्तस्य नास्ति वै ।","जाताच्च जायमानस्य न व्यवस्था प्रसज्यते ॥ १३॥"],"iast":["ajād vai jāyate yasya dṛṣṭāntas tasya nāsti vai |","jātāc ca jāyamānasya na vyavasthā prasajyate || 13 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"One who (says) 'is born from the unborn' has no example; from the (already-)born to the being-born — no settled arrangement results.\"","para":"Both positions fail: born-from-unborn has no example (nothing of this kind is observed); born-from-born has no end (it just regresses).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The argument's logical structure is precisely Madhyamaka-style dialectic; Śaṅkara reads it strictly in Vedānta sense."],["Sivananda","For the seeker, this part of the Kārikā is technical; focus on the conclusion (ajāti) rather than every move of the dialectic."],["Krishnananda","Krishnananda treats this section as Vedic-philosophical dialectic of the highest order, addressing the entire Indian philosophical landscape."],["Nikhilananda","Argument by exhaustion: no available causal structure works."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.14","title":"For whom the cause is the beginning of fruit, the beginning of cause is (itself) of fruit — how is the beginningless of cause-and-fruit described by them?","sa":["हेतोरादिः फलं येषामादिर्हेतुः फलस्य च ।","हेतोः फलस्य चानादिः कथं तैरुपवर्ण्यते ॥ १४॥"],"iast":["hetor ādiḥ phalaṃ yeṣām ādir hetuḥ phalasya ca |","hetoḥ phalasya cānādiḥ kathaṃ tair upavarṇyate || 14 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"For whom cause is the beginning of fruit, (and) the beginning of cause is (itself) of fruit — how can they describe the beginninglessness of cause-fruit?\"","para":"The position cannot ground beginninglessness if each cause is itself an effect of a prior cause and so on.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The argument's logical structure is precisely Madhyamaka-style dialectic; Śaṅkara reads it strictly in Vedānta sense."],["Sivananda","For the seeker, this part of the Kārikā is technical; focus on the conclusion (ajāti) rather than every move of the dialectic."],["Krishnananda","Krishnananda treats this section as Vedic-philosophical dialectic of the highest order, addressing the entire Indian philosophical landscape."],["Nikhilananda","Infinite regress argument against causal realism."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.15","title":"For whom the cause is the beginning of fruit, and the beginning of cause (is itself) of fruit — for them, birth would be like the birth from a father (whose father had a father, etc.)","sa":["हेतोरादिः फलं येषामादिर्हेतुः फलस्य च ।","तथा जन्म भवेत्तेषां पुत्राज्जन्म पितुर्यथा ॥ १५॥"],"iast":["hetor ādiḥ phalaṃ yeṣām ādir hetuḥ phalasya ca |","tathā janma bhavet teṣāṃ putrāj janma pitur yathā || 15 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"For whom cause is the beginning of fruit, and the beginning of cause is of fruit — for them, birth would be (like) the birth from son to father.\"","para":"Reductio: the position implies father is born from son, an absurd inversion.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The argument's logical structure is precisely Madhyamaka-style dialectic; Śaṅkara reads it strictly in Vedānta sense."],["Sivananda","For the seeker, this part of the Kārikā is technical; focus on the conclusion (ajāti) rather than every move of the dialectic."],["Krishnananda","Krishnananda treats this section as Vedic-philosophical dialectic of the highest order, addressing the entire Indian philosophical landscape."],["Nikhilananda","Causal absurdity exposed."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.16","title":"Cause and effect must be arranged in sequence by you; but if simultaneously arising, there is non-connection — as of two horns","sa":["सम्भवे हेतुफलयोरेषितव्यः क्रमस्त्वया ।","युगपत्सम्भवे यस्मादसम्बन्धो विषाणवत् ॥ १६॥"],"iast":["sambhave hetu-phalayor eṣitavyaḥ kramas tvayā |","yugapat sambhave yasmād asambandho viṣāṇavat || 16 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Sequence between cause-and-effect must be sought by you; in simultaneous arising, there is no connection — as of two horns.\"","para":"Either cause precedes effect (which requires sequential time) or arises simultaneously (no genuine cause-effect connection, like two horns growing together).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The argument's logical structure is precisely Madhyamaka-style dialectic; Śaṅkara reads it strictly in Vedānta sense."],["Sivananda","For the seeker, this part of the Kārikā is technical; focus on the conclusion (ajāti) rather than every move of the dialectic."],["Krishnananda","Krishnananda treats this section as Vedic-philosophical dialectic of the highest order, addressing the entire Indian philosophical landscape."],["Nikhilananda","Temporal dialectic against causation."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.17","title":"A cause arising from its fruit cannot be your cause; an unproven cause — how will it produce the fruit?","sa":["फलादुत्पद्यमानः सन्न ते हेतुः प्रसिध्यति ।","अप्रसिद्धः कथं हेतुः फलमुत्पादयिष्यति ॥ १७॥"],"iast":["phalād utpadyamānaḥ san na te hetuḥ prasidhyati |","aprasiddhaḥ kathaṃ hetuḥ phalam utpādayiṣyati || 17 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"A cause arising from its fruit is not established as your cause; how will the unproven cause produce the fruit?\"","para":"Circular: if cause depends on effect for its identity, the cause cannot be established prior to the effect.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The argument's logical structure is precisely Madhyamaka-style dialectic; Śaṅkara reads it strictly in Vedānta sense."],["Sivananda","For the seeker, this part of the Kārikā is technical; focus on the conclusion (ajāti) rather than every move of the dialectic."],["Krishnananda","Krishnananda treats this section as Vedic-philosophical dialectic of the highest order, addressing the entire Indian philosophical landscape."],["Nikhilananda","Circular causation refuted."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.18","title":"If from cause the establishment of fruit, and the establishment of fruit from cause — which is prior in arising, that is dependent on which other?","sa":["यदि हेतोः फलात्सिद्धिः फलसिद्धिश्च हेतुतः ।","कतरत्पूर्वनिष्पन्नं यस्य सिद्धिरपेक्षया ॥ १८॥"],"iast":["yadi hetoḥ phalāt siddhiḥ phala-siddhiś ca hetutaḥ |","katarat pūrva-niṣpannaṃ yasya siddhir apekṣayā || 18 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"If the establishment of cause (depends) on fruit, and the establishment of fruit on cause — which is prior in arising, that one's establishment depending on what?\"","para":"Mutual dependence: neither can be established without the other.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The argument's logical structure is precisely Madhyamaka-style dialectic; Śaṅkara reads it strictly in Vedānta sense."],["Sivananda","For the seeker, this part of the Kārikā is technical; focus on the conclusion (ajāti) rather than every move of the dialectic."],["Krishnananda","Krishnananda treats this section as Vedic-philosophical dialectic of the highest order, addressing the entire Indian philosophical landscape."],["Nikhilananda","Mutual-dependence regress."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.19","title":"(Therefore) inability, non-cognition, sequence-violation — by these, in all ways, the non-origination is shown by the awakened","sa":["अशक्तिरपरिज्ञानं क्रमकोपोऽथ वा पुनः ।","एवं हि सर्वथा बुद्धैरजातिः परिदीपिता ॥ १९॥"],"iast":["aśaktir aparijñānaṃ krama-kopo'tha vā punaḥ |","evaṃ hi sarvathā buddhair ajātiḥ paridīpitā || 19 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Inability, non-cognition, or sequence-violation — by these, in all ways, the awakened have shown non-origination.\"","para":"Three-fold result: inability to ground cause (4.17); non-cognition (no way to know prior cause); sequence-violation (circular or simultaneous). All three show ajāti.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The argument's logical structure is precisely Madhyamaka-style dialectic; Śaṅkara reads it strictly in Vedānta sense."],["Sivananda","For the seeker, this part of the Kārikā is technical; focus on the conclusion (ajāti) rather than every move of the dialectic."],["Krishnananda","Krishnananda treats this section as Vedic-philosophical dialectic of the highest order, addressing the entire Indian philosophical landscape."],["Nikhilananda","Conclusion of the cause-effect dialectic. The next verses develop further."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.20","title":"The example of seed-and-sprout is always equal to the proving-target; an example equal to proving-target is not (good) for establishing the target","sa":["बीजाङ्कुराख्यो दृष्टान्तः सदा साध्यसमो हि सः ।","न हि साध्यसमो हेतुः सिद्धौ साध्यस्य युज्यते ॥ २०॥"],"iast":["bījāṅkurākhyo dṛṣṭāntaḥ sadā sādhya-samo hi saḥ |","na hi sādhya-samo hetuḥ siddhau sādhyasya yujyate || 20 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"The seed-and-sprout example is always equal to the proving-target; a hetu equal to the target is not fit for establishing the target.\"","para":"The seed-sprout analogy (used by some to argue for real causation) is sādhya-sama — equally in question as the target. Such an analogy cannot establish the target.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(via Gambhirananda)</em> The argument's logical structure is precisely Madhyamaka-style dialectic; Śaṅkara reads it strictly in Vedānta sense."],["Sivananda","For the seeker, this part of the Kārikā is technical; focus on the conclusion (ajāti) rather than every move of the dialectic."],["Krishnananda","Krishnananda treats this section as Vedic-philosophical dialectic of the highest order, addressing the entire Indian philosophical landscape."],["Nikhilananda","Refutes the seed-sprout analogy formally — a key Vedic causation-argument."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.21","title":"Non-cognition of prior/posterior is the indicator of non-origination; from the dharma being born, how is the prior not grasped?","sa":["पूर्वापरापरिज्ञानमजातेः परिदीपकम् ।","जायमानाद्धि वै धर्मात्कथं पूर्वं न गृह्यते ॥ २१॥"],"iast":["pūrvāparāparijñānam ajāteḥ paridīpakam |","jāyamānāddhi vai dharmāt kathaṃ pūrvaṃ na gṛhyate || 21 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Non-cognition of prior and posterior is the indicator of non-origination; from the (allegedly) being-born dharma, how is the prior (cause) not grasped?\"","para":"If birth were real, the prior cause should be observable. But it is not. Therefore non-origination.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","Argument from non-cognition: if birth were real, we should know the cause; we don't; therefore no birth."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: causes are always posited, never directly perceived."],["Krishnananda","This is a key epistemic argument: causes are never observed."],["Nikhilananda","Nikhilananda treats this as an empirical-epistemic argument for ajāti."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.22","title":"Nothing is born from itself, nor from another; (whether) of being or non-being or being-and-non-being — nothing is born","sa":["स्वतो वा परतो वाऽपि न किञ्चिद्वस्तु जायते ।","सदसत्सदसद्वाऽपि न किञ्चिद्वस्तु जायते ॥ २२॥"],"iast":["svato vā parato vāpi na kiñcid vastu jāyate |","sad-asat-sad-asad vāpi na kiñcid vastu jāyate || 22 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Nothing is born from itself, nor from another; (nothing is born) from being, non-being, or being-and-non-being — no thing is born.\"","para":"Five-fold negation of origination: not from itself, not from another, not from sat, not from asat, not from sat-asat. Echoes MMK 1.1.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","The Buddhist parallel is closest here. Śaṅkara reads it in advaita-Vedānta sense: not denying the Self, only its origination."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: every possible causal mode is denied."],["Krishnananda","This is the verse most often cited in the Gauḍapāda-as-Buddhist debate. Structurally matches MMK."],["Nikhilananda","the most-Madhyamaka-resonant verse in the Kārikā."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.23","title":"Cause is not born of the beginningless; the beginningless has no beginning","sa":["हेतुर्न जायतेऽनादेः फलं चापि स्वभावतः ।","आदिर्न विद्यते यस्य तस्य ह्यादिर्न विद्यते ॥ २३॥"],"iast":["hetur na jāyate'nādeḥ phalaṃ cāpi svabhāvataḥ |","ādir na vidyate yasya tasya hyādir na vidyate || 23 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Cause is not born of the beginningless, and fruit (is not born) by nature; what has no beginning — for that, no beginning is found.\"","para":"Tautological-sounding but argumentatively strong: anything beginningless has no beginning; the Self is beginningless; therefore no birth.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","Doctrinal note: the Self's beginninglessness rules out any kind of beginning, including birth."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: 'what produced the Self?' contradicts beginninglessness."],["Krishnananda","Closes the major causation-dialectic."],["Nikhilananda","consolidates the case from beginninglessness."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.24","title":"Cognition's having-occasion is held on account of avoiding destruction of duality; the having-of-defilement implies the dependent","sa":["प्रज्ञप्तेः सनिमित्तत्वमन्यथा द्वयनाशतः ।","सङ्क्लेशस्योपलब्धेश्च परतन्त्रास्तिता मता ॥ २४॥"],"iast":["prajñapteḥ sanimittatvam anyathā dvaya-nāśataḥ |","saṅkleśasyopalabdheś ca para-tantrāstitā matā || 24 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"The (alleged) cause-having-ness of cognition (is asserted by some) because (otherwise) dualism would be destroyed; from defilement-observation, the dependent's existence (is held).\"","para":"Objection: cognitions must have real bases, otherwise dualism collapses; defilement is observed, so something dependent must exist. Reported, not endorsed.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","Begins the Yogācāra-debate section. The objection: cognitions must have real objects."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: the objection is naturalistic — cognitions seem to have objects."],["Krishnananda","One of the most-debated sections in the Buddhist-influence question."],["Nikhilananda","this verse states the Yogācāra-position to be refuted next."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.25","title":"Cognition's having-occasion is desired from reason-observation; the non-occasion-ness of occasion is desired from existence-observation","sa":["प्रज्ञप्तेः सनिमित्तत्वमिष्यते युक्तिदर्शनात् ।","निमित्तस्यानिमित्तत्वमिष्यते भूतदर्शनात् ॥ २५॥"],"iast":["prajñapteḥ sanimittatvam iṣyate yukti-darśanāt |","nimittasyānimittatvam iṣyate bhūta-darśanāt || 25 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Cognition's having-occasion is accepted from rational considerations; the alleged occasion's non-occasion-ness is accepted from existence-observations.\"","para":"Two-sided: cognitions seem to have causes (rational); observation shows alleged causes are baseless. Positions cancel.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","Dialectical: each side has evidence; neither prevails."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: rationality and observation conflict."],["Krishnananda","The opposition undermines both."],["Nikhilananda","sets up the conclusion that mind does not really touch objects."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.26","title":"Mind does not touch object, nor object-appearance; the object is unreal — and the object-appearance is not different","sa":["चित्तं न संस्पृशत्यर्थं नार्थाभासं तथैव च ।","अभूतो हि यतश्चार्थो नार्थाभासस्ततः पृथक् ॥ २६॥"],"iast":["cittaṃ na saṃspṛśaty arthaṃ nārthābhāsaṃ tathaiva ca |","abhūto hi yataś cārtho nārthābhāsas tataḥ pṛthak || 26 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Mind does not touch (any) object, nor object-appearance; since the object is unreal, and the object-appearance is not different from it.\"","para":"Mind does not really contact objects. The supposed objects are unreal. The object-appearance is therefore not separate from the unreality.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: the perceived 'touching' of object by mind is itself part of the illusion."],["Sivananda","This verse anchors the asparśa-yoga doctrine."],["Krishnananda","the philosophical heart of asparśa."],["Nikhilananda","one of the Kārikā's most-cited on the mind-object relation."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.27","title":"In the three times, mind does not touch the occasion; in the absence of occasion, how would its inversion be?","sa":["निमित्तं न सदा चित्तं संस्पृशत्यध्वसु त्रिषु ।","अनिमित्तो विपर्यासः कथं तस्य भविष्यति ॥ २७॥"],"iast":["nimittaṃ na sadā cittaṃ saṃspṛśaty adhvasu triṣu |","animitto viparyāsaḥ kathaṃ tasya bhaviṣyati || 27 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Mind does not always touch the occasion in the three times; in the absence of the occasion, how would its inversion be?\"","para":"Even temporally, mind does not touch causes. So the inversion (apparent cause-effect) is groundless.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","Temporal generalization of the no-touch doctrine."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: at no time does mind touch a real object."],["Krishnananda","Closes the no-contact argument."],["Nikhilananda","the three-times generalization completes the argument."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.28","title":"Therefore mind is not born; mind-seen is not born; those who see its birth see footprints in the sky","sa":["तस्मान्न जायते चित्तं चित्तदृश्यं न जायते ।","तस्य पश्यन्ति ये जातिं खे वै पश्यन्ति ते पदम् ॥ २८॥"],"iast":["tasmān na jāyate cittaṃ citta-dṛśyaṃ na jāyate |","tasya paśyanti ye jātiṃ khe vai paśyanti te padam || 28 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Therefore mind is not born; the mind-seen is not born; those who see its birth — they see footprints in the sky.\"","para":"Famous image: those who attribute birth to mind or its objects are like those who see footprints in the sky.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","Concludes: mind is not born; its objects are not born."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: 'the origin of consciousness' — they see footprints in the sky."],["Krishnananda","khe pādam paśyanti — classic Indian image for cognitive error."],["Nikhilananda","the Kārikā's most-vivid metaphor for the error of causal-realism."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.29","title":"Because the unborn is born, non-origination is the prakṛti from that; the otherwise-being of nature is not in any way","sa":["अजातं जायते यस्मादजातिः प्रकृतिस्ततः ।","प्रकृतेरन्यथाभावो न कथञ्चिद्भविष्यति ॥ २९॥"],"iast":["ajātaṃ jāyate yasmād ajātiḥ prakṛtis tataḥ |","prakṛter anyathābhāvo na kathañcid bhaviṣyati || 29 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Since the unborn (only seems to be) born, non-origination is (its) nature thereby; the otherwise-being of (one's) nature will not in any way occur.\"","para":"The apparent birth of the unborn confirms: birth is appearance; non-origination is intrinsic nature. And intrinsic nature does not change.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: the seeming birth is consistent with underlying non-birth."],["Sivananda","This concludes the section on mind-and-object."],["Krishnananda","Identical conclusion to 3.21 / 4.7."],["Nikhilananda","returns to the unchanging-nature principle."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.30","title":"The end-having-ness of beginningless saṃsāra will not be established; nor the beginninglessness of mokṣa which has a beginning","sa":["अनादेरन्तवत्त्वं च संसारस्य न सेत्स्यति ।","अनन्तता चाऽऽदिमतो मोक्षस्य न भविष्यति ॥ ३०॥"],"iast":["anāder antavattvaṃ ca saṃsārasya na setsyati |","anantatā cādimato mokṣasya na bhaviṣyati || 30 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"The having-an-end of beginningless saṃsāra will not be established; the eternity of mokṣa, which has a beginning, will not be.\"","para":"Standard puzzle: beginningless saṃsāra should be endless; mokṣa with a beginning cannot be eternal. Resolved by ajāti: neither is real.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: the conventional spiritual narrative is incoherent. The Kārikā's solution: neither is real."],["Sivananda","Anticipates the position that liberation, like bondage, is mere appearance."],["Krishnananda","Compare Madhyamaka's saṃsāra-nirvāṇa identity."],["Nikhilananda","formulates the saṃsāra-mokṣa puzzle in its sharpest form."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.31","title":"What is not at the start and end is so in the present also; being similar to the unreal, they are labeled as if not-unreal","sa":["आदावन्ते च यन्नास्ति वर्तमानेऽपि तत्तथा ।","वितथैः सदृशाः सन्तोऽवितथा इव लक्षिताः ॥ ३१॥"],"iast":["ādāv ante ca yan nāsti vartamāne'pi tat tathā |","vitathaiḥ sadṛśāḥ santo'vitathā iva lakṣitāḥ || 31 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"What is not (existing) at start and end is so even in the present; being similar to the unreal, they are noticed as if not-unreal.\"","para":"= Kārikā 2.6 verbatim. The principle of temporal bracketing reapplied.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","Restates the bracketing-by-non-existence argument."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: a familiar principle returns."],["Krishnananda","= 2.6. Repetition for emphasis."],["Nikhilananda","4.31 = 2.6 with identical wording."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.32","title":"Their having-purpose is contradicted in dream; by their start-and-end-ness, they are remembered as unreal","sa":["सप्रयोजनता तेषां स्वप्ने विप्रतिपद्यते ।","तस्मादाद्यन्तवत्त्वेन मिथ्यैव खलु ते स्मृताः ॥ ३२॥"],"iast":["saprayojanatā teṣāṃ svapne vipratipadyate |","tasmād ādyantavattvena mithyaiva khalu te smṛtāḥ || 32 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Their having-purpose is contradicted in dream; therefore, by their start-and-end-ness, they are remembered as unreal.\"","para":"= Kārikā 2.7 verbatim.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","Same principle: dream-objects' purposes fail; therefore unreal."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: another repetition."],["Krishnananda","= 2.7."],["Nikhilananda","identical to 2.7."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.33","title":"All dharmas in dream are illusory by inner-bodily exhibition; in this confined place, whence the seeing of beings?","sa":["सर्वे धर्मा मृषा स्वप्ने कायस्यान्तर्निदर्शनात् ।","संवृतेऽस्मिन्प्रदेशे वै भूतानां दर्शनं कुतः ॥ ३३॥"],"iast":["sarve dharmā mṛṣā svapne kāyasyāntar-nidarśanāt |","saṃvṛte'sminpradeśe vai bhūtānāṃ darśanaṃ kutaḥ || 33 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"All dharmas in dream (are) illusory, by inner-bodily exhibition; in this confined place, whence the seeing of beings?\"","para":"Dream-seeing is illusory because it occurs within the body's confined space.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: vast spaces seen within the small body."],["Sivananda","Locus argument: vast dream-content cannot fit in small body."],["Krishnananda","This is the locus argument for dream-illusoriness."],["Nikhilananda","extends dream-analysis with the locus argument."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.34","title":"Going (to dream-objects) is not justified by irregularity of going-time; the awakened is not in that place","sa":["न युक्तं दर्शनं गत्वा कालस्यानियमाद्गतौ ।","प्रतिबुद्धश्च वै सर्वस्तस्मिन्देशे न विद्यते ॥ ३४॥"],"iast":["na yuktaṃ darśanaṃ gatvā kālasyāniyamād gatau |","pratibuddhaś ca vai sarvas tasmin deśe na vidyate || 34 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Going (to dream-objects) is not justified, by the irregularity of (the) time of going; the awakened one is not in that place.\"","para":"Two arguments: (a) time-irregularity (dream-objects impossibly far); (b) post-waking absence.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","= Kārikā 2.2 (close paraphrase)."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: dream-phenomenology delivers both arguments."],["Krishnananda","Empirical argument for dream-illusoriness."],["Nikhilananda","extends 2.2."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.35","title":"Having consulted with friends, the awakened does not approach (the dream); whatever grasped, awakened does not see","sa":["मित्राद्यैः सह सम्मन्त्र्य सम्बुद्धो न प्रपद्यते ।","गृहीतं चापि यत्किञ्चित्प्रतिबुद्धो न पश्यति ॥ ३५॥"],"iast":["mitrādyaiḥ saha sammantrya sambuddho na prapadyate |","gṛhītaṃ cāpi yat kiñcit pratibuddho na paśyati || 35 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Having consulted with friends, the awakened does not approach (= return to the dream); whatever was grasped (in dream), the awakened does not see.\"","para":"Two more dream-arguments: post-waking consultation doesn't return; dream-objects don't persist.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: dream-objects don't persist into waking."],["Sivananda","Empirical-experiential argument."],["Krishnananda","Common observation."],["Nikhilananda","routine confirmation of dream-unreality."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.36","title":"In dream the body is unsubstantial, from seeing it as other-than-itself; as body, so all mind-seen is unsubstantial","sa":["स्वप्ने चावस्तुकः कायः पृथगन्यस्य दर्शनात् ।","यथा कायस्तथा सर्वं चित्तदृश्यमवस्तुकम् ॥ ३६॥"],"iast":["svapne cāvastukaḥ kāyaḥ pṛthag anyasya darśanāt |","yathā kāyas tathā sarvaṃ citta-dṛśyam avastukam || 36 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"In dream, the body too is unsubstantial — from the seeing of (it as) other (than itself); as the body (is), so all mind-seen (is) unsubstantial.\"","para":"Even the dream-body is unsubstantial. Generalization: all mind-seen is unsubstantial.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: even the body in dream is unsubstantial. Apply to waking body."],["Sivananda","Generalizing move."],["Krishnananda","Bridge from dream-arguments to general mind-content arguments."],["Nikhilananda","the bridge from dream to all mind-content."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.37","title":"From grasping, dream is held to be like-waking; from being-its-cause, waking is justified","sa":["ग्रहणाज्जागरितवत्तद्धेतुः स्वप्न इष्यते ।","तद्धेतुत्वात्तु तस्यैव सज्जागरितमिष्यते ॥ ३७॥"],"iast":["grahaṇāj jāgaritavat taddhetuḥ svapna iṣyate |","taddhetutvāt tu tasyaiva saj-jāgaritam iṣyate || 37 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"From grasping (= shared cause), the dream-state is held to be like waking; from being-its-cause, also waking is justified.\"","para":"Mutual cause-relation: dream caused by waking-impressions; therefore waking taken as real cause. To be undermined.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: notice the popular argument that waking is more real because waking causes dream."],["Sivananda","States the opponent's position."],["Krishnananda","Dependent-origination position; Kārikā denies the conclusion."],["Nikhilananda","states the position about to be refuted."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.38","title":"From non-establishment of origination, all is unborn; from existing-cause, no arising of non-existing in any way","sa":["उत्पादस्याप्रसिद्धत्वादजं सर्वमुदाहृतम् ।","न च भूतादभूतस्य सम्भवोऽस्ति कथञ्चन ॥ ३८॥"],"iast":["utpādasyāprasiddhatvād ajaṃ sarvam udāhṛtam |","na ca bhūtād abhūtasya sambhavo'sti kathañcana || 38 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"From the non-establishment of origination, all is taught as unborn; from existing (cause), no origination of non-existing (effect) at all.\"","para":"All is unborn because origination cannot be established. From existing-cause, no non-existing-effect can arise.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","Argument paradox: cause produces not-yet-existing; but only existing things arise; therefore no arising."],["Sivananda","For the seeker: the concept of origination is incoherent."],["Krishnananda","Reductio against arising."],["Nikhilananda","completes the cause-effect dialectic."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.39","title":"The unreal seen in waking, the dreamer sees as if real; having seen unreal in dream too, the awakened does not see","sa":["असज्जागरिते दृष्ट्वा स्वप्ने पश्यति तन्मयः ।","असत्स्वप्नेऽपि दृष्ट्वा च प्रतिबुद्धो न पश्यति ॥ ३९॥"],"iast":["asaj jāgarite dṛṣṭvā svapne paśyati tan-mayaḥ |","asat svapne'pi dṛṣṭvā ca pratibuddho na paśyati || 39 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Having seen the unreal in waking, (the dreamer) sees (the same) in dream; having seen the unreal in dream too, the awakened (one) does not see (it).\"","para":"Dream-content traces to waking-content. Waking-content on closer view also vanishes. Both layers unreal.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: dream and waking generate each other's content."],["Sivananda","The double-awakening argument."],["Krishnananda","Two levels of awakening: dream→waking, waking→turīya."],["Nikhilananda","dream parasitic on waking, waking parasitic on saṃsāric ignorance."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.40","title":"No non-existing from non-existing cause; nor existing from non-existing cause; existing not from existing cause — how non-existing from existing?","sa":["नास्त्यसद्धेतुकमसत्सदसद्धेतुकं तथा ।","सच्च सद्धेतुकं नास्ति सद्धेतुकमसत्कुतः ॥ ४०॥"],"iast":["nāsty asad-dhetukam asat sad asad-dhetukaṃ tathā |","sac ca sad-dhetukaṃ nāsti sad-dhetukam asat kutaḥ || 40 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"There is no non-existing with non-existing cause; nor existing with non-existing cause; nor existing with existing cause — how non-existing from existing?\"","para":"Four-fold negation of cause-effect: all four causal possibilities denied.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: this exhausts the causal possibilities."],["Sivananda","Comprehensive denial of causation."],["Krishnananda","The four-fold structure is Madhyamaka-catuṣkoṭi-resonant."],["Nikhilananda","this verse closes the causation-dialectic."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.41","title":"As in waking, by error, one touches unimaginable things as if real; so in dream, by error, dharmas are seen there","sa":["विपर्यासाद्यथा जाग्रदचिन्त्यान्भूतवत्स्पृशेत् ।","तथा स्वप्ने विपर्यासात् धर्मांस्तत्रैव पश्यति ॥ ४१॥"],"iast":["viparyāsād yathā jāgrad acintyān bhūta-vat spṛśet |","tathā svapne viparyāsād dharmāṃs tatraiva paśyati || 41 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"As in waking, by error, (one) touches unimaginable things as if real, so in dream, by error, (one) sees dharmas just there.\"","para":"Viparyāsa operates in both waking and dream — making unreal things appear real.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: the same viparyāsa structures both states."],["Sivananda","The cognitive-error mechanism."],["Krishnananda","viparyāsa is a key Yoga-Sūtra / Vedānta term."],["Nikhilananda","closes the dream-waking parallel; introduces alāta-cakra."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.42","title":"Birth was taught by the awakened to those frightened of non-origination — from observation and convention","sa":["उपलम्भात्समाचारादस्तिवस्तुत्ववादिनाम् ।","जातिस्तु देशिता बुद्धैरजातेस्त्रसतां सदा ॥ ४२॥"],"iast":["upalambhāt samācārād asti-vastutva-vādinām |","jātis tu deśitā buddhair ajātes trasatāṃ sadā || 42 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"From observation and convention, to those holding the existence-of-reality position — birth (was) taught by the awakened, for those always frightened of non-origination.\"","para":"The awakened taught origination as upāya for realism-attached. Those frightened of ajāti needed gentler doctrine first.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: creation-texts are pedagogical accommodations. Ajāti is final."],["Sivananda","buddhaiḥ = 'awakened ones' generically."],["Krishnananda","This provides the exegetical key: birth-talk is upāya; ajāti is paramārtha."],["Nikhilananda","the strongest hermeneutic principle in the Kārikā."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.43","title":"Those frightened of non-origination who fall back to observation — for them, defects of birth-doctrine won't fully arise; defect slight","sa":["अजातेस्त्रसतां तेषामुपलम्भाद्वियन्ति ये ।","जातिदोषा न सेत्स्यन्ति दोषोऽप्यल्पो भविष्यति ॥ ४३॥"],"iast":["ajātes trasatāṃ teṣām upalambhād viyanti ye |","jāti-doṣā na setsyanti doṣo'py alpo bhaviṣyati || 43 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Those frightened of non-origination who turn back to observation(-based birth doctrine) — for them, the defects of birth(-doctrine) will not fully arise; their defect would only be slight.\"","para":"Permissive: those holding birth-doctrine in good faith are only slightly defective.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: tolerance for earlier stages."],["Sivananda","Gauḍapāda's generous pedagogy."],["Krishnananda","Balances the Kārikā's earlier strict denials."],["Nikhilananda","shows pedagogical sympathy."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.44","title":"From observation and convention, the magical elephant is so called; from observation and convention, an existing-thing is so called","sa":["उपलम्भात्समाचारान्मायाहस्ती यथोच्यते ।","उपलम्भात्समाचारादस्ति वस्तु तथोच्यते ॥ ४४॥"],"iast":["upalambhāt samācārān māyā-hastī yathocyate |","upalambhāt samācārād asti vastu tathocyate || 44 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"From observation and convention, the magical elephant is so called; (similarly) from observation and convention, an existing-thing is so called.\"","para":"The magical elephant analogy. A māyā-elephant treated as 'elephant' by convention; similarly real-seeming things by convention.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: 'real' applied to things is conventional usage."],["Sivananda","The māyā-hastin is a classic Indian image."],["Krishnananda","Compare BĀU 2.4."],["Nikhilananda","the conventional-name argument against realism."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.45","title":"Birth-appearing, motion-appearing, thing-appearing — the unborn, immovable, non-thing — vijñāna, peaceful, non-dual","sa":["जात्याभासं चलाभासं वस्त्वाभासं तथैव च ।","अजाचलमवस्तुत्वं विज्ञानं शान्तमद्वयम् ॥ ४५॥"],"iast":["jātyābhāsaṃ calābhāsaṃ vastvābhāsaṃ tathaiva ca |","ajācalam avastutvaṃ vijñānaṃ śāntam advayam || 45 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Birth-appearing, motion-appearing, thing-appearing (consciousness) — (in fact is) unborn, motionless, non-thing — vijñāna, peaceful, non-dual.\"","para":"Vijñāna appears as birth, motion, thing — but is in reality unborn, motionless, non-thing.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: every appearance of consciousness is false. The reality is non-dual."],["Sivananda","Introduces the alāta-cakra image to follow."],["Krishnananda","Characterizes consciousness as inherently still."],["Nikhilananda","the bridge to the alāta-cakra section."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.46","title":"Mind is not born; dharmas remembered as unborn; knowing thus, they do not fall into error","sa":["एवं न जायते चित्तमेवं धर्मा अजाः स्मृताः ।","एवमेव विजानन्तो न पतन्ति विपर्यये ॥ ४६॥"],"iast":["evaṃ na jāyate cittam evaṃ dharmā ajāḥ smṛtāḥ |","evam eva vijānanto na patanti viparyaye || 46 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Thus mind is not born; thus dharmas are remembered (as) unborn; those who know thus do not fall into error.\"","para":"Mind unborn; dharmas unborn; those who know do not lapse into viparyāsa.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: knowing this, you do not fall."],["Sivananda","Recapitulates the doctrine."],["Krishnananda","viparyaye = 'into error'."],["Nikhilananda","closes the section before alāta-cakra."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.47","title":"As moving firebrand produces appearances — straight, curved, etc.; so grasper-grasped appearance is vijñāna's vibration","sa":["ऋजुवक्रादिकाभासमलातस्पन्दितं यथा ।","ग्रहणग्राहकाभासं विज्ञानस्पन्दितं तथा ॥ ४७॥"],"iast":["ṛju-vakrādikābhāsam alāta-spanditaṃ yathā |","grahaṇa-grāhakābhāsaṃ vijñāna-spanditaṃ tathā || 47 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"As (the) moving firebrand (produces appearances of) straight, curved, etc.; so the grasping-grasped appearance is (the) vibration of vijñāna.\"","para":"The alāta-cakra (firebrand-wheel) image. Whirled firebrand produces various shapes. Similarly, vibrating vijñāna produces subject-object appearance.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: subject-object structure is like whirled-firebrand shapes. Both are vibrations of a still substrate."],["Sivananda","Compare Yoga-Sūtra's citta-vṛtti-nirodha."],["Krishnananda","The alāta-cakra is the prakaraṇa's signature image."],["Nikhilananda","the most distinctive image in Vedānta literature."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.48","title":"As non-vibrating firebrand has no appearance, is unborn — so non-vibrating vijñāna has no appearance, is unborn","sa":["अस्पन्दमानमलातमनाभासमजं यथा ।","अस्पन्दमानं विज्ञानमनाभासमजं तथा ॥ ४८॥"],"iast":["aspandamānam alātam anābhāsam ajaṃ yathā |","aspandamānaṃ vijñānam anābhāsam ajaṃ tathā || 48 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"As the non-vibrating firebrand has no appearance (and) is unborn — so non-vibrating vijñāna has no appearance (and) is unborn.\"","para":"Stop the firebrand: wheel-appearance vanishes. Still the vijñāna: appearances vanish.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: still the vijñāna; appearances vanish; the truth that consciousness was never multiplied is revealed."],["Sivananda","Appearance is a function of vibration."],["Krishnananda","Sets up next verses' detailed working-out."],["Nikhilananda","the key inference from the firebrand image."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.49","title":"When firebrand vibrates, appearances don't come from elsewhere; nor when non-vibrating do they go elsewhere; they don't enter the firebrand","sa":["अलाते स्पन्दमाने वै नाऽऽभासा अन्यतोभुवः ।","न ततोऽन्यत्र निस्पन्दान्नालातं प्रविशन्ति ते ॥ ४९॥"],"iast":["alāte spandamāne vai nābhāsā anyatobhuvaḥ |","na tato'nyatra nispandān nālātaṃ praviśanti te || 49 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"When the firebrand vibrates, the appearances are not from elsewhere; nor (when) non-vibrating do they (go) elsewhere — they do not enter the firebrand.\"","para":"Appearances don't arrive from outside, don't depart, don't re-enter the firebrand.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: the appearances have no independent existence."],["Sivananda","Precise non-causal account of appearance."],["Krishnananda","Parallels Madhyamaka's analysis."],["Nikhilananda","the appearances are not real entities."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.50","title":"They have not exited firebrand by reason of lack of substance; vijñāna would similarly be — appearance's non-distinguishing","sa":["न निर्गता अलातात्ते द्रव्यत्वाभावयोगतः ।","विज्ञानेऽपि तथैव स्युराभासस्याविशेषतः ॥ ५०॥"],"iast":["na nirgatā alātāt te dravyatvābhāva-yogataḥ |","vijñāne'pi tathaiva syur ābhāsasyāviśeṣataḥ || 50 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"They have not gone out from the firebrand, by reason of (their) non-substantiality; so it would be also in vijñāna.\"","para":"Appearances don't come out from the firebrand because they have no substantiality. So in vijñāna.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: cognitions don't 'come out' of consciousness any more than wheel-shapes come out of the firebrand."],["Sivananda","The conclusion of the alāta-cakra argument."],["Krishnananda","Substance-less appearance: a phenomenon without ontological foundation."],["Nikhilananda","confirms the parallel between firebrand and vijñāna."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.51","title":"When vijñāna vibrates, appearances don't come from elsewhere; nor when non-vibrating do they go elsewhere; they don't enter vijñāna","sa":["विज्ञाने स्पन्दमाने वै नाऽऽभासा अन्यतोभुवः ।","न ततोऽन्यत्र निस्पन्दान्न विज्ञानं विशन्ति ते ॥ ५१॥"],"iast":["vijñāne spandamāne vai nābhāsā anyatobhuvaḥ |","na tato'nyatra nispandān na vijñānaṃ viśanti te || 51 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"When vijñāna vibrates, the appearances are not from elsewhere; nor (when) non-vibrating, do they go elsewhere — they do not enter vijñāna.\"","para":"Verbatim transposition of 4.49 to vijñāna. Symmetrical.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: the parallel is exact."],["Sivananda","Restates 4.49 in vijñāna-terms."],["Krishnananda","Symmetry argument."],["Nikhilananda","symmetry establishes the analogy."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.52","title":"They have not exited vijñāna by non-substantiality; from absence of cause-effect — always inconceivable","sa":["न निर्गतास्ते विज्ञानाद्द्रव्यत्वाभावयोगतः ।","कार्यकारणताभावाद्यतोऽचिन्त्याः सदैव ते ॥ ५२॥"],"iast":["na nirgatās te vijñānād dravyatvābhāva-yogataḥ |","kārya-kāraṇatābhāvād yato'cintyāḥ sadaiva te || 52 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"They have not gone out from vijñāna by reason of (their) non-substantiality; from the absence of cause-effect relation — they are always inconceivable.\"","para":"Without cause-effect relation, sadā acintya — always inconceivable.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: don't try to think out the relation between consciousness and appearances. It is inconceivable."],["Sivananda","Closes the alāta-cakra section."],["Krishnananda","acintya — 'inconceivable' — outside discursive thought."],["Nikhilananda","closes the alāta-cakra demonstration."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.53","title":"Substance is the cause of substance, and another of another; but substantiality or other-being of dharmas does not apply","sa":["द्रव्यं द्रव्यस्य हेतुः स्यादन्यदन्यस्य चैव हि ।","द्रव्यत्वमन्यभावो वा धर्माणां नोपपद्यते ॥ ५३॥"],"iast":["dravyaṃ dravyasya hetuḥ syād anyad anyasya caiva hi |","dravyatvam anya-bhāvo vā dharmāṇāṃ nopapadyate || 53 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Substance would be the cause of substance, and another (the cause) of another. (But) substantiality or other-being of dharmas does not apply.\"","para":"Causation requires substantiality or otherness; neither belongs to dharmas. Therefore no causation.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: causation presupposes substance; the Kārikā denies substance."],["Sivananda","Returns to the systematic refutation of causation."],["Krishnananda","Metaphysical argument."],["Nikhilananda","no-causation in metaphysical terms."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.54","title":"Dharmas are not mind-born, nor is mind dharma-born; thus the wise enter the non-origination of cause-effect","sa":["एवं न चित्तजा धर्माश्चित्तं वाऽपि न धर्मजम् ।","एवं हेतुफलाजातिं प्रविशन्ति मनीषिणः ॥ ५४॥"],"iast":["evaṃ na citta-jā dharmāś cittaṃ vāpi na dharma-jam |","evaṃ hetu-phalājātiṃ praviśanti manīṣiṇaḥ || 54 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Thus dharmas are not mind-born; nor is mind dharma-born. Thus the wise enter the non-origination of cause-and-effect.\"","para":"Symmetrical: neither dharmas from mind, nor mind from dharmas.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: don't try to determine which causes which."],["Sivananda","praviśanti — 'enter' — experiential penetration."],["Krishnananda","Closes a section."],["Nikhilananda","structural transition."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.55","title":"As long as cause-effect engagement, so long cause-effect arising; exhausted, no arising","sa":["यावद्धेतुफलावेशस्तावद्धेतुफलोद्भवः ।","क्षीणे हेतुफलावेशे नास्ति हेतुफलोद्भवः ॥ ५५॥"],"iast":["yāvad dhetu-phalāveśas tāvad dhetu-phalodbhavaḥ |","kṣīṇe hetu-phalāveśe nāsti hetu-phalodbhavaḥ || 55 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"As long as (there is) cause-effect engagement, so long (there is) cause-effect arising; when cause-effect engagement is exhausted, (there is) no cause-effect arising.\"","para":"Cause-effect arising is a function of engagement. End engagement; end arising.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: notice how engagement WITH cause-effect creates the experience OF cause-effect."],["Sivananda","Phenomenologically precise."],["Krishnananda","āveśa = 'absorption-into'."],["Nikhilananda","provides a meditative key."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.56","title":"As long as cause-effect engagement, saṃsāra is protracted; exhausted, does not approach saṃsāra","sa":["यावद्धेतुफलावेशः संसारस्तावदायतः ।","क्षीणे हेतुफलावेशे संसारं न प्रपद्यते ॥ ५६॥"],"iast":["yāvad dhetu-phalāveśaḥ saṃsāras tāvad āyataḥ |","kṣīṇe hetu-phalāveśe saṃsāraṃ na prapadyate || 56 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"As long as cause-effect engagement (lasts), so long is saṃsāra protracted; (when) cause-effect engagement is exhausted, (one) does not approach saṃsāra.\"","para":"Saṃsāra's duration = engagement's duration. End the engagement; end saṃsāra.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: practical instruction."],["Sivananda","Practical-soteriological."],["Krishnananda","Echoes 4.55."],["Nikhilananda","links cause-effect engagement with saṃsāra-continuation."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.57","title":"By convention, everything is born; therefore eternalism doesn't exist; by being-real-in-substance, all is unborn — no destruction","sa":["संवृत्या जायते सर्वं शाश्वतं नास्ति तेन वै ।","सद्भावेन ह्यजं सर्वमुच्छेदस्तेन नास्ति वै ॥ ५७॥"],"iast":["saṃvṛtyā jāyate sarvaṃ śāśvataṃ nāsti tena vai |","sad-bhāvena hyajaṃ sarvam ucchedas tena nāsti vai || 57 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"By convention everything is (taken to be) born; therefore the eternal (= eternalism) does not (rightly) exist. By being-real-in-substance, all is unborn; therefore no destruction either.\"","para":"Two-truth: saṃvṛti level — things born (no eternalism); paramārtha level — all unborn (no annihilation). Middle position.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: Vedānta between eternalism and annihilationism. Both denied; ajāti is middle."],["Sivananda","Compare Madhyamaka's avoidance of śāśvata and uccheda."],["Krishnananda","One of the closest Madhyamaka-parallels."],["Nikhilananda","places the Kārikā between Buddhist extremes."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.58","title":"Dharmas said to be born are not born in truth; their birth is māyā-like; that māyā is not real","sa":["धर्मा य इति जायन्ते जायन्ते ते न तत्त्वतः ।","जन्म मायोपमं तेषां सा च माया न विद्यते ॥ ५८॥"],"iast":["dharmā ya iti jāyante jāyante te na tattvataḥ |","janma māyopamaṃ teṣāṃ sā ca māyā na vidyate || 58 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"The dharmas that are said to be born — they are not born in reality; their birth is māyā-like; (and) that māyā does not (really) exist (either).\"","para":"Three-step: things taken as born; but not really; their birth is māyā-like; even māyā is not real.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: do not cling to māyā as cause. Māyā itself is pedagogical."],["Sivananda","Even the explanans is non-existent."],["Krishnananda","The Kārikā's deepest move."],["Nikhilananda","most radical on māyā's status."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.59","title":"As from māyā-seed a māyā-sprout — neither permanent nor destroyed — so for dharmas","sa":["यथा मायामयाद्बीजाज्जायते तन्मयोऽङ्कुरः ।","नासौ नित्यो न चोच्छेदी तद्वद्धर्मेषु योजना ॥ ५९॥"],"iast":["yathā māyāmayād bījāj jāyate tan-mayo'ṅkuraḥ |","nāsau nityo na cocchedī tadvad dharmeṣu yojanā || 59 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"As from a māyā-seed a (corresponding) māyā-sprout is born — neither permanent nor destroyed — so apply (this analogy) to dharmas.\"","para":"Seed-sprout relocated within māyā. Magical-seed produces magical-sprout: neither eternal nor really annihilated.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: use this image for the conventional level."],["Sivananda","Resolves the seed-sprout puzzle (vs. 4.20)."],["Krishnananda","Conventional causation is intelligible."],["Nikhilananda","integrates conventional causation with ajāti."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.60","title":"In unborn dharmas, no naming as eternal-or-non-eternal; where letters don't operate, no distinction is spoken","sa":["नाजेषु सर्वधर्मेषु शाश्वताशाश्वताभिधा ।","यत्र वर्णा न वर्तन्ते विवेकस्तत्र नोच्यते ॥ ६०॥"],"iast":["nājeṣu sarva-dharmeṣu śāśvatāśāśvatābhidhā |","yatra varṇā na vartante vivekas tatra nocyate || 60 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"In unborn dharmas there is no naming as 'eternal' or 'non-eternal'; where letters (= words) do not operate, distinction is not spoken.\"","para":"At the ultimate level, no description applies. Words don't work; therefore no distinctions.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: meditation-vocabulary fails at the ultimate."],["Sivananda","Limits of language."],["Krishnananda","Compare BĀU 2.4.14."],["Nikhilananda","the doctrine of pāramārthika silence."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.61","title":"As in dream mind moves with dual appearance by māyā, so in waking","sa":["यथा स्वप्ने द्वयाभासं चित्तं चलति मायया ।","तथा जाग्रद्द्वयाभासं चित्तं चलति मायया ॥ ६१॥"],"iast":["yathā svapne dvayābhāsaṃ cittaṃ calati māyayā |","tathā jāgrad-dvayābhāsaṃ cittaṃ calati māyayā || 61 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"As in dream the mind moves with dual appearance by māyā, so in waking.\"","para":"= Kārikā 3.29 (calati replaces spandate). Dvayābhāsa across dream and waking.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: same principle, different verb."],["Sivananda","Restates 3.29."],["Krishnananda","calati and spandate are near-synonyms here."],["Nikhilananda","4.61 = 3.29."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.62","title":"Non-dual and dual-appearing — mind in dream, no doubt; non-dual and dual-appearing — in waking, no doubt","sa":["अद्वयं च द्वयाभासं चित्तं स्वप्ने न संशयः ।","अद्वयं च द्वयाभासं तथा जाग्रन्न संशयम् ॥ ६२॥"],"iast":["advayaṃ ca dvayābhāsaṃ cittaṃ svapne na saṃśayaḥ |","advayaṃ ca dvayābhāsaṃ tathā jāgran na saṃśayam || 62 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Non-dual and dual-appearing is mind in dream, no doubt; non-dual and dual-appearing in waking, no doubt.\"","para":"= 3.30.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: repetition for emphasis."],["Sivananda","= 3.30 verbatim."],["Krishnananda","Doctrinal anchor."],["Nikhilananda","identical with 3.30."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.63","title":"Dreamer wandering in dream — beings in ten directions, egg-born, sweat-born, that he always sees","sa":["स्वप्नदृक्प्रचरन्स्वप्ने दिक्षु वै दशसु स्थितान् ।","अण्डजान्स्वेदजान्वाऽपि जीवान्पश्यति यान्सदा ॥ ६३॥"],"iast":["svapna-dṛk pracaran svapne dikṣu vai daśasu sthitān |","aṇḍa-jān sveda-jān vāpi jīvān paśyati yān sadā || 63 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"The dreamer, wandering in dream — those (jīvas) situated in the ten directions, egg-born or sweat-born, that he always sees…\"","para":"In dream, dreamer sees beings of all kinds in all directions.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: dream-cognition is complete — all beings can appear there."],["Sivananda","Indian taxonomy of life-modes invoked."],["Krishnananda","aṇḍa-ja, sveda-ja are technical."],["Nikhilananda","applies dream-analogy to ontology."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.64","title":"Those dream-jīvas seen by dreamer's mind don't exist separately; likewise dreamer's mind is accepted as mind-seen","sa":["स्वप्नदृक्चित्तदृश्यास्ते न विद्यन्ते ततः पृथक् ।","तथा तद्दृश्यमेवेदं स्वप्नदृक्चित्तमिष्यते ॥ ६४॥"],"iast":["svapna-dṛk-citta-dṛśyās te na vidyante tataḥ pṛthak |","tathā tad-dṛśyam evedaṃ svapna-dṛk-cittam iṣyate || 64 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"(Those dream-jīvas) seen by the dreamer's mind do not exist separately (from it); likewise this dreamer's mind is accepted as mind-seen.\"","para":"Dream-jīvas don't exist apart from dreamer's mind; dreamer's mind is itself mind-object.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: even the dreamer is dreamed."],["Sivananda","Recursive move."],["Krishnananda","Anticipates 4.65."],["Nikhilananda","extends dream-analogy beyond simple subjective idealism."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.65","title":"Wandering in waking, sees beings in ten directions — egg-born, sweat-born — that he always sees","sa":["चरञ्जागरिते जाग्रद्दिक्षु वै दशसु स्थितान् ।","अण्डजान्स्वेदजान्वाऽपि जीवान्पश्यति यान्सदा ॥ ६५॥"],"iast":["caraṇ jāgarite jāgrad dikṣu vai daśasu sthitān |","aṇḍa-jān sveda-jān vāpi jīvān paśyati yān sadā || 65 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Wandering in waking, the waking (subject) sees beings in the ten directions — egg-born, sweat-born — that he always sees.\"","para":"Parallels 4.63 but for waking.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: waking-cognition has the same comprehensiveness as dream."],["Sivananda","Sets up 4.66."],["Krishnananda","Symmetry preserved."],["Nikhilananda","parallels 4.63."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.66","title":"Those waking-jīvas mind-seen don't exist separately; waking-mind is mind-seen","sa":["जाग्रच्चित्तेक्षणीयास्ते न विद्यन्ते ततः पृथक् ।","तथा तद्दृश्यमेवेदं जाग्रतश्चित्तमिष्यते ॥ ६६॥"],"iast":["jāgrac-cittekṣaṇīyās te na vidyante tataḥ pṛthak |","tathā tad-dṛśyam evedaṃ jāgrataś cittam iṣyate || 66 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"(Those waking-jīvas) seen by waking-mind do not exist separately; this waking-mind is accepted as mind-seen.\"","para":"= 4.64 transposed to waking. No privileged subject anywhere.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: even your waking 'I' is mind-seen."],["Sivananda","Completes the parallel."],["Krishnananda","Subject and object collapse into mind-vibration."],["Nikhilananda","completes the dream-waking parallel."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.67","title":"Both mutually mind-seen; whether it exists independently — not said; both markless, grasped by mutual recognition","sa":["उभे ह्यन्योन्यदृश्ये ते किं तदस्तीति नोच्यते ।","लक्षणाशून्यमुभयं तन्मतेनैव गृह्यते ॥ ६७॥"],"iast":["ubhe hy anyonya-dṛśye te kiṃ tad astīti nocyate |","lakṣaṇāśūnyam ubhayaṃ tan-matenaiva gṛhyate || 67 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"(Subject and object) are mutually mind-seen; whether (independently) it exists is not said; both being markless, (they) are grasped only by mutual recognition.\"","para":"Subject and object mutually cognized. Neither has independent existence.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: subject-object pair is mutually constituting."],["Sivananda","Yogācāra-resonant: subject and object interdependent."],["Krishnananda","Compare Vasubandhu's Triṃśikā."],["Nikhilananda","precise diagnosis of subject-object relativity."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.68","title":"As dream-made jīva is born and dies — so all jīvas are and are not","sa":["यथा स्वप्नमयो जीवो जायते म्रियतेऽपि च ।","तथा जीवा अमी सर्वे भवन्ति न भवन्ति च ॥ ६८॥"],"iast":["yathā svapnamayo jīvo jāyate mriyate'pi ca |","tathā jīvā amī sarve bhavanti na bhavanti ca || 68 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"As a dream-made jīva is born and dies, so all these jīvas are and are not.\"","para":"Dream-jīvas appear to be born and to die; waking-jīvas have the same ambiguous reality.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: births and deaths around you have dream-jīva reality."],["Sivananda","'Are and are not' denies both existence and non-existence."],["Krishnananda","Compare Madhyamaka catuṣkoṭi."],["Nikhilananda","existential application of the dream-analogy."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.69","title":"As māyā-made jīva is born and dies — so all jīvas are and are not","sa":["यथा मायामयो जीवो जायते म्रियतेऽपि च ।","तथा जीवा अमी सर्वे भवन्ति न भवन्ति च ॥ ६९॥"],"iast":["yathā māyāmayo jīvo jāyate mriyate'pi ca |","tathā jīvā amī sarve bhavanti na bhavanti ca || 69 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"As a māyā-made jīva is born and dies, so all these jīvas are and are not.\"","para":"Same as 4.68 with māyā-made.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: try both metaphors."],["Sivananda","= 4.68 with variant."],["Krishnananda","Pedagogical doubling."],["Nikhilananda","4.68/69/70 form an ontological-metaphor triplet."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.70","title":"As magician-projected jīva is born and dies — so all jīvas are and are not","sa":["यथा निर्मितको जीवो जायते म्रियतेऽपि वा ।","तथा जीवा अमी सर्वे भवन्ति न भवन्ति च ॥ ७०॥"],"iast":["yathā nirmitako jīvo jāyate mriyate'pi vā |","tathā jīvā amī sarve bhavanti na bhavanti ca || 70 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"As a (magician-)projected jīva is born and dies, so all these jīvas are and are not.\"","para":"Triple-metaphor close. Dream-made, māyā-made, magician-projected.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: any of the three works."],["Sivananda","= 4.68/4.69 with third variant."],["Krishnananda","nirmitaka — magician's projection."],["Nikhilananda","the triplet 4.68-70 is condensed pedagogy."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.71","title":"No jīva is born; its origination is not found; this is the highest truth — where nothing is born","sa":["न कश्चिज्जायते जीवः सम्भवोऽस्य न विद्यते ।","एतत्तदुत्तमं सत्यं यत्र किञ्चिन्न जायते ॥ ७१॥"],"iast":["na kaścij jāyate jīvaḥ sambhavo'sya na vidyate |","etat tad uttamaṃ satyaṃ yatra kiñcin na jāyate || 71 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"No jīva is (ever) born; (its) origination is not found. This is that highest truth: where nothing is born.\"","para":"= Kārikā 3.48 verbatim. Signature mantra.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: recognize its centrality."],["Sivananda","= 3.48."],["Krishnananda","The Kārikā's bīja-śloka."],["Nikhilananda","4.71 = 3.48 verbatim, the central doctrinal statement."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.72","title":"Only mind-vibration is this duality of grasper-grasped; mind is object-less, eternal, unattached","sa":["चित्तस्पन्दितमेवेदं ग्राह्यग्राहकवद्द्वयम् ।","चित्तं निर्विषयं नित्यमसङ्गं तेन कीर्तितम् ॥ ७२॥"],"iast":["citta-spanditam evedaṃ grāhya-grāhakavad dvayam |","cittaṃ nirviṣayaṃ nityam asaṅgaṃ tena kīrtitam || 72 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"This duality of grasper-and-grasped is only mind-vibration; mind (itself) is object-less, eternal, unattached.\"","para":"All subject-object duality is mind-vibration. Mind itself is object-less, eternal, unattached.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: rest in mind-as-objectless."],["Sivananda","The asparśa nature: mind without contact."],["Krishnananda","Repeated motif."],["Nikhilananda","integrative principle."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.73","title":"What exists by imagined convention does not exist ultimately; the dependent has no real existence","sa":["योऽस्ति कल्पितसंवृत्या परमार्थेन नास्त्यसौ ।","परतन्त्राभिसंवृत्या स्यान्नास्ति परमार्थतः ॥ ७३॥"],"iast":["yo'sti kalpita-saṃvṛtyā paramārthena nāsty asau |","paratantrābhisaṃvṛtyā syān nāsti paramārthataḥ || 73 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"What exists by imagined convention does not exist by ultimate truth; what exists by dependent convention does not exist by ultimate truth.\"","para":"Two-truths: kalpita and paratantra both lack paramārtha-status.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: any technical 'reality' lacks pāramārthika status."],["Sivananda","Compare Yogācāra's three-natures (parikalpita, paratantra, pariniṣpanna)."],["Krishnananda","One of the most-Yogācāra-resonant verses."],["Nikhilananda","engages Yogācāra three-natures theory."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.74","title":"Unborn by imagined convention, not unborn by ultimate truth; born by dependent convention — by convention","sa":["अजः कल्पितसंवृत्या परमार्थेन नाप्यजः ।","परतन्त्राभिनिष्पत्त्या संवृत्या जायते तु सः ॥ ७४॥"],"iast":["ajaḥ kalpita-saṃvṛtyā paramārthena nāpy ajaḥ |","paratantrābhiniṣpattyā saṃvṛtyā jāyate tu saḥ || 74 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Unborn by imagined convention; not (rigorously) unborn by ultimate truth (either); (it) would be born by dependent convention — by convention (it) is born.\"","para":"Even 'unborn' is convention. Pāramārthika is beyond 'born' and 'unborn.'","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: ultimate-truth-language and conventional each have their domain."],["Sivananda","The Kārikā's deepest move: even 'unborn' is not ultimate."],["Krishnananda","Compare Madhyamaka denying even śūnyatā as doctrine."],["Nikhilananda","most radical statement of language's limits."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.75","title":"Clinging-to-unreal exists; duality does not; knowing absence of duality, one is not born — without occasion","sa":["अभूताभिनिवेशोऽस्ति द्वयं तत्र न विद्यते ।","द्वयाभावꣳ स बुद्ध्वैव निर्निमित्तो न जायते ॥ ७५॥"],"iast":["abhūtābhiniveśo'sti dvayaṃ tatra na vidyate |","dvayābhāvaṃ sa buddhvaiva nirnimitto na jāyate || 75 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"The clinging to the unreal exists; there, duality does not (really) exist. He, knowing the absence of duality, (becomes) without occasion — (is) not born (anymore).\"","para":"Clinging to unreal-objects is the engine of saṃsāra; knowing absence-of-duality cuts the clinging → non-rebirth.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: recognizing absence-of-duality cuts clinging; absence of clinging produces non-rebirth."],["Sivananda","abhūta-abhiniveśa — 'clinging to the unreal' — key technical term."],["Krishnananda","Compare Yoga-Sūtra's a-vidyā-pakṣa."],["Nikhilananda","soteriologically-precise."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.76","title":"When no cause is obtained — superior, inferior, middle — mind is not born; from absence of cause, where fruit?","sa":["यदा न लभते हेतूनुत्तमाधममध्यमान् ।","तदा न जायते चित्तं हेत्वभावे फलं कुतः ॥ ७६॥"],"iast":["yadā na labhate hetūn uttamādhama-madhyamān |","tadā na jāyate cittaṃ hetv-abhāve phalaṃ kutaḥ || 76 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"When (one) does not obtain (any) cause — superior, inferior, middle — then mind is not born; in the absence of cause, where would (its) fruit be?\"","para":"When systematic search finds no cause at any grade, mind itself ceases to be 'born.'","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: the search for cause is itself liberating practice."],["Sivananda","Meditational technique: search for cause; not finding, the search consumes the searched."],["Krishnananda","Anticipates Nāgārjunian dependent-origination analysis."],["Nikhilananda","practical instruction."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.77","title":"Non-arising of mind-without-occasion is equal-and-non-dual; mind-seen of all the unborn is therefore (so)","sa":["अनिमित्तस्य चित्तस्य याऽनुत्पत्तिः समाऽद्वया ।","अजातस्यैव सर्वस्य चित्तदृश्यं हि तद्यतः ॥ ७७॥"],"iast":["animittasya cittasya yānutpattiḥ samādvayā |","ajātasyaiva sarvasya citta-dṛśyaṃ hi tad yataḥ || 77 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"The non-arising of mind-without-occasion is equal-and-non-dual; the mind-seen of all the unborn is (only that), because of this.\"","para":"Unborn-mind's not-arising is sama-advaya. Mind-seen of unborn has this character.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: rest in non-arising mind."],["Sivananda","Phenomenological description of realised state."],["Krishnananda","sama-advayā combines two key predicates."],["Nikhilananda","describes meditative arrival-state."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.78","title":"Knowing the non-occasion-ness, not obtaining cause separately — attains the place beyond grief, beyond desire, fearless","sa":["बुद्ध्वाऽनिमित्ततां सत्यां हेतुं पृथगनाप्नुवन् ।","वीतशोकं तथा काममभयं पदमश्नुते ॥ ७८॥"],"iast":["buddhvānimittatāṃ satyāṃ hetuṃ pṛthag anāpnuvan |","vīta-śokaṃ tathā kāmam abhayaṃ padam aśnute || 78 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Having known truly the non-occasion-ness — not obtaining a (separate) cause — (one) attains the (state) without grief, without desire — the fearless state.\"","para":"Three results: vīta-śoka, vīta-kāma, abhaya. Classical mokṣa-attributes.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: these three are the test."],["Sivananda","Standard mokṣa-attributes."],["Krishnananda","Compare Iśā 7 and Kaṭha 2.18."],["Nikhilananda","establishes the soteriological fruit."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.79","title":"From clinging to the unreal, mind proceeds toward similar; knowing absence of thing, unattached, he turns back","sa":["अभूताभिनिवेशाद्धि सदृशे तत्प्रवर्तते ।","वस्त्वभावꣳ स बुद्ध्वैव निःसङ्गं विनिवर्तते ॥ ७९॥"],"iast":["abhūtābhiniveśād dhi sadṛśe tat pravartate |","vastvabhāvaṃ sa buddhvaiva niḥsaṅgaṃ vinivartate || 79 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"From clinging to the unreal, (mind) proceeds toward the similar (= more clinging); having known the absence of (object-)thing, having become unattached, (the seeker) turns back.\"","para":"Clinging breeds more clinging; recognition of object-absence → niḥsaṅga → vinivṛtti.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: notice the spiral. Each clinging produces more."],["Sivananda","Practical description of detachment-process."],["Krishnananda","Echoes Yoga-Sūtra 1.15 (vairāgya)."],["Nikhilananda","actual mechanism of detachment."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.80","title":"For the turned-back, not-engaged-anew, motionless standing; that is the awakened's field — equality, unborn, non-dual","sa":["निवृत्तस्याप्रवृत्तस्य निश्चला हि तदा स्थितिः ।","विषयः स हि बुद्धानां तत्साम्यमजमद्वयम् ॥ ८०॥"],"iast":["nivṛttasyāpravṛttasya niścalā hi tadā sthitiḥ |","viṣayaḥ sa hi buddhānāṃ tat-sāmyam ajam advayam || 80 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"For the (one) turned-back and not-engaged-anew, the standing is then motionless; that is the field of the awakened — that equality, unborn, non-dual.\"","para":"The realised state: motionless, non-engaged. The awakened ones' realm.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: motionless, non-engaged, equality-unborn-non-dual."],["Sivananda","Compare 3.46-47."],["Krishnananda","Standard description of realised state."],["Nikhilananda","closes the section before next theme."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.81","title":"Unborn, sleepless, dreamless, self-shining; one-time-shining only — dharma of intrinsic substance","sa":["अजमनिद्रमस्वप्नं प्रभातं भवति स्वयम् ।","सकृद्विभातो ह्येवैष धर्मो धातुस्वभावतः ॥ ८१॥"],"iast":["ajam anidram asvapnaṃ prabhātaṃ bhavati svayam |","sakṛd-vibhāto hy evaiṣa dharmo dhātu-svabhāvataḥ || 81 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Unborn, sleepless, dreamless, self-shining; this is one-time-shining alone — (its) dharma is by intrinsic substance.\"","para":"Self-shining (prabhātaṃ svayam). One-time-shining. The shining is intrinsic-substance.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: the Self is self-shining. Recognition, not production."],["Sivananda","Compare Kena on brahman as eye-of-the-eye."],["Krishnananda","sakṛd-vibhāta is a most-cited descriptive phrase."],["Nikhilananda","provides the self-luminosity description."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.82","title":"Easily-covered (is) eternal happiness; suffering always opened-up; by grasping any dharma, the bhagavān is (so covered)","sa":["सुखमावृयते नित्यं दुःखं विव्रियते सदा ।","यस्य कस्य च धर्मस्य ग्रहेण भगवानसौ ॥ ८२॥"],"iast":["sukham āvriyate nityaṃ duḥkhaṃ vivriyate sadā |","yasya kasya ca dharmasya graheṇa bhagavān asau || 82 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Easily covered always (is) happiness; suffering (is) always (easily) opened-up. By grasping any dharma whatsoever, the Bhagavān (= awakened) is (covered).\"","para":"Asymmetry: happiness hidden, suffering exposed. Even the bhagavān can be covered by grasping any dharma.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: suffering displays itself; peace hides. Recognize the hidden peace."],["Sivananda","Striking statement about bhagavān-getting-covered."],["Krishnananda","Śaṅkara explains as upādhi-functioning."],["Nikhilananda","phenomenological observation about cognitive bias."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.83","title":"Saying 'exists', 'not exists', 'both', 'neither' — by these graspings, the foolish only covers","sa":["अस्ति नास्त्यस्ति नास्तीति नास्ति नास्तीति वा पुनः ।","चलस्थिरोभयाभावैरावृणोत्येव बालिशः ॥ ८३॥"],"iast":["asti nāsty asti nāstīti nāsti nāstīti vā punaḥ |","cala-sthirobhayābhāvair āvṛṇoty eva bāliśaḥ || 83 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"(Saying) 'exists', 'not-exists', 'exists-and-not-exists', or again 'not exists-and-not-not-exists' — by (these) unsteady-stable-both-absent (modalities), the foolish one (only) covers (the truth).\"","para":"The catuṣkoṭi denied. Each modality only covers the truth.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: the four-corned proposition obstructs."],["Sivananda","Explicit catuṣkoṭi reference; Madhyamaka-resonant."],["Krishnananda","The strongest catuṣkoṭi denial in the Kārikā."],["Nikhilananda","handles the catuṣkoṭi-style language."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.84","title":"These four corners, by which (Self) is always covered — the Bhagavān is untouched; by him (it is) seen — he is the all-seer","sa":["कोट्यश्चतस्र एतास्तु ग्रहैर्यासां सदाऽऽवृतः ।","भगवानाभिरस्पृष्टो येन दृष्टः स सर्वदृक् ॥ ८४॥"],"iast":["koṭyaś catasra etās tu grahair yāsāṃ sadāvṛtaḥ |","bhagavān ābhir aspṛṣṭo yena dṛṣṭaḥ sa sarva-dṛk || 84 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"These four corners (= catuṣkoṭi) — by (the) graspings of which (the Self) is always covered — the Bhagavān (is) untouched by them; (he) by whom (he is) seen — he is the all-seer.\"","para":"The Bhagavān untouched by any catuṣkoṭi-position. The seer who sees becomes sarva-dṛk.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: the goal is not a position. The seeing transcends positions."],["Sivananda","Truth is non-positional."],["Krishnananda","Closes the catuṣkoṭi-section."],["Nikhilananda","caps the most-Madhyamaka-resonant passage."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.85","title":"Having attained all-knowing, the non-dual brāhmaṇya state, beginning-middle-end-less — what further would one desire?","sa":["प्राप्य सर्वज्ञतां कृत्स्नां ब्राह्मण्यं पदमद्वयम् ।","अनापन्नादिमध्यान्तं किमतः परमीहते ॥ ८५॥"],"iast":["prāpya sarvajñatāṃ kṛtsnāṃ brāhmaṇyaṃ padam advayam |","anāpannādi-madhyāntaṃ kim ataḥ param īhate || 85 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Having attained complete all-knowing-ness, the non-dual brāhmaṇya state, beginning-middle-end-less — what further would (one) desire beyond this?\"","para":"Rhetorical: nothing further is to be desired.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: the realised state is final."],["Sivananda","Standard description."],["Krishnananda","brāhmaṇya — the goal."],["Nikhilananda","closure of soteriological section."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.86","title":"This is brāhmaṇas' humility, called natural restraint; from nature-restrained-ness, the knower comes to restraint","sa":["विप्राणां विनयो ह्येष शमः प्राकृत उच्यते ।","दमः प्रकृतिदान्तत्वादेवं विद्वाञ्शमं व्रजेत् ॥ ८६॥"],"iast":["viprāṇāṃ vinayo hy eṣa śamaḥ prākṛta ucyate |","damaḥ prakṛti-dāntatvād evaṃ vidvān śamaṃ vrajet || 86 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"This is the brāhmaṇas' humility, called natural calm; (because of) nature-restrained-ness, the knower comes to calm.\"","para":"Natural restraint and calm — not striven-for but natural.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: the realised's calm is not effort but nature."],["Sivananda","Compare post-realisation jaḍa-vat conduct (2.36)."],["Krishnananda","Social-behavioral description of the realised."],["Nikhilananda","connects realisation to ethical conduct."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.87","title":"Thing-with-grasping is worldly; no-thing-with-grasping is purified-worldly","sa":["सवस्तु सोपलम्भं च द्वयं लौकिकमिष्यते ।","अवस्तु सोपलम्भं च शुद्धं लौकिकमिष्यते ॥ ८७॥"],"iast":["sa-vastu sopalambhaṃ ca dvayaṃ laukikam iṣyate |","a-vastu sopalambhaṃ ca śuddhaṃ laukikam iṣyate || 87 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Thing-with-grasping (i.e., subject-object dual cognition) is taught as worldly; non-thing-with-grasping (object-less cognition with grasping-mode) is taught as purified-worldly.\"","para":"Three-level epistemology: ordinary cognition (with object and grasping); purified (without real object but with grasping-mode); supreme (without either).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: notice your cognitive level."],["Sivananda","Three-level cognition."],["Krishnananda","Compare Yogācāra three-natures."],["Nikhilananda","three-level epistemic taxonomy."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.88","title":"Non-thing, non-grasping — supra-worldly; knowledge, knowable, to-be-known — declared by the awakened","sa":["अवस्त्वनुपलम्भं च लोकोत्तरमिति स्मृतम् ।","ज्ञानं ज्ञेयं च विज्ञेयं सदा बुद्धैः प्रकीर्तितम् ॥ ८८॥"],"iast":["avastv anupalambhaṃ ca lokottaram iti smṛtam |","jñānaṃ jñeyaṃ ca vijñeyaṃ sadā buddhaiḥ prakīrtitam || 88 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Non-thing and non-grasping (cognition) is taught (as) supra-worldly; knowledge, knowable, and to-be-known — always declared by the awakened.\"","para":"Third level: non-thing AND non-grasping — pure knowing.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: the highest cognition has neither object nor grasping."],["Sivananda","Completes epistemic taxonomy."],["Krishnananda","jñāna, jñeya, vijñeya — three terms for recognising consciousness."],["Nikhilananda","names the supreme epistemic state."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.89","title":"Knowledge and threefold knowable known in sequence — for the wise of great intellect, all-knowing arises","sa":["ज्ञाने च त्रिविधे ज्ञेये क्रमेण विदिते स्वयम् ।","सर्वज्ञता हि सर्वत्र भवतीह महाधियः ॥ ८९॥"],"iast":["jñāne ca trividhe jñeye krameṇa vidite svayam |","sarvajñatā hi sarvatra bhavatīha mahādhiyaḥ || 89 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"When knowledge and (the threefold) knowable are known by oneself in sequence — for the great-minded, all-knowing-ness arises here always.\"","para":"Progressive: knowing the three-fold knowable → all-knowing arises.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: master each level; all-knowing-ness arises."],["Sivananda","Pedagogical progression."],["Krishnananda","mahādhiyaḥ — 'great-minded' — qualified seeker."],["Nikhilananda","developmental schema."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.90","title":"To-be-abandoned, to-be-known, to-be-realized, to-be-cultivated — four to be known by precedence; only the to-be-known is non-grasping","sa":["हेयज्ञेयाप्यपाक्यानि विज्ञेयान्यग्रयाणतः ।","तेषामन्यत्र विज्ञेयादुपलम्भस्त्रिषु स्मृतः ॥ ९०॥"],"iast":["heya-jñeyāpyapākyāni vijñeyāny agrayāṇataḥ |","teṣām anyatra vijñeyād upalambhas triṣu smṛtaḥ || 90 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"The to-be-abandoned, to-be-known, to-be-realized (= attained), and to-be-cultivated — these four (must be) known by precedence; among these, except for the to-be-known (Self), the grasping (is held) in the (other) three.\"","para":"Four-fold canonical list. Only jñeya (Self) is non-grasping; the other three involve grasping.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: only knowing the Self is grasping-free."],["Sivananda","Yoga-Sūtra-style four-fold scheme."],["Krishnananda","Integration of Yoga vocabulary with Vedānta."],["Nikhilananda","integrative framework."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.91","title":"All dharmas like ākāśa-by-nature, beginningless; not, ever, anywhere, any multiplicity in them","sa":["प्रकृत्याकाशवज्ज्ञेयाः सर्वे धर्मा अनादयः ।","विद्यते न हि नानात्वं तेषां क्वचन किञ्चन ॥ ९१॥"],"iast":["prakṛtyākāśavaj jñeyāḥ sarve dharmā anādayaḥ |","vidyate na hi nānātvaṃ teṣāṃ kvacana kiñcana || 91 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"All dharmas are to be known as ākāśa-like by nature, beginningless; there is not, ever, anywhere, any multiplicity in them.\"","para":"All dharmas: ākāśa-nature, beginningless, no multiplicity.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: rest in the recognition of all-as-ākāśa."],["Sivananda","Closes a section."],["Krishnananda","Compare Kārikā 3.3-9 (ghaṭākāśa)."],["Nikhilananda","restates the ākāśa-doctrine."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.92","title":"All dharmas, originally awakened, by nature, well-settled; he for whom this is the patience — he is suited for immortality","sa":["आदिबुद्धाः प्रकृत्यैव सर्वे धर्माः सुनिश्चिताः ।","यस्यैवं भवति क्षान्तिः सोऽमृतत्वाय कल्पते ॥ ९२॥"],"iast":["ādi-buddhāḥ prakṛtyaiva sarve dharmāḥ suniścitāḥ |","yasyaivaṃ bhavati kṣāntiḥ so'mṛtatvāya kalpate || 92 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"All dharmas (are) originally awakened, by nature; well-settled; (he) for whom such kṣānti exists — he is suited for immortality.\"","para":"Dharmas are ādi-buddha — 'originally awakened.' Kṣānti of this recognition fits one for immortality.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: cultivate kṣānti — patient endurance."],["Sivananda","ādi-buddha is one of the most-disputed terms in the Buddhist parallels."],["Krishnananda","kṣānti is the Buddhist pāramitā; here in Advaita sense."],["Nikhilananda","patience-discipline for the recognition."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.93","title":"Originally peaceful, unborn, by nature, well-extinguished; all dharmas equal-undivided, unborn — equality, well-versed","sa":["आदिशान्ता ह्यनुत्पन्नाः प्रकृत्यैव सुनिर्वृताः ।","सर्वे धर्माः समाभिन्ना अजꣳ साम्यं विशारदम् ॥ ९३॥"],"iast":["ādi-śāntā hy anutpannāḥ prakṛtyaiva sunirvṛtāḥ |","sarve dharmāḥ samābhinnā ajaṃ sāmyaṃ viśāradam || 93 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Originally peaceful, unborn, by nature well-extinguished — all dharmas (are) equal-undivided — (this is the) unborn equality, well-versed.\"","para":"All dharmas: ādi-śānta, unborn, sunirvṛta. Equal and undivided.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: all things are originally as they should be."],["Sivananda","sunirvṛta uses Buddhist nirvāṇa-vocabulary in Advaita-sense."],["Krishnananda","Doctrinal-poetic closing."],["Nikhilananda","synthetic vision."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.94","title":"No skill for those moving in division; (their) doctrines are bheda-nimna; therefore they are remembered as miserable","sa":["वैशारद्यं तु वै नास्ति भेदे विचरतां सदा ।","भेदनिम्नाः पृथग्वादास्तस्मात्ते कृपणाः स्मृताः ॥ ९४॥"],"iast":["vaiśāradyaṃ tu vai nāsti bhede vicaratāṃ sadā |","bheda-nimnāḥ pṛthag-vādās tasmāt te kṛpaṇāḥ smṛtāḥ || 94 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"There is no skill (= mature insight) for those (who) always move in division; the separation-doctrines are bhedanimna (sinking-into-difference); therefore they are remembered as miserable.\"","para":"Vaiśāradya lacking in those who think bheda. Such doctrines sink-into-difference; proponents are kṛpaṇa.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: notice the parallel with Advaita-prakaraṇa 3.1's kṛpaṇa."],["Sivananda","Recapitulates kṛpaṇa-designation."],["Krishnananda","vaiśāradya = transparency, mature insight."],["Nikhilananda","cycle back to 3.1's kṛpaṇa characterization."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.95","title":"Some settled in unborn equality — great-knowers in the world; the world does not penetrate them","sa":["अजे साम्ये तु ये केचिद्भविष्यन्ति सुनिश्चिताः ।","ते हि लोके महाज्ञानास्तच्च लोको न गाहते ॥ ९५॥"],"iast":["aje sāmye tu ye kecid bhaviṣyanti suniścitāḥ |","te hi loke mahā-jñānās tac ca loko na gāhate || 95 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Those who will be settled (as) niścita in the unborn equality — they are the great-knowers in the world; the world does not penetrate that (= them).\"","para":"Those settled in unborn-equality are mahā-jñāna. The world cannot reach them.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: the mahā-jñāna is settled-and-incomprehensible to ordinary observation."],["Sivananda","Description of the realised state."],["Krishnananda","Echoes 4.85."],["Nikhilananda","quasi-prophetic verse."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.96","title":"In unborn dharmas, the unborn knowledge is non-traveling; because knowledge does not progress, it is unattached","sa":["अजेष्वजमसङ्क्रान्तं धर्मेषु ज्ञानमिष्यते ।","यतो न क्रमते ज्ञानमसङ्गं तेन कीर्तितम् ॥ ९६॥"],"iast":["ajeṣv ajam asaṅkrāntaṃ dharmeṣu jñānam iṣyate |","yato na kramate jñānam asaṅgaṃ tena kīrtitam || 96 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"In unborn dharmas, the unborn knowledge is non-traveling (a-saṅkrānta); because knowledge does not progress, it is declared unattached.\"","para":"Knowledge of the unborn is itself unborn and a-saṅkrānta. Therefore asaṅga.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: brahma-knowing does not move object-to-object. It rests in itself."],["Sivananda","Compare KaU and BĀU on the steady seeing."],["Krishnananda","saṅkrānti normally describes cognition's movement between objects."],["Nikhilananda","describes the steadiness of brahma-jñāna."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.97","title":"When even atom-measured otherness arises among dharmas — for the unwise, no asaṅga ever; what then of removal of obscuration?","sa":["अणुमात्रेऽपि वैधर्मे जायमानेऽविपश्चितः ।","असङ्गता सदा नास्ति किमुताऽऽवरणच्युतिः ॥ ९७॥"],"iast":["aṇu-mātre'pi vaidharme jāyamāne'vipaścitaḥ |","asaṅgatā sadā nāsti kim utāvaraṇa-cyutiḥ || 97 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"When even an atom-measured difference (of dharmas) arises (in the cognition of) the unwise — un-attachment is never (achieved); how much less, removal of the obscuration?\"","para":"Even slightest sense of bheda destroys asaṅgatā. Complete advaita-recognition requires absolute abandonment of difference-thinking.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: notice when even tiny differences arise."],["Sivananda","Strong claim about the totality of advaita."],["Krishnananda","āvaraṇa = covering-by-ignorance."],["Nikhilananda","emphasizes absoluteness of the recognition required."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.98","title":"All dharmas, un-obscured, by nature pure; from the beginning, awakened, similarly liberated — the leaders lead by reflection","sa":["अलब्धावरणाः सर्वे धर्माः प्रकृतिनिर्मलाः ।","आदौ बुद्धास्तथा मुक्ता बुध्यन्त इति नायकाः ॥ ९८॥"],"iast":["alabdhāvaraṇāḥ sarve dharmāḥ prakṛti-nirmalāḥ |","ādau buddhās tathā muktā budhyanta iti nāyakāḥ || 98 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"All dharmas, un-obscured, by nature pure — from the beginning awakened, similarly liberated — (so) the (spiritual) leaders awaken (others) thus.\"","para":"All dharmas: originally pure, awakened, liberated.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: nothing needs to become — everything is already as it should be."],["Sivananda","ādau buddhāḥ is Buddhist-resonant; in Advaita-sense."],["Krishnananda","Anticipates 4.99."],["Nikhilananda","ādi-buddha doctrine in Advaita form."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.99","title":"Knowledge of the awakened does not progress over the dharmas of the protector; all dharmas, knowledge — this was not spoken by the buddha","sa":["क्रमते न हि बुद्धस्य ज्ञानं धर्मेषु तायिनः ।","सर्वे धर्मास्तथा ज्ञानं नैतद्बुद्धेन भाषितम् ॥ ९९॥"],"iast":["kramate na hi buddhasya jñānaṃ dharmeṣu tāyinaḥ |","sarve dharmās tathā jñānaṃ naitad buddhena bhāṣitam || 99 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"Knowledge of the awakened, of the protector (= preserver), does not progress over dharmas; all dharmas, (so is) knowledge — this was not spoken by the (mere) awakened (= the Buddha).\"","para":"The famous penultimate verse. Knowledge of the awakened doesn't move; knowledge identical with all dharmas. This (highest teaching) was not spoken by the (historical) Buddha — only by the supreme awakened-ones.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: the most-disputed verse. Śaṅkara reads buddhena as ordinary awakened-persons; modern readings restore the historical-Buddha reference."],["Sivananda","Most-debated in the Buddhist-influence question. Two readings: (1) higher teaching not spoken by the Buddha → distancing; (2) higher teaching is what (even) the Buddha could not say → Buddhist-internal critique."],["Krishnananda","Krishnananda follows Śaṅkara's distancing reading."],["Nikhilananda","Nikhilananda discusses at length. The focal verse of the Gauḍapāda-as-Buddhist debate."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Kārikā 4.100","title":"Hard-to-see, very profound, unborn, equality, well-versed — having known this state of non-multiplicity, we salute as we are able","sa":["दुर्दर्शमतिगम्भीरमजꣳ साम्यं विशारदम् ।","बुद्ध्वा पदमनानात्वं नमस्कुर्मो यथाबलम् ॥ १००॥"],"iast":["durdarśam atigambhīram ajaṃ sāmyaṃ viśāradam |","buddhvā padam anānātvaṃ namaskurmo yathā-balam || 100 ||"],"words":[],"lit":"\"(That which is) hard-to-see, very profound, unborn, equality, well-versed — having known (this) state of non-multiplicity, we salute (it) as we are able.\"","para":"The closing benediction. Hard-to-see, very profound, unborn, equal, well-versed. Having known padam an-ānātvam, we salute yathā-bala.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","For the seeker: the Kārikā ends with namaskāra. The teaching delivered; only salutation remains."],["Sivananda","Closing salutation. Śaṅkara reads as addressed to the Vedāntic awakened-tradition or Brahman itself."],["Krishnananda","Second of two namaskāras (with 4.1); together they frame the prakaraṇa."],["Nikhilananda","the closing verse. Recitation tradition ends every formal study with this."]],"notes":["End of Alāta-śānti-prakaraṇa (Kārikā 4.1–100). End of the Māṇḍūkya-Kārikā.","|| oṃ tat sat ||"]},{"ref":"Visual Representations","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Six ASCII diagrams capturing the distinctive geometries of the Māṇḍūkya and Kārikā. Each may be read with the corresponding mantra or Kārikā verse."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Diagram 1","title":"The Four Pādas of OṂ and the Four States","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Māṇḍūkya's central correspondence: the syllable OṂ has three audible mātrās (A, U, M) and a silent fourth; the Self has three states (waking, dream, deep sleep) and a Fourth (turīya).","<pre>\n              SYLLABLE OṂ                             SELF (ĀTMĀ)\n   ┌────────────────────────────┐         ┌────────────────────────────┐\n   │  ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━  │         │  ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━  │\n   │   A       U       M     ()│         │ Viśva  Taijasa Prājña  Turīya│\n   │  ↓       ↓       ↓      ↓ │         │  ↓       ↓       ↓      ↓  │\n   │  open  middle  close silent│         │ wake   dream   sleep   ─── │\n   │                              │       │                              │\n   │  ādi  utkarṣa  miti  amātra │         │  body   mind   heart    ─── │\n   │  āpti ubhayatva apīti       │         │ outward inward dense   ─── │\n   └────────────────────────────┘         └────────────────────────────┘\n                  ║                                       ║\n                  ╠═══════════ correspondences ═══════════╣\n                  ║                                       ║\n        (mantras 9, 10, 11 spell out each correspondence;\n         mantra 12 names the silent fourth as turīya, &#x27;a-mātra&#x27;)\n\n   Reading: each audible mātrā &#x27;leads to&#x27; (nayate) its pāda;\n   the silent fourth is &#x27;where there is no going&#x27; (nāmātre vidyate gatiḥ — K1.23).</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Diagram 2","title":"Turīya by Negation (Mantra 7)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Fourth is approached only by sustained negation. The chain of na- prefixes (six prajñā-negations + six in-prefixed negations) strips the Self of every familiar attribute.","<pre>\n                       ┌──────────────────┐\n                       │     T U R Ī Y A     │\n                       │      (caturtha)    │\n                       └────────┬─────────┘\n                                │\n       ┌────────────────────────┼────────────────────────┐\n       │  Prajñā-negations:                              │\n       │    ─ not antaḥ-prajña (≠ Taijasa)              │\n       │    ─ not bahiḥ-prajña (≠ Viśva)                │\n       │    ─ not ubhayataḥ-prajña                       │\n       │    ─ not prajñāna-ghana (≠ Prājña)             │\n       │    ─ not prajña                                 │\n       │    ─ not a-prajña                              │\n       │                                                  │\n       │  In-attribute-negations:                        │\n       │    ─ a-dṛṣṭa (unseen)                          │\n       │    ─ a-vyavahārya (not-to-be-dealt-with)       │\n       │    ─ a-grāhya (ungraspable)                    │\n       │    ─ a-lakṣaṇa (mark-less)                     │\n       │    ─ a-cintya (unthinkable)                    │\n       │    ─ a-vyapadeśya (indesignable)               │\n       │                                                  │\n       │  Positive (only by indication):                  │\n       │    ─ ekātma-pratyaya-sāra (one-Self-cognition)  │\n       │    ─ prapañcopaśama (cessation of phenomena)    │\n       │    ─ śānta (peaceful)                          │\n       │    ─ śiva (auspicious)                         │\n       │    ─ advaita (non-dual)                        │\n       └─────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n                       saḥ ātmā · saḥ vijñeyaḥ\n                       &quot;He is the Self; He is to be known&quot;</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Diagram 3","title":"The Praṇava-Dhanus from Muṇḍaka 2.2.4 (Recalled here for OṂ-meditation context)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Though Muṇḍaka 2.2.4 supplies this image, it is the meditative back-drop for the Māṇḍūkya's OṂ-pāda mapping. The arrow's piercing (vyadh) of the target completes the meditation.","<pre>\n             A───U───M───(silent)\n             │   │   │      │\n             │   │   │      └──→ turīya (target)\n             │   │   └────────→ Prājña\n             │   └────────────→ Taijasa\n             └────────────────→ Vaiśvānara\n\n   praṇava (OṂ) = bow; ātman = arrow; Brahman = target.\n   The arrow does not return; śaravat tan-mayo bhavet —\n   &#x27;like an arrow, of-That-substance.&#x27;\n\n   Practical meditation:\n     1. recite A slow &amp; open (Vaiśvānara — waking)\n     2. recite U with elevation (Taijasa — dream)\n     3. recite M with closure (Prājña — deep sleep)\n     4. rest in the silence (a-mātra — turīya)\n   The silence after M is the meditation; the mātrās are the vehicle.</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Diagram 4","title":"The Ghaṭākāśa Analogy (Kārikā 3.3–9)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Advaita's pedagogical-central image. The one ākāśa appears as many pot-spaces; the one Self appears as many jīvas. When the pot breaks, the pot-space rejoins ākāśa.","<pre>\n                  ░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░\n                  ░          M A H Ā K Ā Ś A        ░\n                  ░       (the unbounded ākāśa /     ░\n                  ░             para-ātmā)          ░\n                  ░                                 ░\n                  ░  ╔═══╗   ╔═══╗   ╔═══╗  ╔═══╗  ░\n                  ░  ║ ░░║   ║ ░░║   ║ ░░║  ║ ░░║  ░\n                  ░  ║ ●║   ║ ●║   ║ ●║  ║ ●║  ░\n                  ░  ╚═══╝   ╚═══╝   ╚═══╝  ╚═══╝  ░\n                  ░  ghaṭa-  ghaṭa-  ghaṭa-  ghaṭa- ░\n                  ░  ākāśa   ākāśa   ākāśa   ākāśa  ░\n                  ░  (jīva)  (jīva)  (jīva)  (jīva) ░\n                  ░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░░\n\n   ─ Each pot&#x27;s interior space is the same one ākāśa, locally bounded.\n   ─ Pot breaks (death): the bounded space merges back into the unbounded.\n   ─ When one pot is dusty (one jīva suffers), the others are not.\n   ─ The variation (form, effect, name) is in pots; the ākāśa is one.\n\n   Kārikā 3.4: ghaṭādiṣu pralīneṣu ghaṭākāśādayo yathā / ākāśe\n               sampralīyante tadvaj jīvā ihātmani.</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Diagram 5","title":"The Alāta-Cakra (Firebrand-Wheel, Kārikā 4.47–52)","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The whirled firebrand produces apparent shapes (straight, curved, wheel); when the whirling stops, no shape exists. Similarly, consciousness vibrating produces appearance; still consciousness, no appearance.","<pre>\n   Vibrating (saspanda):                    Still (aspanda):\n   ═════════════════════                    ═════════════════\n                                                   \n       .─────────.                                 │\n      ╱ alāta-   ╲                                 │\n     ╱  cakra     ╲   ← apparent                   │  \n    │   ⊙─────⊙   │     wheel/                     │ ← single point;\n    │    ╲   ╱    │     curve/                     │   no wheel,\n    │     ╲ ╱     │     straight                   │   no shape,\n    │      ●      │     etc.                       │   no curve.\n    │     ╱ ╲     │                                │ ← the firebrand\n    │    ╱   ╲    │                                │   itself unborn,\n    │   ⊙─────⊙   │                                │   self-shining.\n     ╲           ╱                                 │\n      ╲_________╱                                  │\n                                                   \n   &quot;Consciousness vibrating          &quot;Consciousness still\n    produces grasper-grasped          produces no appearance —\n    appearance&quot;                        non-arisen, non-dual&quot;\n   (Kārikā 4.47)                     (Kārikā 4.48)\n\n   Doctrinal point: appearances do NOT come from outside the firebrand;\n   appearances do NOT go to anywhere; appearances do NOT re-enter the\n   firebrand. They are functions of the vibration only — no real\n   ontological status.  Apply to consciousness.</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Diagram 6","title":"The Four-State Discipline Mapped Across Time","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Māṇḍūkya's practical contribution: cultivate awareness across all four states. Each day repeatedly contains the four; the discipline is to sustain recognition through the transitions.","<pre>\n   T I M E ────►\n\n   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n   │                                                                  │\n   │  Wake─►──Wake──►──Wake─┐  Dream  ┌─Wake──►──Wake──►──Wake─┐  Sleep │\n   │                          ╲___╱                            ╲___╱   │\n   │  (Viśva)                 (Taijasa)                       (Prājña) │\n   │                                                                  │\n   │     A─────U─────M─────silence─────A─────U─────M─────silence       │\n   │                                                                  │\n   │  ╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗│\n   │  ║         T U R Ī Y A    (the silent witness)                  ║│\n   │  ║      (sarvadṛk sadā — all-seeing always)                     ║│\n   │  ╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝│\n   │                                                                  │\n   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n\n   ─ Each cycle (waking/dream/sleep) repeats through the day.\n   ─ Turīya is not a fourth segment, but the continuous witness\n     beneath the three.\n   ─ Practice: cultivate recognition of turīya through state-transitions.\n     The transition wake↔sleep is the practical training-ground.</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical Steps and Observations","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Eight contemplative practices extracted from the Māṇḍūkya and Kārikā. Each can stand as a daily contemplation; together they form a complete Māṇḍūkya sādhanā."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"1. OṂ-meditation with the four-mātrā mapping","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Māṇḍūkya's signature practice. Sit in stable posture. Settle the breath. Then:","(a) A — recite the A-sound slowly. Feel the mouth open. Feel awareness expand outward into the waking-world. This is the Vaiśvānara-recognition.","(b) U — recite U with rising pitch. Feel awareness elevate from outer to inner. This is the Taijasa-recognition: dream-imagination's subtle world.","(c) M — recite M, closing the lips. Feel awareness gather into the heart-cavity. This is the Prājña-recognition: the dense undifferentiated cognition.","(d) Silence — do not begin the next OṂ. Rest in the silence after M. This silence is the fourth mātrā (a-mātra); resting here is touching turīya.","Sit for ten to twenty minutes. The most common error: not pausing in the silence. Always pause after M, longer than you think necessary. Most of the practice is in the silence."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"2. The four-state observation discipline","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["From mantras 3–7. The practice has two parts: vertical (during meditation) and horizontal (across the day).","Vertical: while sitting, deliberately notice the state of attention. Is it outward (Vaiśvānara), inward-imaginative (Taijasa), or dense-quiescent (Prājña)? Notice which state you are in. The noticing-itself is the touch of turīya.","Horizontal: through the day, recognize each state. While working: Vaiśvānara. While daydreaming or in creative mind-wandering: Taijasa. While drifting into nap or deep stillness: Prājña. The recognition that the same Self underlies all three is the practical fruit.","Key: do not try to abolish any state. They are pādas of the Self. The discipline is to recognize the Self across them, not to eliminate any."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"3. The sleep-as-yoga practice","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["From mantras 5–6 and Kārikā 1.13–14. The transition into sleep is a practical opportunity. Most fall unconscious into Prājña; the discipline is to maintain a thin trace of awareness through the dissolution.","Practice: as you fall asleep, mentally hold OṂ. Do not try to control the falling-asleep; only maintain the trace of mantric awareness. With practice, the awareness can persist through deep sleep.","Sign of success: you wake refreshed but with a definite sense of having been somewhere conscious. The deep-sleep experience is no longer just unconsciousness.","Warning from Kārikā 1.13: do not confuse Prājña-with-bīja-nidrā for turīya. Deep sleep contains seeds (saṃskāras) that will re-manifest. Turīya has no seeds."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"4. The neti-neti meditation on mantra 7","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Mantra 7's long string of negations is itself a meditation. Sit. Mentally recite each negation, allowing each to actually remove its referent from awareness:","\"Not inward-knowing\" — drop the dream-self orientation. \"Not outward-knowing\" — drop the waking-self orientation. \"Not both\" — drop the transition-state. \"Not dense-cognition\" — drop deep-sleep. \"Not knowing\" — drop the active knowing-mode. \"Not non-knowing\" — drop the unconsciousness.","Then the in-prefix attributes: not seen, not dealt-with, not graspable, not marked, not thought, not designated. Each negation is to be felt as the dropping of a layer.","After all twelve negations, what remains? That residual is what the Upaniṣad calls eka-ātma-pratyaya-sāra. Do not name it; rest in it."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"5. The rope-snake recognition","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["From Kārikā 2.17–18. When the world appears as differentiated (jīvas, things, problems), recall the analogy: rope misperceived as snake in the dark. The remedy is light, not the killing of the snake.","Daily practice: in moments when the world appears solidly real and differentiated, deliberately recall: rajjur eva (\"only rope\"). Notice that the differentiation is mind-projected. Do not try to dissolve the differentiation by force; recognize its illusion-status.","Particularly useful in interpersonal conflict: the other person's perceived hostility is your rope-snake. Behind it is the rope of one Self."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"6. The asparśa-yoga of Kārikā 3.39–46","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Gauḍapāda's signature practice. Most yogas touch something — a breath, an image, an object. Asparśa-yoga touches nothing.","Sit. Settle the breath. Notice attention. Now: do not direct attention to anything. Do not contemplate an object. Do not visualize. Do not recite a mantra. Do not even try to keep the mind still. Simply allow attention to be without contact.","Two failure modes (Kārikā 3.42 and 44): laya — drifting into sleep-like dullness; vikṣepa — being distracted into objects. Both must be guarded. The middle is the asparśa-state: alert without object, awake without effort.","Discipline (3.44): if laya, wake; if vikṣepa, calm; if sakaṣāya (subtly defiled), recognize; if sama-prāpta (equipoised), do not disturb. Four-fold response to four-fold mind-state.","Kārikā 3.41's image of patience: \"as filling the ocean drop by drop with the tip of a kuśa-grass blade.\" Asparśa-yoga is not fast; it is patient."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"7. The ajāti contemplation","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["From Kārikā 3.20–48 and 4.4–6. The doctrine of non-origination as practice.","Whenever something arises in experience — a thought, a feeling, an event — recall: na kaścij jāyate (\"nothing is born\"; Kārikā 3.48 / 4.71). The seeming arising is just citta-spanda — mind-vibration. The substrate is unborn.","Most powerful application: in suffering. When grief or fear arises, instead of relating to it as a real arising thing, recall: this is mind-vibration; the substrate did not lose anything; the substrate is unborn. The suffering's status is the same as the apparent wheel from the whirled firebrand.","This is not denial of the felt experience. The experience is real-at-its-level; the doctrine concerns its ultimate ontology. The combination produces an unusually steady response to inner storms."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"8. The jaḍa-vat ācaraṇa (post-realisation conduct)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["From Kārikā 2.36–38. The realised one conducts himself \"like a jaḍa (insensate one)\" — outwardly indistinguishable from an ordinary unobtrusive person.","Even for seekers, the principle is useful in practice: do not display your sādhanā. The seeker who advertises his practices is invested in social recognition of those practices; the investment slows progress.","Discipline: when in social settings, notice the impulse to mention your meditation, your insights, your spiritual life. Each such impulse is the residual ego seeking recognition. The advanced practitioner is silent about all of this.","Sign of maturity: others cannot tell that you have a spiritual life unless they share one. The realised yati of 2.37 is \"without praise, without salutation, without (formal) oblation-cry, with shifting-or-fixed abode, of spontaneous action.\" The realised does not perform realisation."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Cross-references in the Upaniṣadic Corpus","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Māṇḍūkya and Kārikā draw on and are drawn from a wide range of Upaniṣadic, Vedic, and (in the Kārikā's case) Buddhist sources. The table below tracks the principal intertextual relations.","<table><tr><td>Locus</td><td>Parallel / quoted text</td><td>Relation</td></tr><tr><td>Śānti-pāṭha (Atharva-Veda)</td><td>Muṇḍaka 1.1.1 · Praśna 1.1 (shared śānti)</td><td>All three Atharvan Upaniṣads share the bhadraṃ karṇebhiḥ peace mantra; Māṇḍūkya adds the bhadraṃ no api vātaya manaḥ half-line.</td></tr><tr><td>Mantra 1 (OṂ-akṣaram)</td><td>Praśna 5.5 (Oṃ as means of reaching Brahman) · Kaṭha 1.2.15-17 (Oṃ as the supreme) · Bhagavad-Gītā 8.13 (Oṃ at death) · 17.23 (Oṃ-Tat-Sat triad)</td><td>Māṇḍūkya integrates the diffuse Oṃ-tradition into a systematic four-mātrā analysis.</td></tr><tr><td>Mantra 2 (ayam ātmā brahma)</td><td>Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 2.5.19 (ayam ātmā idaṃ sarvam) · Chāndogya 3.14.1 (sarvaṃ khalv idaṃ brahma) · Aitareya 3.1.3 (prajñānam brahma)</td><td>Māṇḍūkya states the canonical mahāvākya 'ayam ātmā brahma' — one of the four mahāvākyas of Vedānta.</td></tr><tr><td>Mantras 3-5 (Vaiśvānara, Taijasa, Prājña)</td><td>Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 4.3 (the famous waking/dream/deep-sleep analysis by Yājñavalkya) · Chāndogya 8.6-12 (the four states of the Self per Prajāpati's instruction to Indra)</td><td>Māṇḍūkya compresses the longer BĀU and Chāndogya analyses into prose mantras. Chāndogya 8.12 is particularly close in identifying turīya as the para-Self.</td></tr><tr><td>Mantras 3-4 (saptāṅga, ekonaviṃśati-mukha)</td><td>Chāndogya 5.18 (Vaiśvānara's seven cosmic limbs)</td><td>Māṇḍūkya borrows the seven-limbed Vaiśvānara from Chāndogya 5.18.2.</td></tr><tr><td>Mantra 6 (Prājña as Lord)</td><td>Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 3.7 (antaryāmī-brāhmaṇa — Yājñavalkya to Uddālaka)</td><td>Māṇḍūkya's identification of Prājña with antaryāmin echoes the BĀU's longest exposition of the inner-controller doctrine.</td></tr><tr><td>Mantra 7 (turīya)</td><td>Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 4.3.21 (after dream — 'in this state he sees not anything outside') · Chāndogya 8.7.1 (Prajāpati's promise of the supreme Self)</td><td>The negations of mantra 7 echo BĀU's neti-neti formula; the positive eka-ātma-pratyaya-sāra is the Māṇḍūkya's distinctive contribution.</td></tr><tr><td>Mantra 12 (saṃviśaty ātmanā ātmānam)</td><td>Bhagavad-Gītā 13.24 (ātmanā ātmānaṃ vetti — 'by Self knows the Self')</td><td>The reflexive Self-by-Self-knowing structure is the canonical Advaita formulation; both Māṇḍūkya and BG state it independently.</td></tr><tr><td>Kārikā 1.10 (turyo Lord)</td><td>Śvetāśvatara 3.7-9 (the supreme Lord description) · BĀU 4.4.22 (the antaryāmin)</td><td>The Kārikā's identification of turīya with Lord-status anticipates later Vaiṣṇava-Advaita.</td></tr><tr><td>Kārikā 1.16 (anādimāyayā supto)</td><td>Brahma-Sūtra 2.1.34 (anādi-māyā) · Yoga-Sūtra 2.5 (avidyā as anādi)</td><td>The māyā-supti (sleep in māyā) image becomes canonical in later Advaita.</td></tr><tr><td>Kārikā 2.17-18 (rope-snake)</td><td>Śaṅkara cites this analogy extensively in his Brahma-Sūtra-Bhāṣya · Vivekacūḍāmaṇi 196-202</td><td>The Kārikā provides the locus classicus; Śaṅkara made the analogy the central Advaita pedagogical image.</td></tr><tr><td>Kārikā 2.32 (na nirodho na cotpattiḥ)</td><td>Mūlamadhyamaka-Kārikā 25.3 (na sā svabhāvataḥ siddhā) · Lankāvatāra-sūtra 2.171-2</td><td>The most Madhyamaka-resonant verse in the Kārikā. Identical structural negations to Nāgārjuna; doctrinal context advaita-Vedāntic.</td></tr><tr><td>Kārikā 2.36 (jaḍa-vat ācaraṇa)</td><td>Bhagavad-Gītā 2.55-58 (sthita-prajña description) · Aṣṭāvakra-Saṃhitā 18 (vidvad-avadhūta)</td><td>The Kārikā's jaḍa-vat doctrine becomes the founding text of the later avadhūta tradition.</td></tr><tr><td>Kārikā 3.3-9 (ghaṭākāśa analogy)</td><td>Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka 2.5.1-15 (the madhu-brāhmaṇa, less explicit) · Pañcadaśī 6 (where Vidyāraṇya develops ghaṭākāśa fully)</td><td>The Kārikā is the principal scriptural authority for ghaṭākāśa-pedagogy. Later Vedānta develops it extensively.</td></tr><tr><td>Kārikā 3.39 (asparśa-yoga)</td><td>Yoga-Sūtra 1.20 (śraddhā-vīrya-smṛti-samādhi-prajñā-pūrvaka itareṣām)</td><td>Asparśa-yoga is the Kārikā's distinctive contribution; it both echoes and supersedes the standard Yoga-Sūtra meditation-vocabulary.</td></tr><tr><td>Kārikā 3.48 / 4.71 (na kaścij jāyate jīvaḥ)</td><td>(unique to Kārikā; restated in Advaita-Siddhi by Madhusūdana)</td><td>The Kārikā's bīja-mantra of non-origination, restated in two prakaraṇas for emphasis.</td></tr><tr><td>Kārikā 4.1 (vande dvi-padāṃ varam)</td><td>(no Vedāntic parallel; closest Buddhist parallel: salutation-style of Mahāyāna sūtras)</td><td>One of the most-disputed verses for Buddhist parallels; Śaṅkara reads it advaita-Vedāntically.</td></tr><tr><td>Kārikā 4.22 (na svato nāpi parataḥ)</td><td>Mūlamadhyamaka-Kārikā 1.1 (na svato nāpi parato na dvābhyāṃ nāpy ahetutaḥ)</td><td>Direct structural parallel to Nāgārjuna's foundational verse. The Kārikā's argument structure is Madhyamaka.</td></tr><tr><td>Kārikā 4.31-32</td><td>= Kārikā 2.6-7 verbatim</td><td>Internal repetition: the temporal-bracketing argument restated in Alāta-śānti.</td></tr><tr><td>Kārikā 4.42 (jātiḥ tu deśitā buddhaiḥ)</td><td>Bhagavad-Gītā 4.34 (tad viddhi praṇipātena) · Praśna 1.2 (Pippalāda's 'one further year of tapas')</td><td>The doctrine that texts teach progressively becomes a Vedāntic hermeneutic principle after Gauḍapāda.</td></tr><tr><td>Kārikā 4.47-52 (alāta-cakra)</td><td>(unique to Kārikā; later Vedānta extensively cites this image)</td><td>Gauḍapāda's signature image. Used by Śaṅkara in his bhāṣya as the central diagnostic image for citta-spanda.</td></tr><tr><td>Kārikā 4.81 (sakṛd-vibhāta)</td><td>Bhagavad-Gītā 13.18 (jyotiṣām api taj jyotiḥ) · Muṇḍaka 2.2.10 (na tatra sūryo bhāti) · Śvetāśvatara 6.14 (the same)</td><td>The 'one-time-shining' formulation echoes the Upaniṣadic light-of-lights tradition.</td></tr><tr><td>Kārikā 4.99 (naitad buddhena bhāṣitam)</td><td>(disputed reference; possibly to historical Buddha)</td><td>Most-disputed verse in the Gauḍapāda-as-Buddhist debate. Śaṅkara reads buddha as 'ordinary awakened person'; Vidhushekhara Bhattacharya restores historical-Buddha reference.</td></tr><tr><td>Kārikā 4.100 (closing namaskāra)</td><td>(no direct Vedāntic parallel)</td><td>Closing salutation of the entire work; recited at the end of every formal Māṇḍūkya-Kārikā study in the Advaita tradition.</td></tr></table>","On the Buddhist parallels: the verses listed as Madhyamaka-parallel (especially 2.32 and 4.22) are textually close to Nāgārjuna's formulations. Modern scholarship (King 1995, Comans 2000) treats these as evidence of Gauḍapāda's familiarity with Mahāyāna terminology used for advaita-Vedāntic ends — not as evidence that the doctrine itself is Buddhist. The historical question of Gauḍapāda's Buddhist exposure remains open; the doctrinal question of whether the Kārikā teaches advaita or śūnyavāda is settled — it teaches advaita."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Bibliography","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Selections drawn upon in this volume, organised by category."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Sanskrit text","title":"primary editions","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Hattangadi, Sunder (et al.). Māṇḍūkyopaniṣat with Gauḍapāda-Kārikā. sanskritdocuments.org ITRANS file (kaarikaa.itx, prepared by Anshuman Pandey, M. Giridhar, and Kim Poulsen; proofed by Aikya Param and David Lyttle).","Bhattacharya, Vidhushekhara. The Āgamaśāstra of Gauḍapāda. Edited with English translation, notes, and introduction. University of Calcutta, 1943. The most important modern critical edition of the Kārikā; argues for substantial Buddhist influence on Gauḍapāda.","Limaye, V. P., and R. D. Vadekar. Eighteen Principal Upaniṣads, Volume 1. Poona: Vaidika Saṃśodhana Maṇḍala, 1958. Includes Māṇḍūkya with critical apparatus.","Olivelle, Patrick. The Early Upaniṣads: Annotated Text and Translation. New York: Oxford University Press, 1998. Critical edition of the Upaniṣad (without Kārikā)."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Translations and commentaries","title":"the five used in this volume","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Gauḍapāda. Māṇḍūkya-Kārikā. Original Sanskrit text in the editions of Bhattacharya 1943 and Nikhilananda 1949.","Śaṅkara. Māṇḍūkyopaniṣad-Bhāṣya and Gauḍapādīya-Kārikā-Bhāṣya. Via Swami Gambhirananda, Eight Upaniṣads with the Commentary of Śaṅkarācārya, Volume 2 (Aitareya, Māṇḍūkya including Kārikā, Muṇḍaka, Praśna). Kolkata: Advaita Ashrama, 1958; many reprints. The most-important Vedāntic commentary; treats the Upaniṣad and the Kārikā as one work and defends Gauḍapāda's positions against Buddhist-parallel objection.","Sivananda, Swami. The Māṇḍūkya Upaniṣad and Kārikā (in The Principal Upanishads, 1937; and in lectures published by Divine Life Society). Rishikesh.","Krishnananda, Swami. The Māṇḍūkya Upaniṣad. Lecture series at Divine Life Society, 1968–69; published as a book by Divine Life Society, 1995; also available at swami-krishnananda.org. The most thorough modern Indian engagement with the Kārikā's metaphysics.","Nikhilananda, Swami. The Māṇḍūkyopaniṣad with Gauḍapāda's Kārikā and Śaṅkara's Commentary. Mysore: Sri Ramakrishna Ashrama, 1932; many editions. Most-cited English-language reference. Provides line-by-line Sanskrit, IAST, literal English, and full Śaṅkara-bhāṣya in English."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Other consulted translations","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Hume, Robert Ernest. The Thirteen Principal Upanishads. Oxford University Press, 1921; 2nd edition 1931. The first scholarly English translation of the Māṇḍūkya.","Radhakrishnan, S. The Principal Upaniṣads. London: George Allen &amp; Unwin, 1953. Includes Māṇḍūkya with cross-references.","Olivelle, Patrick. Upaniṣads. Oxford World's Classics, 1996. Critical philological translation."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Secondary scholarship on Gauḍapāda","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[["Mahadevan, T. M. P. Gauḍapāda","A Study in Early Advaita. Madras: University of Madras, 1952; 4th edition 1975. The classical Indian academic study."]],"notes":["King, Richard. Early Advaita Vedānta and Buddhism: The Mahāyāna Context of the Gauḍapādīya-Kārikā. Albany: SUNY Press, 1995. The standard modern monograph on the Buddhist-influence question. Concludes that Gauḍapāda uses Buddhist vocabulary for advaita-Vedāntic ends.","Comans, Michael. The Method of Early Advaita Vedānta: A Study of Gauḍapāda, Śaṅkara, Sureśvara, and Padmapāda. Delhi: Motilal Banarsidass, 2000. Useful comparative study.","Bhattacharya, Vidhushekhara. The Āgamaśāstra of Gauḍapāda. Calcutta, 1943 (already cited).","Wood, Thomas E. The Māṇḍūkya Upaniṣad and the Āgama Śāstra: An Investigation into the Meaning of the Vedānta. Honolulu: University of Hawaii Press, 1990. Modern analytical reconstruction."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"On Buddhist parallels","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Murti, T. R. V. The Central Philosophy of Buddhism. London: Allen &amp; Unwin, 1955. Comparative work locating Madhyamaka in Indian thought; treats Gauḍapāda as a Vedāntic counterpart.","Saksena, Shri Krishna. Nature of Consciousness in Hindu Philosophy. Delhi: Motilal Banarsidass, 1972. Comparative study of Gauḍapāda and Yogācāra.","Sengaku Mayeda. \"On Gauḍapāda's Position in Vedānta.\" Journal of the American Oriental Society 87 (1967): 153-181. Important philological study.","Karl Potter (ed.). Encyclopedia of Indian Philosophies, Volume 3: Advaita Vedānta up to Śaṅkara and his Pupils. Princeton, 1981. Includes a comprehensive section on Gauḍapāda's positions."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Cross-Upaniṣadic studies","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Deussen, Paul. Sechzig Upaniṣad's des Veda, 1897. Includes Māṇḍūkya; treats it as the foundation of Advaita.","Belvalkar, S. K. and R. D. Ranade. History of Indian Philosophy, Volume 2: The Creative Period. Poona, 1927.","Frauwallner, Erich. History of Indian Philosophy, Volume 1. Delhi: Motilal Banarsidass, 1973.","Cohen, Signe. The Upaniṣads: A Complete Guide. London: Routledge, 2018.","--- end of Mandukya_Upanishad.docx ---"],"kind":"note"}]}]};
